hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3445","(3445) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Dalaj ibn Ahmad al-Sajazi narrated to us — Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Turk and Musa ibn Muhammad, meaning al-Dhuhli, both said — Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. And Abu Ja'far Kamil ibn Ahmad al-Mustamli informed us — Abu Sahl Bishr ibn Ahmad al-Isfaranin narrated to us — Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi narrated to us — Yahya ibn Yahya said: I read to Malik — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id — from Abi Sa'id al-Khudri that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""When one of you is praying, let no one pass in front of him, and let him push him back as much as he can. If the person refuses, then let him fight him, for he is but a devil."""
"3446","(3446) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' narrated to us — Abu Khalid narrated from Ibn 'Ajlan — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri — from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""When one of you prays, let him pray toward a sutrah and let him come close to it."" Then he proceeded with the same meaning."
"3447","(3447) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū 'Alī al-Ḥusayn ibn 'Alī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; 'Imrān ibn Mūsā and Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn both said: Shaybān ibn Farrūkh narrated to us; Sulaymān al-Mughīrah narrated to us from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, who said: ""While I and a companion of mine were discussing a hadith, Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Sammān suddenly said: 'I shall narrate to you what I heard from Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī and saw him do.' He said: 'While I was with Abū Sa'īd performing the Friday prayer, behind a barrier screening him from the people, a young man from Banī Abī Mu'ayṭ came and attempted to pass in front of him. He pushed him away by the chest. The man looked around but found no way through except in front of Abū Sa'īd. So he returned and tried again, and Abū Sa'īd pushed him even more forcefully than the first time. The man then stood up and spoke harshly to Abū Sa'īd, then jostled through the crowd and went out. He went to Marwān and complained to him about what had happened. Meanwhile, Abū Sa'īd entered upon Marwān, and Marwān said to him: 'What is the matter with you and your nephew? He has come complaining about you.' Abū Sa'īd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""When one of you prays behind a barrier screening him from the people, and someone attempts to pass in front of him, let him push him away by the chest. If he refuses, let him fight him, for he is but a shayṭān.""'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 487.]"
"3448","(3448) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-'Adl informed us in Baghdad; Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Miqdām ibn Dāwūd narrated to us; al-'Abbās ibn Ṭālib narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wārith ibn Sa'īd narrated to us; Yūnus ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl al-'Adawī, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, that Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) was praying when a man from the family of Abī Mu'ayṭ passed in front of him. He prevented him, but the man refused to desist, so he shoved him. The man still refused, so he pushed him in the chest. At that time, Marwān was the governor over Madīnah. The man complained about this to Marwān, and Marwān mentioned this to Abū Sa'īd. Abū Sa'īd said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'When something passes in front of one of you while he is praying, let him prevent it. If it refuses, let him fight it, for it is but a shayṭān.'"" And I had been forbidding him, but he refused to desist. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ma'mar from 'Abd al-Wārith, using the wording of the hadith of Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah appended to that chain. This was a liberty on his part, except that he (may Allah have mercy on him) singled it out with this wording in the book Bad' al-Khalq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"3449","(3449) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī narrated to us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn 'Uthmān narrated to us; Ṣadaqah ibn Yasār narrated to me: I heard Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not pray except behind a sutrah (barrier), and do not let anyone pass in front of you. If he refuses, then fight him, for indeed the qarīn (companion shayṭān) is with him."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī, without the mention of the sutrah at the beginning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 506; Ibn Ḥibbān, 2369.]"
"3450","(3450) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Baghdādī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār, from Ṣuhayy al-Baṣrī, from Ibn 'Abbās, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was praying when a young goat attempted to pass in front of him, and he kept warding it off. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. Narrated by Abū Ya'lā, 2422; Abū Dāwūd, 709.]"
"3451","(3451) Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; 'Īsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Hishām ibn al-Ghāz narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""We descended with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from the pass of Athākhir. The time for prayer arrived, so he prayed facing a wall, making it his qiblah, and we were behind him. Then a young sheep came, attempting to pass in front of him. He kept fending it off until his belly was pressed against the wall, and it passed behind him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 708.]"
"3452","(3452) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Qa'nabī narrated to us from Mālik. (Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdillāh informed me; Abū 'Alī al-Ḥusayn ibn 'Alī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; 'Alī ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik from Abū al-Naḍr, from Busr ibn Sa'īd, that Zayd ibn Khālid sent him to Abū Juhaym to ask him what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding one who passes in front of a person praying. Abū Juhaym said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""If the one who passes in front of a person praying knew what sin was upon him, he would find it better for him to stand for forty [units of time] than to pass in front of him."" Abū al-Naḍr said: ""I do not know whether he said forty days, forty months, or forty years."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdillāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā."
"3453","(3453) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr both said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; al-'Abbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; 'Abdullāh ibn Yazīd, Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Muqri', narrated to us; Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ narrated to us from Abū al-Aswad Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Asadī, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Ā'ishah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked, during the expedition of Tabūk, about the sutrah of the one who prays. He said: 'Like the back of a saddle (mu'akhkharat al-raḥl).'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn Numayr from al-Muqri'. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 500.]"
"3454","(3454) Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; 'Alī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī 'Īsā informed us; 'Abdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqri' narrated to us; Sa'īd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to me; Abū al-Aswad narrated to me, and he mentioned it similarly. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from 'Abdullāh ibn Yazīd, in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reference as above.]"
"3455","(3455) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismā'īl ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū Sa'd al-Zāhid informed us by dictation, and Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us; Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd al-Thaqafī narrated to us; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us from Simāk, from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: ""When one of you places before him something like the back of a saddle, it will not harm him who passes behind that."" In the hadith of Abū 'Abdillāh: ""Let him pray and not concern himself with whoever passes behind that."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 499.]"
"3456","(3456) Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; 'Umar ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us; Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated to us from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah ibn 'Ubaydillāh, from his father, who said: ""We used to pray while the animals would pass in front of us. We mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: 'Something like the back of a saddle should be placed in front of one of you, and then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"3457","(3457) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʬAṭāʾ, who said: ""The rear piece of the saddle-pole is a cubit or more."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 686]"
"3458","(3458) And Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us, my grandfather narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʬAṭāʾ, who said: ""The rear piece of the saddle-pole is a cubit."" And Maʿmar said, from Qatādah: ""A cubit and a hand-span."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Rāhawayh, 315]"
"3459","(3459) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us, Muʿtamir narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: ""That the Prophet ﷺ would position his mount across and pray towards it."" He (the narrator) said: ""I said: 'What do you think if the camels have moved on?' He said: 'He would take the saddle-pole and set it upright and pray towards its rear end' — or he said, 'its back part.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī, and he added therein: ""And Ibn ʿUmar used to do the same."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 485]"
"3460","(3460) ʿAlī ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: ""That he would pray towards his camel while it was positioned across between him and the qiblah."" His statement ""a-fa-raʾayta"" (""what do you think?"") is from the speech of ʿUbayd Allāh to Nāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding one.]"
"3461","(3461) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥammādī narrated, and he mentioned the hadith similar to the hadith of al-Muqriʾ. The shaykh Abū Bakr said: ""It is likely that his statement 'a-fa-raʾayta' is from the speech of ʿUbayd Allāh to Nāfiʿ, not from the speech of Nāfiʿ to ʿAbd Allāh. That is because Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā and al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyāʾ both informed me, saying: Khallād ibn Aslam narrated to us, ʿUbaydah ibn Ḥumayd narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray and position the camel across between him and the qiblah.'"" Al-Qāsim said in his hadith: ʿUbayd Allāh said: ""I asked Nāfiʿ: 'When the camels have moved on, what is done?' He said: 'He would position the rear piece of the saddle-pole between him and the qiblah.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reference as above.]"
"3462","(3462) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him: ""That when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out on the day of ʿĪd, he would order a spear to be brought and placed before him, and he would pray towards it with the people behind him. He used to do this while travelling as well. It is for this reason that the commanders adopted the practice of carrying it."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr and others from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 472]"
"3463","(3463) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, Abū al-ʿUmays narrated from ʿAwn ibn Abī Juhayfah, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at al-Abṭaḥ. Then Bilāl came and gave him the call to prayer. He called for water for ablution and performed ablution. The people began to come and take the water of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ's ablution and wipe themselves with it. Then Bilāl took the spear and walked with it alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he established the prayer and planted it before him, and he prayed two rakʿahs. The riders were passing before him — women, donkeys, and camels."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd, all from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn. And Shuʿbah narrated it from ʿAwn, from his father, and said: ""Women and donkeys would pass behind the spear."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 473]"
"3464","(3464) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyāʾ ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Ḥarmalah — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Sabrah — narrated to us. He said: My uncle narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Let one of you screen his prayer, even if only with an arrow."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Aḥmad, 3/404]"
"3465","(3465) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh and Abū Saʿīd informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Hishām ibn Malās al-Numayrī al-Dimashqī narrated to us, Ḥarmalah ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Juhanī narrated to us. He said: My uncle ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""Screen yourselves in your prayers, even if only with an arrow."" [Ḥasan. Preceded in the one before it.]"
"3466","(3466) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah narrated to me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥurayth narrated to me that he heard his grandfather Ḥurayth narrating from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you prays, let him place something in front of his face. If he does not find anything, let him set up a staff. If he does not have a staff, let him draw a line. Then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him."" And likewise Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim narrated it from Ismāʿīl, and Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it in one of the two narrations from him from Ismāʿīl. And Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Ismāʿīl as well. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 689]"
"3467","(3467) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr and Abu Nasr — Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Fami — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Asid ibn 'Asim narrated to us — al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us — from Sufyan — Isma'il ibn Umayyah narrated to me — Abu 'Amr ibn Hurayth narrated to me — from his father — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you prays, let him place something before him. If he finds nothing, let him draw a line. Then whatever passes before him will not harm him."" [Da'if; previously referenced in the preceding entry.]"
"3468","(3468) Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated it from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abu 'Amr ibn Muhammad ibn Hurayth ibn Sulaym, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, with the wording of the hadith of Bishr — Abu al-Hasan al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad informed us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us — Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

Wuhayb and 'Abd al-Warith also narrated it from Isma'il, from Abu 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from his grandfather Hurayth. And 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who heard Isma'il from Hurayth ibn 'Ammar, from Abu Hurayrah, in an abridged form.

Ibn 'Uyyaynah also narrated it in the transmission of al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, and al-Humaydi and a group from him, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abu Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from his grandfather Hurayth al-'Udhri, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. Then it was reported that he (Hurayth) was uncertain about it.

Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus informed me — I read to him, he said: I heard 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi saying: I heard 'Ali, i.e., 'Abd Allah ibn al-Madini, saying: Sufyan said regarding the hadith of Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abu Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ: ""When one of you prays in an open desert, let him set up a staff before him.""

'Ali said: I said to Sufyan: They differ regarding it — some of them say Abu 'Amr ibn Muhammad, and some say Abu Muhammad ibn 'Amr. Sufyan reflected for a while, then said: I only remember Abu Muhammad ibn 'Amr.

I said to Sufyan: But Ibn Jurayj says Abu 'Amr ibn Muhammad. Sufyan remained silent for a while, then said: Abu Muhammad ibn 'Amr, or Abu 'Amr ibn Muhammad.

Then Sufyan said: I used to consider him a brother of 'Amr ibn Hurayth, and on another occasion he said al-'Udhri.

'Ali said: Sufyan said: A man from among the people of Egypt came to us — his name was Abu Mu'adh — and he said: I met this man from whom Isma'il narrated. This was after Isma'il ibn Umayyah had died, and so he sought this elder until he found him.

'Utbah said: I asked him about it, and he confused it for me.

Sufyan said: We found nothing to strengthen this hadith, and it did not come except from this direction.

Sufyan said: Isma'il, when he narrated this hadith, would say: Do you have anything by which you can strengthen it?

The elder said: Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, used this hadith as evidence in the earlier period, then he suspended judgment regarding it in the later period. In the book al-Buwayti he stated: ""The worshipper should not draw a line before him unless there is a firmly established hadith, in which case he should follow it."" As if he, may Allah have mercy on him, had stumbled upon what we have transmitted regarding the difference in its chain of narration. There is no harm in it for a ruling such as this, if Allah, the Most High, wills. And through Him is success. [Da'if; previously referenced in the preceding entry.]"
"3469","(3469) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — he said: Abu Dawud said: I heard Ahmad ibn Hanbal describing the line, and he said: ""Like this,"" meaning across, in the shape of a crescent."
"3470","(3470) Abu Sa'id al-Khatib informed us — Abu Bahr al-Barbahari informed us — Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us — he said: I asked al-Humaydi about the line, and he gestured to me like a large crescent. [Da'if]"
"3471","(3471) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me — my father narrated to me — Makki narrated to us — Yazid ibn Abi 'Ubayd narrated to us, saying: I used to come with Salamah to the mosque, and he would pray near the column that was by the place where the mushaf was kept. I said: O Abu Muslim, I see you seeking to pray at this column. He said: For I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking to pray at it.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Makki ibn Ibrahim, and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn Mannah, from Makki. [Sahih; al-Bukhari reported it (480).]"
"3472","(3472) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us — 'Ali ibn 'Ayyash al-Alhani informed us (in one chain) — and Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Mahmud ibn Khalid al-Dimashqi narrated to us — 'Ali ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us — Abu 'Ubaydah: al-Walid ibn Kamil narrated to us — from al-Muhallab ibn Hajr al-Bahrani — from Duba'ah bint al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad — from her father, who said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying towards a stick, a pole, or a tree without placing it on his right eyebrow or his left, and he would not face it directly. This is the wording of the hadith of al-Dimashqi. In the narration of al-Saghani, al-Walid ibn Kamil al-Bajali narrated to me — al-Muhallab ibn Hajr al-Bahrani narrated — he said: Duba'ah narrated to me, and he did not say ""ibn al-Aswad."" [Munkar; Abu Dawud reported it (693).]"
"3473","(3473) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed me — Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us — al-Walid ibn Kamil narrated to us — from al-Muhallab ibn Hajr al-Bahrani — from Duba'ah bint al-Miqdam — from her father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he prayed towards a sutrah, he would place it on his right eyebrow or his left, and he would not center it before him.

Muhammad ibn Himyar and Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid also narrated it from al-Walid ibn Kamil, and they said: ""al-Miqdad."" And in another narration from Baqiyyah, it is said: ""al-Miqdam."" And ""al-Miqdad"" is more correct. And Allah, the Most High, knows best.

This hadith is singular with al-Walid ibn Kamil al-Bajali al-Shami. Al-Bukhari said: ""With him are wonders."" And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Munkar; previously referenced in the preceding entry.]"
"3474","(3474) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Shadan and Ahmad ibn Salamah said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim said: My father narrated to me — from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: Between the prayer place of the Prophet ﷺ and the wall was a passage for a goat.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Amr ibn Zurarah, from 'Abd al-'Aziz, and Muslim narrated it from Ya'qub al-Dawraqi. [Sahih; al-Bukhari reported it (474).]"
"3475","(3475) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Ahmad — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrajani — both said: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us — Abu 'Asim narrated to us — Yazid ibn Abi 'Ubayd narrated to us — from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', who said: Between the minbar and the wall there was only enough space for a goat to pass.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Makki ibn Ibrahim, from Yazid ibn Abi 'Ubayd. [Sahih; al-Bukhari reported it (475).]"
"3476","(3476) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and Hamid ibn Yahya and Ibn al-Sarh said: Sufyan narrated to us — from Safwan ibn Sulaym — from Nafi' ibn Jubayr — from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, narrating up to the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: ""When one of you prays towards a sutrah, let him draw close to it, so that Satan does not interrupt his prayer.""

Abu Dawud said: Waqid ibn Muhammad also narrated it from Safwan, from Muhammad ibn Sahl, from his father, or from Muhammad ibn Sahl, from the Prophet ﷺ.

And some of them said: from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Sahl ibn Sa'd. And there is a difference regarding its chain of narration. [Sahih; Abu Dawud reported it (695).]"
"3477","(3477) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Ghadā'irī in Baghdad informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik al-Daqīqī informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Shu'bah from Wāqid ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd that he heard Ṣafwān narrating from Muhammad ibn Sahl from his father, or from Muhammad ibn Sahl from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When one of you prays towards something, let him draw close to it, so that Satan does not interrupt his prayer.""

The Shaykh said: Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated it from Nāfi' ibn Jubayr, mursal. [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than this; it was presented in the one before it.]"
"3478","(3478) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-'Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Dāwūd ibn Qays al-Madanī informed you that Nāfi' ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭ'im narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When one of you prays, let him pray towards a barrier (sutrah) and let him draw close to his barrier, for Satan passes between him and it.'""

The Shaykh said: Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah established its isnād. (c) He is a ḥāfiẓ, an authority. [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than this; see the one before it.]"
"3479","(3479) Abū Aḥmad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-'Adl informed us: Abū Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utban ibn Mas'ūd from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbās that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in Minā without a wall (in front of him). I came riding on my donkey, and I had on that day nearly reached the age of puberty. I passed in front of some of the rows, then I dismounted, let my donkey graze, and joined the people. No one objected to that.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismā'īl ibn Abī Uways from Mālik.

Abū 'Abd Allah al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-'Abbās informed us: al-Rabī' informed us: al-Shāfi'ī said: ""The statement of Ibn 'Abbās 'without a wall' means, and Allah knows best, without a barrier (sutrah).""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: These words were mentioned by Mālik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy on him, in this ḥadīth in the book al-Manāsik, and he narrated it in the book al-Ṣalāh without these words. Al-Shāfi'ī, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated from him in the older version as he narrated in al-Manāsik, and in the newer version as he narrated in al-Ṣalāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī narrated it.]"
"3480","(3480) Abū Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yūsuf informed us verbally: Abū Sa'īd Aḥmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah informed us. (ḥ) And Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Ismā'īl ibn Muhammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: both said: Sa'dān ibn Naṣr al-Makhramī narrated to us: Abū Mu'āwiyah from al-Ḥajjaj ibn Arṭāḥ from al-Ḥakam ibn 'Utaybah from Yaḥyā al-Jazzār from Ibn 'Abbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in an open space with nothing in front of him.

It has a witness with an isnād more authentic than this, from al-Faḍl ibn 'Abbās, and it will be mentioned after this, if Allah the Most High wills. [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than this; Aḥmad narrated it (1/224).]"
"3481","(3481) Abū al-Ḥusayn 'Alī ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishrān in Baghdad informed us: Abū Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Sa'dān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah from Kathīr ibn Kathīr ibn al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wada'ah al-Sahmī from some of his family, that he heard his grandfather, al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wada'ah, saying: ""I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying near the door of Banū Sahm, with people passing in front of him, and there was no barrier between him and the ṭawāf."" [Ḍa'īf; Abū Dāwūd narrated it (2016).]"
"3482","(3482) Abū 'Abd Allah al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn 'Abdūs informed me: I heard 'Uthmān ibn Sa'īd saying: I heard 'Alī, meaning Ibn al-Madīnī, saying regarding this ḥadīth: Sufyān said: ""I heard Ibn Jurayj saying: 'Kathīr ibn Kathīr informed me from his father, from his grandfather, saying: I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying while people were passing by.'"" Sufyān said: ""So I went to Kathīr and asked him: 'A ḥadīth that you narrate from your father?' He said: 'I did not hear it from my father; rather, some of my family narrated it to me from my grandfather, al-Muṭṭalib.'""

'Alī said: His statement 'I did not hear it from my father' is severe upon Ibn Jurayj. Abū Sa'īd 'Uthmān said: Meaning Ibn Jurayj did not get it right.

The Shaykh said: And it has been narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Kathīr from his father, he said: ""Some of the elders of Banū al-Muṭṭalib narrated to me from al-Muṭṭalib."" And the narration of Ibn 'Uyaynah is more reliable. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"3483","(3483) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muhammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us: Abū 'Amr 'Uthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Abū Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah al-Munādī narrated to us: Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us: Shu'bah from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl al-'Adawī, who said: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ṣāmit saying: I heard Abū Dharr saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""The prayer of a man is interrupted when there is not in front of him the like of the back of a saddle pole — a woman, a donkey, and a black dog."" I said: ""O Abū Dharr, what is the matter with the black (dog) as opposed to the white or the red?"" He said: ""O son of my brother, I asked the Prophet ﷺ just as you have asked me, and he said: 'The black dog is a devil (shayṭān).'""

Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shu'bah and Yūnus ibn 'Ubayd and Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah and Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim and Salm ibn Abī al-Dhayyāl and 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl. He presented the ḥadīth of Yūnus first, then referred the rest to it, and this from him, may Allah have mercy on him and on us, is a concession. So the ḥadīth of some of them is like this. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim narrated it (510).]"
"3484","(3484) Abū 'Abd Allah al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Muhammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānī' narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr ibn 'Abd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Shaybān ibn Farrūkh narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us: Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ṣāmit from Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The prayer of a man is interrupted when there is not in front of him the like of the back of a saddle pole — a woman, a donkey, and a black dog."" I said: ""O Abū Dharr, what about the black dog as opposed to the red dog or the white dog?"" He said: ""O son of my brother, I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ just as you have asked me, and he said: 'The black dog is a devil (shayṭān).'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh, except that he did not present it in full. And thus 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal said from Ḥumayd: he made the beginning of the ḥadīth from the statement of Abū Dharr, then made it marfū' with the question at the end.

Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl al-Bukhārī objected to using the narration of 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ṣāmit as evidence. Other than him among the ḥuffāẓ used it as evidence. Al-Shāfi'ī, may Allah have mercy on him, indicated the weakness of the ḥadīth in this chapter and its contradiction of what is more established than it.

(q) Either it is not preserved (ghayr maḥfūẓ), or what is intended by it is that he becomes distracted by something that passes in front of him, so it cuts him off from being occupied with it — not that it invalidates the prayer. And this is what is most appropriate to carry the ḥadīth upon. So we use as evidence an isnād like that of this ḥadīth.

It has witnesses, some of which have an authentic isnād like it. [Ṣaḥīḥ; it was presented in the one before it.]"
"3485","(3485) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: al-Makhzūmī narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Aṣamm narrated to us: Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm narrated to us from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""A prayer is interrupted by a woman, a dog, and a donkey, and that is prevented by the like of the back of a saddle pole.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm.

And it is narrated from Qatādah from Zurārah ibn Awfī from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. And it is said: from him from Zurārah from Sa'd ibn Hishām from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. And it is said: from him from al-Ḥasan from 'Abd Allah ibn Mufayyil — both of them — from the Prophet ﷺ, in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim narrated it (511).]"
"3486","(3486) And Abū Naṣr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatādah informed us: 'Alī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqīl al-Khuzā'ī informed us: Abū Shu'ayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: 'Alī ibn 'Abd Allah al-Madanī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd narrated to us: Shu'bah from Qatādah, who said: I heard Jābir ibn Zayd narrating from Ibn 'Abbās from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The prayer is interrupted by a menstruating woman and a dog."" Yaḥyā — he is al-Qaṭṭān — said: ""No one raised this ḥadīth from Qatādah other than Shu'bah."" Yaḥyā said: ""And I distinguish between them.""

He said: And it was narrated by Ibn Abī 'Arūbah and Hishām from Qatādah, i.e., as mawqūf. Yaḥyā said: ""And it has reached me that Hammām inserts between Qatādah and Jābir ibn Zayd Abū al-Khalīl."" 'Alī said: ""And Hammām did not raise the ḥadīth.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: What is firmly established from Ibn 'Abbās is that none of that invalidates the prayer, but it is disliked (yukrah). This is indicated by his statement along with his statement ""it interrupts"" — that what is intended by ""interruption"" (al-qaṭ') is not invalidation (al-ifsād).

And it is narrated from another route from Ibn 'Abbās, may Allah be pleased with them both. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Abū Dāwūd narrated it (703).]"
"3487","(3487) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Baḥr al-Qaṭṭan narrated to us: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us: my father narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Ikrimah, the mawla of Ibn 'Abbas, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: I think he traced it back to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The dog, the donkey, the menstruating woman, the Jew, the Christian, the Magian, and the pig interrupt the prayer. But if they pass at a distance of a stone's throw from you, they do not interrupt your prayer."" [Munkar. Abu Dawud narrated it (704)]"
"3488","(3488) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Basri, the mawla of Banu Hashim, narrated to us: Mu'adh — he mentioned something similar to it, except that he said: I think it was from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""When one of you prays without a sutrah, it interrupts his prayer."" He did not mention the Christian, and he said: ""And the woman,"" but did not mention the menstruating woman. He said: ""And it suffices if they pass in front of him at a distance of a stone's throw."" [Munkar. Previously referenced in the hadith before it]"
"3489","(3489) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Anbari narrated to us: Waki' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from a mawla of Yazid ibn Nimran, from Yazid ibn Nimran, who said: ""I saw a man in Tabuk who could not stand. He said: 'I passed in front of the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying and I was on a donkey, and he said: O Allah, sever his stride (i.e., paralyze his legs).' After that, I could no longer walk on them."" [Da'if. Abu Dawud narrated it (705)]"
"3490","(3490) Abu 'Ali informed us: Abu Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Kathir ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: Abu Haywah narrated to us, from Sa'id, with its chain and meaning, and added: ""He said: 'He has cut off our prayer; may Allah cut off his stride.'"" Abu Dawud said: Abu Mushir narrated it from Sa'id: ""He has cut off our prayer."" [Da'if. Previously referenced in the hadith before it]"
"3491","(3491) Abu 'Ali informed us: Abu Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Hamdani and Sulayman ibn Dawud both narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Mu'awiyah informed me, from Sa'id ibn Ghazwan, from his father, who said: ""He stopped at Tabuk while he was performing Hajj, and there was a man who could not stand. I asked him about his condition, and he said: 'I will narrate to you a hadith, but do not narrate it to anyone as long as I am alive: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped at Tabuk near a date palm and said: This is our qiblah. Then he prayed toward it. I was a young boy running, and I passed between him and it, and he said: He has cut off our prayer; may Allah cut off his stride. I have not been able to stand on them to this day.'"" [Da'if. Abu Dawud narrated it (707)]"
"3492","(3492) Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf — narrated to us: Abu Sa'id al-A'rabi narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'faranī narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be praying during the night, and I would be lying across between him and the qiblah, like a funeral bier lies across."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of 'Uqayl and the brother of al-Zuhri, from al-Zuhri. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari narrated it (376)]"
"3493","(3493) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi — narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mas'ud narrated to us: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, who said: ""I heard 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: 'What do you say about what interrupts the prayer?' He said: 'The woman and the donkey.' She said: 'The woman is a wretched beast! I have seen myself lying across in front of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like a funeral bier while he was praying.'"" Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Sahih. Muslim narrated it (512)]"
"3494","(3494) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, who said: ""I heard 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be praying and I would be lying across in front of him.'"" Shu'bah said: Sa'd said: ""And I think she said: 'While I was menstruating.'"" [Sahih. Al-Tayalisi narrated it (1457)]"
"3495","(3495) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih — informed us: Abu al-Muthanna and Abu Muslim both narrated to us: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us: Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, the mawla of 'Umar ibn 'Ubayd Allah, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""I used to sleep in front of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with my legs in his qiblah. When he prostrated, he would nudge me and I would draw my legs in. When he stood, I would extend them."" She said: ""And the houses at that time had no lamps."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya, from Malik. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari narrated it (375)]"
"3496","(3496) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah, from 'A'ishah, who said: ""I used to be lying across in the qiblah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be praying while I was in front of him. When he wanted to pray the Witr, he said: Move aside."" And 'Urwah narrated from 'A'ishah: ""When he wanted to pray the Witr, he would wake me and I would pray the Witr."" And this is more correct. [Sahih ghayrihi. Abu Dawud narrated it (711)]"
"3497","(3497) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us: my father narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated to us, he said: Ibrāhīm narrated to me from al-Aswad from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. He said: and Muslim narrated to me from Masrūq from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. The matter of what invalidates the prayer — the dog, the donkey, and the woman — was mentioned in her presence, so ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""You have likened us to donkeys and dogs! By Allah, I used to see the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) praying while I was lying on the bed between him and the qiblah. When I needed something, I disliked sitting up and thereby troubling the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so I would slip away from the side of his feet."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ, and Muslim narrated it from ʿUmar and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 492.]"
"3498","(3498) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us from al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm from al-Aswad from ʿĀʾishah. He said: It was said to her: ""Some people say that the prayer is invalidated by the dog, the donkey, and the woman."" She said: ""Behold, they have equated us with dogs and donkeys! I would often see the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) praying at night while I was on the bed between him and the qiblah. When I had a need, I would slip away from the side of the bed's legs, disliking that I should turn my face toward him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"3499","(3499) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿImrān ibn Mūsá narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Jarīr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated to us from Jarīr from Manṣūr from Ibrāhīm from al-Aswad from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. She said: ""You have equated us with dogs and donkeys! I remember myself lying on the bed when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would come and place himself in the middle of the bed and pray. I disliked sitting before him, so I would slip away from the side of the bed's legs until I slipped out from under my blanket.""

Qutaybah said in his ḥadīth: Jarīr narrated to us from Manṣūr from Ibrāhīm, who said: al-Aswad said it from ʿĀʾishah.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"3500","(3500) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us from al-Zuhrī — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed him — that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās saying: ""I and al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās came on the day of ʿArafah while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was leading the people in prayer, and we were on a she-donkey of ours. We passed by a portion of the row, so we dismounted from it and left it to graze, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said nothing to us.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 504. The statement ""I and al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās"" is not in Muslim's version.]"
"3501","(3501) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. The group said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated to us from Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""I came riding upon a she-donkey, and I was at that time approaching the age of puberty, while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was leading the people in prayer at Minā. I passed in front of a portion of the row, so I dismounted and sent the she-donkey to graze, and I joined the row, and no one objected to that.""

In the ḥadīth of al-Shāfiʿī: ""I sent my donkey to graze and entered the row, and no one objected to that."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 76.]"
"3502","(3502) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read before Mālik, and he mentioned it with the same meaning as the ḥadīth of al-Qaʿnabī, except that he said: ""in front of the row,"" and he said: ""and no one objected to that.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated it from Ibn Shihāb, and he said: ""during the Farewell Pilgrimage.""

Maʿmar narrated it from Ibn Shihāb, and he said: ""during the Farewell Pilgrimage,"" or he said: ""on the Day of the Conquest."" The Farewell Pilgrimage narration is more accurate.

It has been narrated to us in the narration of Mālik in the book of al-Manāsik from al-Muwaṭṭaʾ that he said in this ḥadīth: ""toward other than a wall.""

al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""Meaning — and Allah knows best — toward other than a sutrah (screen).""

This indicates the error of the one who claims that he (ﷺ) prayed toward a sutrah, and that the sutrah of the imām is the sutrah of the follower. For this reason, the passing of the donkey in front of them did not invalidate their prayer. In the narration of Mālik there is evidence that he (ﷺ) prayed without a sutrah, and Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"3503","(3503) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl al-Khuzāʿī informed us: Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us from Manṣūr from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār from Abī al-Ṣahbāʾ, who said: ""We were in the presence of Ibn ʿAbbās, and the matter of what invalidates the prayer was mentioned before him, so he said: 'The dog, the woman, and the donkey.'""

Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""I and a young man from Banī Hāshim — or Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib — came riding together upon a donkey while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was leading the people in prayer in an open space. We dismounted from the donkey and left it in front of them, and he paid no attention to it. And two young girls from Banī Hāshim came rushing while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was leading the people in prayer, and they fought with each other, so he took hold of them and separated one from the other, and he paid no attention to it."" [Jayyid. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah, 835.]"
"3504","(3504) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us from al-Ḥakam from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār from Ṣuhayb. I said: ""Who is Ṣuhayb?"" He said: ""A man from the people of Baṣrah."" He reported from Ibn ʿAbbās that he and a young man from Banī Hāshim were upon a donkey and passed in front of the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was praying, and he did not turn away because of that. And two young girls from Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib came and took hold of the knees of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he separated between them — meaning he reconciled between them — and he did not turn away because of that."" [Qawī. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"3505","(3505) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us from Sufyān from Salamah ibn Kuhayl from al-Ḥasan al-ʿUranī from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""I and a young man from Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib came upon a donkey while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in prayer. We sent the donkey away and entered into the prayer. And two young girls from Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib came racing, so the Prophet (ﷺ) separated between them and did not let anything interrupt his prayer. And a young goat fell in front of him from an opening, and he did not let it interrupt his prayer."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in its meaning through other chains. Narrated by Aḥmad, 1/308.]"
"3506","(3506) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī — by recitation — informed us, and ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Mahdī al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us verbatim. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Munqidh al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Idrīs, meaning Ibn Yaḥyā, narrated to us from Bakr ibn Muḍar from Ṣakhr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥarmalah, that he heard ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrate from Anas ibn Mālik: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led the people in prayer, and a donkey passed in front of them. So ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah said: 'Subḥān Allāh! Subḥān Allāh!' When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had given the salām, he said: 'Who was the one who just glorified Allāh — Subḥān Allāh wa bi-ḥamdihī?' He said: 'It was I, O Messenger of Allah. I had heard that the donkey invalidates the prayer.' He (ﷺ) said: 'Nothing invalidates the prayer.'"" [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī, 1/367.]"
"3507","(3507) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Shaybān narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from Sālim, who said: It was said to Ibn ʿUmar, ""Indeed, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah says: The dog and the donkey break the ṣalāh."" So Ibn ʿUmar said: ""Nothing breaks the ṣalāh of a Muslim."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah (2885)]"
"3508","(3508) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, who said: Ibn Jurayj said: Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī informed me from ʿAbbās ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbbās from al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) visited ʿAbbās in one of our desert encampments, and we had a young dog and a she-donkey that were grazing. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed ʿAṣr while both of them were in front of him; they were not moved away nor were they driven off. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd (718)]"
"3509","(3509) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me from my grandfather from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb from Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī from ʿAbbās ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbbās from al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us while we were in a desert encampment, and ʿAbbās was with him. He prayed in open ground with no sutrah (screen) in front of him, while our she-donkey and young dog were frolicking before him, and he paid no attention to that. [Ḍaʿīf. Referred to in the preceding narration]"
"3510","(3510) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us: Mujālid narrated from Abū al-Wadāk from Abū Saʿīd from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Nothing breaks the ṣalāh, but ward off (the passer-by) as much as you can, for he is a shayṭān (devil)."" [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd (697, 719)]"
"3511","(3511) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Mujālid narrated to us: Abū al-Wadāk said: A young man from Quraysh passed in front of Abū Saʿīd while he was praying, so he pushed him away. Then he came back, so he pushed him away. Then he came back again, so he pushed him away — three times. When he finished (the prayer), he said: ""Nothing breaks the ṣalāh, but the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Ward off (the passer-by) as much as you can, for he is a shayṭān (devil).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi. The hadith is ṣaḥīḥ with the wording ""Nothing breaks the ṣalāh,"" but without that wording it is not established. See the preceding narration]"
"3512","(3512) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Saʿīd ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Hishām and Shuʿbah both said: Qatādah narrated to us from Saʿīd that ʿUthmān and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: ""Nothing breaks the ṣalāh of a Muslim, but ward off (the passer-by) as much as you can."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭaḥāwī in Sharh al-Maʿānī (1/464)]"
"3513","(3513) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb from Sālim from his father, who used to say: ""Nothing that passes in front of the one praying breaks the ṣalāh.""

Abū ʿUqayl Yaḥyā ibn al-Mutawakkil al-Bāhilī narrated it from Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Makkī from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh, and he raised it (to the Prophet), but the correct version is that it is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik (369)]"
"3514","(3514) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us from Sufyān al-Thawrī from Simāk from ʿIkrimah, who said: Ibn ʿAbbās was asked, and it was said to him, ""Do the dog, the donkey, and the woman break the ṣalāh?"" So Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""{To Him ascends the good word, and righteous work raises it} [Fāṭir: 10]"" — so these do not break this (the ṣalāh), but it is disliked (for them to pass).""

Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Sufyān narrated it with something similar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd (694)]"
"3515","(3515) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad ibn Ayman narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq, from one who narrated to him from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī, who said: I said to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, ""Narrate to me from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Do not pray behind one who is sleeping or one who is conversing.'"" This is the best narration reported on this topic, and it is mursal.

Hishām ibn Ziyād, Abū al-Miqdām, narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb, and he is matrūk (abandoned).

The most authentic athar narrated on this topic. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd (694). It is mursal.]"
"3516","(3516) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us from Sufyān from Abū Isḥāq from Maʿdīkarib al-Hamdānī, who said: ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — said: ""Do not form rows between the pillars, and do not pray while in front of you are people who are pulling (ropes in a contest) or playing.""

This mawqūf narration pertains to people pulling (in a contest) in front of him, whose voices and speech distract him from khushūʿ (devotion) in ṣalāh; so he should avoid that as much as he can. As for praying while in front of him is a sleeping person from whom no distraction is feared, the Prophet (ﷺ) used to do that. And that is in what... [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr (9295)]"
"3517","(3517) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Muḥāḍir ibn al-Mūriʿ narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And he said: Abū al-Walīd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) used to pray his night prayer while I was lying across between him and the qiblah. When he wished to perform the witr, he would wake me, and I would perform the witr.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Musaddad from Yaḥyā from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its takhrīj was given previously in ḥadīth no. 3492.]"
"3518","(3518) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Masʿūdī informed us: Abū Sinān informed me, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), that he was asked about the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {Those who are humble in their prayer} [al-Muʾminūn: 2]. He said: ""Khushūʿ (humility) is in the heart, and that you soften your shoulder for the Muslim, and that you do not turn around in your prayer.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ḥākim narrated it in 2/426.]"
"3519","(3519) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawīyah and Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar both said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah, meaning Ibn Ṣāliḥ, narrated to us, from Rabīʿah, meaning Ibn Yazīd, from Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī. He said: And Abū ʿUthmān narrated it to me, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, who said: ""We were assigned the tending of camels. My turn came, and I drove them back in the evening. I came upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) standing, addressing the people. I heard part of his words: 'There is no Muslim who performs wuduʾ and does it well, then stands and prays two rakʿahs, devoting his heart and face to them, except that Paradise becomes obligatory for him.' I said: 'How excellent is this!' Then someone in front of me said: 'The one before it is even more excellent.' I looked and it was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: 'I saw you just now arriving.' He said: 'There is none among you who performs wuduʾ and then says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, except that the eight gates of Paradise are opened for him, and he may enter through whichever of them he wishes.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and he said: from Abū Idrīs, from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir. He said: And Abū ʿUthmān narrated it to me. Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ is the one who says this.

And it has already passed in the Book of Purification from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he performed wuduʾ: ""Whoever performs wuduʾ similar to this wuduʾ of mine, then stands and prays two rakʿahs in which he does not speak to himself, his past sins will be forgiven.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim narrated it as 234.]"
"3520","(3520) Abū al-Qāsim ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us: Wakīʿ narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ, from Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw us raising our hands in the prayer, so he said: 'Be tranquil in the prayer.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim narrated it as 430.]"
"3521","(3521) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated it with his isnād. He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us while we were raising our hands in the prayer, so he said: 'What is the matter with you that I see you raising your hands as if they were the tails of restive horses? Be tranquil in the prayer.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ashajj from Wakīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was given in the one before it.]"
"3522","(3522) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, who said: ""Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him), when he stood in the prayer, was as still as a piece of wood. And it was narrated that Abū Bakr used to be the same."" He said: ""And it used to be said: that is khushūʿ (humility) in the prayer."" And it has been narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd that he said: ""Be firm in the prayer,"" meaning: be tranquil in it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad narrated it in Faḍāʾil al-Ṣaḥābah as 230.]"
"3523","(3523) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān. He said: al-Aʿmash narrated to me, from Abū al-Ḍuḥā, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: ""Be firm in the prayer.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Kabīr as 9343.]"
"3524","(3524) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, regarding His words {Those who are humble in their prayer} [al-Muʾminūn: 2]. He said: ""Tranquility in it.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarī narrated it in his Tafsīr as 9/196.]"
"3525","(3525) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us: Saʿīd narrated to us, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, regarding {Those who are humble in their prayer} [al-Muʾminūn: 2]. He said: ""Those who are fearful.""

[Ḥasan. Al-Ṭabarī narrated it in his Tafsīr.]"
"3526","(3526) And with his isnād, from Qatādah, regarding His words {Those who are humble in their prayer} [al-Muʾminūn: 2]. He said: ""Khushūʿ (humility) is in the heart, and lowering the gaze in the prayer.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ through other than this chain. Al-Ṭabarī narrated it in his Tafsīr as 9/196.]"
"3527","(3527) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh al-Mihrānjānī informed us: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī informed us: Abū Muslim narrated to us: Abū ʢĀṣim narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from al-Maqburī, from ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakam, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAnamah, that ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir entered the mosque and performed a prayer in which he made it brief. So I said: ""O Abū al-Yaqẓān, you have indeed made it light."" He said: ""Did you see me omit anything from its prescribed limits? Rather, I hastened it out of precaution against the inattentiveness of Shayṭān. Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'A man may perform the prayer, yet nothing is recorded for him of it except a tenth, a ninth, an eighth, a seventh, a sixth, a fifth, a quarter, a third, or a half thereof.'"" This is how Ibn ʿAjlān narrated it from Saʿīd al-Maqburī.

It was also narrated by ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from ʿUmar ibn Abī Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām, from his father, that ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir performed two rakʿahs, whereupon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith said to him: ""O Abū al-Yaqẓān, I think you have made it brief.""

It was also narrated by Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith, from ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakm ibn Thawbān, from Abū Lās al-Khuzāʿī, who said: ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir entered and he mentioned it.

It was also narrated by ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakm, from Abū al-Yusr, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Among you are those who perform the prayer in its complete form, and among you are those who perform a half, a third, a quarter, a fifth,"" until he mentioned the tenth. (T) It was also narrated by Khālid ibn Yazīd, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""A servant performs the prayer, yet nothing is recorded for him except a tenth of his prayer, a ninth, an eighth, a seventh, until his prayer is recorded for him in its complete form."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad 4/321]"
"3528","(3528) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Tājir narrated to us: Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Ḥanẓalī narrated to us, and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us, both saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated to us, from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade a man from being restless in his prayer. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥākim 1/405]"
"3529","(3529) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī and Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl narrated to us, both saying: Musaddad narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us: Ashʿath ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from his father, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about turning (one's gaze) during the prayer. He said: ""It is a snatching by which the Shayṭān snatches (reward) from the prayer of the servant."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad.

Similarly, it was narrated by Shaybān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah, from Ashʿath, from his father.

It was also narrated by Misʿar, from Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from Abū Wāʾil, from Masrūq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī 718]"
"3530","(3530) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Zakariyyā al-Sājī and Ibn Nājiyah narrated to us, both saying: Muḥammad ibn Khallād al-Bāhilī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Misʿar narrated it, except that al-Sājī said: from ʿĀʾishah, who raised it (as a marfūʿ narration). [Has been mentioned in the preceding hadith]"
"3531","(3531) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us in al-Mustadrak: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: I heard Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrating to us in the gathering of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, saying: Abū Dharr said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Allah, exalted be His praise, continues to face the servant while he is in his prayer, so long as he does not turn (his gaze). When he turns, He turns away from him."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd 909]"
"3532","(3532) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Layth narrated to us: Yūnus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: I heard Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrating in the gathering of Ibn al-Musayyib that Abū Dharr said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, continues to face the servant so long as he does not turn (his gaze). When he turns his face away, He turns away from him.""

It was also narrated by al-Ḥārith al-Ashʿarī from the Prophet (ﷺ) with the same meaning. [Ḍaʿīf. Has been mentioned in the preceding hadith]"
"3533","(3533) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Dallūyah al-Daqqāq narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar ibn Manīʿ narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām narrated to me: my brother Zayd ibn Salām informed me, that he heard his grandfather Abū Salām say: al-Ḥārith al-Ashʿarī narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed to Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā, so he stood and praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'Allah has commanded you with the prayer. When the servant stands to pray, Allah faces him with His countenance; so let him not turn his face away from Him until the servant himself turns his face away.'""

It was also narrated by Abū Tawbah from Muʿāwiyah, and he said in the hadith: ""When you have set your faces (toward the qibla), do not turn (your gazes)."" It was also narrated by Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Zayd ibn Salām, and he said: ""When you stand for the prayer, do not turn (your gazes)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī 2863]"
"3534","(3534) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Shādhān narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed in a khamīṣah (garment) that had markings, and he said: ""The markings of this khamīṣah have distracted me. Take it to Abū Jahm and bring me his anbijānīyah (a plain, thick garment).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī 366]"
"3535","(3535) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had a khamīṣah, which he gave to Abū Jahm, and in exchange he took an anbijānīyah from him. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the khamīṣah is better than the anbijānīyah."" He said: ""Indeed, I was looking at its markings during the prayer."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim 556. See also the preceding hadith]"
"3536","(3536) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us, both from Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Anas, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""What is the matter with people that they raise their gazes toward the sky during their prayer?"" He spoke so sternly about it that he said: ""They must desist from that, or their eyesight will be snatched away.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī 717]"
"3537","(3537) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed you from Jaʿfar ibn Rabīʿah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Let people desist from raising their gazes toward the sky during supplication in Ṣalāh, or their eyesight will be snatched away.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and others, from Ibn Wahb, and he also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn Samurah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 429]"
"3538","(3538) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us from al-Aʿmash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ, from Tamīm ibn Ṭarfah, from Jābir ibn Samurah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Let people desist from raising their gazes toward the sky during Ṣalāh, lest their eyesight not return to them.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 428]"
"3539","(3539) Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwn, from Muḥammad, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed, he would raise his head toward the sky, his eyes moving about, looking this way and that. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {Successful indeed are the believers, those who are humble in their prayers} [al-Muʾminūn: 1–2]. So Ibn ʿAwn lowered his head and bowed it toward the ground.

And something similar was narrated from Abū Zayd Saʿīd ibn Aws, from Ibn ʿAwn, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, but the correct one is the disconnected (mursal) version. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī in his Tafsīr, 9/196]"
"3540","(3540) Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mihrānī and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, saying: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn al-Raffāʾ al-Harawī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Saʿīd Abū Zayd al-Anṣārī narrated to us, mentioning it, except that he said: He used to look around during Ṣalāh until this verse was revealed: {Successful indeed are the believers, those who are humble in their prayers} [al-Muʾminūn: 1–2]. So he lowered his head. Abū Zayd described it for us. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ḥākim, 2/393]"
"3541","(3541) ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Ḍabbī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad, who said: I was informed that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed, he would raise his gaze toward the sky. Then a verse was revealed—if it was not {those who are humble in their prayers} [al-Muʾminūn: 2], then I do not know which verse it was. Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn used to love that one's gaze should not go beyond one's place of prayer. This is the preserved narration, and it is mursal. It was also narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm, that is Ibn ʿUlayyah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration. [Ḍaʿīf, as mentioned in the preceding narration.]"
"3542","(3542) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī narrated to me; Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to me; my father informed me; Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed, he would raise his gaze toward the sky. Then {those who are humble in their prayers} [al-Muʾminūn: 2] was revealed, and he lowered his head.

Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated it from Ayyūb as a mursal narration, and this is the preserved version. [Munkar, as mentioned in the preceding narration.]"
"3543","(3543) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Ibn Salm narrated to us; Duḥaym narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us from Ṣadaqah ibn ʿAbdillāh, from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Khawlānī, who said: I heard Abū Qilābah al-Jarmī say: Ten of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) narrated to me about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)—his standing, his bowing, and his prostration—in a manner similar to the prayer of Amīr al-Muʾminīn, meaning ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu). Sulaymān said: I observed ʿUmar during his prayer, and his gaze was fixed upon his place of prostration. And he mentioned the remainder of the ḥadīth, but it is not strong. [Munkar. Narrated by Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil, 3/275]"
"3544","(3544) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā, that is Ibn Jaʿfar al-ʿAṭṭār al-Baghdādī, narrated to us; Naṣr ibn Ḥammād narrated to me; al-Rabīʿ ibn Badr narrated to me from ʿUnẓuwānah—and in the narration of Abū Ṣādiq, from ʿUnẓuwānah—from al-Ḥasan, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, where should I place my gaze during Ṣalāh?"" He said: ""At the place of your prostration, O Anas."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is difficult; I cannot do that."" He said: ""Then in the obligatory prayer (al-Maktūbah).""

Abū ʿAbdillāh said: Abū al-ʿAbbās said: ""It has reached me that it should be ʿUnẓuwānah, but thus it is in my book."" The Shaykh said: A group narrated it from al-Rabīʿ ibn Badr from ʿUnẓuwānah. (j) And al-Rabīʿ ibn Badr is weak, and what has preceded is sufficient. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ḥākim in Maʿrifat ʿUlūm al-Ḥadīth, 252]"
"3545","(3545) ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdān informed it to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥinnāʾī narrated to us; al-Fuḍayl ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; ʿUlaylah ibn Badr narrated to us; ʿUnẓuwānah narrated to us from al-Ḥasan, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: ""O Anas, place your gaze where you prostrate.""

And it has been narrated to us from Mujāhid and Qatādah that they both used to dislike closing the eyes during Ṣalāh. And a musnad ḥadīth has been narrated concerning it, but it is nothing. [Munkar, as mentioned previously.]"
"3546","(3546) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, from Abū Dharr, reaching the Prophet (ﷺ).

And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustūyah narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; al-Zuhrī narrated to us, saying: I heard Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrating from Abū Dharr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When one of you stands for Ṣalāh, mercy is facing him, so let him not sweep away the pebbles.""

Sufyān said: Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm al-Zuhrī said: ""Who is Abū al-Aḥwaṣ?"" al-Zuhrī said: ""Have you not seen the old man who prays in the garden?"" al-Zuhrī began to describe him, while Saʿd did not know him.

The wording is that of al-Ḥumaydī's ḥadīth. In the narration of Yaḥyā: ""When one of you stands for Ṣalāh, mercy is facing him, so let him not sweep away the pebbles."" The story of Saʿd was not mentioned. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 945. As mentioned in the preceding narration.]"
"3547","(3547) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʪsā narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Shaybān narrated from Yaḥyā, from Abū Salamah, who said: Muʿayqib narrated to me that the Prophet ﷺ said regarding a man who levels the earth where he prostrates: ""If you must do so, then only once.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it through another route from Shaybān, and through other routes from Hishām al-Dastawāʾī from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 1149]"
"3548","(3548) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdād informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ayyūb al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated from Abū Salamah, from Muʿayqib, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Do not wipe away the pebbles while you are praying. If you must do so, then only once — leveling the pebbles."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to earlier]"
"3549","(3549) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abū Baṣrah al-Ghifārī, from Abū Dharr, who said: Wiping the pebbles is only once, and that I should not do it is dearer to me than a hundred black-eyed she-camels.

Mujāhid narrated it from Abū Dharr from the Prophet ﷺ regarding wiping the pebbles: ""Only once."" And it was also narrated from Mujāhid from Abū Wāʾil from Abū Dharr.

And we have narrated from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, that he leveled the pebbles with his shoes before entering the prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī, 469]"
"3550","(3550) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Abū Jaʿfar al-Qāriʾ, who said: I saw ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, when he would go down to prostrate, lightly wipe the pebbles from the place of his forehead.

The Shaykh said: This much is permitted. The disapproval pertains only to playing with them. If one were to level them before entering the prayer, as ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, did, that would be more appropriate. And with Allah lies success.

And we have narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab that he saw a man playing with the pebbles and said: ""Had this man's heart been humble, his limbs would have been humble."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik, 371]"
"3551","(3551) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform iʿtikāf during the middle ten days of Ramadan. One year he performed iʿtikāf until the night of the twenty-first — which was the night he would emerge from his iʿtikāf — and he said: ""Whoever performed iʿtikāf with me, let him perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days. I was shown this night, then I was made to forget it. I saw myself prostrating in water and mud the following morning. So seek it in the last ten days, and seek it in every odd-numbered night.""

Abū Saʿīd said: The sky rained that night, and the mosque was built upon palm branches, so the mosque dripped. Abū Saʿīd said: My eyes saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turn toward us, and upon his forehead and nose were traces of water and mud, on the morning of the twenty-first.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways from Mālik.

Al-Bukhārī said: Al-Ḥumaydī used to cite this hadith as evidence that one should not wipe the forehead during prayer, because the Prophet ﷺ was seen with water and mud on the tip of his nose and his forehead after he had prayed. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 1983]"
"3552","(3552) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Ibn Masʿūd, who used to say: Four things are among the acts of coarseness (jafāʾ): a man urinating while standing; a man praying while people pass in front of him and there is nothing between his hands to screen him; a man wiping the earth from his face while he is in prayer; and a man hearing the muʾadhdhin and not responding to what he says.

Similarly, al-Jurayrī narrated it from Ibn Buraydah from Ibn Masʿūd. And Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ziyād ibn Jubayr ibn Ḥayyah narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ with the same meaning, except that he said: ""And blowing during the prayer"" instead of ""passing,"" and he did not say ""four.""

Al-Bukhārī said: This is a munkar hadith; they are inconsistent in it. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Tārīkh, 3/495]"
"3553","(3553) The Shaykh said: Hārūn ibn Hārūn al-Taymī, a Medinan, narrated it from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Four things are among the acts of coarseness: a man urinating while standing; or excessively wiping his forehead before completing his prayer; or hearing the muʾadhdhin give the adhān and not saying what he says; or praying on a path where people would interrupt his prayer.""

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Dimashqī narrated to us: Ḥudhaym narrated to us: Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to me: Hārūn ibn Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Hudayr al-Taymī narrated it.

Abū Aḥmad said: His narrations from al-Aʿraj and others are not corroborated by trustworthy narrators.

Abū Aḥmad said: al-Junaydī narrated to us: al-Bukhārī said: Hārūn ibn Hārūn is not corroborated in his hadith. He narrates from al-Aʿraj. It is said he is the brother of Muḥarrar al-Taymī al-Madanī.

The Shaykh said: It has been narrated through other routes, all of which are weak. And it was narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās that he said: ""One should not wipe the earth from his face during prayer until he has recited the tashahhud and given the salām."" [Munkar. Al-Arwāʾ, 1/97]"
"3554","(3554) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Muḥāḍir narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī, from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, who said: The angels continue to send blessings upon a person so long as the trace of prostration remains on his face.

Al-ʿAbbās said: No one other than him narrated it.

The Shaykh said: We have narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr that he counted it among the acts of coarseness, and from al-Ḥasan that he saw no harm in it. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym in al-Ḥilyah, 3/272]"
"3555","(3555) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding His statement {Their mark is on their faces from the trace of prostration} [al-Fatḥ: 29], he said: It means the beautiful countenance (al-samt al-ḥasan). [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī in his Tafsīr, 11/369]"
"3556","(3556) Abū Muḥammad Junāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Junāḥ al-Muḥāribī in al-Kūfah informed us: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Ḥudhaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: al-ʿUmarī narrated from Sālim Abū al-Naḍr, who said: A man came to Ibn ʿUmar and greeted him. He said: ""Who are you?"" He said: ""I am your foster-son, so-and-so."" He saw between his eyes a black mark of prostration and said: ""What is this mark between your eyes? I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with them — do you see anything here [i.e., any such mark]?"" [Ḍaʿīf. This chain is weak.]"
"3557","(3557) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from his father, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he saw a mark (of prostration) and said: ""O servant of Allah, a man's form is his face, so do not disfigure your form."" [Sound. This is a sound, connected chain of narration.]"
"3558","(3558) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us; Yazīd ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Sufyān, from Thawr ibn Yazīd, from Abī ʿAwn, who said: ""Abū al-Dardāʾ saw a woman with a mark on her face like the callus of a goat's knee, and he said: 'Had this not been on your face, it would have been better for you.'""

And it has been narrated to us from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd: that he disapproved of it and said: ""By Allah, it is not a sign (of piety)."" [Very weak.]"
"3559","(3559) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Sahl — Bishr ibn Aḥmad ibn Bishr al-Mihrajānī — informed us; Abū Muḥammad — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Qaṭṭān al-Baghdādī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Khurāsānī narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā narrated from Ḥumayd, who is Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ""We were with al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd when al-Zubayr ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf came to him, and he said: 'He has disfigured his face. By Allah, it is not a sign (of piety). By Allah, I have prayed upon my face for such and such (a long time), and prostration has not left any mark on my face.'"" [Sound. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Kabīr, 668.]"
"3560","(3560) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū Manṣūr — al-ʿAbbās ibn Faḍl al-Ḍabbī al-Harawī — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, who said: ""I asked Mujāhid about the verse: {Their mark is on their faces from the trace of prostration} [al-Fatḥ: 29] — 'Is it the trace of prostration on a person's face?' He said: 'No. One of them may have between his eyes something like the knee of a goat, yet he is as Allah wills (in terms of) evil. Rather, it is from humility (khushūʿ).'""

He said: ""And Jarīr narrated to us from Thaʿlabah, from Jaʿfar ibn Abī Mughīrah, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: 'It refers to the purity of the dew and the moisture of the earth.'"" [Sound. Al-Ṭabarī narrated it in other than his Tafsīr, 11/369.]"
"3561","(3561) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""One has been forbidden from al-taṣarruʿ in prayer.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Nuʿmān, from Ḥammād, and he said: ""One has been forbidden from al-khaṣr in prayer."" [Sound. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, 1161.]"
"3562","(3562) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade al-taṣarruʿ in prayer."""
"3563","(3563) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ḥibbān narrated to us; Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us; Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man from praying while placing his hand on his waist (mutakhaṣṣir).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā, from Ibn al-Mubārak. He also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abī Khālid and Abī Usāmah, from Hishām, in this manner. Al-Bukhārī alluded to it, but he narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān, from Hishām: ""He forbade."" [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 1162, and Muslim, 545.]"
"3564","(3564) Abū al-Ḥusayn — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān — informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Mālik ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""One has been forbidden from al-ikhtiṣār in prayer."" I said to Hishām: ""Did he attribute it to the Prophet ﷺ?"" He indicated with his head: ""Yes."" [Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 947.]"
"3565","(3565) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us; ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal — narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated it with a similar wording, and added: ""We said to Hishām: 'What is al-ikhtiṣār?' He said: 'A person places his hand on his waist while he is praying.'""

And Salamah ibn ʿAlqamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with the meaning of this explanation. [Sound. Narrated by Aḥmad, 2/290.]"
"3566","(3566) It has been narrated from ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus, from Hishām, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Al-ikhtiṣār in prayer is the comfort of the people of the Fire.""

The Imam Abū ʿUthmān informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us; my grandfather informed me; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Mughīrah al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated it. [Denounced (munkar). Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah, 909.]"
"3567","(3567) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar al-Muqriʾ informed us in Baghdad: Abū Muḥammad Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAlī al-Khaṭabī informed us: Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan al-Nasāʾī informed us: Muʿallā ibn Asad informed us: Wahyib, from Ibn ʿAwn, from Muḥammad, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace be upon him) prohibited placing the hands on the waist during prayer (al-taṣṣur fī al-ṣalāh).

And Abū Hilāl al-Rāsibī similarly narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced as no. 3562]"
"3568","(3568) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ al-Mihrajānī informed us therein: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Saʿīd ibn Ziyād, who said: Ziyād ibn Ṣubayḥ narrated to me, saying: I prayed alongside Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) while I did not recognize him. I placed my hand on my waist, so he removed my hand. When I completed the prayer, I said: ""What did you object to in me?"" He said: ""You did, you did"" — he said: ""Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to prohibit al-ṣalbr (crossing/placing hands on the waist) during prayer."" (T) And Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated it from Saʿīd, saying: ""About al-taṣṣur (placing hands on the waist) during prayer."" And it has been narrated to us from ʿĀʾishah and Ibn ʿAbbās that they both considered it disliked (karihā dhālik). [Jayyid. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 903]"
"3569","(3569) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj informed us: Baqiyyat ibn al-Walīd informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: ""Two steps — one of them is dearer to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and the other is more hateful to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. As for the step that Allah loves, it is when a man notices a gap in the row and fills it. And as for the step that Allah hates, it is when a man wishes to stand, he extends his right leg and places his hand upon it, and plants his left leg, and then rises."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥākim, 1/506]"
"3570","(3570) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Saʿd ibn Ḥamawyah al-Nasawī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azdraqī informed us: Abū al-Naḍr informed us: Shuʿbah, from Maysarah, from al-Minhāl, from Abū ʿUbaydah, from ʿAbd Allāh, that he saw a man who had placed his feet together during prayer, and he said: ""He has erred against the Sunnah. Had he kept space between them, it would have been more beloved to me.""

And it has been narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr that he placed his feet together during prayer. In what has passed, he said: ""Placing the feet together and placing the hand upon the hand is from the Sunnah.""

The ḥadīth of Ibn al-Zubayr is connected (mawṣūl), and the ḥadīth of Abū ʿUbaydah from his father is mursal (disconnected), and Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī, 892]"
"3571","(3571) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Shaybānī in al-Kūfah informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Zuhrī informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Shaybān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Hilāl ibn Yisāf, who said: I arrived in al-Raqqah, and one of my companions said to me: ""Would you like to meet a companion of the Prophet (peace be upon him)?"" I said: ""A great fortune indeed!"" So he directed us to Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad. I said to my companion: ""Let us first observe his outward appearance."" He was wearing a closely-fitting qalansūtah with two flaps, and a coarse woolen burnūs of dusty hue. He was leaning on a staff during his prayer. We spoke to him after he had offered the salām, and he said: ""Umm Qays bint Miḥṣan (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated to me that when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) grew old and heavy, he set up a pillar in his prayer place and would lean upon it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ḥākim, 1/397]"
"3572","(3572) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah, from al-Ḥajjāj, from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: ""The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to lean upon staffs during prayer."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah, 3407]"
"3573","(3573) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Bishr ibn Hilāl informed us: ʿAbd al-Wārith, from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, who said: I asked Nāfiʿ about a man who prays while interlocking his fingers. He said: Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""That is the prayer of those upon whom wrath has fallen.""

And the ḥadīth of Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah regarding the prohibition of interlocking the fingers (al-tashbīk) after performing ablution or after entering into prayer — its place is in the Book of Friday Prayer (Kitāb al-Jumuʿah). And if it is established, it is general, applying to all prayers. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 993; Abū Dāwūd, 562]"
"3574","(3574) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed me: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid informed me: Ibn Abī Maryam informed me: al-Layth ibn Saʿd, from Zabbān ibn Fāʾid, that Sahl ibn Muʿādh narrated to him from his father Muʿādh, a companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Laughing aloud in prayer, turning around (al-multafit), and cracking one's fingers (al-mutaqqiʿ aṣābiʿah) are of the same status.""

Muʿādh here is Ibn Anas al-Juhanī. And Zabbān ibn Fāʾid is not strong (ghayr qawī), and Allah knows best. [Munkar. Narrated by Aḥmad, 3/438]"
"3575","(3575) And Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūd al-Tamīmī informed us. (Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Makhlad ibn Jaʿfar al-Bāqarḥī informed us. They both said: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān al-Marwazī informed us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhʾib, from al-Maqburī, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: ""Indeed, Allah, the Most High, loves sneezing (al-ʿuṭās) and dislikes yawning (al-thaʾūb). So when one of you sneezes and praises Allah, it is a right upon every Muslim who hears him to say: 'Yarḥamuk Allāh (May Allah have mercy on you).' As for yawning, it is from Satan. So when one of you yawns, let him suppress it as much as he can. For indeed, when one of you says 'Hāh,' Satan laughs at him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 5872]"
"3576","(3576) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me: Abū Yaʿlā informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed us. They both said: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Yawning is from Satan. So when one of you yawns, let him restrain it (yakẓim) as much as he is able.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 2994]"
"3577","(3577) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn al-ʿAllāʾ narrated from Wakīʿ.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān, from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""When one of you yawns during the prayer, let him suppress it as much as he can, for indeed the Shayṭān enters.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 2995]"
"3578","(3578) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, and he mentioned it with a similar wording, except that he said: ""Let him place his hand over his mouth."" And he did not mention the prayer. And Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal and ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī, from Suhayl, with the meaning of this wording. [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī, 2746]"
"3579","(3579) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAṭṭār both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, from Sumayy, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he sneezed, would lower his voice and cover his face. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ, 1849]"
"3580","(3580) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ibn ʿAjlān, who said: Sumayy narrated to me, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he sneezed, would hold his hand or his garment over his mouth, and thereby lower his voice. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously]"
"3581","(3581) And Yaḥyā ibn Yazīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Nawfalī narrated from his father, from Dāwūd ibn Farāhīj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to dislike a forceful sneeze in the mosque.

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī narrated it to us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us; ʿUmar ibn Sinān al-Manbajī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿīd al-Jawharī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yazīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated it.

Abū Aḥmad said: Yaḥyā ibn Yazīd is weak (ḍaʿīf), and his father Yazīd is weak.

The Shaykh said: And in the first ḥadīth there is sufficiency. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil, 7/247]"
"3582","(3582) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me from his father, who said: I was with ʿUthmān when he called for his ablution water, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""There is no Muslim person for whom an obligatory prayer becomes due, and who then perfects its ablution, its humility, and its bowing, except that it serves as an expiation for the sins that preceded it, so long as he does not commit a major sin — and that is for all time.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir and others, from Abū al-Walīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 228]"
"3583","(3583) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated from al-Walīd, meaning Ibn Kathīr, who said: Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated to me, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day prayed and then turned and said: ""O so-and-so, do you not perfect your prayer? Let the one who prays consider how he prays, for indeed he prays for himself. By Allah, I see from behind me just as I see from in front of me.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb, from Abū Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 423]"
"3584","(3584) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan, that is ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān, narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, meaning al-Juʿfī, narrated from Zāʾidah, from Ibrāhīm, meaning al-Hajarī, from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever perfects his prayer where people see him and performs it poorly when he is alone — that is a belittlement by which he belittles his Lord."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā, 5117]"
"3585","(3585) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated from Saʿd ibn Isḥāq ibn Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, from Maḥmūd ibn Labīd, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet ﷺ came out and said: ""O people, beware of the hidden shirk."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the hidden shirk?"" He said: ""A man stands and prays, and strives to adorn his prayer because of what he sees of the people's gaze upon him — that is the hidden shirk."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. In the possession of Ibn Khuzaymah, 928]"
"3586","(3586) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān; Abū Naṣr narrated to us; Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd narrated from Salmān al-Fārisī, that he said: ""The prayer is a measure (mikyāl). Whoever fulfills it, it will be fulfilled for him. And whoever falls short — you know well what has been said regarding those who give short measure."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah, 3750]"
"3587","(3587) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Abū Naṣr — and he is ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — with the same meaning. [He (the narrator) is weak; this has been mentioned previously.]"
"3588","(3588) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Asadī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: Qatādah narrated to us. He said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Spitting in the mosque is a sin, and its expiation is burying it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 405]"
"3589","(3589) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Spitting in the mosque is a sin, and its expiation is burying it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ; this has been mentioned previously]"
"3590","(3590) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated to us: Wāṣil, the freedman of Abū ʿUyaynah, narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Tamīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Shaybān ibn Farūkh narrated to us: Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated to us: Wāṣil narrated from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUqayl, from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar, from Abī al-Aswad, from Abī Dharr. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The deeds of my Ummah were presented to me — their good and their evil. I found among the good deeds of their actions the removal of harm from the road, and I found among the evil deeds of their actions phlegm that is in the mosque and is not buried.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ and Shaybān ibn Farūkh. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim narrated it, no. 553]"
"3591","(3591) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd said: al-Qaʿnabī informed us: Abū Mawdūd narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥadrad al-Aslamī. He said: I heard Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever enters the mosque and spits or expectorates in it, let him dig and bury it. If he is unable to do so, then let him spit into his garment and then take it out with him."" [Ḥasan; Abū Dāwūd narrated it, no. 477]"
"3592","(3592) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us: Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī ʿUthmān al-Zāhid narrated to us by dictation, and Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir narrated to us by recitation: Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-Faḍl Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us. He said: I heard al-Qāsim ibn Mihrān narrating from Abī Rāfiʿ, from Abī Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ): He saw phlegm or spittle in the direction of the qiblah, so I stood and scraped it. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Would any one of you like a man to come to him while he is praying and spit or expectorate in his face? When any one of you prays, let him not spit in front of him nor to his right, but to his left or beneath his foot. If he cannot do so, then let him spit into his garment and rub it.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Ghundar. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim narrated it, no. 550]"
"3593","(3593) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym narrated from al-Qāsim ibn Mihrān, from Abī Rāfiʿ, from Abī Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu). He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When any one of you is in his prayer, let him not spit in front of him, for he is facing his Lord, and not to his right, but to his left or beneath his left foot. If he is unable to do so, then let him spit into the edge of his garment, and then fold part of it over the other.""

Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: It is as though I am looking at the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) folding part of his garment over the other.

Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ; this is the wording of Muslim, no. 550]"
"3594","(3594) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Bakr narrated to us: Ḥumayd narrated from Anas. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw phlegm in the direction of the qiblah, and he disliked it until that was seen on his face. He scratched it and then said: ""When any one of you stands in his prayer, he is conversing with his Lord"" — or he said: ""His Lord is between him and the qiblah"" — ""so let him spit to his left or beneath his foot."" Then he took the edge of his garment, spat into it, and folded part of it over the other, and then said: ""Or let him do like this."" [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 397]"
"3595","(3595) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Anas ibn Mālik: The Prophet (ﷺ) saw phlegm in the direction of the qiblah, and it distressed him until that was seen on his face. He stood and scratched it with his hand, and then said: ""When any one of you stands in his prayer, he is conversing with his Lord, or his Lord is between him and the qiblah, so let none of you spit in the direction of his qiblah, but to his left or beneath his foot."" Then he took the edge of his cloak and spat into it, then folded part of it over the other, and said: ""Or let him do like this.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ; this has been mentioned previously]"
"3596","(3596) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed me: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: Qatādah narrated to us. He said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When the believer is in his prayer, he is conversing with his Lord, so let him not spit in front of him nor to his right, but to his left beneath his foot."" [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 403]"
"3597","(3597) Abū ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī narrated from Shuʿbah: ""Rather, to his left or beneath his foot."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me, who said that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, who said that Abū ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī narrated to us from Shuʿbah, from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Let none of you spit in front of him or to his right, but rather to his left or beneath his foot."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās and from Abū ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah, similar to the ḥadīth of Ādam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously.]"
"3598","(3598) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, who said that Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, who said that al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, from Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muḥāribī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""When you pray, do not spit in front of you or to your right, but spit to your left if it is clear, or beneath your foot."" And he said, ""With his foot,"" as though he was rubbing it with his foot. Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated it from Manṣūr and said: ""Or beneath his left foot."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 478.]"
"3599","(3599) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, who said that Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, who said that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, who said that Maʿmar informed us from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you stands for prayer, let him not spit in front of him, for he is in private converse with Allah so long as he is in his place of prayer, nor to his right, for there is an angel to his right, but let him spit to his left or beneath his foot, and then bury it."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 406.]"
"3600","(3600) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, who said that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, who said that Musaddad narrated to us, who said that Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated to us, saying: al-Jurayrī narrated to me from Abū al-ʿAlāʾ from his father, that he prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and he expectorated and then rubbed it with his left shoe. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ, and Abū al-ʿAlāʾ is Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 554.]"
"3601","(3601) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāh al-Muzakkī informed us, saying that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, who said that Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, who said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me that he heard Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw phlegm on the qiblah wall, took a pebble, and scraped it. Then he said: ""Let none of you spit towards the qiblah or to his right, but let him spit to his left or beneath his left foot."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah and others from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through other routes from Ibn Shihāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 400.]"
"3602","(3602) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, who said that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, who said that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, who said that Mālik informed us from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw spittle on the qiblah wall and scraped it. Then he turned to the people and said: ""When one of you is praying, let him not spit in the direction of his face, for Allah, the Most High, is before his face when he prays."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 398.]"
"3603","(3603) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me, who said that Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām and Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl al-ʿAnbarī both narrated to us, saying that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, who said: I read to Mālik, and he mentioned it similarly. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Referred to previously.]"
"3604","(3604) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, who said that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, who said that Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, who say that Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet ﷺ, while he was delivering a sermon, saw phlegm on the qiblah wall of the mosque. He became angry with the people of the mosque and then said: ""Allah, the Most High, is before any one of you when he prays, so let him not spit or expectorate."" Then he came down and scraped it with his hand, and then he rubbed it—I think he said—with saffron. Ibn ʿUmar said: ""When one of you expectorates, let him expectorate to his left."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb without the phrase about rubbing with saffron, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUlayyah from Ayyūb, without that, in the meaning of the ḥadīth of Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 720.]"
"3605","(3605) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, who said that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us, who said that Qutaybah narrated to us from Mālik, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he saw spittle, or mucus, or phlegm on the qiblah wall and scraped it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. They both also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 399. Narrated by Muslim, 549.]"
"3606","(3606) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Adamī in Baghdad informed us, who said that Aḥmad ibn Ziyād ibn Mihrān al-Simsār narrated to us, who said that Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf narrated to us, who said that Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us from Yaʿqūb ibn Mujāhid Abū Ḥazrah, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Walīd ibn ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: We visited Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh in his mosque, and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us in this mosque of ours, and in his hand was a branch of a date-palm (ʿurjūn ibn ṭāb). He saw phlegm on the qiblah wall of the mosque and scraped it with the branch. Then he turned to us and said: ""Which of you would love for Allah to turn away from him?"" He said: We were filled with awe. Then he said: ""Which of you would love for Allah to turn away from him?"" He said: We were filled with awe. Then he said: ""Which of you would love for Allah to turn away from him?"" He said: We said: None of us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""When one of you stands to pray, Allah is before his face, so let him not spit before his face or to his right, but let him spit beneath his left foot. And if something urgent overtakes him, let him do thus with his garment."" Then he folded part of his garment over another part. He said: ""Bring me perfume (ʿabīr)."" A young man from the tribe hastened to his family and returned with khalūq perfume in his palm. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it and placed it on the tip of the branch, then applied it to the trace of the phlegm. Jābir said: ""It is from this that you came to use perfume in your mosques."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf, and he said: ""Let him spit to his left beneath his left foot."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 3008.]"
"3607","(3607) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Muslim narrated to us, Muslim — meaning Ibrāhīm — narrated to us, Hishām — meaning al-Dastawā'ī — narrated to us, Yaḥyā — meaning Ibn Abī Kathīr — narrated from al-Ḥaḍramī, from a man among the Anṣār, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""When one of you finds a louse while he is praying, let him not kill it; rather, let him hold it until he finishes his prayer."" And ʿAlī ibn Mubārak said, on the authority of Yaḥyā: ""Let him hold it and then remove it"" — meaning from the mosque. [Weak. Narrated by al-Ḥārith, 135]"
"3608","(3608) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān informed us, Abū Yaḥyā al-Rāzī narrated to us, Hannād narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated from ʿAlī ibn Mubārak, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from al-Ḥaḍrimī ibn Lāḥiq, from a man among the Anṣār, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When one of you finds a louse in the mosque, let him hold it until he removes it (from the mosque)."" This is a good mursal narration of its kind. [Weak. Referred to previously]"
"3609","(3609) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Muslim al-Mulā'ī narrated from Zādhān, from al-Rabīʿ ibn Khuthaym, who said: ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) — saw a louse on a man's garment in the mosque. He took it and buried it in the pebbles, then he recited: {Did We not make the earth a container, for the living and the dead?} [al-Mursalāt: 25–26]. And something similar is narrated from Mujāhid and from Ibn al-Musayyab: ""He should bury it as one would spit (phlegm).""

And it has been narrated to us from Mālik ibn Yakhāmir that he said: ""I saw Muʿādh ibn Jabal killing lice and fleas during the prayer.""

And from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""There is no harm in killing lice during the prayer, but one should not fidget excessively."""
"3610","(3610) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Abū Usāmah narrated from al-Aʿmash.

(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us, Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr, from al-Aswad, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: ""Let none of you give the Shayṭān a share of his prayer by believing that it is an obligation upon him to turn only to his right. Indeed, I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) most often turn to his left."" This is the wording of Shuʿbah's ḥadīth. In the ḥadīth of Abū Usāmah, the word ""naṣīban"" (share) is replaced with ""juz'an"" (a portion), and instead of ""ʿan yamīnihi"" (to his right), it says ""ʿan shimālihi"" (to his left). Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 814]"
"3611","(3611) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Sulaymān, from ʿUmārah, from al-Aswad ibn Yazīd, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""Let none of you give the Shayṭān a share of his prayer by turning only to his right. And I have seen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) most often turn to his left.""

ʿUmārah said: ""I came to Madīnah afterwards and saw that the apartments of the Prophet (ﷺ) were to his left."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously]"
"3612","(3612) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Abī al-Awbar, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: ""I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) praying barefoot and in sandals, standing and sitting, and he would turn to his right and to his left."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī, 186]"
"3613","(3613) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, and Sufyān said: Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, from Qabīṣah ibn Hulb, from his father, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) would sometimes turn to his right and sometimes to his left, and he would place one of his hands upon the other.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""If he had no need in a particular direction, and he could face whichever way he wished, I would prefer that he face to his right, because of the Prophet's (ﷺ) preference for the right side, without making anything obligatory for the people in any of this.""

The Shaykh said: ""The ḥadīth of ʿĀ'ishah regarding the Prophet's (ﷺ) preference for the right side in all his affairs has already been mentioned."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī, 301]"
"3614","(3614) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī — the brother of Abī Ḥāmid — narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Abū Qutaybah narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from al-Suddī, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) would turn to his right.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Wakīʿ from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 708]"
"3615","(3615) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Faḍl informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Abū ʿAwwānah narrated from al-Suddī, who said: ""I asked Anas ibn Mālik: 'How should I turn when I finish my prayer — to my right or to my left?' He said: 'As for me, I most often saw the Prophet (ﷺ) turn to his right.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously]"
"3616","(3616) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us by dictation, Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīh ibn Bālawīyah al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: ""This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'When the call to prayer is given, come to it walking, and upon you should be tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray it, and whatever you miss, complete it.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 602]"
"3617","(3617) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr, and Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; Ibn Shihāb narrated to me from the ḥadīth of ʿAbbād ibn Ziyād that ʿUrwah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah narrated to him that al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah informed him: He went on a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the ablution of the Prophet ﷺ and his wiping over the leather socks. He said: Then they came forward. Al-Mughīrah said: I went along with him until he found the people had already placed ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf in front, and he led them in prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ caught one of the two rakʿahs, so he prayed the remaining rakʿah with the people. When ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf pronounced the salām, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to complete his prayer, and this alarmed the Muslims, so they increased in saying Subḥān Allāh. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, he turned toward them and said: ""You have done well,"" or he said: ""You have acted correctly."" He was expressing admiration for them that they had performed the prayer on time.

Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Shihāb said, from Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿd, from Ḥamzah ibn al-Mughīrah, something similar to the ḥadīth of ʿAbbād. Al-Mughīrah said: I intended to delay ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, but the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Leave him.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and Ḥasan al-Ḥulwānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim narrated it as 274.]"
"3618","(3618) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Sadūsī narrated to us; ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; al-Masʿūdī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Murrah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā narrated to us; from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, who said: Prayer was prescribed in three stages. He mentioned the matter of the qiblah and the matter of the adhān — these are two stages. He said: They used to come to the prayer when the Prophet ﷺ had already prayed part of it. He would signal to them with his fingers how much he had prayed — one, two. Muʿādh came and the Prophet ﷺ had already prayed part of the prayer. He said: ""I would not find him in any state except that I would join him in it, and then I would make up what remained."" So he entered the prayer. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, Muʿādh stood to make up what he had missed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Muʿādh has set a precedent for you, so do likewise.""

Shuʿbah narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān: ""Our companions narrated to us, saying: When a man would come…"" and he mentioned its meaning, and that is more correct, because ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā did not meet Muʿādh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its grading was discussed previously in ḥadīth 1828.]"
"3619","(3619) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ narrated to us; from a shaykh from the Anṣār, who said: A man came while the Prophet ﷺ was praying, and he heard the sound of his sandals. When he finished, he said: ""Which of you entered?"" The man said: ""I did, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""And how did you find us?"" He said: ""In prostration, so I prostrated."" He said: ""Do likewise. If you find him standing, standing; or bowing, bowing; or prostrating, prostrating; or sitting, sitting — do as you find him. And do not count a prostration if you have not caught the rakʿah."" [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated it as 3373.]"
"3620","(3620) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed me; Shuʿayb, meaning Ibn Abī Ḥamzah, said: Nāfiʿ said: Ibn ʿUmar, when he found the imām had already prayed part of the prayer, would pray with the imām whatever he caught. If the imām stood, he stood; if the imām sat, he sat — until the imām completed his prayer, without differing from him in anything. He said: And Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: ""If the rakʿah escapes you, the sajdah escapes you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik narrated it as 16.]"
"3621","(3621) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān; Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated to us; from Nāfiʿ; from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""When you find the imām in a certain state, do as he does.""

The meaning of this has also been narrated as a marfūʿ ḥadīth from Muʿādh ibn Jabal. [Jayyid. Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it as 26608.]"
"3622","(3622) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; it was read to Ibn Wahb: Ibn Jurayj informed you that Nāfiʿ informed him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, when a rakʿah or something of the prayer was missed with the imām, and the imām pronounced the salām, would stand at once to make up his prayer and would not wait for the imām to stand.

He said: And Baḥr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: al-Ḥārith ibn Nabhān informed you from Abū Hārūn al-ʿAbdī that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: ""That is the Sunnah.""

And also from Ibn al-Musayyib. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"3623","(3623) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed me; Shuʿayb narrated to me, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me that Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""When the prayer is established, do not come to it running. Come to it walking. Upon you is tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray it, and whatever you miss, complete it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd from Ibn Shihāb in the same manner. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was discussed previously in ḥadīth 3616.]"
"3624","(3624) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Barṭī al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Warkānī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd and Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah. Abū Saʿīd added in his ḥadīd: ""and his father,"" from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the prayer is established, do not come to it running. Come to it walking, and upon you is tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray it, and whatever you miss, complete it.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, without the narration of Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd from his father Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī Dhiʾb from al-Zuhrī from both of them with this wording.

Shuʿbah narrated it from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah, and he said: ""And make up what preceded you."" The narration of his son from him, along with al-Zuhrī's corroboration of him, is more correct. And Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated it similarly from Abū Salamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was discussed previously.]"
"3625","(3625) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed him; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Marwazī narrated to us; al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the iqāmah is called for the prayer, observe tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray it, and whatever you miss, complete it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik narrated it as 150.]"
"3626","(3626) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, narrating from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When you come to the prayer, do not come to it while you are running. Come to it while you are walking. Upon you is tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray it, and whatever you miss, make it up.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, incorporated into what precedes it, in the wording of the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd.

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Naṣr ibn ʿUmar informed us; he said: I heard Aḥmad ibn Salamah say: I heard Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj say: ""I do not know of anyone other than Ibn ʿUyaynah who narrated this wording from al-Zuhrī: 'And make up what you missed.'"" Muslim said: ""Ibn ʿUyaynah erred in this wording."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Nasāʾī narrated it as 861.]"
"3627","(3627) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAttāb informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Marzūq narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Mālik narrated —
(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Ibn Mahdī narrated from Mālik ibn Anas, from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Do not come to the prayer while running; rather come to it with tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, complete it."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Muqriʾ. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ in some copies from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its takhrīj was given previously in ḥadīth 3616.]"
"3628","(3628) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the call to prayer is given, come to it walking, and upon you should be tranquillity and solemnity. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you have been preceded in, complete it.""
Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.
And with the meaning of this wording, Jaʿfar ibn Rabīʿah narrated it from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was given previously.]"
"3629","(3629) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ḥāmid ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us —
(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Makkī narrated to us; Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the iqāmah for the prayer is called, none of you should run to it; rather, let him walk with tranquillity and solemnity. Pray what you catch, and make up what you have been preceded in.""
Muslim extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ and Ibn ʿUlayyah from Hishām.
And Abū Rāfiʿ narrated it from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with the meaning of this. And those who said ""faʾatimmū"" (complete it) are more numerous, more precise in memorisation, and more consistent with Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, so it is more deserving. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim extracted it, 602.]"
"3630","(3630) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us by dictation; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Shaybān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah from his father, who said: While we were praying with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he heard the clamour of men. When he finished the prayer, he called them and asked: ""What is the matter with you?"" They said: ""We hurried to the prayer."" He said: ""Do not do so. When you come to the prayer, upon you should be tranquillity. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you have been preceded in, complete it.""
al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām and Shaybān from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr likewise. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī extracted it, 609.]"
"3631","(3631) Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl al-Naysābūrī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb al-ʿAdl informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬAṭāʾ informed us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq from al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""What you catch (with the imām) is the beginning of your prayer.""
He said: And ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Saʿīd, meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, narrated from Ayyūb from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, the like of it. [Ḍaʿīf. al-Dāraquṭnī extracted it, 1/401.]"
"3632","(3632) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us; al-Walīd, meaning Ibn Muslim, narrated to us; Ismāʿīl narrated from Rabīʿah that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with both of them, said: ""Whatever you catch of the end of the imām's prayer, make it the beginning of your prayer.""
al-Walīd said: I mentioned that to Abū ʿAmr, meaning al-Awzāʿī, and to Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and they said: ""Whatever you catch of the imām's prayer is the beginning of your prayer.""
The Shaykh said: And we have narrated it from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib and ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rābāḥ and al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn and Abū Qilābah.
And from Qatādah that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""What you catch with the imām is the beginning of your prayer, and make up what the imām has preceded you in from the Qurʾān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah extracted it, 7114.]"
"3633","(3633) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed it to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Qatādah, and he mentioned it. He said: And Maʿmar narrated from Qatādah from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib the like of the statement of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, this — and even if it is a mursal narration from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, it is a witness for the narration of al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq extracted it, 3160.]"
"3634","(3634) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us; Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated from al-Zuhrī; Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib narrated: ""The established practice (sunnah) is that when a man catches one rakʿah of the Maghrib prayer with the imām, he should sit with the imām. When the imām says the salām, he should stand and perform the second rakʿah, sitting in it for the tashahhud, then stand and perform the third rakʿah, performing the tashahhud in it, then say the salām. And the prayers follow this sunnah in whatever sitting is prescribed in them.""
al-Zuhrī said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib said: ""Tell me of a prayer of three rakʿahs in which the tashahhud is performed three times."" When he was asked about it, he said: ""That is the Maghrib prayer, in which a man is preceded by one rakʿah, then he catches two rakʿahs with the imām and performs the tashahhud in them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. This isnād is ṣaḥīḥ and connected.]"
"3635","(3635) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us; al-Walīd said: And Ibn Jurayj informed me from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr that one rakʿah of the Maghrib prayer was missed by him. When the imām said the salām, he stood and raised his voice in recitation, and it was as though I could hear his recitation: ""And I have warned you of a blazing fire"" (Fa-andhartukum nāran taluẓẓā) [al-Layl: 14]. [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq extracted it, 3172.]"
"3636","(3636) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Ayyūb from Abū al-ʿĀliyah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: One of the commanders used to delay the prayer. I asked Abū Dharr, and he struck my thigh and said: ""I asked my beloved, meaning the Prophet ﷺ, and he struck my thigh and said: 'Pray the prayer at its appointed time. If you then catch the prayer with them, pray with them, and do not say: I have already prayed, so I will not pray with them.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim extracted it, 648.]"
"3637","(3637) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — that is al-Asamm — informed us, that Asyid ibn 'Asim informed us, that al-Husayn ibn Hafs informed us, from Sufyan, that Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed me, from Abu al-'Aliyah, who said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad delayed the Salah. I met 'Abd Allah ibn al-Samit and asked him about it. He struck my thigh and said: ""I asked my intimate companion Abu Dharr, and he struck my thigh and said: 'I asked my intimate companion, meaning the Prophet ﷺ,' and he struck my thigh and said: 'Perform the Salah at its prescribed time. If you catch up with the congregation, then pray with them, and do not say: I have already prayed, so I shall not pray.'"" Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from the hadith of Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah, from Ayyub. [Sahih. Previously referenced.]"
"3638","(3638) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki — said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us, that Bahr ibn Nasr informed us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Malik ibn Anas informed you, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man of Banu al-Dil called Busr ibn Mihjan, from his father Mihjan, who said: He was sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the Adhan for Salah was called. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and prayed, then returned, and Mihjan was still in his place as he was. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: 'What prevented you from praying with the people? Are you not a Muslim man?' He said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah, but I had already prayed at home.' He said: 'When you come, pray with the people, even if you have already prayed.'"" [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Recorded by Malik (2096).]"
"3639","(3639) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us, that Abu al-'Abbas informed us, that al-Rabi' informed us, that al-Shafi'i informed us, that Malik informed us, mentioning it with a similar wording. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Recorded by al-Shafi'i (1038).]"
"3640","(3640) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Umar ibn Hafs al-Muqri', Ibn al-Hammani, in Baghdad — informed us, that Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih informed us, that 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad informed us, that Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, that Shu'bah informed us, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from Jabir ibn Yazid ibn al-Aswad, from his father, who said: We prayed Fajr with the Prophet ﷺ in Mina. Then two men came and stopped at their mounts. The Prophet ﷺ ordered that they be brought, and they were brought, their limbs trembling. He said to them: 'What prevented you from praying with the people? Are you not Muslims?' They said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah, we had already prayed in our homes.' He said: 'When you have prayed in your homes and then come upon the Imam, pray with him, for it shall be for you a supererogatory prayer (naflah).'"" [Sahih. Recorded by Abu Dawud (575).]"
"3641","(3641) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us, that Abu Bakr ibn Dassah informed us, that Abu Dawud informed us, that Ahmad ibn Salih informed us, saying: I read to Ibn Wahb: ""'Amr informed us, from Bukayr, that he heard 'Afif ibn 'Amr ibn al-Musayyib say: A man from Asad ibn Khuzaymah informed me — that he asked Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, saying: 'One of us prays the Salah in his house, then comes to the mosque and the Salah is being performed — should I pray with them? I find something within myself concerning that.' Abu Ayyub said: 'We asked the Prophet ﷺ about that, and he said: ""That is for him a share of the collective reward (sahm jama'ah)."""" [Da'if. Recorded by Abu Dawud (178).]"
"3642","(3642) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us, that Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far informed us, that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim informed us, that Ibn Bukayr informed us, that Malik informed us, from 'Afif ibn 'Amr al-Sahmi, from a man of Bani Asad, that he asked Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, saying: 'I pray in my house, then come to the mosque and find the Imam praying — should I pray with him?' Abu Ayyub said: 'Yes. Whoever does that, then for him is a share of the collective reward (sahm jama'ah) — or: the like of a share of the collective reward.'"" [Da'if. Recorded by Malik (299).]"
"3643","(3643) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us, that 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us, that Yunus ibn Habib informed us, that Abu Dawud informed us, that Shu'bah informed me, that Abu 'Imran informed me, saying: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn al-Samit narrating from Abu Dharr — may Allah be pleased with him — that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'There will be leaders (umara') who will delay the Salah from its prescribed times. Beware! Perform the Salah at its time, then come to them. If they have already prayed, then you have secured your Salah. If not, then pray with them, and it shall be for you a supererogatory prayer (naflah).'"" Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from the hadith of 'Abd Allah ibn Idris, from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Recorded by Muslim (648).]"
"3644","(3644) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us, that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, that Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi informed us, that Abu al-Rabi' informed us, that Hushaym informed us, that Ya'la ibn 'Ata' informed us, that Jabir ibn Yazid ibn al-Aswad informed us, from his father, who said: I witnessed the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ and prayed Fajr with him in the Mosque of al-Khayf. He said: When he completed his Salah and turned around, he saw two men at the rear of the people who had not prayed with him. He said: 'Bring them to me.' They were brought, their limbs trembling. He said: 'What prevented you from praying with us?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we had already prayed in our homes.' He said: 'Do not do so. When you have prayed in your homes and then come to the mosque of a congregation, pray with them, for it shall be for you a supererogatory prayer (naflah).'"" [Sahih. Its recording has preceded in hadith 3640.]"
"3645","(3645) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us, that Asyid ibn 'Asim informed us, that al-Husayn ibn Hafs informed us, from Sufyan, who said: Ya'la ibn 'Ata' informed us, that Jabir ibn Yazid ibn al-Aswad al-Khuza'i informed us, from his father, who said: We prayed Fajr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Mina. He turned and saw two men behind the people. He called for them, and they were brought, their limbs trembling. He said: 'What prevented you from praying with the people?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we had prayed in our homes.' He said: 'Do not do so. When one of you prays in his home and then catches the Salah with the Imam, let him pray with the Imam, for it shall be for him a supererogatory prayer (naflah).'"" 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi and Waki' ibn al-Jarrah and others narrated it similarly from Sufyan al-Thawri. Abu 'asim al-Nabil differed from them, narrating it from Sufyan. [Sahih. Previously referenced; see the preceding.]"
"3646","(3646) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Faqih — informed us, that 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us, that Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi informed us, that Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Junayd informed us, that Abu 'Asim informed us, from Sufyan, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from Jabir ibn Yazid, from his father, who said: I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ. When he turned away, he saw two men at the rear of the people. He said: 'Bring them to me.' They came, their limbs trembling. He said: 'What is the matter with you two, that you did not pray with us?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we had prayed in our homes.' He said: 'Do not do so. When one of you prays in his home, then comes to the Imam, let him pray with him, and let him make the one he prayed in his home a supererogatory prayer (naflah).'"" 'Ali said: The companions of al-Thawri differed with him, and among them were the companions of Ya'la ibn 'Ata', including Shu'bah, Hisham ibn Hassan, Sharik, Ghaylan ibn Jami', Abu Khalid al-Dalani, Mubarak ibn Fadlah, Abu 'Awanah, Hushaym, and others, and they narrated from Ya'la ibn 'Ata' a statement similar to that of Waki', meaning from Sufyan. 'Ali said: Hajjaj ibn Artatah narrated it from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from his father, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet ﷺ, with a similar version, saying: 'It shall be for you two a supererogatory prayer (naflah), and the one in your homes shall be the obligatory prayer (faridah).'"" 'Ali said: Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi and others informed us, they said: 'Ali ibn Harb informed us, that Ibn Numayr informed us, from Hajjaj, with that. The Shaykh — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: Hajjaj ibn Artatah erred in his chain of narration (isnad), even if he was correct in the text (matn). The correct narration is that of the majority. Al-Shafi'i — may Allah have mercy upon him — mentioned in the older transmission the argumentation of those who used the hadith of Ya'la ibn 'Ata' as evidence, then said: 'And this is an unknown chain of narration (isnad majhul).' He said that — and Allah knows best — because Yazid ibn al-Aswad has no narrator other than his son Jabir ibn Yazid, and Jabir ibn Yazid has no narrator other than Ya'la ibn 'Ata'. Yahya ibn Ma'in and a group of imams declared Ya'la ibn 'Ata' to be trustworthy. This hadith has corroborating witnesses (shawahid) that have been previously mentioned, so the argumentation by it and by its corroborating witnesses is sound. And Allah knows best. [Shadhdh. Recorded by al-Daraqutni (1/414).]"
"3647","(3647) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Qutaybah narrated to us; Maʿn ibn ʿĪsā narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Sāʾib from Nūḥ ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah from Yazīd ibn ʿĀmir, who said:

I came while the Prophet (ﷺ) was in prayer, so I sat down and did not join them in the prayer. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) finished, he saw Yazīd sitting and said: ""Have you not submitted to Islam, O Yazīd?"" He said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah, I have indeed submitted."" He said: ""What prevented you from joining the people in their prayer?"" He said: ""I had already prayed in my house, and I reckoned that you had already prayed."" He said: ""When you come to the prayer and find the people [praying], then pray with them, even if you have already prayed. Let that be for you a supererogatory prayer, and this one [with the congregation] the obligatory prayer.""

This hadith is consistent with what has preceded regarding the repetition of prayer in congregation, but differs from it concerning which of the two is counted as the obligatory prayer. The preceding narrations are more numerous and more well-known, and they are more appropriate. And Allah knows best. [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 577]"
"3648","(3648) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — recitation before him; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind informed us, saying:

I asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab about a man who prays in his house and then catches the congregation. He said: ""He should pray with them."" I said: ""Then which of the two does he count [as the obligatory prayer]?"" He said: ""The one he prayed with the imam, for Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The prayer of a man in congregation exceeds his prayer alone by twenty-five times.'"" [Sound. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī, 486]"
"3649","(3649) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Nāʿif:

That a man asked ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, saying: ""I pray in my house, and then I catch the prayer with the imam. Should I pray with him?"" ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""Yes, pray with him."" The man said: ""Then which of the two do I make my [obligatory] prayer?"" ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said to him: ""That is not for you [to decide]. That is for Allah, the Most High; He makes whichever of the two He wills [the obligatory one]."" [Sound. Narrated by Mālik, 997]"
"3650","(3650) And with his chain of narration, Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd:

That a man asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, saying: ""I pray in my house, and then I come to the mosque and find the imam praying. Should I pray with him?"" Saʿīd said: ""Yes."" The man said: ""Then which of the two do I make my [obligatory] prayer?"" Saʿīd said: ""Are you the one who decides that? That is for Allah; He makes whichever of the two He wills.""

The first opinion is more correct, due to the hadith of Abū Dharr and Yazīd ibn al-Aswad.

It is narrated from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ that he said: ""I asked Ibn ʿUmar about repeating the prayer, and he said: 'The first one is the obligatory prayer.'"" It is as though he had received a report about this matter that had not reached him at the time when he did not give a definitive ruling concerning it. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Sound. Narrated by Mālik, 298]"
"3651","(3651) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ʿAbdus ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Simsār narrated to us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us; al-Anṣārī — meaning Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muthannā — narrated to me; Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated, saying:

Anas said: ""We arrived with Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, and he led us in the morning prayer at al-Mirbad. Then we went to the mosque, and the prayer was being established, so we prayed with al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah."" [Sound. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah, 6661]"
"3652","(3652) And with this chain of narration, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated, saying:

Anas said: ""Abū Mūsā was in charge of the army of the people of Baṣrah, and al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqarrin was in charge of the army of the people of Kūfah. I was between the two of them. They made an appointment to meet with me in the morning. One of them prayed with his companions, then came and prayed with us."" [Sound. This chain of narration is sound and connected.]"
"3653","(3653) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram al-Bazzāz and Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-ʿAṭṭār both said: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us — Ibn Mukram added: ""and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ"" — and this is the hadith of Yazīd: al-Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb; Sulaymān, the freed slave of Maymūnah, narrated to me that he heard Ibn ʿUmar say:

""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Do not pray a single prayer twice in one day.'"" [Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 579]"
"3654","(3654) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh both said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Bahlūl narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Abū Usāmah informed me; Ḥusayn ibn Dhakwān informed me; ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb informed me; Sulaymān, the freed slave of Maymūnah, informed me, saying:

I came to Ibn ʿUmar one day while he was sitting in the Balāṭ, and the people were in the ʿAṣr prayer. I said: ""O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the people are in prayer!"" He said: ""I have already prayed. I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'There is no obligatory prayer [to be repeated] twice in one day.'""

ʿAlī said: ""Al-Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim is the sole narrator of this from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb. And Allah, the Most High, knows best.""

The Sheikh said: ""If this hadith is sound, it is to be understood as referring to one who has already prayed it in congregation and therefore does not repeat it. His statement, 'There is no obligatory prayer [to be repeated] twice in one day,' means: not both of them on the basis of being obligatory. This indicates that the command to repeat it is a matter of choice, not an obligation. And Allah, the Most High, knows best."" [Sound. Preceded earlier.]"
"3655","(3655) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Yaḥyā Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Marwazī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī from Anas ibn Mālik, who said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell from a horse, and his right side was scratched. We visited him [to enquire after his health], and the prayer time came, so he prayed sitting, and we prayed sitting. When he finished the prayer, he said: 'The imam is appointed only to be followed. When he says the takbīr, say the takbīr; when he bows, bow; when he raises [his head], raise [your heads]; when he says ""Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,"" say ""Rabbanā wa laka al-ḥamd""; when he prostrates, prostrate; and when he prays sitting, pray sitting — all of you.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym from Sufyān, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others from Sufyān. They both also narrated this incident from the hadith of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 371]"
"3656","(3656) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; ʿAbdah narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill, and some of his Companions came to visit him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed sitting, and they prayed behind him standing. He gestured to them to sit, so they sat. When he finished, he said: 'The imam is appointed only to be followed. When he bows, bow; when he raises [his head], raise [your heads]; and when he prays sitting, pray sitting.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the hadith of Mālik from Hishām. [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 656]"
"3657","(3657) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us that Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us that Yahya ibn Yahya informed us that Abu Mu'awiya reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, who said: ""When the illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) became severe, Bilal came to inform him of the prayer. He said: 'Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.' She said: I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr is a man of tender heart, and when he stands in your place, he will not be able to make the people hear. If only you would command 'Umar.' He said: 'Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.' She said: I said to Hafsah: 'Tell him that Abu Bakr is a man of tender heart, and when he stands in your place, he will not be able to make the people hear. If only you would command 'Umar.' So she said it to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Indeed, you are like the companions of Yusuf. Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.' She said: So they commanded Abu Bakr, and he led the people in prayer. She said: When he entered into the prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) found some relief in himself. She said: So he got up, being supported between two men, his feet dragging on the ground. She said: When he entered the mosque, Abu Bakr sensed his presence and moved to step back. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) gestured to him: 'Stay in your place.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came and sat to the left of Abu Bakr. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed seated with the people, while Abu Bakr stood, following the prayer of the Prophet (peace be upon him), and the people followed the prayer of Abu Bakr.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Qutaybah from Abu Mu'awiya. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 633]"
"3658","(3658) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us that 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Bayan narrated to us that 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — and Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us that 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us that 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-'Ayshiyy narrated to us that Hammad ibn Salamah reported from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was ill, and he commanded Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) then found some relief in himself, so he came and sat beside Abu Bakr. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) led Abu Bakr in prayer while he (the Prophet) was sitting, and Abu Bakr led the people in prayer while he was standing. The wording of both their narrations is the same.

And in his (the Prophet's) praying seated during his illness there is evidence for what we intended with this chapter, and in his praying with Abu Bakr while he (the Prophet) was sitting and Abu Bakr was standing, there is evidence that the first command became abrogated and that the correct position is that the follower prays standing, even if his imam prays seated due to an excuse. And with Allah is the granting of success. [Sahih. See before it]"
"3659","(3659) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us that 'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us that Abu Ishaq al-Talaqani narrated to us that Ibn al-Mubarak reported from Ibrahim ibn Tahman — Abu Ishaq said: and I heard Ibn al-Mubarak say: ""Ibrahim ibn Tahman was firm (thabat) in hadith"" — from Husayn al-Mukattib, from 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, who said: ""I was suffering from hemorrhoids (bawasir), so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and he said: 'Pray standing; if you are unable, then sitting; and if you are unable, then on your side.'"" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 1066]"
"3660","(3660) Abu al-Husayn informed us that Isma'il narrated to us that 'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us that 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Ibn Buraydah, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), a similar narration.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdan from Ibn al-Mubarak. [Sahih. See before it]"
"3661","(3661) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Ya'qub al-Thaqafi narrated to us that Musa ibn Harun ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated to us that Hafs ibn Ghayath reported from Humayd, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) praying in a cross-legged position (mutarabbic).""

And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us that Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us that Ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari, Muhammad ibn Yahya, and Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas said: Yusuf al-Qattan narrated to us that Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: ""from Humayd al-Tawil."" [Sahih in its meaning. Narrated by al-Nasa'i in al-Kubra, 1363]"
"3662","(3662) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to us that al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Isbahani narrated to us that Hafs ibn Ghayath reported from Humayd ibn Qays, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from 'A'ishah, who said: ""I saw the Prophet (peace be upon him) praying in a cross-legged position (mutarabbic).""

And it has been narrated to us in the authentic hadith from 'Uthman ibn Hakim, from 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sat in the prayer, he placed his left foot between his thigh and his shin, and he spread out his right foot — except that this was in the sitting for the tashahhud. And perhaps that was due to his illness. And Allah knows best."" [Sahih in its meaning. See before it]"
"3663","(3663) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us that Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us that 'Abdan narrated to us that 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us that Sufyan reported from Ibn 'Ajlan, from 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, who said: ""I saw the Prophet (peace be upon him) supplicating thus,"" and he placed his hands on his knees while he was sitting cross-legged.

The Sheikh said: And 'Uqbah, the brother of Sa'id ibn 'Ubaid al-Ta'i, narrated that he saw Anas ibn Malik praying in a cross-legged position. And 'Umar, a sheikh from the Ansar, also narrated it from him. [Strong. And the hadith is a sahih witness for the one before it]"
"3664","(3664) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us that Abu 'Amr ibn al-Sammak narrated to us that Hanbal ibn Ishaq narrated to us that Abu 'Abdullah — meaning Ahmad ibn Hanbal — narrated to us that 'Umar ibn 'Ali al-Muqaddami narrated to us. He said: I heard Humayd al-Tawil say: ""I saw Anas ibn Malik praying in a cross-legged position on his bed.""

Abu 'Abdullah said: ""I do not know that I heard it except from him."" He said: And 'Abbad used to narrate it without saying in it ""mutarabbic"" (cross-legged). [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn Abi Shaybah, 6132]"
"3665","(3665) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu Bakr ibn Balawayh narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us that Rawh narrated to us that Shu'bah reported from Qatadah, from Anas, that he used to sit cross-legged in the prayer.

And with his chain he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. He said: I asked Qatadah about sitting cross-legged in the prayer, and he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin said: ""'Abdullah ibn 'Umar used to do it.""

The Sheikh said: It has been narrated to us from Ibn 'Umar that he only sat like that in the tashahhud, and he excused himself for that by saying that his feet could not bear him. And that will be mentioned, if Allah the Most High wills. [Sahih. See before it]"
"3666","(3666) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us that Isma'il al-Saffar narrated to us that Sa'dan narrated to us that Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us that Humayd al-Tawil said: ""I saw Bakr ibn 'Abdullah praying in a cross-legged position and leaning (on something).""

And it has been narrated to us from Mujahid and Ibrahim al-Nakha'i that a sick person may pray in a cross-legged position.

And it has been narrated to us from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz that he did so. And it is mentioned from Ibn 'Abbas that he disliked it. [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn Abi Shaybah, 6126]"
"3667","(3667) Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī, the Imām, informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shurayḥī informed us; Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us; Shuʿbah said: I asked al-Ḥakam about sitting cross-legged (al-tarraʿuʿ) in prayer, and he disliked it and said: ""I believe Ibn ʿAbbās also disliked it.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah, 6132]"
"3668","(3668) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh informed us; my father informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥuṣayn from al-Haytham from ʿAbd Allāh, i.e., Ibn Masʿūd, who said: ""That I sit upon a coal or two coals is more beloved to me than that I sit cross-legged in prayer.""
Al-Shāfiʿī mentioned this in the book of ʿAlī and ʿAbd Allāh in an absolute sense, and he said: ""That which Ibn Masʿūd disliked — a man sitting cross-legged in prayer — is disliked."" The Iraqīs oppose Ibn Masʿūd and say: ""The standing posture of one who prays sitting is sitting cross-legged."" Then, in the book of al-Buwayṭī, he said: ""He should sit in the place of standing, cross-legged, in whatever manner he is able."" It is as though he restricted it to a specific case or inclined toward some of what has preceded. And Allāh knows best.
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah, 6131]"
"3669","(3669) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī in Baghdād informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us; Abū Bakr Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī informed us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a sick person and saw him praying upon a cushion. He took it and threw it away. Then the sick person took a stick to pray upon, so he took that and threw it away, and said: ""Pray upon the earth if you are able; otherwise, gesture with your head, and make your prostration lower than your bowing.""
Muḥammad ibn Maʿmar al-Baḥrānī also narrated it from Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī. This hadith is counted among the unique narrations (afrād) of Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī from al-Thawrī.
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym in al-Ḥilyah, 7/92]"
"3670","(3670) Abū Sahl al-Marwazī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Khubayb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a sick person and saw him praying upon a cushion. He took it and threw it away, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: ""Pray upon the earth if you are able.""
[Ḍaʿīf. Preceded above]"
"3671","(3671) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: ""When the sick person is unable to prostrate, he should gesture with his head, and he should not raise anything to his forehead.""
A group narrated it in this manner from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, as a mawqūf (stopped) narration. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir al-Aslamī narrated it from Nāfiʿ as a marfūʿ (raised) narration, but it is not established. It has also been narrated through other routes from Ibn ʿUmar as a mawqūf narration.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik, 403]"
"3672","(3672) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh informed us; my father informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from Jabalah, who said: Ibn ʿUmar was asked — and I was listening — about praying upon a cushion (marwahah). He said: ""Do not take alongside Allāh another god,"" or he said: ""Do not set up rivals for Allāh. Pray sitting and prostrate upon the earth. If you are unable, then gesture with your head, and make the prostration lower than the bowing.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. This is a ṣaḥīḥ, connected chain.]"
"3673","(3673) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-ʿAdl informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh informed us; my father informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Zayd ibn Muʿāwiyah from ʿAlqamah, who said: I entered with ʿAbd Allāh upon his brother ʿUtbah to visit him while he was sick. He saw his brother prostrating upon a cushion, so ʿAbd Allāh snatched it from him and said: ""Prostrate upon the earth. If you are unable, then gesture with your head, and make the prostration lower than the bowing.""
[Ḍaʿīf]"
"3674","(3674) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; al-Thiqah narrated from Yūnus from al-Ḥasan from his mother, who said: I saw Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, prostrating upon a leather cushion due to an eye ailment she had.
[Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Narrated by Ibn al-Jaʿd, 3189]"
"3675","(3675) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Shaybān al-Harawī informed us; Muʿādh ibn Najdah informed us; Kāmil ibn Ṭalḥah narrated; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī, ʿAlī ibn Zayd, and Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd from al-Ḥasan from Umm al-Ḥasan: that she saw Umm Salamah praying upon a cushion due to an eye ailment she had. He said: And Kāmil informed us; Mubārak ibn Faḍālah narrated from al-Ḥasan from his mother with the like of it.
It has also been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās that he granted permission (rukhṣah) for prostrating upon a cushion and a pillow.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
"3676","(3676) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; Bakr ibn Bakkār Abū ʿAmr informed us; Isrāʾīl informed us; Abū Isḥāq said: I saw ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim prostrating upon a wall in the mosque, the height of which was approximately one cubit.
[Ḍaʿīf]"
"3677","(3677) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Hibban — he is ibn Musa — informed us; 'Abd Allah — he is ibn al-Mubarak — informed us from Isra'il; Majza'ah ibn Zahir narrated to us from a man among them, from the Companions of the Tree, whose name was Uhb ibn Aws, and he used to complain of pain in his knee or his knees. So when he prostrated, he would place a cushion beneath his knees.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi from Isra'il. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 3940]"
"3678","(3678) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih narrated to us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Batiha narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hiri narrated to us; Hasan ibn Husayn al-'Urani narrated to us; Husayn ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from 'Ali ibn Husayn, from al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""The sick person prays standing if he is able. If he is unable, he prays sitting. If he is unable to prostrate, he gestures, making his gesture for prostration lower than his gesture for bowing. If he is unable to pray sitting, he prays lying on his right side, facing the qiblah. If he is unable to pray on his right side, he lies on his back with his feet toward the qiblah.""

[Very weak. Narrated by al-Daraqutni, 2/42]"
"3679","(3679) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih narrated to us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar informed us; Ibrahim ibn Hammad narrated to us; 'Abbas ibn Yazid narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Abu Bakr ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar narrated to us from his father, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""The sick person prays lying on his back with his feet toward the qiblah."" This is a mawquf narration.

And it is understood to apply to the case where one is unable to pray on one's side. And with Allah lies success. [Weak. Narrated by 'Abd al-Razzaq, 4130]"
"3680","(3680) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran al-'Adl narrated to us in Baghdad; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz informed us; Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; al-Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from 'Imran ibn Husayn: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was asked about the prayer of one who is sitting. He, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever prays standing, that is best. Whoever prays sitting receives half the reward of the one who stands. Whoever prays lying down receives half the reward of the one who sits.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih through various routes from al-Husayn al-Mu'allim. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 1065]"
"3681","(3681) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us; Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us; Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us from 'Amr, who said: When the water (of illness) afflicted the eyes of Ibn 'Abbas, he wished to have it treated. It was said to him: ""You must remain so-and-so many days without praying except while lying down."" He disliked that.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 639]"
"3682","(3682) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam al-Warraq narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. He said: I read to Malik from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid and Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to pray sitting and recite while sitting. When approximately thirty or forty verses of his recitation remained, he would stand and recite while standing, then bow and prostrate. He would do the same in the second rak'ah.

Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik. [Narrated by al-Bukhari, 1067]"
"3683","(3683) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us; Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us; Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us from 'Amr, who said: When the water (of illness) afflicted the eyes of Ibn 'Abbas, he wished to have it treated. It was said to him: ""You must remain so-and-so many days without praying except while lying down."" He disliked that. [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn al-Ja'd, 2336]"
"3684","(3684) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih narrated to us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad narrated to us; Harun ibn Sulayman narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Sharik narrated to us from Simak, from 'Ikrimah: When the water (of illness) afflicted the eyes of Ibn 'Abbas, he wished to have it removed from his eyes. It was said to him: ""You will lie on your back for seven days and not pray except while lying down."" He disliked that and said: ""It has reached me that whoever abandons the prayer while being able to pray will meet Allah, the Most High, while He is angry with him.""

[Hasan through other chains. Narrated by Ibn Abi Shaybah, 6285]"
"3685","(3685) Abu Bakr ibn Ibrahim al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Amr informed us; Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us; 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us from Jabir, from Abu al-Duha: 'Abd al-Malik or someone else sent physicians to Ibn 'Abbas on a striped cloak, as the water (of illness) had afflicted his eyes. They said: ""You must pray for seven days lying on your back."" So he asked Umm Salamah and 'A'ishah about that, and they forbade him from it.

And from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi': Ibn 'Abbas said: ""What do you think if death comes before that?""

[Weak. Narrated by Ibn Abi Shaybah, 6285]"
"3686","(3686) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Makhlad ibn Ja'far al-Daqqaq informed me; Ja'ri al-Firyabi narrated to us.

(Additionally,) Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Muqr'i informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us. Both said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from al-Mustawrid ibn al-Ahnaf, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah, who said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He opened with Surah al-Baqarah, and I said (to myself): ""He will pray with it in one rak'ah."" Then he continued, and I said: ""He will bow with it."" Then he opened Surah al-Nisa' and recited it. Then he opened Surah Al 'Imran and recited it, reciting with measured deliberation. When he passed by a verse containing glorification, he glorified; when he passed by a question, he asked; and when he passed by a seeking of refuge, he sought refuge. Then he bowed and said: ""Subhani Rabbiya al-'Azim (Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent)."" His bowing was approximately as long as his standing. Then he said: ""Sami' Allahu liman hamidah (Allah hears those who praise Him)."" Then he stood for nearly as long as he had bowed. Then he prostrated and said: ""Subhani Rabbiya al-A'la (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High)."" His prostration was approximately as long as his standing.

Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim, 772]"
"3687","(3687) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us. He said: I said to Sulaymān, meaning al-Aʿmash: ""Should I supplicate in the prayer when I pass by a verse of warning?"" He narrated to me from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Mustawrid, from Ṣilah ibn Zufar, from Ḥudhayfah, who said: He prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would say in his rukūʿ: ""Subḥāna Rabbiya al-ʿAẓīm"" (Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent), and in his sujūd: ""Subḥāna Rabbiya al-Aʿlā"" (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High). He would not pass by a verse of mercy except that he would stop at it and ask, nor by a verse of punishment except that he would stop at it and seek refuge."
"3688","(3688) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; my father narrated to us. He said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrating from al-Ḥārith ibn Yazīd al-Ḥaḍramī, from Ziyād ibn Nuʿaym al-Ḥaḍramī, from Muslim ibn Mikhraq, who said: I said to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: ""There are men among whom one recites the Qurʾān twice or three times in a single night."" She said: ""They have recited and not recited. I used to stand with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the entire night, and he would recite Sūrat al-Baqarah, Āl ʿImrān, and al-Nisāʾ. When he passed by a verse containing glad tidings, he would supplicate and express hope, and when he passed by a verse containing a threat, he would supplicate and seek refuge."""
"3689","(3689) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me from ʿAmr ibn Qays, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḥumayd, from ʿAwff ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, who said: I stood in prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night. He stood and recited Sūrat al-Baqarah. He would not pass by a verse of mercy except that he would stop and ask, nor would he pass by a verse of punishment except that he would stop and seek refuge. He said: Then he performed rukūʿ for a duration equal to his standing, saying in his rukūʿ: ""Subḥāna Dhī al-Jabarūt wa al-Malakūt wa al-Kibriyāʾ wa al-ʿAẓamah"" (Glory be to the Lord of Dominion, Sovereignty, Greatness, and Majesty). Then he performed sujūd for a duration equal to his standing, and he said in his sujūd the same as that. Then he stood and recited Āl ʿImrān, then he recited one sūrah after another."
"3690","(3690) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying a voluntary prayer, and I heard him say: ""Allāhumma innī aʿūdhu bika min al-nār, wa waylun li-ahl al-nār"" (O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the Fire, and woe to the people of the Fire)."
"3691","(3691) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us, from Isrāʾīl, from Abū Isḥāq, from Muslim al-Baṭīn, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ, when he recited ""Sabbih isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā"" (Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High), would say: ""Subḥāna Rabbiya al-Aʿlā"" (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High).

Abū Dāwūd said: Wakīʿ was contradicted regarding this ḥadīth. Abū Wakīʿ and Shuʿbah narrated it from Abū Isḥāq, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, as a mawqūf narration."
"3692","(3692) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Mūsā ibn Abī ʿĀʾishah, who said: A man was praying on top of his house. When he recited ""A-lādhālika bi-qādirin ʿalā an yuḥyiya al-mawtā"" (Is not that One able to give life to the dead?) [al-Qiyāmah: 40], he said: ""Subḥānaka fa-balā"" (Glory be to You, indeed He is). They asked him about that, and he said: ""I heard it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."""
"3693","(3693) Abū ʿAlī informed us; Muḥammad informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to me; Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah narrated to me. He said: I heard a Bedouin say: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever among you recites al-Tīn wa al-Zaytūn and reaches its conclusion, 'A-laysa Allāhu bi-aḥkami al-ḥākimīn' (Is not Allah the most just of judges?) [al-Tīn: 8], let him say: 'Wa anā ʿalā dhālika min al-shāhidīn' (And I am among those who bear witness to that). And whoever recites 'Lā uqsimu bi-yawm al-qiyāmah' and reaches 'A-lādhālika bi-qādirin ʿalā an yuḥyiya al-mawtā' [al-Qiyāmah: 40], let him say: 'Balā' (Indeed). And whoever recites wa al-Mursalāt and reaches 'Fa-bi-ayyi ḥadīthi baʿdahu yuʾminūn' (So in what statement after it will they believe?) [al-Mursalāt: 50], let him say: 'Āmannā bi-Allāh' (We believe in Allah).""

Ismāʿīl said: I went to repeat it back to the Bedouin and to look at him, perhaps he had made an error. He said: ""O son of my brother, do you think I have not memorized it? I have performed sixty pilgrimages, and there is not a single one of them except that I know the very camel upon which I performed it."""
"3694","(3694) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from al-Suddī, from ʿAbd Khayr, who said: I heard ʿAlī reciting ""Sabbih isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā"" and he would say: ""Subḥāna Rabbiya al-Aʿlā"" (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High).

He said: And Wakīʿ narrated to us from Miʿsar, from ʿUmayr ibn Saʿīd, who said: I heard Abū Mūsā reciting in the Jumuʿah prayer ""Sabbih isma Rabbika al-Aʿlā"" and he would say: ""Subḥāna Rabbiya al-Aʿlā"" and ""Hal atāka ḥadīth al-Ghāshiyah."""
"3695","(3695) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; al-Ustādh Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Būshanjī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar informed us, from Bishr ibn Jābān al-Ṣaghānī, from Ḥujr ibn Qays al-Madarī, who said: I spent the night at the house of the Commander of the Faithful ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, and I heard him praying during the night and reciting. When he passed by this verse: ""A-fa-raʾaytum mā tumnūn, a-antum takhluqūnahu am naḥnu al-khāliqūn"" (Have you seen that which you emit? Do you create it, or are We the Creator?) [al-Wāqiʿah: 58–59], he said: ""Bal anta yā Rabb"" (Rather, You, O Lord) three times. Then he recited: ""A-fa-raʾaytum mā taḥruthūn, a-antum tazraʿūnahu am naḥnu al-zāriʿūn"" (Have you seen that which you cultivate? Do you sow it, or are We the Sower?) [al-Wāqiʿah: 63–64], and he said: ""Bal anta yā Rabb, bal anta yā Rabb"" (Rather, You, O Lord; rather, You, O Lord). Then he recited: ""A-fa-raʾaytum al-māʾa alladhī tashrabūn, a-antum anzaltumhu min al-muzn am naḥnu al-munzilūn"" (Have you seen the water that you drink? Do you send it down from the clouds, or are We the Sender?) [al-Wāqiʿah: 68–69], and he said: ""Bal anta yā Rabb"" three times. Then he recited: ""A-fa-raʾaytum al-nāra allatī tūrūn, a-antum anshaʾtum shajaratahā am naḥnu al-munshiʾūn"" (Have you see the fire that you kindle? Do you produce its tree, or are We the Producer?) [al-Wāqiʿah: 71–72], and he said: ""Bal anta yā Rabb"" three times."
"3696","(3696) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be praying his night prayer while I was lying between him and the qiblah, like a funeral bier laid out.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from a group of narrators from Ibn ʿUyaynah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī."
"3697","(3697) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī reported to us in Baghdād: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Minjāb ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Mushhir narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Muslim, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that those things which invalidate the prayer were mentioned in her presence. The people said: ""The prayer is invalidated by the dog, the donkey, and the woman."" ʿĿʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""You have indeed likened us to dogs. I used to see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) praying while I was lying on the bed between him and the qiblah. When I needed to attend to something, I disliked facing him directly, so I would slip away quietly."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn al-Khalīl from ʿAlī ibn Mushhir, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its citation was given previously under hadith no. 3497.]"
"3698","(3698) Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated. He said: I read before Mālik ibn Anas, from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqī, from Abū Qatādah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray while carrying Umāmah, the daughter of Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and the daughter of Abī al-ʿĀṣ ibn Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbd Shams. When he prostrated, he would set her down, and when he stood up, he would carry her. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others, and al-Bukhārī narrated it as was previously mentioned. An adduced argument was made based on what was narrated regarding this from ʿUmar. The narration from ʿUmar, according to us, is as follows: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us: Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī reported to us: Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Abū al-ʿAlāʾ Burd ibn Sinān, from ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy, from Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith al-Kindī, who said: ""I asked ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. I said: 'We are Bedouins and we live in tents. If I go out, I feel the cold, and if my wife goes out, she feels the cold.' ʿUmar said: 'Place a garment between you and her, and then let each one of you pray.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its citation was given previously under hadith no. 613.]"
"3699","(3699) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī reported to us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā both said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb reported to us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī reported to us: Abū al-Qāsim, who is al-Manīʿī, narrated to us: Abū Khaythamah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh narrated. He said: Nāfiʿ reported to me, from ʿAbd Allāh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he used to recite the Qurʾān and would come upon a sūrah containing a verse of prostration, whereupon he would prostrate, and we would prostrate with him, until some of us could find no place to put their foreheads. This is the wording of the hadith of Abū Khaythamah. In the hadith of the other two, it states: ""He would recite the Qurʾān to us."" And they both said: ""Until none of us could find a place for his forehead."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad and others from Yaḥyā, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Khaythamah, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī cited it as no. 1026.]"
"3700","(3700) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the son of Ādam recites the verse of prostration and prostrates, the devil withdraws, weeping, and says: 'Woe to me! The son of Ādam was commanded to prostrate and he prostrated, so for him is Paradise. And I was commanded to prostrate but I refused, so for me is the Fire.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Abū Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim cited it as no. 81.]"
"3701","(3701) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū Qudāmah narrated from Maṭar al-Warrāq, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās. (Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak reported to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Ḥārith, Abū Qudāmah, narrated from Maṭar al-Warrāq, or from a man, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not prostrate in anything of the Mufaṣṣal after he migrated to Madīnah."" Its meaning was narrated by Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from Azhar ibn al-Qāsim from al-Ḥārith from Maṭar. And it was narrated by Bakr ibn Khalaf, the son-in-law of al-Muqriʾ, from Azhar, and he said in its text: ""Indeed the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prostrated in al-Najm while he was in Makkah, but when he migrated to Madīnah, he abandoned it."" ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān reported it to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Bakr ibn Khalaf, the son-in-law of al-Muqriʾ, narrated it and did not express doubt in its chain. This hadith revolves around al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUbayd Abī Qudāmah al-Iyādī al-Baṣrī, whom Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn declared weak. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from him and said: ""He was one of our shaykhs, and I saw nothing but good from him. And Allah knows best."" The preserved narration from ʿIkrimah is from Ibn ʿAbbās. [Munkar. al-Ṭayālisī cited it as no. 2688.]"
"3702","(3702) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us. He said: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) recited al-Najm and the Muslims, the polytheists, the jinn, and mankind prostrated with him. al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. It does not contain the additional wording that Azhar ibn al-Qāsim brought from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUbayd. And in what al-Shāfiʿī narrated in al-Qadīm through his chain from Mujāhid and from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a mursal narration is the meaning of this additional wording. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī cited it as no. 4581.]"
"3703","(3703) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan reported to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayṭ, from ʬṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Zayd ibn Thābit, who said: ""I recited before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): 'Wa al-najmi idhā hawā' (Sūrat al-Najm), and he did not prostrate in it."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Ibn Qusayṭ. And it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not prostrate only because Zayd did not prostrate, and he was the one reciting. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī cited it as no. 1022.]"
"3704","(3704) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh reported to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sahl al-Muṭarriz narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dimashqī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Fāʾid narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah, from al-Mahdī ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd. He said: My paternal grandmother Umm al-Dardāʾ narrated to me from Abū al-Dardāʾ, who said: ""I prostrated with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) eleven prostrations, none of which were from the Mufaṣṣal: al-Aʿrāf, al-Raʿd, al-Nahl, Banī Isrāʾīl, Maryam, al-Ḥajj (one prostration), al-Furqān, Sulaymān in Sūrat al-Naml, al-Sajdah, Ṣād, and the prostration of the Ḥawāmīm."" It was narrated with this chain. [Munkar. Ibn Mājah cited it as no. 1056.]"
"3705","(3705) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī reported to us. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated. He said: It was read before ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed you from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from one who informed him, from Abū al-Dardāʾ that he prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) eleven prostrations, among which was al-Najm. And Sufyān ibn Wakīʿ narrated it from Ibn Wahb from ʿAmr from Saʿīd from ʿUmar al-Dimashqī from Umm al-Dardāʾ from Abū al-Dardāʾ. And Layth ibn Saʿd narrated it from Khālid ibn Yazīd from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl from ʿUmar, who is Ibn Ḥayyān al-Dimashqī. He said: ""I heard a narrator reporting from Umm al-Dardāʾ from Abū al-Dardāʾ."" [Ḍaʿīf. al-Tirmidhī cited it as no. 568.]"
"3706","(3706) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī reported to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī narrated. He said: It was narrated from Abū al-Dardāʾ from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that there were eleven prostrations, and its chain is weak. The Shaykh said: And we have narrated from Abū al-Dardāʾ that he prostrated in al-Ḥajj two prostrations. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its chain is ṣaḥīḥ up to Abū Dāwūd: as recorded in his Sunan, 1/445.]"
"3707","(3707) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Raffāʾī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us that Zuhayr narrated to us that ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwali narrated from al-ʿUryān — or Abū al-ʿUryān — who said: Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""There is no prostration (sajdah) in the Mufaṣṣal."" He (the narrator) said: ""I met Abū ʿUbaydah and mentioned to him what Ibn ʿAbbās had said. He said: ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated in Sūrat al-Najm, and so did the believers and the polytheists, and he continued to prostrate in it thereafter.'"" [Weak. Its meaning is narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq (5900)]"
"3708","(3708) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us that Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd informed us that al-Ḥārith ibn Saʿīd al-ʿUtqī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Munayyin, from Banī ʿAbd Kulāl, from ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited to him fifteen prostrations in the Qurʾān, three of which are in the Mufaṣṣal, and Sūrat al-Ḥajj contains two prostrations. [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd (401)]"
"3709","(3709) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Sulmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Faqīh in Baghdād informed us that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us that Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm and Abū ʿUmar both narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq from al-Aswad, from ʿAbdullāh, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he recited Sūrat al-Najm and prostrated, and there remained no one among the people except that he prostrated — except one man who raised a handful of pebbles and placed it upon his forehead, saying: ""This is sufficient for me."" ʿAbdullāh said: ""I later saw him killed while he was a disbeliever."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū ʿUmar Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī (3640)]"
"3710","(3710) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq al-Baghawī in Baghdād narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to me that my father narrated to me that Ayyūb narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet ﷺ prostrated in it — meaning Sūrat al-Najm — and the Muslims, the polytheists, the jinn, and mankind prostrated in it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Maʿmar and others from ʿAbd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī (1021)]"
"3711","(3711) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkari in Baghdād informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar narrated from Ibn Ṭāwūs from ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadāʿah, who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrate in al-Najm, and the people prostrated with him."" Al-Muṭṭalib said: ""But I did not prostrate, for I was at that time a disbeliever."" Al-Muṭṭalib said: ""I will never abandon prostration in it."" [Munkar. Narrated by Aḥmad (3/420, 15544; 4/215, 18052)]"
"3712","(3712) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me that my father narrated to me that Ibrāhīm ibn Khālid narrated to us that Rabbāḥ narrated from Maʿmar from Ibn Ṭāwūs from ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid from Jaʿfar ibn al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadāʿah al-Sahmī from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited Sūrat al-Najm in Makkah and prostrated, and those who were with him prostrated. I raised my head and refused to prostrate, and al-Muṭṭalib had not yet accepted Islam at that time. Thereafter, he would never hear anyone recite it without prostrating."" [Ḥasan. As preceded in the one before it]"
"3713","(3713) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from Ibn Shihāb from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāḍ — may Allah be pleased with him — recited {Wa al-najmi idhā hawā} for them and prostrated in it, then stood up and recited another sūrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik in al-Muwaṭṭa (481)]"
"3714","(3714) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Sufyān. (Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim from Zirr from ʿAlī, who said: ""The obligatory prostrations (ʿazāʾim al-sujūd) in the Qurʾān are four: {Alif Lām Mīm Tanzīl}, {Ḥā Mīm} al-Sajdah, al-Najm, and {Iqraʾ bismi Rabbik}."" Yaʿlā said: ""And Sufyān narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq from al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī the like of that."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Ḥākim in al-Mustadrak (3957)]"
"3715","(3715) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed me that Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us that ʿAmr ibn Marzūq and Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm both narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated. (Ḥ) And Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us that Naṣr ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Surah narrated to us that ʿAmr ibn Ḥakkām narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh from ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — that he said: ""The obligatory prostrations are four: {Alif Lām Mīm Tanzīl}, {Ḥā Mīm} al-Sajdah, {Iqraʾ bismi Rabbika alladhī khalaq}, and al-Najm."" This is how the group narrated it from Shuʿbah, and it is mentioned from Hushaym from Shuʿbah something similar to the narration of Sufyān. [Ḥasan]"
"3716","(3716) ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrawī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that Hushaym informed us that Shuʿbah narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah from Zirr from ʿAlī, who said: ""The obligatory prostrations are four: {Alif Lām Mīm Tanzīl}, {Ḥā Mīm} al-Sajdah, al-Najm, and {Iqraʾ bismi Rabbik}."" [Ḥasan]"
"3717","(3717) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed me: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥuṣayn al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read to Mālik, from ʿAbdillāh ibn Yazīd, the freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyān, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that Abū Hurayrah recited for them {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and performed a prostration therein. When he finished, he informed them that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had performed a prostration therein.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 766.]"
"3718","(3718) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Bakr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: al-Ḥārith — that is, ibn Abī Usāmah — narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated, from Abū Salamah, who said: I saw Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) recite {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and perform a prostration therein. I said: ""O Abū Hurayrah, did I not see you prostrate?"" He said: ""Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prostrate, I would not have prostrated.""

al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their two Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 766.]"
"3719","(3719) Abū al-Khayr Jāmiʿ ibn Aḥmad al-Wakīl informed us: Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: al-Muʿtamir — meaning Ibn Sulaymān al-Taymī — narrated, saying: I heard my father say: Bakr — meaning Ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī — narrated to us, from Abū Rāfiʿ, who said: I prayed with Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) the night prayer (al-ʿAtamah), and he recited {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and performed a prostration. I said: ""What is this prostration?"" He said: ""I performed this prostration behind Abū al-Qāsim (ﷺ), and I shall continue to perform it until I meet him.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from ʿUbaydillāh ibn Muʿādh and others from al-Muʿtamir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated previously in the one before it. It will also come under no. 3758.]"
"3720","(3720) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated, from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Maymūnah, who said: I heard Abū Rāfiʿ narrate from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he performed a prostration in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and said: ""I saw my Khalīl (ﷺ) prostrate therein, and I shall continue to prostrate therein until I meet him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated previously in the one before it.]"
"3721","(3721) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated, mentioning something similar to it, except that he said: from Abū Rāfiʿ, who said: ""I saw Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) prostrate in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1]."" I said: ""Do you prostrate in it?"" He mentioned the hadith, and at the end of it Shuʿbah said: I said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ)?"" He said: ""Yes.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār and others from Ghundar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated previously in the one before it.]"
"3722","(3722) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Yazīd informed us: Sufyān, Shuʿbah, and Sharīk narrated, from ʿĀṣim, from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh, who said: I saw ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir recite {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] from the minbar, then he came down and performed the prostration. [Ḥasan.]"
"3723","(3723) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdillāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Saʿdān narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated, from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā, from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Mīnā, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We performed prostration with the Prophet (ﷺ) in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and {Read in the name of your Lord} [al-ʿAlaq: 1].""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim narrated it, no. 578.]"
"3724","(3724) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to me: al-Layth narrated. And Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, the freedman of Banī Makhzūm, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed prostration in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and {Read in the name of your Lord} [al-ʿAlaq: 1]."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated previously in the one before it.]"
"3725","(3725) Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ. And ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I performed prostration with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and {Read in the name of your Lord who created} [al-ʿAlaq: 1] — two prostrations.""

Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it: Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿAmr informed me, from ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar, and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated previously in the one before it.]"
"3726","(3726) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Qurrah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated to us: Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated, saying: ""Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) performed prostration in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqāq: 1] and {Read in the name of your Lord who created} [al-ʿAlaq: 1], and so did the one who is better than them both.""

And the prostration in {Read in the name of your Lord} has also been narrated from ʿAlī and ʿAbdillāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with them both). [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī narrated it, nos. 965, 966.]"
"3727","(3727) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū 'Abdillāh Muhammad ibn 'Alī al-Jawharī narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ismā'īl al-Sulamī narrated to us: Sa'īd ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Nāfi' ibn Yazīd informed us, from al-Ḥārith ibn Sa'īd, from 'Abdillāh ibn Munayn, from 'Amr ibn al-'Āṣ (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) taught him that there are fifteen prostrations (sajdahs) in the Qur'an, three of which are in the Mufaṣṣal, and two in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Ḥasan for other than its own chain. It was also narrated via another chain by Abū Dāwūd (1401).]"
"3728","(3728) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-'Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Ibn Lahī'ah informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and others informed us, saying: Abū al-'Abbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Ibn Lahī'ah informed you, from Mushriḥ ibn Hā'ān Abī al-Muṣ'ab, who narrated to him, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Āmir, who narrated to him, saying: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, are there two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj?"" He said: ""Yes. And whoever does not prostrate them should not recite them.""

It was narrated by 'Amr ibn al-Ḥārith and a group of senior scholars from Ibn Lahī'ah, and Abū Dāwūd included it along with the first hadith in the book of Sunan.

And Abū Dāwūd narrated in al-Marāsīl from Aḥmad ibn 'Amr ibn al-Sarḥ: Ibn Wahb informed us: Mu'āwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me, from 'Āmir ibn Jushayb, from Khālid ibn Ma'dān, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Sūrat al-Ḥajj has been distinguished over the rest of the Qur'an by two prostrations.""

Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī informed us: Abū 'Alī al-La'lu'ī informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated it with this chain.

Abū Dāwūd said: ""This has been narrated with a chain (musnad), but it is not ṣaḥīḥ.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""And this has been narrated from a group of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them)."" [Ḥasan for other than its own chain. What precedes it serves as corroboration for it.]"
"3729","(3729) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-'Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn and Sa'īd ibn 'Āmir narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrāhīm, from 'Abdillāh ibn Tha'labah, that he prayed Fajr with 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he performed two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"3730","(3730) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-'Adl informed us in Baghdād: Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from 'Ubaydillāh, meaning Ibn 'Umar, from Nāfi', who said: A man from the people of Egypt informed me that he prayed Fajr with 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) at al-Jābiyah, and he recited the Sūrah in which al-Ḥajj is mentioned, and he performed two prostrations in it. Nāfi' said: When he finished, he said: ""Indeed, this Sūrah has been distinguished by having two prostrations."" And Ibn 'Umar used to perform two prostrations in it.

This narration from 'Umar, even though it is from Nāfi' in the sense of a mursal (disconnected chain), due to Nāfi' omitting the name of the Egyptian who narrated to him — the first narration from 'Abdillāh ibn Tha'bayr ibn Ṣu'ayr from 'Umar is a ṣaḥīḥ, fully connected narration, and likewise the narration of Nāfi' from Ibn 'Umar is fully connected. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"3731","(3731) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-'Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam informed me: Ibn Wahb informed me: Makhramah ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he performed two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj.

And it has been narrated to us from 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to perform two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by Mālik in al-Muwaṭṭa' (482).]"
"3732","(3732) Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr informed us: Abū al-'Abbās informed us: al-Rabī' said: al-Shāfi'ī reported, on the authority of Hushaym, from Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ju'fī, from Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, from 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to perform two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Very weak (ḍa'īf jiddan). It was narrated by al-Shāfi'ī in al-Umm (7/169).]"
"3733","(3733) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: Mu'ādh ibn Najdah narrated to us: Qabīṣah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from 'Āṣim, from Zirr, from 'Abdillāh ibn Mas'ūd and 'Ammār ibn Yāsir, that they both used to perform two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Ḥasan]"
"3734","(3734) And Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-'Adl informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: Ismā'īl ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us: Yūnus ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, from Bakr ibn 'Abdillāh al-Muzanī, from Ṣafwān ibn Muḥriz, that Abū Mūsā performed two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj, and that he recited the verse of prostration which is at the end of Sūrat al-Ḥajj, and he prostrated and we prostrated with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by al-Ḥākim (3433).]"
"3735","(3735) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāni' narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: 'Umar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us: My father narrated to us, from 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal, from Abū al-'Āliyah, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: ""In Sūrat al-Ḥajj there are two prostrations."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah (4290).]"
"3736","(3736) And Abū Muḥammad 'Abdillāh ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Sa'īd ibn al-A'rābī informed us: Sa'dān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Mu'āwiyah narrated to us, from Ḥajjāj, from 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal, from Abū al-'Āliyah, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: ""Sūrat al-Ḥajj has been distinguished by two prostrations."" And likewise Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal. [Weak (ḍa'īf)]"
"3737","(3737) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated to us: Shuʿbah reported to us, from Yazīd ibn Khumayr, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abū al-Dardāʾ: that he used to perform two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Ḥasan]"
"3738","(3738) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Shuʿbah reported to us, from Yazīd ibn Khumayr, who said: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr report from his father: that Abū al-Dardāʾ used to perform two prostrations in Sūrat al-Ḥajj. [Ḥasan]"
"3739","(3739) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Sulaymān — (ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd reported to us, from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah: that Ibn ʿAbbās was asked about the prostration in {Ṣād}, and he said: ""It is not among the obligatory prostrations. However, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrate in it.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Ḥumayd, 595]"
"3740","(3740) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in al-Mustadrak: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us by dictation: Baḥr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlānī narrated to us in Egypt: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith reported to me, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited {Ṣād} while upon the pulpit. When he reached the verse of prostration, he descended and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. Then on another day, he recited it, and when he reached the verse of prostration, the people prepared for prostration. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""It is the repentance of a Prophet, but I saw you preparing for prostration."" So he descended and prostrated, and they prostrated as well.

This is a ḥadīth with a ḥasan isnād, ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Dārimī, 1466, 1554]"
"3741","(3741) And in what al-Shāfiʿī narrated in al-Qadīm, from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, from ʿUmar ibn Dharr, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""David, peace be upon him, performed this prostration for repentance, and we perform it in gratitude"" — meaning {Ṣād}.

The Imām al-Sharīf Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī narrated it to us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Firās informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: My grandfather narrated to us: Sufyān narrated it.

This is the preserved version, and it is mursal. It has also been narrated through other routes from ʿUmar ibn Dharr, from his father, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, but it is not strong. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī, 957]"
"3742","(3742) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash reported to us, from Muslim, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — said: ""In {Ṣād} is the repentance of a Prophet that has been mentioned."" He said: And Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Has not Allah, the Most High, said: {Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so follow their guidance} [al-Anʿām: 90]?""

[Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 5873. The grading of the statement of Ibn ʿAbbās will follow under number 3746]"
"3743","(3743) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Ḍabbī al-Naḍrawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd reported to us, from ʿĀṣim, from Zirr, from ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — that he did not used to prostrate in {Ṣād}, and he would say: ""It is only the repentance of a Prophet.""

He said: And Saʿīd narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from ʿAbdah ibn Abī Lubābah, from Zirr — who is ibn Ḥubaysh — that ʿAbd Allāh did not used to prostrate in {Ṣād}. And it has been narrated to us from a group of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) that they used to prostrate in {Ṣād}. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah, 4268]"
"3744","(3744) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Saʿīd ibn Muslim narrated to us: Ḥajjāj reported to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid informed me that Saʿīd ibn Jubayr informed him that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""I saw ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) recite {Ṣād} upon the pulpit; he descended and then prostrated, and then ascended the pulpit."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī in his Sunan, 5]"
"3745","(3745) Abū Bakr informed us: ʿAlī informed us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Saʿīd ibn Muslim narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah reported to us, from al-Aʿraj, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd: that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) recited {Ṣād} upon the pulpit, then descended and prostrated. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī in his Sunan, 6]"
"3746","(3746) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muslim informed us: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Shuʿbah reported to us, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard Mujāhid say: ""Ibn ʿAbbās was asked about the prostration in {Ṣād}, and he said: {Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so follow their guidance} [al-Anʿām: 90]."""
"3747","(3747) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Ḥasan informed me; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Bundār narrated to us; Muḥammad narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from al-ʿAwwām, who said: I asked Mujāhid about the prostration in Sūrah Ṣād, and he said: Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about it, and he said: ""{Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so follow their guidance}"" [al-Anʿām: 90]. And Ibn ʿAbbās used to prostrate in it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Bundār. And Yazīd ibn Hārūn and Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī added in it, from al-ʿAwwām, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbās: ""And Dāwūd (peace be upon him) was among those whom your Prophet (ﷺ) was commanded to follow.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 4806.]"
"3748","(3748) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī informed us; Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; al-ʿAwwām ibn Ḥawshab informed us.

And Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to us; al-ʿAwwām ibn Ḥawshab narrated to us — and he mentioned it with their two additions, but without the action of Ibn ʿAbbās.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it with their two additions. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Refer to the one before it.]"
"3749","(3749) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Ḥinnāʾī narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us; Khuṣayf narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: Ibn ʿUmar said to me: ""Do you prostrate in Sūrah Ṣād?"" I said: ""No."" He said to me: ""Then prostrate in it, for Allah, the Most High, says: '{Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so follow their guidance}' [al-Anʿām: 90]."" Thus said Ibn ʿUmar. And it is narrated from another chain from Ibn ʿUmar that he used to say: ""In Sūrah Ṣād there is a prostration.""

[Ḥasan. Al-Ṭaḥāwī narrated it in Mushkil al-Āthār, 7/237.]"
"3750","(3750) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Bakr ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: An informant informed me from Abū Saʿīd, who said: ""I saw in a dream as though I was reciting Sūrah Ṣād. When I reached the prostration verse, everything prostrated — I saw the inkpot, the pen, and the tablet. I went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the morning and informed him, and he commanded prostration in it.""

[Ḍaʿīf. It has a witness (shāhid). Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated it in al-Ḥujjah, 1/112.]"
"3751","(3751) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ḥārith informed us.

(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; al-Bāghandī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yazīd ibn Khunays narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Yazīd, who said: Ibn Jurayj said to me, ""O Ḥasan, your grandfather ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Yazīd narrated to me from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, last night I saw in what the sleeper sees that I was praying behind a tree, and I recited {Ṣād} [Ṣād: 1]. When I reached the prostration verse, I prostrated, and the tree prostrated with my prostration, and I heard it saying: ""O Allah, write for me thereby a remembrance near You, and make for me thereby a treasure near You, and magnify for me thereby a reward near You.""'"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) recite {Ṣād}, and when he reached the prostration verse, he prostrated, and I heard him say in his prostration what the man had reported about the tree's words."" The wording is that of Abū Bakr's narration, except that he did not say ""with my prostration.""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"3752","(3752) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAlī ibn Mukram al-Bazzār in Baghdād narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yazīd ibn Khunays narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, and he mentioned something similar to it, except that he said in the supplication: ""O Allah, write for me thereby a reward near You, and make it for me a treasure near You, and remove from me thereby a burden, and accept it from me as You accepted it from Your servant Dāwūd."" And he did not say ""{Ṣād}""; rather he said: ""I saw as though I had recited a prostration verse, so I prostrated.""

And he added at the end: Muḥammad ibn Yazīd ibn Khunays said: ""Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Yazīd used to lead us in prayer in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām during the month of Ramaḍān. He would recite the prostration verse and prostrate, and he would lengthen the prostration. He was asked about that, and he said: 'Ibn Jurayj told me; your grandfather ʿUbaydillāh ibn Abī Yazīd narrated to me this.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Refer to the one before it.]"
"3753","(3753) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated from Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah, from Ibn Qusaīṭ, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, who informed him from Zayd ibn Thābit, who said: ""He recited before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) {By the star when it descends} [al-Najm: 1], and he did not prostrate.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū al-Rabīʿ from Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 1072.]"
"3754","(3754) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) proscribed in al-Najm, and the people prostrated with him except for two men who intended to become notorious.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The two men did not abandon the obligation, if Allah wills. Had they left it, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would have commanded them to repeat it. As for the ḥadīth of Zayd, then — and Allah knows best — Zayd did not prostrate, and he was the reciter, so the Prophet (ﷺ) did not prostrate, and it was not an obligation such that the Prophet (ﷺ) would command him to do it.""

He cited as evidence the previously mentioned ḥadīth of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding the obligation of the five prayers, in which a man asked: ""Is there anything else obligatory upon me?"" He said: ""No, unless you volunteer."" [Ḥasan. Without the story of the two men; Ibn Abī Ḥātim said in al-ʿIlal, 468.]"
"3755","(3755) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us; ʿAbdillāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Adhramī narrated to us; Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Abī Mulaykah informed me that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUthmān al-Taymī informed him from Rābiʿah ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) recited Sūrah al-Naḥl on Friday until, when the prostration verse came, he descended and prostrated, and the people prostrated. When the second Friday came, he recited it until, when the prostration verse came, he said: 'O people, we have not been commanded to prostrate. So whoever prostrates has done well and done good, and whoever does not prostrate, there is no sin upon him.'"" He said: ""And ʿUmar did not prostrate."" Nāfiʿ added: ""Your Lord has not made the prostration obligatory upon us unless we will.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā from Hishām ibn Yūsuf from Ibn Jurayj with its meaning, except that he said: Ibn Jurayj said, and Nāfiʿ added from Ibn ʿUmar: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has not made the prostration obligatory unless we will."" Its witness (shāhid) is the mursal. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, no. 1077.]"
"3756","(3756) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Najīd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) recited the prostration verse while he was on the pulpit on Friday, so he descended and prostrated, and they prostrated with him. Then he recited it on the following Friday, and they prepared to prostrate, so ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Be at ease. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has not prescribed it upon us unless we will."" So he recited it and did not prostrate, and he prevented them from prostrating.

Al-Bukhārī said: ""It was said to ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn: 'A man hears the prostration verse but did not sit for it.' He said: 'What if he had sat for it?' As though he did not consider it obligatory upon him.""

And in the narration of Sufyān al-Thawrī from ʢĀṣim from Ibn Sīrīn, who said: ""ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) was asked about the prostrations of the Qurʾān, and she said: 'It is a right of Allah that you fulfill, or a voluntary act that you volunteer. There is no Muslim who prostrates to Allah a single prostration except that Allah raises him thereby a degree, or removes from him thereby a sin, or combines both for him.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Due to the discontinuity between ʿUrwah and ʿUmar, and Allah knows best.]"
"3757","(3757) Abu Bakr ibn Ibrahim informed us; Abu Nasr al-Iraqi informed us; Sufyan al-Jawhari narrated to us; Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Walid narrated from Sufyan, and he mentioned it. [Weak. Ibn Sirin did not hear from A'ishah, nor did he narrate from her.]"
"3758","(3758) Abu al-'Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; al-Hasan ibn al-Tayyib al-Balkhi narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh al-'Anbari narrated to us; Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman al-Taymi narrated from his father. Abu Bakr said: and Abu Ya'la narrated to me; 'Amr al-Naqid narrated to us; 'Isa ibn Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq al-Suba'i narrated to us; Sulayman al-Taymi narrated; Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Muzani narrated from Abu Rafi', who said: I prayed with Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the 'Isha' prayer, and he recited {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqaq] and performed a prostration. I said to him: ""What is this prostration?"" He said: ""I performed this prostration with Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ), and I shall continue to perform it until I meet him.""

'Isa ibn Yunus said: [He said] al-'Isha'. And he said: When he finished, he said: ""O Abu Hurayrah, what is this?"" He said: ""He performed a prostration with it,"" and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh and from 'Amr al-Naqid, and al-Bukhari also narrated it as was mentioned earlier. [Sound. Previously referenced as no. 3719.]"
"3759","(3759) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Sulayman al-Taymi informed us; Abu Mijlaz narrated — and I did not hear it from Abu Mijlaz — from Ibn 'Umar: that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed a prostration in the first rak'ah of the Zuhr prayer, and his Companions saw that he had recited {Tanzeel} al-Sajdah [Surah al-Sajdah]. [Weak.]"
"3760","(3760) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us; Isma'il al-Saffar informed us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Tayalisi, Abu al-Fadl, narrated to me; Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us; Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated from his father; from Maytah; from Abu Mijlaz; from Ibn 'Umar: that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed a prostration in the Zuhr prayer, then he stood, and they thought that he had recited a surah containing a prostration. Maytah said this, while others said: Umayyah. [Weak. Previously referenced in the one before it.]"
"3761","(3761) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said: Abu al-'Abbas, al-Asamm, narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to me; Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us; Mu'tamir narrated from his father; from a man called Umayyah, and he mentioned it similarly. [Previously referenced in the one before it.]"
"3762","(3762) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakka and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, al-Asamm, narrated to us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us; it was recited to Ibn Wahb: Yunus ibn Yazid informed you from Ibn Shihab, who said: al-A'raj informed me from Abu Hurayrah, who said: I saw 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, perform a prostration in [Surah] al-Najm during the Fajr prayer, then he began with another surah. [Sound.]"
"3763","(3763) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; Asid ibn 'Asim narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated from Sufyan; from Abu Ishaq; from al-Aswad; from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, that he said: ""When the prostration occurs at the end of the surah, one may perform ruku' if one wishes, or perform sajdah if one wishes."" [Sound. Narrated by 'Abd al-Razzaq, 5918.]"
"3764","(3764) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us; al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Abu Ishaq; from al-Aswad; from Abdullah, regarding a man who recites a surah whose end contains a prostration: ""If he wishes, he may perform ruku', and if he wishes, he may perform sajdah, then stand and recite, perform ruku', and perform sajdah."" [Sound. Previously referenced in the one before it.]"
"3765","(3765) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Birti al-Qadi narrated to us; Muslim — (H) and Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu 'Ali Hamid ibn Muhammad al-Raffa' informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Muzani narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""Two men, each of them better than me — unless I think he said Abu Bakr or 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both — and I do not know which of them it was — one of them performed a prostration in {When the sky is split open} [al-Inshiqaq] and in {Read in the name of your Lord who created} [al-'Alq]."" He said: ""And when 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud recited al-Najm with the people, he performed a prostration, and when he recited it in prayer. And when Ibn 'Umar reached it as part of the Qur'an, he performed a prostration, and when he did not reach it as part of the Qur'an, he performed ruku'. And 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, when he recited it, he performed a prostration, then he stood and recited al-Tin wa al-Zaytun [Surah al-Tin] or a surah similar to it."" He said: ""And the Prophet (ﷺ) performed a prostration with it."" And in the hadith of al-Birti: ""Unless it was the Prophet (ﷺ) or 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."" [Hasan.]"
"3766","(3766) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Hafs ibn 'Umar narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Abu Ishaq; from al-Aswad; from 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited Surah al-Najm and performed a prostration in it, and there remained no one among the people except that he performed a prostration. Then a man from the people took a handful of pebbles or dust and raised it to his face, saying: ""This is sufficient for me.""

'Abdullah said: ""And I later saw him killed as a disbeliever.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Hafs ibn 'Umar, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shu'bah. [Sound. Agreed upon. Previously referenced as no. 3709.]"
"3767","(3767) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Ḥāmid al-Qāḍī informed me, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us, Minjāb narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Mushhir narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Sometimes the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite the Qurʾān and pass by a verse of prostration, and he would perform the prostration along with us, until we crowded around him to the point that none of us could find a place to prostrate upon — and this was outside of the prayer (fī ghayr al-ṣalāh).""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Bishr. And in the ḥadīth of Ibn Mushhir: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite a verse of prostration while we were with him, and he would prostrate and we would prostrate with him, until we crowded together to the point that some of us could not find a place for our foreheads — outside of prayer.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bishr ibn Ādam from ʿAlī ibn Mushhir, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 1075.]"
"3768","(3768) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated from Sufyān from ʬAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib from Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ""Salmān passed by a people who were reciting a verse of prostration. They said: 'Shall we prostrate?' He said: 'We did not set out for it.'""

And from Sufyān, Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʬAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The prostration is only upon the one who sits for it.""

And from Sufyān, Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, who said: ""The prostration is only upon the one who hears it.""

And it has been narrated from another chain from Ibn al-Musayyab from ʿUthmān, who said: ""The prostration is only upon the one who sits for it and listens attentively."" And something similar is narrated from Ibn ʿUmar from Ibn al-Musayyab himself.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 5909.]"
"3769","(3769) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qusayṭ from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Zayd ibn Thābit, who said: ""I recited Sūrat al-Najm before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not prostrate therein.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, and Muslim narrated it as previously mentioned.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under no. 3753.]"
"3770","(3770) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Hishām ibn Saʿd and Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah informed you from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, who said: 'It reached me that a man recited a verse of prostration containing a sajdah before the Prophet ﷺ, so the man prostrated and the Prophet ﷺ prostrated with him. Then another man recited a verse of prostration containing a sajdah while he was with the Prophet ﷺ. The man waited for the Prophet ﷺ to prostrate, but he did not prostrate. So the man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I recited the verse of prostration and you did not prostrate."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'You were the imam (leader). Had you prostrated, I would have prostrated with you.'""

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated it and said: ""I reckon it was Zayd ibn Thābit, because he narrates that he recited before the Prophet ﷺ and he did not prostrate. And ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār narrated both ḥadīths together.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""What al-Shāfiʿī mentioned is possible. And Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Farwah narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration — but Isḥāq is weak (ḍaʿīf). And it was narrated from al-Awzāʿī from Qurrah from al-Zuhrī from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah — and this is also weak. The preserved narration is that of ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār as a mursal narration, and his narration from Zayd ibn Thābit is connected (mawṣūl) and abbreviated. And Allah, the Most High, knows best.""

[Ḍaʿīf. It is from the balāghāt or mursalāt of ʬAṭāʾ.]"
"3771","(3771) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān narrated to us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us, Isḥāq al-Azraq narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Sulaymān ibn Ḥanẓalah, who said: ""I recited a verse of prostration before Ibn Masʿūd. He looked at me and said: 'You are our imam; prostrate, and we shall prostrate with you.'""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"3772","(3772) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn al-Furāt Abū Masʿūd al-Rāzī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite the Qurʾān to us. When he passed by a verse of prostration, he would say the takbīr, prostrate, and we would prostrate."" ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: ""Al-Thawrī used to admire this ḥadīth."" Abū Dāwūd said: ""He admired it because it contains the takbīr.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 1413.]"
"3773","(3773) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us, Ibn ʿAwn narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muslim, meaning Ibn Yasār, from his father, who said: ""When a man recites a verse of prostration, let him not prostrate until he has completed the entire verse. When he has completed it, let him raise his hands, say the takbīr, and prostrate."" He said: ""And I heard Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Sīrīn, say the same.""

And it is narrated from al-Rabīʿ ibn Ṣubayḥ from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī that he said: ""When you recite a verse of prostration, say the takbīr and prostrate, and when you raise your head, say the takbīr."" And it is narrated from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū al-Aḥwaṣ that they both gave one salām in the prostration — a salām to the right. Some of them raised it from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd.

And it is narrated from Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that he prostrated and did not give salām. And from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī that he said: ""There is no salām in the prostration of recitation.""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"3774","(3774) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated to us, Khālid narrated from Abū al-ʿĀliyah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Ismāʿīl, meaning Ibn ʿUlayyah, narrated to us, Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ narrated from a man from Abū al-ʿĀliyah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say in the prostration of the Qurʾān at night, saying in the prostration repeatedly: 'My face has prostrated to the One who created it, and cleft open its hearing and its sight by His power and His strength (sajada wajhiya lladhi khalaqahu wa shaqqa samʿahu wa baṣarahu biḥawlihi wa quwwatihi).'""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh added in his narration: ""So blessed is Allah, the Best of creators (fa tabāraka Allāhu aḥsan al-khāliqīn).""

And what has been narrated concerning this from Ibn ʿAbbās in a marfūʿ narration has already passed.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad, 6/30.]"
"3775","(3775) Abū Saʿīd Sharīk ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī narrated to us in it: Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us, Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: ""A man does not prostrate except in a state of purity.""

[Ḥasan. Its chain is continuous between trustworthy narrators.]"
"3776","(3776) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Abū al-Jumāhir narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, meaning Ibn Muḥammad, from Muṣʿab ibn Thābit ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited a verse of prostration on the Year of the Conquest (Fatḥ), and all the people prostrated — among them were those riding and those prostrating on the ground — to the point that the one riding would prostrate upon his hand.

And it is narrated from ʿAlī and Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, that they both prostrated while riding, by gesture (ishārah). And from Ibn ʿUmar that he was asked about prostrating while on a mount, and he said: ""Prostrate by gesture."" Al-Zuhrī said: ""Do not prostrate except in a state of purity. When you prostrate while in a state of residence (ḥaḍar), face the qiblah. And if you are riding, then there is no blame upon you whichever direction your face is oriented.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 1411.]"
"3777","(3777) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — that is, al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affān narrated to us: Abu Usāmah narrated to us, from Shu'bah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, from Umm Salamah al-Azdiyyah, who said: I saw 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reciting from the muṣḥaf, and when she passed by a verse of prostration (āyah al-sajdah), she would stand and perform prostration."
"3778","(3778) Abu 'Ali al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-'Aṭṭār narrated to us: Abū Baḥr narrated to us: Thābit ibn 'Umārah narrated to us: Abū Tamīmah al-Hujaymī narrated, who said: I used to deliver sermons after the morning prayer (ṣalāt al-ṣubḥ) and would perform prostration [upon reciting a verse of prostration]. Ibn 'Umar forbade me, but I did not cease. He did this three times, then he came back and said: ""Indeed, I performed prayer behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and alongside Abū Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthmān, may Allah be pleased with them, and they did not perform prostration until the sun had risen.""

Furthermore, if this narration is established as marfū' (elevated to the Prophet), then it is preferred (yukhtāru) to delay the prostration until the time of prohibition (karāhah) passes. And if its elevation (raf') is not established, then it is as though he (Ibn 'Umar) analogized it to the voluntary prayer (ṣalāt al-taṭawwu'). And we shall demonstrate, if Allah wills, the specification of that which has a cause (sabab) from the absolute prohibition (al-nahy al-muṭlaq).

It is related from 'Aṭā', Sālim, al-Qāsim, and 'Ikrimah that they permitted prostration [of recitation] after the morning prayer and after the afternoon prayer (al-'aṣr). And it is related from Ka'b ibn Mālik that he performed a prostration of gratitude (sajdah al-shukr) after the dawn prayer (ṣalāt al-fajr) when he received the glad tidings of his repentance being accepted, and that was during the time of the Prophet ﷺ."
"3779","(3779) Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Khaṭmī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us: 'Aṭā' ibn al-Sā'ib narrated to us, from Sa'īd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbās, that he used to perform prostration at the last two verses of Ḥā Mīm al-Sajdah, and that Abu 'Abd al-Raḥmān — meaning Ibn Mas'ūd — used to perform prostration at the first two of them."
"3780","(3780) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us, among the last [of those who said]: They said: Abu al-'Abbas Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us: al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfi'ī informed us: Mālik informed us, from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the Ka'bah, and with him were Bilāl, Usāmah, and 'Uthmān ibn Ṭalḥah. Ibn 'Umar said: I asked Bilāl what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done. He said: ""He placed a pillar to his left, a pillar to his right, and three pillars behind him, and then he prayed."" He said: ""And the House at that time was [supported] upon six pillars.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abd Allah ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik, and he said: ""Two pillars to his left."" And al-Shāfi'ī said likewise in one of the two places. Al-Bukhārī said: And Ismā'īl said to us — Mālik narrated to me and said: ""Two pillars to his right."" The Shaykh [al-Ḥāfiẓ] said: And Ibn Bukayr said likewise from Mālik, and that is the correct version."
"3781","(3781) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed him: Abū Bakr ibn Ja'far informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-'Abdī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated to us, from Nāfi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the Ka'bah — he, Usāmah ibn Zayd, 'Uthmān ibn Ṭalḥah al-Ḥajabī, and Bilāl ibn Rabbāḥ — and he closed it upon them and remained inside it. 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: I asked Bilāl when he came out, ""What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do?"" He said: ""He placed a pillar to his left, two pillars to his right, and three pillars behind him. And the House at that time was [supported] upon six pillars. Then he prayed."" And al-Qa'nabī said likewise in one of the two narrations from him."
"3782","(3782) And 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated it from Mālik and said: ""He left two pillars to his right, one pillar to his left, and three pillars behind him, then he prayed, and between him and the qiblah there were three cubits (dhirā').""

Abū 'Amr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed us: al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā informed us: Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us: 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us from Mālik, and he mentioned it."
"3783","(3783) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed me — I read it from the original of his book — Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us: Surayj ibn al-Nu'mān narrated to us: Fulayḥ narrated to us, from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet ﷺ arrived in the Year of the Conquest, with Usāmah mounted behind him on al-Qaṣwā', and with him were Bilāl and 'Uthmān ibn Ṭalḥah, until he halted by the House. Then he said to 'Uthmān: ""Bring us the key."" So he brought him the key and he opened the door. The Prophet ﷺ, Usāmah, Bilāl, and 'Uthmān entered, and then they closed the door upon them. He remained [inside] for a long part of the day. Then he came out, and the people rushed to enter. I outran them and found Bilāl standing behind the door. I said to him: ""Where did the Prophet ﷺ pray?"" He said: ""He prayed between the two front pillars."" And the House at that time was [supported] upon six pillars in two halves. He prayed between the two pillars of the front half, and he placed the door of the House behind his back, and he turned his face toward the direction that faces you when you enter the House, between it and the wall. He said: ""And I forgot to ask him how many [rak'ahs] he prayed."" And at the place where him prayed there was a red marble stone.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad from Surayj."
"3784","(3784) Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn 'Abdūs al-Sijistānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl ibn Mihrān informed us: Abū Mas'ūd Ismā'īl ibn Mas'ūd al-Jaḥdarī informed us: al-Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān al-Numayrī informed us: Mūsā ibn 'Uqbah informed us — Nāfi' informed me — that when Ibn 'Umar entered the Ka'bah, he would walk straight ahead as he entered, and he would place the door behind his back, and he would walk until there was between him and the wall ahead of him a distance of approximately three cubits, and he would pray there, seeking the place about which Bilāl had informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed in it. And there is no harm upon anyone to pray in any part of the House that he wishes.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir from Abī Ḍamrah from Mūsā."
"3785","(3785) Abū 'Uthmān Sa'īd ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: al-Layth —

(Ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed me and said: 'Ali ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to me: Aḥmad ibn Salamah, Muḥammad ibn Nu'aym, and Muḥammad ibn Shādhān narrated to us, they said: Qutaybah narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the House — he, Usāmah ibn Zayd, Bilāl, and 'Uthmān ibn Ṭalḥah — and they closed it upon them. When they opened it, I was the first to enter. I met Bilāl and asked him, ""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray inside it?"" He said: ""Yes, he prayed between the two right-hand (yamānī) pillars.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd."
"3786","(3786) Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib informed us: Abū Nu'aym informed us: Sayf narrated — I heard Mujāhid saying: Ibn 'Umar was approached in his house and it was said to him: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has entered the Ka'bah."" He said: ""So I went forward and found that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had already come out, and I found Bilāl standing at the door. I said: 'O Bilāl, did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray in the Ka'bah?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'Where?' He said: 'Between the two pillars, two rak'ahs.' Then he went out and prayed two rak'ahs facing the Ka'bah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad from Yaḥyā from Sayf ibn Sulaymān.

And it is said that Abū Nu'aym also narrated from him that he prayed two rak'ahs inside the Ka'bah.

And the narrations of Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān, Ibn 'Awn, and others — from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar — are agreed upon: that he forgot to ask him how many [rak'ahs] he prayed. And in this hadith: that he prayed two rak'ahs in it.

So it is possible that he informed of the minimum that the prayer could consist of, and remained silent about what was in addition to that, because he did not ask Bilāl."
"3787","(3787) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us that Jarir narrated from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan, who said: I asked 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: ""How did the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, act when he entered the Ka'bah?"" He said: ""He prayed two rak'ahs."""
"3788","(3788) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad narrated to us that Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us that Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated from Simak al-Hanafi, who said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, prayed inside the Ka'bah. Soon someone will come and forbid you from that—do not obey him""—meaning Ibn 'Abbas."
"3789","(3789) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed us that Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us that Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us that Muhammad ibn Bakr—

(He said:) And Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed me that Ahmad ibn Sahl ibn Bahr narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Ma'mar ibn Rabi' narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us that Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': ""Did you hear Ibn 'Abbas saying: 'You were only commanded to perform tawaf, not to enter it [the Ka'bah]'?"" He said: ""He did not forbid entering it, but I heard him saying: 'Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that when the Prophet, peace be upon him, entered the House, he supplicated in all its corners and did not pray inside it until he came out. When he came out, he prayed two rak'ahs facing the House and said: This is the qiblah.'"" I said: ""What are its corners—are they its sides?"" He said: ""Every direction of the House is a qiblah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of 'Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj, as mentioned earlier.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Whoever says ""he prayed"" is himself a witness, and whoever says ""he did not pray"" is not a witness. We take the statement of Bilal, and that is the established proof with us.

The Shaykh said: We have also narrated something similar from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him.

And that was also narrated from Shaybah ibn 'Uthman ibn Talhah al-Hajabi, and from 'Uthman ibn Talhah al-Hajabi."
"3790","(3790) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us that Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'Uthman ibn Talhah: that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, prayed inside the Ka'bah. Hammad ibn Salamah is the sole narrator of this hadith, and in it there is an irsal (broken link) between 'Urwah and 'Uthman."
"3791","(3791) Abu Bakr also informed us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Faqih al-Isfahani informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us that 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us that Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us that Khalid narrated from Ibn Abi Laylah, from 'Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, entered the House, then came out with Bilal behind him. I asked Bilal: ""Did he pray?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""The next day he entered, and I asked Bilal: 'Did he pray?' He said: 'Yes, he prayed two rak'ahs, facing the corner [al-Jadh'ah], and placing the second pillar to his right.'"""
"3792","(3792) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Umar narrated to us that al-Husayn ibn Isma'il narrated to us that 'Isa ibn Abi Harb al-Saffar narrated to us that Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated from 'Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim, who said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to me, who said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated to me from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, entered the House and prayed two rak'ahs between the two pillars. Then he came out and prayed two rak'ahs between the Door and the Hijr, and then said: ""This is the qiblah."" Then he entered a second time and stood inside supplicating, then came out and did not pray.

These two narrations, if authenticated, provide evidence that he, peace be upon him, entered it twice—praying once and omitting once. However, the authentication of both hadiths is subject to scrutiny. What is established from Bilal, and he is a positive narrator, is stronger than what is established from Usamah, and he is a negating narrator. And with Bilal there are others besides him."
"3793","(3793) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn—

(He said:) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ala' ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sa'id al-Mihrani narrated to us that Abu Sahl: Bishr ibn Ahmad ibn Bishr narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn 'Ali al-Dhuhli narrated to us; both said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us that Hushaym narrated from Sayyar, from Yazid al-Faqir, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""I have been given five things which no one before me was given: every prophet was sent only to his own people specifically, but I have been sent to every red and black person; the spoils of war were made lawful for me and were not made lawful for anyone before me; the earth has been made for me pure, clean, and a place of prostration—so when the time of prayer overtakes any man, let him pray wherever he is; I have been aided by terror [cast into the hearts of my enemies] at a distance of a month's journey; and I have been given the right of intercession.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and others, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Muhammad ibn Sinan and others from Hushaym."
"3794","(3794) Abu Zakariyya: Yahya ibn Ibrahim al-Muzakka and Abu Bakr: Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, who said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Yahya ibn Ayyub informed you from Zayd ibn Jubayrah al-Ansari, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Nafi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, forbade prayer in seven places: the graveyard, the slaughterhouse, the refuse heap, the bathhouse, the middle of the road, the roof of the House of Allah the Most High, and the resting places of camels."""
"3795","(3795) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Isfahani narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us that 'Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Hilali narrated to us that 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us that Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated it with a similar narration. Zayd ibn Jubayrah is the sole narrator of this hadith.

Abu Sahl al-Mihrani narrated to us: Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hamid informed me that Abu 'Abd Allah al-Rawasani said: I heard al-Bukhari saying: ""Zayd ibn Jubayrah, Abu Jubayrah, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn—his hadith is munkar (objectionable).""

This hadith was also narrated from 'Abd Allah al-'Umari, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from 'Umar, from the Prophet, peace be upon him.

And the hadith of Dawud is more similar, and Allah knows best—thus said Abu 'Isa. And we have narrated from Ibn 'Abbas and Usamah ibn Zayd that the Prophet, peace be upon him, prayed two rak'ahs facing the House and said: ""This is the qiblah."""
"3796","(3796) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Balawayh narrated to us that 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us that Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us that Hammam narrated to us that Qatadah narrated from Anas ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it—there is no expiation for it other than that."" Qatadah said: [Allah says:] ""Establish prayer for My remembrance"" [Taha: 14].

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Hudbah, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Abi Nu'aym from Hammam."
"3797","(3797) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār and Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, from Misʿar, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I am merely a human being; I forget just as you forget. So whoever among you has doubt regarding his prayer, let him consider what is most likely correct, complete upon it, and then perform two prostrations."" Muslim transmitted it from the ḥadīth of Misʿar ibn Kidām, and al-Bukhārī transmitted it through another chain from Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir."
"3798","(3798) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I recited to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you stands to pray, the Satan comes to him and confuses him, so that he does not know how many he has prayed. So when one of you experiences that, let him perform two prostrations while seated."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik."
"3799","(3799) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""When the call to prayer is given, the Satan retreats, passing wind, so that he does not hear the call. When the call is completed, he returns. When the iqāmah is given, he retreats again. When the iqāmah is completed, he returns and insinuates between a man and his soul, saying: 'Remember such and such,' things he was not remembering. So when one of you does not know whether he has prayed three or four, let him perform two prostrations while seated."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muʿādh ibn Faḍālah from Hishām, and Muslim transmitted it through another chain from Hishām."
"3800","(3800) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us: Ḥamzah ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥārith al-ʿAqabī in Baghdad narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Dawrī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Dāwūd narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you has doubt regarding his prayer, and does not know whether he has prayed three or four, let him discard the doubt and proceed upon what is certain, and let him perform two prostrations while seated before he gives the salām. If it was five, the two prostrations will make it even, and if he completed the four, the two prostrations will be a humiliation for the Satan."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Khalaf from Mūsā ibn Dāwūd."
"3801","(3801) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and others informed us: they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: ""Mālik ibn Anas and Dāwūd ibn Qays and Hishām ibn Saʿd informed you that Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to them, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When one of you has doubt regarding his prayer and does not know whether he has prayed three or four, let him stand and pray one rakʿah, then let him perform two prostrations while seated before the salām. If the rakʿah he prayed was the fifth, these two prostrations will make it even, and if it was the fourth, the two prostrations will be a humiliation for the Satan.'"" Except that Hishām made it reach Abū Saʿid al-Khudrī. Baḥr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlānī and others narrated it thus from Ibn Wahb, and Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Wahb narrated it from his uncle Ibn Wahb, making the connection for Dāwūd ibn Qays."
"3802","(3802) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to me: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Wahb narrated to us: my uncle narrated to me: he said: Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿid al-Khudrī. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Wahb, and the narration of Baḥr ibn Naṣr is as if it were more authentic. A group has connected the ḥadīth from Zayd ibn Aslam along with Sulaymān ibn Bilāl and Hishām ibn Saʿd."
"3803","(3803) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally: the Shaykh Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah narrated to us: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿid al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you does not know whether he has prayed three or four, let him complete and pray one rakʿah, then perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness while seated. If his prayer was five, his prayer will be made even, and if it was four, they will be a humiliation for the Satan."" Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān and Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān and Muḥammad ibn Muṭarrif narrated its meaning from Zayd ibn Aslam with a connected chain."
"3804","(3804) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Zurʿah ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAmr al-Dimashqī and Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī — he separated them in two places — both said: Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from Makḥūl, from Kurayb, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: I was sitting with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: ""O Ibn ʿAbbās, have you heard from the Prophet ﷺ regarding the man who forgets his prayer and does not know whether he added or subtracted what he was commanded regarding it?"" I said: ""And what have you heard, O Commander of the Faithful, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning that?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, I have not heard anything from him regarding it, nor did I ask him about it."" Then ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf came and said: ""What are you discussing?"" So ʿUmar informed him and said: ""I asked this young man about such and such, and I did not find any knowledge with him."" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: ""But I have knowledge of it; I have heard it from the Prophet ﷺ."" ʿUmar said: ""You are the trustworthy, acceptable one with us. So what did you hear?"" He said: ""I heard the Prophet ﷺ saying: 'When one of you has doubt regarding his prayer — if he doubts between one and two, let him make it one; if he doubts between two and three, let him make it two; and if he doubts between three and four, let him make it three — so that the doubt is only regarding addition — let him perform two prostrations before he gives the salām, then let him give the salām.'"" A group narrated its meaning from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār. Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUlayyah narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq similarly."
"3805","(3805) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: al-Faḍl ibn al-Faḍl, Abū ʿUbaydah al-Saqaṭī, narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from Makḥūl, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: We were with ʿUmar, and we mentioned the man who is forgetful in his prayer and does not know how many he has prayed. He said: I said: ""I have not heard anything concerning that."" He said: ""While we were thus, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf came and said: 'What are you discussing?' We said: 'The man who is forgetful in his prayer and does not know how many he has prayed.' ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""When a man is forgetful and does not know whether he has prayed two, three, or four, let him consider the forgetfulness to be regarding addition, and let him perform two prostrations.""'"" Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq said: ""I met Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbd Allāh and mentioned this ḥadīth to him. He said: 'Did you chain it for me?' I said: 'No.' He said: 'But Makḥūl narrated to me, from Kurayb, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, from the Prophet ﷺ, a similar ḥadīth.'"" Al-Muḥāribī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq with the meaning of the narration of Ibn ʿUlayyah, and thus the ḥadīth was connected for Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbd Allāh — though he is weak — except that it has a corroborating witness from the ḥadīth of Makḥūl."
"3806","(3806) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, known as Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī, narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr, meaning Ibn Abī ʿĀṣim, narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Sayf narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wāqid al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thābit ibn Thawbān narrated to us, from his father, from Makḥūl, who mentioned it with a meaning similar to the narration of Ibn Isḥāq from Makḥūl, from Kurayb, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, from the Prophet ﷺ. It was also narrated from Thawr ibn Yazīd from Makḥūl, thus connected. And it was narrated from another direction from Ibn ʿAbbās."
"3807","(3807) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, saying: Isma'il al-Makki narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""I was discussing with 'Umar something about the prayer, when 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us and said: 'Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him?' We said: 'Yes, indeed.' He said: 'I bear witness by the testimony of Allah that I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: ""When one of you is in doubt about a deficiency in his prayer, let him pray until he is in doubt about an excess.""'""

And 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak similarly narrated it from Isma'il ibn Muslim al-Makki. And it was also narrated by Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid from Bakr ibn Kathir al-Saqqa' from al-Zuhri. And it was similarly narrated from Sufyan ibn Husayn from al-Zuhri."
"3808","(3808) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us in al-Fawa'id al-Kabir li-Abi al-'Abbas, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Gharzah narrated to us, saying: Ja'far narrated to us, saying: Sa'id — meaning Ibn Abi 'Arubah — narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""When one of you is in doubt about his prayer and does not know whether he prayed two or three, let him cast aside the doubt and build upon certainty.""

This Ja'far is Ibn 'Awn. And thus it was in the original: ""Sa'id, meaning Ibn Abi 'Arubah."" And Allah knows best."
"3809","(3809) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, saying: my brother narrated to me, from Sulayman, from 'Umar ibn Muhammad —

(And in another chain:) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, saying: Mukram ibn Ahmad al-Qadi and others narrated to us, saying: Abu Isma'il Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us, saying: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from 'Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Salim ibn 'Abd Allah, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""When one of you prays and does not know whether he prayed three or four, let him perform one rak'ah, perfecting its bowing and prostration, and then let him prostrate two prostrations.""

Its narrators are trustworthy. However, Malik ibn Anas stopped it in al-Muwatta'."
"3810","(3810) 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-'Adl narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, saying: Malik narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Salim ibn 'Abd Allah, that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar used to say: ""When one of you is in doubt about his prayer, let him determine what he thinks he has missed from his prayer and then pray it, and then let him prostrate two prostrations while sitting."""
"3811","(3811) And by his chain of narration, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, when asked about forgetfulness in the prayer, would say: ""Let one of you determine what he thinks he has missed from his prayer and then pray it."""
"3812","(3812) And by his chain of narration, he said: Malik narrated to us, from 'Afif ibn 'Amr al-Sahmi, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, who said: ""I asked 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As and Ka'b al-Ahbar about the one who is in doubt about his prayer and does not know whether he prayed three or four. Both of them said: 'Let him stand and pray another rak'ah, and let him prostrate two prostrations when he has finished praying.'"""
"3813","(3813) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, saying: Malik narrated to us —

(And in another chain:) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, saying: I read to Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from 'Abd Allah ibn Malik ibn Buhaynah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, prayed two rak'ahs of one of the prayers and then stood up without sitting. The people stood up with him. When he completed his prayer and we were waiting for his taslim, he said the takbir and prostrated two prostrations while sitting, before the taslim, and then he gave the taslim.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya."
"3814","(3814) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us, saying: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, saying: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj, from 'Abd Allah ibn Buhaynah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, prayed one of the two evening prayers. He stood up after two rak'ahs and did not sit. When he was at the end of his prayer, we waited for his taslim — that is, for him to give the taslim — and he prostrated two prostrations before giving the taslim, and then he gave the taslim."""
"3815","(3815) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, saying: Abu Salih al-Juhani narrated to us, saying: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, from al-'Ajlan, the freedman of Fatimah, who reported to him that Muhammad ibn Yusuf, the freedman of 'Uthman, reported to him from his father, that Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him, led them in prayer and forgot. He had to sit but did not sit. When he was at the end of his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations before the taslim and then said: 'Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, do it.' His father said: 'And that is my opinion.'""

The Sheikh said: And 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani did the same.

Abu Dawud al-Sijistani said: And Ibn al-Zubayr also performed the two prostrations and stood up after two rak'ahs before the taslim. And that is the opinion of al-Zuhri.

The Sheikh said: There has been disagreement concerning this from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr."
"3816","(3816) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, saying: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, saying: Malik narrated to us —

(And in another chain:) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to me, saying: Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us, saying: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us, saying: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan, the freedman of Ibn Abi Ahmad, who said: ""I heard Abu Hurayrah say: 'The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, led us in the 'Asr prayer and gave the taslim after two rak'ahs. Dhu al-Yadayn stood up and said: ""Has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""None of that happened."" He said: ""Some of that has indeed happened, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, turned to the people and said: ""Did Dhu al-Yadayn speak the truth?"" They said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, completed what remained of the prayer, and then prostrated two prostrations after the taslim while sitting."" This is the wording of the hadith of Qutaybah. Al-Shafi'i did not mention his words: ""None of that happened."" And he said: ""Then he prostrated two prostrations while sitting after the taslim.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id, and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of Abu Salamah and Ibn Sirin from Abu Hurayrah with the same meaning."
"3817","(3817) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us, from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Muhallab, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) concluded the ʿAṣr prayer after three rakʿahs, then stood and entered the chamber. Thereupon al-Khirbāq, a man with long hands, stood and called out: 'O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened?' The Prophet (ﷺ) came out dragging his cloak in anger. He asked and was informed, so he performed the rakʿah that he had omitted, then made salām, then prostrated two prostrations, then made salām.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from al-Thaqafī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 574]"
"3818","(3818) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr, Abū Bakr, and ʿUthmān — the two sons of Abī Shaybah — said: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed."" Ibrāhīm said: ""I do not know whether he added or omitted something."" When he made salām, it was said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, has something new occurred in the prayer?"" He said: ""What is that?"" They said: ""You prayed such and such."" He then folded his legs, faced the qiblah, and prostrated two prostrations, then made salām. When he turned away, he faced us with his countenance and said: 'Had something new occurred in the prayer, I would have informed you of it. However, I am but a human being; I forget just as you forget. So when I forget, remind me. And when any one of you is in doubt regarding his prayer, let him seek out the correct course, complete upon it, then make salām, then prostrate two prostrations.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr and ʿUthmān, the two sons of Abī Shaybah, and from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm — except that he did not include the wording of the salām. Al-Bukhārī and other imāms from among the authorities confirmed it from these narrators. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 401]"
"3819","(3819) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir narrated to us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Jarīr narrated, and he mentioned the hadith in a similar manner, except that he said: ""He prayed a prayer, adding or omitting something."" When he made salām, he turned to the people with his countenance, and he did not mention his words: ""and he faced the qiblah."" And at the end he said: ""When he made salām, he prostrated the two prostrations of sahw.""

Sufyān al-Thawrī, Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, and Wuhaib ibn Khālid also preserved it from Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir.

Misʿar ibn Kidām, Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ, and ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated it from Manṣūr, and they did not mention the wording of the salām or the word al-taḥarrī (seeking the correct course).

A group narrated it from Ibrāhīm, among them al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah and Sulaymān ibn Mihrān al-Aʿmash, and they did not mention this wording or the word al-taḥarrī.

Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd al-Nakhaʿī narrated it from ʿAlqamah and did not mention the two wordings. He is other than Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Nakhaʿī al-Faqīh. He preserved what Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd did not preserve in narrations other than that of al-Ḥakam from him regarding addition or omission, and said: ""He prayed five rakʿahs.""

Al-Aswad ibn Yazīd narrated it from Ibn Masʿūd, agreeing with Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd from ʿAlqamah in that he prayed five rakʿahs, and he did not mention the two wordings. And Allah, the Most High, knows best.

A narration from Ibn Masʿūd has been reported contradicting this regarding the salām, though its soundness is subject to consideration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Refer to the one before it]"
"3820","(3820) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated it to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Nufaylī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khuṣayf, from Abū ʿUbaydah ibn ʿAbdullāh, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you are in prayer and are in doubt whether you have prayed three or four rakʿahs, and your stronger assumption is four, then make tashahhud, then prostrate two prostrations while you are sitting, before you make salām, then make tashahhud again, then make salām.""

Abū Dāwūd said: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid likewise narrated it from Khuṣayf and did not raise it to the Prophet (ﷺ). ʿAbd al-Wāḥid was also in agreement with Sufyān, Sharīk, and Isrāʾīl. They differed regarding the wording of the hadith and did not chain it. [Ḍaʿīf. The stronger position is that it is mawqūf]"
"3821","(3821) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us: Ḥajjāj, meaning Ibn Muḥammad, said: Ibn Jurayj said.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ḥajjāj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Musāfiʿ informed me that Muṣʿab ibn Shaybah informed him from ʿUtbah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever is in doubt regarding his prayer, let him prostrate two prostrations after he makes salām.""

This is a chain that is not bad, except that the hadith of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī has a sounder chain than it, along with the hadith of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and Abū Hurayrah, as we shall mention. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 1033, with another chain]"
"3822","(3822) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sukkārī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbayd, meaning al-Kilāʿī, narrated to us, from Zuhayr, meaning al-ʿAnsī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr, meaning Ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Thawbān, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""For every sahw (forgetfulness) there are two prostrations after making salām."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 1039]"
"3823","(3823) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān and al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us, from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah and Shujāʿ ibn Makhlad, that Ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to them, and he mentioned it in a similar manner, except that ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān alone mentioned ""from his father.""

And Zuhayr, meaning Ibn Sālim al-ʿAnsī, said: ""This chain contains weakness.""

And the hadith of Abū Hurayrah, ʿImrān, and others regarding the occurrence of multiple instances of sahw (forgetfulness) upon the Prophet (ﷺ) and his restricting himself to two prostrations contradicts this. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Refer to the one before it]"
"3824","(3824) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Jushamī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us: al-Masʿūdī narrated to us, from Ziyād ibn ʿIlāqah, who said: ""Al-Mughīrah led us in prayer and stood up after two rakʿahs. We said: 'SubḥānAllāh.' He said: 'SubḥānAllāh,' and continued. When he completed his prayer and made salām, he prostrated the two prostrations of sahw. When he turned, he said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do as I did.'""

Abū Dāwūd said: Ibn Abī Laylā likewise narrated it from al-Shaʿbī from al-Mughīrah, raising it to the Prophet (ﷺ).

The Shaykh said: And the hadith of Ibn Buḥaynah is sounder than this, along with the narration of Muʿāwiyah, and in their hadith is that the Prophet (ﷺ) prostrated them before the salām. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad, 17767]"
"3825","(3825) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, from Mālik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When any one of you is in doubt regarding his prayer and does not know whether he prayed three or four rakʿahs, let him pray one rakʿah and prostrate two prostrations while he is sitting, before making salām. If the rakʿah that he prayed is the fifth, these two will make it even (shafʿ), and if it is the fourth, then the two prostrations are a humiliation for the Shayṭān.""

We have also narrated it from the hadith of Sulaymān ibn Bilāl and Hishām ibn Saʿd, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd, in a connected (mawṣūl) chain. It has also been narrated from the hadith of Mālik in a connected chain. [Ṣaḥīgh for other than it. Refer to no. 3801]"
"3826","(3826) Abū Ḥāzim ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn ʿUmayr ibn Yūsuf al-Dimashqī narrated to us in Damascus: Muḥammad ibn Wazīr, meaning Ibn al-Ḥakam al-Sulamī, narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, and he said: Mālik ibn Anas interpreted what he narrated to us from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When any one of you is in doubt regarding his prayer and does not know whether he prayed three or four rakʿahs, let him cast aside the doubt and build upon certainty, then let him prostrate two prostrations before he makes salām. If his prayer was odd (witr), these two prostrations will make it even (shafʿ), and if it was even (shafʿ), then the two prostrations are a humiliation for the Shayṭān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Refer to no. 2800]"
"3827","(3827) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us: Abu al-'Abbas, he is al-Aṣamm — informed us: Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi — informed us: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi — informed us: Muhammad ibn Isḥaq — from Makhul — from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas — from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: I sat with 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and he mentioned the hadith as narrated from Abu 'Abd Allah, and in it is that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf said: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: ""When one of you is uncertain in his prayer — uncertain between one and two — let him consider it one. And if he is uncertain between two and three, let him consider it two. And if he is uncertain between three and four, let him consider it three — so that the doubt is always about an addition — and then let him prostrate two prostrations before he gives the salam, and then let him give the salam."" [Ṣaḥīḥ by other than this chain. It was previously cited under number 3804.]"
"3828","(3828) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Ahmad ibn Salmān — informed us: 'Ubayd ibn 'Abd al-Wāḥid — (ḥ) and 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār — informed us: 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk and Ibn Milḥān — both said: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr — informed us: al-Layth — from Ibn Shihāb — from Abu Salamah — from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The Shayṭān comes to one of you in his prayer and confuses him until he does not know how many he has prayed. So when one of you finds that, let him prostrate two prostrations while he is sitting."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd. And likewise it was narrated by Mālik ibn Anas, Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah, Ma'mar ibn Rāshid, and the brother of al-Zuhrī and Muhammad ibn Isḥaq ibn Yasār from al-Zuhrī, and they added in it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was previously cited under number 3798.]"
"3829","(3829) Abu 'Ali al-Rūdhbārī — informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah — informed us: Abu Dāwūd — informed us: Ḥajjaj ibn Abi Ya'qub — informed us: Ya'qub, meaning Ibrāhīm ibn Sa'd — informed us: the brother of al-Zuhrī — from Muhammad ibn Muslim, with this hadith by its chain, and he added: ""while he is sitting, before the taslīm."" [Ṣaḥīḥ Shādhdh. Ibn Rajab said in al-Fatḥ 7/225.]"
"3830","(3830) And Abu al-Ḥasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muhammad ibn Isḥaq — informed us: Yūsuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qāḍī — informed us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr and Naṣr ibn 'Ali — both said: Yazīd ibn Hārūn — informed us: Muhammad ibn Isḥaq — from al-Zuhrī — from Abu Salamah — from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""When one of you prays and does not know whether he added or subtracted, let him prostrate two prostrations while he is sitting, and then let him give the salam."" [Shādhdh. Ibn Rajab said in al-Fatḥ 7/225.]"
"3831","(3831) Abu 'Ali al-Rūdhbārī — informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah — informed us: Abu Dāwūd — informed us: Ḥajjāj — informed us: Ya'qub — informed us: my father — from Ibn Isḥaq, who said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhrī narrated to me by his chain, and he said: ""Let him prostrate two prostrations before he gives the salam, and then let him give the salam."" And Ibn Isḥaq has another chain for it. [Shādhdh. It was previously cited in the one before it.]"
"3832","(3832) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ash'ath, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismā'īl, and Muhammad ibn Makhlad, and Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr — they said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Sa'd — informed us: my uncle, Ya'qub ibn Ibrāhīm. This is shādhdh. [It was previously cited in the one before it…]"
"3833","(3833) He said, and al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismā'īl — informed us: Muhammad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī — informed us: Ya'qub ibn Ibrāhīm — informed us: my father — from Ibn Isḥaq — informed us: Salamah ibn Ṣafwān ibn Salamah al-Anṣārī, then al-Zuraqī — from Abu Salamah — from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""When the mu'adhdhin gives the adhān, the Shayṭān leaves the mosque, and he has a hissing sound. When the mu'adhdhin falls silent, he returns. When the mu'adhdhin gives the iqāmah, he leaves the mosque, and he has a flatulent sound. When he falls silent, he returns — until he comes to the Muslim man in his prayer and enters between him and his own self, so that he does not know whether he added to his prayer or subtracted from it. So when one of you finds that, let him prostrate two prostrations while he is sitting, before he gives the salam, and then let him give the salam."" And Hishām al-Dastawā'ī and al-Awzā'ī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abi Kathīr from Abu Salamah, without this addition; and 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammār narrated it from Yaḥyā and mentioned it. [Shādhdh. Ibn Rajab said in al-Fatḥ 7/225.]"
"3834","(3834) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me: Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān — informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Rūmī — informed us: 'Umar ibn Yūnus — informed us: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammār — informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abi Kathīr, who said: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān narrated to me, who said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""When one of you is forgetful and does not know whether he added or subtracted, let him prostrate two prostrations while he is sitting, and then let him give the salam."" And likewise it was narrated by Muhammad ibn Marzūq from 'Umar ibn Yūnus. And all of that is in agreement with the second narration from 'Aṭā' ibn Yasār from Abu Sa'īd al-Khudrī from the Prophet (peace be upon him). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Dāraquṭnī said in al-'Ilal 1761.]"
"3835","(3835) Abu Zakariyyā ibn Abi Isḥāq al-Muzakkī — informed us: Ahmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād — who said: It was read to al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram while I was listening — informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn — informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd — from 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz — from Ibn Buḥaynah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stood up in two of the ẓuhr or the 'aṣr, and when he stood upright he did not return until he had completed his prayer. Then he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness before he gave the salam, and then he gave the salam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. The origin of the hadith is agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh). It was previously cited under numbers 3813 and 3814.]"
"3836","(3836) And Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abu al-Ḥasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Qurqub al-Tammār in Hamadhān — informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn — informed us: Ādam ibn Abi Īyās — informed us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b — from al-Zuhrī — from al-A'raj — from 'Abd Allah ibn Buḥaynah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prayed with us and stood up in the first two rak'ahs before sitting. He continued in his prayer, and when he had completed it — and the people were waiting for him to give the salam — he said the takbīr, and prostrated before giving the salam, then raised his head, then said the takbīr and prostrated, then raised his head and gave the salam. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abi Īyās, and Muslim extracted it from various routes from al-Zuhrī, and they both extracted it from the hadith of Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd al-Anṣārī from al-A'raj. So it is a firmly established hadith, and there is no doubt among hadith scholars regarding its authenticity. And al-A'raj is 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz, one of the trustworthy narrators of the Medinans. And 'Abd Allah ibn Buḥaynah is 'Abd Allah ibn Mālik ibn al-Qishb, from Azd Shanū'ah, and his mother is Buḥaynah bint al-Ḥārith ibn al-Muṭṭalib — al-Bukhārī mentioned him from 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Madīnī. Al-Bukhārī said: his son 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah narrated from him. Muhammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us of that: Ibrāhīm ibn 'Abd Allah al-Aṣbahānī — informed us: Abu Aḥmad ibn Fāris — informed us: al-Bukhārī, who mentioned it from 'Ali. Al-Shāfi'ī (may Allah have mercy on him) said in the al-Qadīm: ""Ibn Buḥaynah is well known for his companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and others have narrated this from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in agreement with the narration of Ibn Buḥaynah."" The Sheikh said: Al-Shāfi'ī narrated it, and we have narrated in what passed from Mu'āwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace be upon him) with its meaning. And al-Shāfi'ī narrated in the al-Qadīm from Muṭrif ibn Māzin from Ma'mar from al-Zuhrī, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prostrated before the salam and after it, and the last of the two matters was before the salam."" And he also mentioned it in the narration of Ḥarmalah, except that the statement of al-Zuhrī is munqaṭi' (disconnected), for he did not trace it back to any of the Companions. (j) And Muṭrif ibn Māzin is not strong. And Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī — informed us: Ismā'īl ibn Muhammad al-Ṣaffār — informed us: Ahmad ibn Manṣūr — informed us: 'Abd al-Razzāq — informed us: Ma'mar — from al-Zuhrī — from Abu Salamah and Abu Bakr ibn Sulaymān — from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who mentioned the prayer of the Prophet (peace be upon him) and his forgetfulness, and then al-Zuhrī said: ""And that was before Badr, and then matters became firmly established afterward."" And this is what has reached us from al-Zuhrī in this meaning — except that the one who narrated this story to al-Zuhrī did not mention the prostrations of forgetfulness to him, and he used to claim that the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness on the Day of Dhū al-Yadayn or the Day of Dhū al-Shimālayn, as we shall mention, if Allah, the Most High, wills. And others have affirmed his two prostrations from Abu Salamah and Ibn Sīrīn and Abū Sufyān from Abu Hurayrah on the Day of Dhū al-Yadayn, and the well-known fatwā from al-Zuhrī is regarding the prostration of forgetfulness before the salam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was previously cited under number 3813.]"
"3837","(3837) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī in Baghdād informed us that Simāk ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us that Abū Muhir ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Muhir al-Dimashqī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated from his brother ʿAmr ibn Muhājir al-Dimashqī that al-Zuhrī said to ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, may Allah have mercy on him: ""The two prostrations are before the salām.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr narrated it in al-Istidhkār 1/524]"
"3838","(3838) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us that ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us that Abū al-Walīd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from al-Ḥakam from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah from ʿAbd Allāh that the Prophet ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr with five rakʿahs. It was said to him: ""Has something been added to the prayer?"" He said: ""What is that?"" They said: ""You prayed five rakʿahs."" So he performed two prostrations while he was sitting, and he said once: ""After he had finished.""
[Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and he said: ""He performed two prostrations after he had given salām,"" and that is because he only mentioned it after the taslīm.]
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it (1226)]"
"3839","(3839) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated with his chain, similarly, saying: ""The Prophet ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr with five rakʿahs. When he gave salām, it was said: 'Has something been added to the prayer?' He said: 'What is that?' They said: 'You prayed five rakʿahs.' So he performed two prostrations.""
[Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh.]
[Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3840","(3840) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us that my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Isḥāq, meaning Ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī, informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrating from Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd al-Nakhaʿī al-Aʿwar.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us that Jarīr narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUbayd Allāh from Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd, saying: ""ʿAlqamah led us in Ẓuhr with five rakʿahs. When he gave salām, the people said: 'O Abā Shibl, you have prayed five rakʿahs!' He said: 'No, I did not do that.' They said: 'Yes indeed.' He said: 'I was on one side of the people and I was a boy, so I said: 'Yes, you have prayed five rakʿahs.' He said: 'And you too, O Aʿwar, say that?' I said: 'Yes.' So he turned and performed two prostrations, then gave salām, then he said: ʿAbd Allāh said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in five rakʿahs. When he turned, the people whispered among themselves. It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, has something been added to the prayer?' He said: 'No.' They said: 'You have prayed five rakʿahs.' So he turned, then performed two prostrations, then gave salām, then he said: 'I am only a human being like you; I forget as you forget. So when one of you forgets, let him perform two prostrations.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Jarīr. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Numayr from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs, and from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, according to the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿUthmān, except that he placed his saying: ""So when one of you forgets, let him perform two prostrations"" in the narration of Ibn Numayr from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs. Our shaykh Abū ʿAbd Allāh narrated it as I have written it.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it (401)]"
"3841","(3841) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us that Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us that Abū Bakr al-Nahshalī narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad from his father from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, saying: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed one of the two evening prayers. When he turned, they said: 'You have prayed five rakʿahs.' He said: 'I am only a human being; I remember as you remember, and I forget as you forget.' Then he turned and performed the two prostrations of sahw.""
[Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAwn ibn Salām from Abū Bakr al-Nahshalī.]
[Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3842","(3842) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us that Ḥafṣ and Abū Muʿāwiyah both said: al-Aʿmash narrated to us from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah from ʿAbd Allāh that the Prophet ﷺ performed the two prostrations of sahw after the salām and the speech.
[Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Numayr.]

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""That is because he only mentioned the sahw after the speech, so he asked, and when he became certain that he had forgotten, he performed the two prostrations of sahw.""

The Shaykh said: ""That is clear in the ḥadīth of al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah from Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Nakhaʿī, then in the narration of Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd al-Nakhaʿī from ʿAlqamah, then in the narration of al-Aswad from ʿAbd Allāh.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned before (no. 3838)]"
"3843","(3843) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Minjāb ibn al-Ḥārith al-Tamīmī narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn Muhir narrated from al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah from ʿAbd Allāh, saying: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed and added or decreased"" — Ibrāhīm said: ""The doubt is from me"" — ""It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, has anything been added to the prayer?' He said: 'I am only a human being; I forget as you forget. So when one of you forgets, let him perform two prostrations while he is sitting.'"" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned and performed two prostrations.
[Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Minjāb ibn al-Ḥārith.]

In this ḥadīth and in the ḥadīth of al-Aswad from ʿAbd Allāh: ""His prostration was after his saying: 'I am only a human being…'""

There has already passed in the narration of Manṣūr from Ibrāhīm that which indicates that he ﷺ prostrated first, then gave salām, then turned to the people and said what he said.

There has already passed in this chapter from Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd from ʿAlqamah something similar to that, and it is more deserving of being correct than the narration of the one who omitted the order in his account.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3844","(3844) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us that al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān; and Jābir narrated to us that al-Mughīrah ibn Shubayl al-Aḥmasī narrated to us that Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the imām stands up in the two rakʿahs, if he remembers before he fully stands, let him sit; and if he fully stands, let him not sit, and he should perform the two prostrations of sahw."""
"3845","(3845) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran informed us: Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us: Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us: Ibn 'Awn informed us, from 'Amir, who said: I prayed behind al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, and he rose after the second unit [of prayer], so the people said ""Subhan Allah,"" whereupon he sat back down. When he had finished [the prayer], he performed the two prostrations of forgetfulness, and we prostrated along with him.

This, in our view, indicates that he had not yet stood fully upright.

And it has been related to us from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Anas ibn Malik: that he moved to stand after the second unit of the 'Asr prayer, so they said ""Subhan Allah"" to him, whereupon he sat back down, and then performed the two prostrations of forgetfulness while seated. [Weak. Its chain revolves around Jabir al-Ju'fi]"
"3846","(3846) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlīyy narrated to us that Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me that my maternal uncle Mālik ibn Anas narrated to me.

(Ḥ) And Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī informed us that Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾīnī narrated to us that Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read before Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buḥaynah, who said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs of one of the prayers with us, then he stood up and did not sit. So the people stood up with him. When he completed his prayer and we were waiting for his taslīm, he said the takbīr and performed two prostrations while he was seated, before the taslīm, then he pronounced the taslīm.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Weak]"
"3847","(3847) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq of Naysābūr and Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ of Baghdād narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from al-Aʿraj, from Ibn Buḥaynah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up in two rakʿahs of the Ẓuhr prayer without sitting therein. When he completed his prayer, he performed two prostrations and then gave salām after that.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was previously cited under no. 3813.]"
"3848","(3848) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Yaḥyā, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up in the shafʿ in which he was meant to sit during his prayer, so he continued in his prayer. When he was at the end of the prayer, he prostrated before giving salām, and then gave salām.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī.

And we have narrated it from the ḥadīth of Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān (may Allah be pleased with him) with the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was cited before it.]"
"3849","(3849) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā, from ʿĀmir, who said: al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah led us in prayer, and he stood up in the two rakʿahs. The people said ""Subḥān Allāh"" to alert him, but he did not sit. When he gave salām, he performed the two prostrations of sahw, and then said: ""I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) do that.""

And we have narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Masʿūdī from Ziyād ibn ʿIlāqah from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah similarly. However, the ḥadīth of Ibn Buḥaynah regarding the prostration before the salām is more authentic than that. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. It was previously cited under no. 3824.]"
"3850","(3850) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, who said: Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ led us in prayer, and he rose in the two rakʿahs. The people said ""Subḥān Allāh"" to alert him, but he continued in his prayer. Then, when he turned (to conclude), he said: ""I did as I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do.""

Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated it from Abī Muʿāwiyah and added in it: ""Then he performed the two prostrations of sahw when he turned (to conclude)."" [Munkar. The marfūʿ version is mentioned, but the mawqūf version is the authentic one.]"
"3851","(3851) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿIṣmah ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAdl narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, mentioning it with its meaning. (T) And Bayān narrated from Qays, and he stopped it at Saʿd. [Mankūr. It was cited in the one before it.]"
"3852","(3852) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Munqidh al-Khawlānī narrated to us: Idrīs ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb that he heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shumāsah al-Mahrī say: ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī led us in prayer, and he stood up when he was supposed to sit. The people said: ""Subḥān Allāh! Subḥān Allāh!"" But he did not sit and continued standing. When he was at the end of his prayer, he performed the two prostrations of sahw while he was sitting. When he gave salām, he said: ""I heard you just now saying 'Subḥān Allāh' so that I would sit, but the Sunnah is what I have done.""

And we have narrated that from a group of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them). And what we have mentioned is sufficient, and with Allah is the success. [Ḥasan.]"
"3853","(3853) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī narrated to us: Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdūyah ibn Sahl al-Muṭṭawwiʿī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥammād al-Āmulī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāliḥ—

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Wuḥāẓī narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-ʿAnasī narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""There is no sahw in a momentary movement during prayer, except standing up instead of sitting, or sitting instead of standing.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Dārimī. And in the ḥadīth of al-Āmulī: ""Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to us."" This is a ḥadīth in which Abū Bakr al-ʿAnasī is alone. (J) And he is unknown (majhūl). [Ḍaʿīf. al-Dāraquṭnī narrated it in his Sunan, 1/378/2.]"
"3854","(3854) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, saying: Khuṣayf narrated to me from Abī ʿUbaydah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: Sahl occurs when one stands in a place where one should sit, or sits in a place where one should stand, or gives salām after two rakʿahs. So when one finishes one's prayer, one performs two prostrations while sitting, recites tashahhud in them, and gives salām.

[Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated it, 3491.]"
"3855","(3855) The Imām and Faqīh Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shurayḥī narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Muslim narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: Thābit narrated, saying: Anas led us in prayer, and he stood where he should have sat and sat where he should have stood, so he performed two prostrations. And he narrated from his companions that they used to do that.

[Ḥasan. Ibn al-Jaʿd narrated it in his Musnad, 1372.]"
"3856","(3856) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥām al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Marādī narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated from Ayyūb, from Abī Qilābah. Abū Sulaymān Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to us: ""Pray as you have seen me pray. When the time for prayer comes, let one of you give the adhān, and let the eldest of you lead you in prayer.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī narrated it, 631.]"
"3857","(3857) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; al-Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl informed us; Hammām informed us; Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah informed us; ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād narrated from his father, from his uncle Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ, who said:

He was sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man came and entered the mosque and prayed. When he finished his prayer, he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Upon you — go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" So he went back and prayed, and we watched his prayer, not knowing what was deficient in it. When he finished his prayer, he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" He mentioned this either two or three times. The man said: ""I do not know what you find deficient in my prayer."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The prayer of any one of you is not complete until he performs wuḍūʾ as Allah, the Exalted, has commanded — washing his face and his hands up to the elbows, wiping his head, and washing his feet up to the ankles. Then he says the takbīr, praises Allah, and glorifies Him, and recites of the Qurʾān what Allah has permitted for him. Then he says the takbīr and goes into rukūʿ, placing his hands upon his knees until his joints settle and he is at ease. Then he says: 'Samiʿa Allāhu li-man ḥamidah,' and stands upright until every limb returns to its place. Then he straightens his back. Then he says the takbīr and prostrates, placing his forehead upon the earth until his joints settle and he is at ease. Then he says the takbīr and raises his head, and sits upright upon his seat, straightening his back."" He described the prayer in this manner until he finished, then said: ""The prayer of any one of you is not complete until he does that.""

[Ṣaḥīh. It has been previously cited under no. 2635.]"
"3858","(3858) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥassān al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak informed me; Ismāʿīl ibn Muslim narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said:

I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""When any one of you is in doubt regarding his prayer — whether something was deficient — let him pray until he is in doubt about having added (extra).""

Its meaning has already passed in the ḥadīth of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Ibn ʿUmar, and Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with them.

[Ḍaʿīf. al-Bazzār]"
"3859","(3859) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Ḥajjāj said; Yazīd ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that:

The Prophet ﷺ prayed one of the two evening prayers — either Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr, and the narrator's stronger opinion was that he said Ẓuhr — and he gave salām after two rakʿahs. He then stood by a piece of wood at the front of the mosque, and he was angry. (Ḥajjāj did not mention ""and he was angry."") He placed his hand upon it. Among the people were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and they were too awed to speak to him. The people hurried out, saying: ""Has the prayer been shortened? Has the prayer been shortened?"" Among the people was a man whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to call Dhū al-Yadayn. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, have you forgotten, or has the prayer been shortened?"" He said: ""I have not forgotten, and the prayer has not been shortened."" He said: ""Yes, you have forgotten, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Has Dhū al-Yadayn spoken the truth?"" Then he prayed two rakʿahs, then said the takbīr and prostrated with a prostration like his usual one or longer. Then he raised his head and said the takbīr. Then he placed his head and said the takbīr, then prostrated with a prostration like his usual one or longer. Then he raised his head and said the takbīr. The wording is that of Sulaymān.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar from Yazīd ibn Ibrāhīm, except that he said: ""And my stronger opinion is that it was ʿAṣr,"" and he said: ""Then he gave salām, then said the takbīr.""

[Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, nos. 714, 715, 482.]"
"3860","(3860) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakkār informed us; Ḥakīm ibn Nāfiʿ al-Raqīyy narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd al-Māliniyy informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdiyy informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ informed us; al-Tarjumāniyy informed us; Ḥakīm ibn Nāfiʿ al-Raqīyy informed us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The two prostrations of sahw compensate for every addition and every omission."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Māliniyy. In the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbdān it reads: ""The two prostrations of sahw are for every addition and every omission."" This ḥadīth is counted among the unique narrations (afrād) of Ḥakīm ibn Nāfiʿ al-Raqīyy. Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn used to consider him trustworthy (wa-thaqqaqahu). And Allah knows best.

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā, nos. 4592, 4684.]"
"3861","(3861) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿImrān, from Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā, from his father, who said:

I prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and he would not complete the takbīr.

This, according to us, is interpreted to mean that he ﷺ forgot (to say) it and therefore did not prostrate for sahw.

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭayālīsī, no. 1278.]"
"3862","(3862) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Najayd informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that:

ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, was leading the people in Maghrib prayer and did not recite in it. When he finished, it was said to him: ""You did not recite."" He said: ""How was the rukūʿ and sujūd?"" They said: ""Good."" He said: ""Then there is no harm.""

This, according to the old position of al-Shāfiʿī, is interpreted as referring to the obligatory recitation. Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""He did not mention that he prostrated for sahw or repeated the prayer, and he did that in the presence of the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""It is interpreted, according to us, as referring to the recitation of a sūrah (in addition to Umm al-Qurʾān), or to reciting silently in prayers where he should have recited aloud."" It has also been narrated from ʿUmar that he repeated it, and that will come under the chapter of ""The minimum that suffices,"" if Allah, the Exalted, wills.

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm, 7/238.]"
"3863","(3863) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Hishām narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from his father, who said:

The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the first two rakʿahs of Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, and he would let us hear a verse at times. He would lengthen the first rakʿah and shorten the second, and he would recite in the first two rakʿahs of Maghrib.

[Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, nos. 759, 776.]"
"3864","(3864) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsiyy said; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd said; my father informed me; al-Awzāʿī informed me; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah informed me; my father informed me that:

The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite Umm al-Qurʾān and two sūrahs along with it in the first two rakʿahs of Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, and he would let us hear a verse at times. He would lengthen the first rakʿah.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf from al-Awzāʿī, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Yaḥyā.

And it has been narrated to us from Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣunābihī that he heard Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, recite in the third rakʿah of Maghrib Umm al-Qurʾān and this verse: ""Rabbanā lā tuzigh qulūbanā baʿda idh hadaytanā wa-hab lanā min ladunka raḥmatan innaka anta al-Wahhāb"" [Āl ʿImrān: 8].

[Ṣaḥīh. It has been cited previously.]"
"3865","(3865) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ said; al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, reported from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, from al-Thawrī, from Ashʿath ibn Sulaym, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ziyād, who said:

I heard ʿAbd Allāh reciting in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""This, according to us, does not necessitate a sahw (prostration for forgetfulness).""

[Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm, 7/188.]"
"3866","(3866) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbāriyy informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind narrated from ʿĀmir, from Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that:

He recited aloud in Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr — Dāwūd was uncertain — and the people said ""Subḥān Allāh,"" but he continued. When he finished the prayer, he said: ""In every prayer there is recitation, and what prompted me to do that was not opposition to the Sunnah; rather, I had begun reciting out of forgetfulness, and I disliked interrupting the recitation.""

It is mentioned from Qatādah that Anas ibn Mālik recited aloud in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr and did not prostrate (for sahw). And something similar is narrated from Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt. It has also been narrated regarding this matter from ʿUmar and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with them both.

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"3867","(3867) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — 'Abdullah, meaning Ibn Maslamah, narrated from Malik, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, regarding the departure of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Banu 'Amr ibn 'Auf, and the prayer of Abu Bakr, and the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the people's clapping. He said: ""Abu Bakr would not turn around during the prayer. When the people increased their clapping, he turned, and he mentioned the hadith. At the end of it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'What is the matter with you that you have increased in clapping? Whoever is afflicted by something during his prayer, let him say SubhanAllah (glory be to Allah), for when he says SubhanAllah, attention is turned toward him. Clapping is only for women.'""

And it has been narrated to us previously from Jabir, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ill, so we prayed behind him while he was sitting. He turned toward us and saw us standing."" [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari, 784.]"
"3868","(3868) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — al-Rabi' ibn Nafi' narrated to us — Mu'awiyyah ibn Salam narrated from Zayd, who said he heard Abu Salam say — al-Saluli narrated from Sahl ibn al-Hanzaliyyah, who said: ""The call to prayer was made — meaning the Fajr prayer — and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, turning toward the mountain pass.""

Abu Dawud said: ""He had sent a horseman to the mountain pass during the night to keep watch."" [Sahih. Reported by Abu Dawud, 916.]"
"3869","(3869) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri informed us — Malik ibn Yahya ibn Malik narrated to us — Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us — Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Hurayradh, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""My Ummah is forgiven for what their souls whisper to them or what their souls converse with them, so long as they do not speak of it or act upon it.""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim reported it in their Sahih collections from the hadith of Mis'ar ibn Kidam and others. [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari, 2528.]"
"3870","(3870) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i informed us — Abu 'Abd al-Rahman 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me — my father narrated to us — Ruh narrated to us — 'Umar ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Husayn narrated to me — 'Abdullah ibn Abi Mulyakah narrated from 'Uqbah ibn al-Harith, who said: ""I prayed 'Asr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he gave the salam, he stood quickly and entered upon one of his wives. Then he came out and saw the astonishment on the faces of the people at his haste. He said: 'I remembered, while I was in the prayer, that we had some gold (or silver), and I disliked that it should remain with us overnight or for a day, so I ordered that it be distributed.'""

Al-Bukhari reported it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Mansur, from Ruh.

And it has been narrated to us from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""I used to calculate the tribute of Bahrain while I was standing in the prayer."" [Sahih. Reported by Ahmad, 15718, 18933.]"
"3871","(3871) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir informed us — my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur informed us — Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us — Sufyan narrated from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in a garment that had markings. He said: 'These markings have distracted me. Take this to Abu Jahm, and bring me the anbijaniyyah (a thick, unadorned garment).'""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim reported it from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyaynah. (T) And Yunus said, from al-Zuhri: ""For it distracted me during my prayer."" [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari, 373.]"
"3872","(3872) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Khusrujirdi in Khusrujird informed us — Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi narrated to us — Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us — he said: ""I read to Malik, from 'Alqamah ibn Abi 'Alqamah, from his mother, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfah gifted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a fine garment from Sham with markings. He prayed in it, and when he finished, he said: 'Return this garment to Abu Jahm, for I looked at its markings during the prayer, and it nearly caused me to be tempted (or distracted).'""

Al-Shafi'i said: ""We do not know whether he prostrated for forgetfulness."" And Abu Talhah looked toward a wall, and he mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, but we do not know whether he ordered him to prostrate for forgetfulness. [Sahih. Previously referenced.]"
"3873","(3873) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajanih informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far informed us — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Malik narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, that Abu Talhah al-Ansari was praying in his garden when a wild dove flew in, going back and forth seeking an exit. That amazed him, so he followed it with his gaze for a while, then returned to his prayer, and he did not know how many (rak'ahs) he had prayed. He said: ""A trial has befallen me in my wealth."" So he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned what had befallen him in his garden — the trial. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, it is charity; place it wherever you wish."" [Da'if. Reported by Malik in al-Muwatta', 222.]"
"3874","(3874) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Abu 'Asim narrated from 'Imran al-Qattan, from al-Hasan, regarding one who forgets the qunut in the Fajr prayer: ""Upon him are two prostrations of forgetfulness."" [Da'if. Reported by al-Daraqutni, 2/42/17.]"
"3875","(3875) Abu 'Abdillah informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazyad informed me — my father narrated to me — from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, regarding one who forgets the qunut in the Fajr prayer: ""Upon him are two prostrations of forgetfulness."" [Sahih. Reported by al-Daraqutni, 2/14/18.]"
"3876","(3876) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Amr informed us — Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — 'Abdullah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: ""Whoever forgets the qunut in the witr prayer should perform two prostrations of forgetfulness."" Sufyan, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""And upon this we act."" [Da'if.]"
"3877","(3877) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, saying: Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us, from Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, from his father, who said: I prayed Fajr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not perform qunūt. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad, 3/472/15974]"
"3878","(3878) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī informed us in Marw, saying: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us, saying: Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us, saying: Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī informed us, saying: I asked my father about qunūt, and he said: I prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and behind Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them), and I did not see any of them ever perform it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3879","(3879) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad and ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, who both said: We prayed Fajr behind ʿUmar, and he did not perform qunūt.

We have narrated in the chapter on qunūt from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and from the Caliphs after him that they performed qunūt in the Ṣubḥ prayer. It is well-known from ʿUmar through authentic chains that he used to perform qunūt in the Ṣubḥ prayer. If they omitted it at times, whether inadvertently or deliberately, that indicates that it is not obligatory. And since it is not reported from any of them that they performed the two prostrations of forgetfulness for that, it indicates that there is no prostration for forgetfulness regarding it. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 4948]"
"3880","(3880) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād, saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd narrated to us, saying: Shādhān narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from al-Ḥakam, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, from ʿAbd Allāh, that the Prophet ﷺ prayed five rakʿahs of Ẓuhr. When he gave the salām, it was said: Has something been added to the prayer? He said: No. They said: You prayed five rakʿahs. So he performed two prostrations. The wording is that of Sulaymān's ḥadīth, and Shādhān did not mention Ẓuhr, and he said: When he turned away, and he said: He performed two prostrations of forgetfulness. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah as has preceded.

We have also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm ibn Suwayd from ʿAlqamah, more complete than that, and its mention has preceded. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 401, 404, 1226, 7249]"
"3881","(3881) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Nubayṭ, who said: I prayed in my house and forgot (made an error). Then I came to al-Ḍaḥḥāk, meaning Ibn Muzāḥim, and said to him: I prayed in my house and forgot. He said: Prostrate now. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"3882","(3882) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed me, saying: Abū Isḥāq al-Thaʿālibī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Ghāzī narrated to us, saying: Abū Ḥafṣ ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, saying: Abū Qutaybah narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, from al-Ḥasan, who said: If a person forgets (makes an error) in the mosque and does not prostrate until he leaves the mosque, then there is nothing upon him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"3883","(3883) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us, saying: Abū Khālid narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you is doubtful about his prayer, let him cast aside the doubt and build upon certainty. When he is certain of having completed the prayer, let him perform two prostrations. If his prayer was complete, the extra rakʿah and the two prostrations become supererogatory, and if it was deficient, the rakʿah completes his prayer, and the two prostrations humiliate the Shayṭān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned previously under number 3800]"
"3884","(3884) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rustah narrated to us, saying: Ibn Kāsib narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Dāwūd narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, from Abū al-Ḥusayn, from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim came to Ibn ʿUmar and said: O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, what did the Commander of the Faithful ʿUmar say about the imām who leads the people? Ibn ʿUmar said: ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The imām is sufficient for those behind him. If the imām forgets (makes an error), then upon him are the two prostrations of forgetfulness, and those behind him should prostrate with him. And if one of those behind him forgets (makes an error), there is no obligation upon him to prostrate, for the imām is sufficient for him.""

Khārijah ibn Muṣʿab narrated from Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Madanī, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, from ʿUmar, from the Prophet ﷺ, with the same meaning.

This Abū al-Ḥusayn is unknown (majhūl), and al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAbd Allāh is weak (ḍaʿīf). And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"3885","(3885) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Baghdādī informed us, saying: ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Uways and ʿĪsā ibn Mīnāʾ both narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to us, from his father, from the jurists of the people of Madīnah, who used to say: The imām's sutrah (screen) is a sutrah for those behind him, whether they are few or many, and he bears their lapses (awhām). [Ḥasan]"
"3886","(3886) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Mālik ibn Anas, al-Layth ibn Saʿd, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, and Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you that Ibn Shihāb informed them, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buḥaynah narrated to him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up in the second rakʿah of Ẓuhr without sitting. When he completed his prayer, he performed two prostrations, saying the takbīr with each prostration, while sitting before the salām. And the people performed the two prostrations with him in place of the sitting (for the tashahhud) that he had forgotten. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd from al-Layth ibn Saʿd, and they both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Mālik as has preceded. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned many times; see, for example, number 3813]"
"3887","(3887) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us, ʿAmr ibn Thawr al-Jidhāmī and Ibn Abī Maryam narrated, both saying: al-Firyābī narrated, Sufyān narrated from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from ʿAmr ibn Wahb, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who said: I and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came upon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and he had already led the people in one rakʿah. He began to move back, but the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gestured to him to remain in place. So we prayed what we caught up with, and we made up what we had missed.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī, 82, 109.]"
"3888","(3888) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated, Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated, Hammām narrated from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, and from Zurārah ibn Awfā, that al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell behind. Then he mentioned the story and said: We came to the people while ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf was leading them in the Fajr prayer. When he saw the Prophet (ﷺ), he wanted to move back, but the Prophet (ﷺ) gestured to him to continue. He said: So I and the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed one rakʿah behind him. When he (ʿAbd al-Raḥmān) gave the salām, the Prophet (ﷺ) stood and prayed the rakʿah that he had missed, and he did not add anything further to it.

Abū Dāwūd said: Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Ibn ʿUmar, and Ibn al-Zubayr say: Whoever catches a single rakʿah of the prayer, upon him are the two prostrations of forgetfulness (sajdatā al-sahw).

The Sheikh said: And the ḥadīth of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is more deserving of being followed.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3889","(3889) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated, Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām narrated, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated. He said: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When one of you stands to pray, the Satan comes to him and confuses him, so that he does not know how many he has prayed. So when one of you finds that, let him prostrate two prostrations while sitting.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 1232.]"
"3890","(3890) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated, al-Layth narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated, Qutaybah and Ibn Rumḥ narrated, both saying: al-Layth narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from al-Aʿraj, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buḥaynah al-Asadī, the ally of Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up in the Ẓuhr prayer when there was a sitting (i.e., he forgot the sitting). When he completed his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations, saying the takbīr with each prostration, while sitting, before giving the salām. And the people prostrated them with him in place of the sitting that he had forgotten.

The wording of both their narrations is the same, except that Abū ʿAmr did not say ""al-Asadī"" or ""the ally of Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah and Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under numbers 3813, 3814, 2835.]"
"3891","(3891) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed me, my father narrated. He said: I heard al-Awzāʿī say: Ibn Shihāb narrated to me, Ibn Hurmuz narrated to me, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buḥaynah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forgot a sitting that he was supposed to perform and stood up from it. He said: We waited for his salām. Then he said the takbīr, then prostrated, then said the takbīr and raised his head, then said the takbīr and prostrated, then said the takbīr and raised his head, then gave the salām.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3892","(3892) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated, Yazīd ibn Ibrāhīm narrated, Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed one of the two afternoon prayers—Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr—and my strongest guess is that he said Ẓuhr—and gave the salām after two rakʿahs. Then he went to a wooden post at the front of the mosque, and he was angry, and he placed his hand upon it. Among the people were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, and they were too awed to speak to him. The people hurried out and said: ""Has the prayer been shortened? Has the prayer been shortened?"" Among the people was a man whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to call Dhū al-Yadayn. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, have you forgotten or has the prayer been shortened?"" He said: ""I have not forgotten and the prayer has not been shortened."" He said: ""Rather, you have forgotten, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Has Dhū al-Yadayn spoken the truth?"" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed two rakʿahs, then gave the salām, then said the takbīr and prostrated—a prostration like his usual one or longer—then raised his head and said the takbīr, then placed his head and said the takbīr, then prostrated a prostration like his usual one or longer, then raised his head and said the takbīr.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar from Yazīd ibn Ibrāhīm, except that he said: ""And my strongest guess is that it was ʿAṣr.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 482, 1229.]"
"3893","(3893) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated, ʿAlī ibn Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, Hishām, Yaḥyā ibn ʿAtīq, and Ibn ʿAwn, from Muḥammad, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), in the story of Dhū al-Yadayn: that he said the takbīr and prostrated.

Hishām, meaning Ibn Ḥassān, said: He said the takbīr, then said the takbīr and prostrated.

Ḥammād ibn Zayd is alone in narrating this from Hishām. The rest of the narrators from Ibn Sīrīn, and then the rest of the narrators from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, did not preserve the first takbīr. Only Ḥammād ibn Zayd preserved it (may Allah have mercy on him).

[Shādh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 1008.]"
"3894","(3894) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Khaṭīb al-Isfarāʾinī informed us, Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Kawthar narrated, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated, al-Ḥumaydī narrated, Sufyān narrated, Ayyūb narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in one of the two afternoon prayers—either Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr—two rakʿahs—and my strongest guess is that it was ʿAṣr. Then he turned away to a trunk in the mosque and leaned against it, and he was angry. The people hurried out saying: ""The prayer has been shortened! The prayer has been shortened!"" Among the people were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and they were too awed to speak to him. Dhū al-Yadayn stood and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened or have you forgotten?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What does Dhū al-Yadayn say?"" They said: ""He has spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in two rakʿahs, then gave the salām, then said the takbīr and prostrated, then said the takbīr and raised his head, then said the takbīr and prostrated—a prostration like his first one or longer—then said the takbīr and raised his head. Muḥammad said: And I was informed from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn that he said: ""And he gave the salām.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Sufyān.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned under number 3892.]"
"3895","(3895) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated, Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ narrated from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Muhallab, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave the salām after three rakʿahs of the ʿAṣr prayer, then stood and entered the chamber. A man with long arms stood and said: ""Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah?"" He came out angry and prayed the rakʿah that he had omitted, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave the salām.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, except that he said: ""He prayed the rakʿah that he had omitted, then gave the salām, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave the salām.""

And likewise al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated it from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, saying: ""Then he gave the salām, then prostrated two prostrations, then gave the salām."" And likewise Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah and a group narrated it from Khālid.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī, 887.]"
"3896","(3896) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī al-Wazīr al-Tājir informed us, Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Ḥanẓalī narrated, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated, Ashʿath ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Ḥumrānī narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Muhallab, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave the salām.

[Shādh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 1039.]"
"3897","(3897) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Fāris narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Muthannā narrated it with his chain of narration: that the Prophet (ﷺ) led them in prayer, then forgot and performed two prostrations, then recited the tashahhud afterwards, then gave the salām. Al-Ashʿath al-Ḥumrānī is alone in narrating it.

And Shuʿbah, Wuhaib, Ibn ʿUlayyah, al-Thaqafī, Hushaym, Ḥammād ibn Zayd, Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ, and others narrated it from Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ, and none of them mentioned what al-Ashʿath mentioned from Muḥammad from him.

And Ayyūb narrated from Muḥammad, saying: ""I was informed from ʿImrān,"" and he mentioned the salām without the tashahhud.

And in the narration of Hushaym, the tashahhud is mentioned before the two prostrations, and that indicates the error of al-Ashʿath in what he narrated.

[Shādh. It was discussed in the one before it.]"
"3898","(3898) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Khālid informed us from Abū Qilābah; Abū al-Muhallab narrated to us from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed the Ẓuhr or the ʿAṣr three rakʿahs. A man called al-Khirbāq said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, you only prayed three rakʿahs."" He said: ""Is that so?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""So he stood and prayed, then prostrated, then recited the tashahhud and gave the salām, and then performed the two prostrations of sahw, then gave the salām.""

This is the correct version with this wording, and Allah knows best. [Shādh]"
"3899","(3899) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd said: Salamah ibn ʿAlqamah said: I said to Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn: ""Is there a tashahhud in them?"" meaning in the two prostrations of sahw. He said: ""I did not hear it in the hadith of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), but I would prefer that he recite the tashahhud.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb in abbreviated form.

Al-Bukhārī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Anas and al-Ḥasan gave the salām and did not recite the tashahhud."" And Qatādah said: ""He does not recite the tashahhud.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The authentic reports on this indicate that even if he performed the two prostrations after the salām, he did not recite the tashahhud for them. And upon Allah is the reliance."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"3900","(3900) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥulwānī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿImrān ibn Abī Laylā narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Ibn Abī Laylā narrated to me; al-Shaʿbī narrated from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah: that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited the tashahhud after raising his head from the two prostrations of sahw.

Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā is alone in narrating this from al-Shaʿbī, and one should not rejoice in what he is alone in narrating, and Allah knows best.

[Shādh. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Awsaṭ (8124).]"
"3901","(3901) Abū al-Faḍl ibn Abī Saʿd al-Harawī came to us as a pilgrim; Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Jawharī informed us in Marw; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us; al-Nufaylī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated from Khuṣayf from Abī ʿUbaydah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you are in prayer and you are in doubt whether you prayed three or four, and your predominant thought is that you prayed four, then recite the tashahhud, then perform two prostrations while you are seated before you give the salām, then recite the tashahhud again, then give the salām."" And this is not strong, and there is disagreement regarding its elevation (rafʿ) and its text (matn). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"3902","(3902) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū Qilābah, meaning al-Raqāshī, narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh in Baghdād informed us; it was recited to ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī while I was listening; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Abū ʿAwānah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We used to greet the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was praying, and he would respond to us. When we returned from the land of Abyssinia, I greeted him, but he did not respond to me. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you used to respond to us.' He said: 'The prayer is sufficient as an occupation.'""

The wording is that of the hadith of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ḥammād, and Muslim narrated it as mentioned above.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it (1199, 1216, 3875).]"
"3903","(3903) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated among a group; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd from Abī Wāʾil from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""We used to greet the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was in prayer before we went to the land of Abyssinia, and he would respond to us while he was in prayer. When we returned from the land of Abyssinia, I came to him to greet him. I found him praying, so I greeted him, but he did not respond to me. I was overcome by anxiety over what was near and what was far. I sat until he finished his prayer, then I came to him. He said: 'Indeed, Allah—exalted be His praise—ordains from His affair what He wills, and indeed, among what Allah has newly ordained is that you do not speak during the prayer.'""

And the hadith of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh and Zayd ibn Arqam has already been mentioned on this topic.

All of that, according to us, is interpreted as referring to deliberate speech. [Ḥasan. Al-Ḥumaydī narrated it (94).]"
"3904","(3904) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally among a group; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed me; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik, reciting to him.

(Ḥ) And Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah al-Sakhtiyānī from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Abī Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned away after two rakʿahs. Dhu al-Yadayn said to him: ""Has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget, O Messenger of Allah?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Did Dhu al-Yadayn speak the truth?"" The people said: ""Yes."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood and prayed two more rakʿahs, then gave the salām, then said the takbīr and prostrated with a prostration like his usual prostration or longer, then raised his head, then said the takbīr and prostrated with a prostration like his usual prostration or longer, then raised his head.

The wording of their hadith is the same, except that al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) did not say ""ibn Abī Tamīmah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī and others, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ibn ʿUyaynah and Ḥammād ibn Zayd from Ayyūb.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it (482).]"
"3905","(3905) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb from Muḥammad from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in one of the afternoon prayers—the Ẓuhr or the ʿAṣr. He prayed two rakʿahs for us, then gave the salām, then stood up at a piece of wood at the front of the mosque and placed his hands upon it, one upon the other, and anger was visible on his face. Then the quickest of the people went out saying: 'The prayer has been shortened! The prayer has been shortened!' Among the people were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), but they were too awed to speak to him. A man stood up whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to call Dhu al-Yadayn. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, did you forget, or has the prayer been shortened?' He said: 'I did not forget, and the prayer has not been shortened.' He said: 'No, you forgot, O Messenger of Allah.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned to the people and said: 'Did Dhu al-Yadayn speak the truth?' They nodded, meaning 'yes.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) returned to his place and prayed the remaining two rakʿahs, then gave the salām, then said the takbīr and prostrated with a prostration like his usual prostration or longer, then raised his head and said the takbīr, then said the takbīr and prostrated with a prostration like his usual prostration or longer, then raised his head and said the takbīr."" It was said to Muḥammad: ""Did he give the salām during the sahw?"" He said: ""I did not preserve that from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), but I was informed that ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn said: 'Then he gave the salām.'"" Abū Dāwūd said: ""None mentioned 'they nodded' except Ḥammād ibn Zayd."" The Shaykh said: ""And it has not reached us except from the narration of Abū Dāwūd from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, and they are trustworthy Imams."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been narrated more than once before.]"
"3906","(3906) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; ʿAlī ibn Dāwūd narrated to us; Ādam narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed the Ẓuhr or the ʿAṣr for us—two rakʿahs—and gave the salām. Dhu al-Yadayn said: 'Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah, or did you forget?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to his Companions: 'Is what he says true?' They said: 'Yes.' So he prayed two more rakʿahs, then performed the two prostrations of sahw."" Shuʿbah said: Saʿd said: ""And I saw ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr pray two rakʿahs of the Maghrib and give the salām, then speak, then pray what remained, and he said: 'Thus did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).'""

The wording is that of the hadith of Ādam, and in the hadith of Bishr there is no story of ʿUrwah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated a little before.]"
"3907","(3907) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Sābiq narrated to us, saying: Shaybān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us — from Shaybān, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

""While I was praying Ẓuhr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave salām after two rakʿahs. A man from Banū Sulaym stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget?' He said: 'It has not been shortened, nor did I forget.' The man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you only prayed two rakʿahs.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Is Dhū al-Yadayn speaking the truth?' They said: 'Yes.' So he prayed with them two further rakʿahs.""

He said: ""And Ḍamḍam narrated to me that he heard Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: 'Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed two prostrations.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Ibn Sābiq. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr, except that he narrated only part of the ḥadīth, not all of it. He said: ""And he abridged the ḥadīth.""

Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr did not preserve the two prostrations of sahw from Abū Salamah; rather, he preserved them from Ḍamḍam ibn Jawsh. And Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm preserved them from Abū Salamah. Al-Zuhrī did not preserve them — neither from Abū Salamah nor from a group who narrated this story to him from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. Ṣaḥīḥ. It was presented in the one before it."
"3908","(3908) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd said: My father narrated to me from Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān, from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, that Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah informed him that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs and then gave salām. Dhū al-Shimālayn ibn ʿAbd said: ""O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The prayer has not been shortened, and I did not forget."" Dhū al-Shimālayn said: ""Something of that did happen, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to the people and said: ""Has Dhū al-Shimālayn spoken the truth?"" They said: ""Yes."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and completed what remained of the prayer, and he did not perform the two prostrations that are performed when a man doubts in his prayer — at the time when the people met him.

Ibn Shihāb said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab narrated this report to me from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him.

Ibn Shihāb said: And Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith, and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me — from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the like of it.

This is a ḥadīth in which there is disagreement regarding it from al-Zuhrī. Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān narrated it thus, and it is the most authentic of the narrations, as far as we can see. His ḥadīth from Ibn Abī Ḥathmah is mursal, and his ḥadīth from the rest is mawṣūl.

Mālik ibn Anas narrated it as mursal from him, from Ibn Abī Ḥathmah, Ibn al-Musayyab, and Abū Salamah.

Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated it as musnad from him, from their group, excluding his narration from Ibn Abī Ḥathmah.

Maʿmar narrated it as musnad from him, from Abū Salamah and Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"3909","(3909) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, saying: Maʿmar informed us — from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah and Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

""The Prophet ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr, and he was forgetful in two rakʿahs. He turned away, and Dhū al-Shimālayn ibn ʿAbd ʿAmr — who was an ally of Banū Zuhrah — said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been lightened, or did you forget?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'What does Dhū al-Yadayn say?' They said: 'He has spoken the truth, O Prophet of Allah.' He said: 'So he completed with them the two rakʿahs that were deficient.'""

Al-Zuhrī said: ""Then he performed two prostrations after he had finished.""

This indicates that he did not hear them mention his two prostrations, yet he performed them, until he was informed about them on his own authority.

There is disagreement regarding this story from Ibn Abī Dhiʿb, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him.

It is established from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ performed them (the two prostrations)."
"3910","(3910) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Mālik ibn Anas informed you from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, that Abū Sufyān, the mawlā of Ibn Abī Aḥmad, informed him that he heard Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say:

'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed ʿAṣr and gave salām after two rakʿahs. Dhū al-Yadayn said: ""Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah, or did you forget?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""None of that occurred."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, something of that did happen."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to the people and said: ""Has Dhū al-Yadayn spoken the truth?"" They said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed what remained of the prayer, then gave salām, and performed two prostrations while he was seated, after the salām.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, from Mālik, with his isnād, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed for us."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was presented in the one before it.]"
"3911","(3911) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us, saying: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ Abū Kurayb al-Hamdānī narrated to us, saying: Abū Usāmah narrated to us — from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed and was forgetful. He gave salām after two rakʿahs. A man called Dhū al-Yadayn said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget?"" He said: ""The prayer has not been shortened, and I did not forget."" He said: ""You prayed only two rakʿahs."" He said: ""Is it as Dhū al-Yadayn has said?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""So he moved forward and prayed two rakʿahs, then gave salām, then performed the two prostrations of sahw."""
"3912","(3912) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, saying: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us — from Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us — and the wording is his — saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, saying: Musaddad informed us, saying: Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us, saying: Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ informed us, saying: Abū Qilābah narrated to us — from Abū al-Muhallab, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave salām after three rakʿahs of ʿAṣr, then he entered (his house). A man called al-Khirbāq — who was long-armed — came to him and said: 'Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah?' He came out dragging his cloak in anger and said: 'Has he spoken the truth?' They said: 'Yes.' He stood and prayed that rakʿah, then gave salām, then performed the two prostrations, then gave salām.""

Ibn ʿUlayyah said: ""Then he entered his house,"" and the rest is to the same meaning.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Ibn ʿUlayyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim narrated it, no. 574.]"
"3913","(3913) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, saying: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk and Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīb ibn Milḥān narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā — that is, Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us, saying: al-Layth narrated to us — from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, that Suwayd ibn Qays informed him — from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥudayj, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day prayed and turned away when one rakʿah of the prayer remained. A man caught up with him and said: ""You forgot a rakʿah of the prayer."" He returned, entered the mosque, and commanded Bilāl, who established the prayer. He prayed one rakʿah with the people. I informed the people of that, and they said: ""Do you know the man?"" I said: ""No, unless I see him."" He passed by me and I said: ""This is he."" They said: ""This is Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad narrated it, 6/401/27796.]"
"3914","(3914) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, saying: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Daqqāq in Baghdād informed me, saying: ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, saying: Wahb ibn Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, saying: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrating from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Suwayd ibn Qays, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥudayj, who said:

""I prayed Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he was forgetful and gave salām after two rakʿahs. Then he turned away, and a man said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, you were forgetful and gave salām after two rakʿahs.' He commanded Bilāl, who established the prayer. Then he completed that rakʿah. I asked the people about the man who had said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ""You were forgetful."" It was said to me: 'Do you know him?' I said: 'No, unless I see him.' A man passed by me and I said: 'This is he.' They said: 'This is Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was presented in the one before it.]"
"3915","(3915) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, saying: Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us, saying: Ḥammād narrated to us, saying: ʿIsl ibn Sufyān narrated to us — from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ, that Ibn al-Zubayr led the people in Maghrib and gave salām after two rakʿahs. Then he stood to kiss the Black Stone. He looked and saw the people sitting. He came back and prayed the remaining rakʿah for us, then gave salām, then performed two prostrations. He said: ""I went immediately to Ibn ʿAbbās and asked him about it."" He said: ""Stop! By Allah, what did your father do?"" I repeated it to him and he said: ""He did not deviate from the Sunnah of his Prophet ﷺ."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it, no. 4504.]"
"3916","(3916) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Isḥāh al-Baghawī informed us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hāshimī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us, saying: Hishām — that is, Ibn Ḥassān — narrated to us from ʿIsl, from ʬAṭāʾ, mentioning the same meaning, and he added: ""We said 'Subḥān Allāh,' and he turned to us and said: 'Have we not completed the prayer?' We indicated with our heads, saying 'Subḥān Allāh,' meaning 'no.'"" He did not mention more of Ibn ʿAbbās's statement than: ""He did not deviate from the Sunnah of his Prophet ﷺ."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was presented in the one before it.]"
"3917","(3917) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUbayd, Abū Qudāmah al-Iyādī, narrated to us that ʿĀmir narrated from ʬAṭāʾ who said: Ibn al-Zubayr prayed Maghrib and saluted after two rakʿahs, then stood up, and the people said ""Subḥān Allāh."" He said: ""What is the matter with them?"" Then he came back and performed one rakʿah, then two prostrations. He (the narrator) said: ""I went to Ibn ʿAbbās and informed him of what Ibn al-Zubayr had done, and he said: 'He did not depart from the Sunnah of his Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).'""

The Shaykh said: This Ibn al-Zubayr is ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr. [Authenticated; narrated previously.]"
"3918","(3918) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us that Ismāʿīl narrated to us. He said: and Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, he said: al-Ḥajjāj ibn Abī ʿUthmān narrated to us, he said: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me from Hilāl ibn Abī Maymūnah from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Muʿāwiyah ibn al-Ḥakam al-Sulamī who said: While we were praying with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a man from the people sneezed, so I said: ""Yarḥamuka Allāh (May Allah have mercy on you)."" The people glared at me with their eyes, so I said: ""Woe to you! What is the matter with you that you are looking at me?"" They began striking their hands upon their thighs. When I saw them silencing me, I remained quiet. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) finished praying — and by my father and mother, I have never seen a teacher before him or after him better in teaching than him — by Allah, he did not rebuke me, nor revile me, nor strike me. He said: ""Indeed, in this prayer, nothing of the speech of people is permissible; it is only tasbīh (glorification), takbīr (magnification), and recitation of the Qurʾān,"" or as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others. [Authenticated; narrated by Aḥmad 5/447/24163.]"
"3919","(3919) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Ḥudayj ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent us to al-Najāshī, and we were eighty men, and among us was Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib. Then he mentioned the hadith regarding their entering upon al-Najāshī, and at the end of it he said: Then Ibn Masʿūd came and was the first to arrive, and he witnessed Badr."
"3920","(3920) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdad that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAttāb narrated to us that al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah al-Jawharī narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated to me from his uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah who said: Among those who are mentioned is that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) in Makkah among the first emigrants from the land of Abyssinia, then he emigrated to Madīnah. He mentioned them and mentioned among them ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, and he said: He was among those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).

All the people of maghāzī mentioned this without any difference among them.

As for Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), it has been narrated to us from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah and Ḥammād ibn Zayd from Ayyūb from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in one of the two evening prayers.

And it has been narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah that he said: While I was praying Ẓuhr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), he mentioned the story of Dhū al-Yadayn. And it has been narrated in the hadith of Abū Sufyān, the freedman of Ibn Abī Aḥmad, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in prayer. [Authenticated; narrated by al-Ḥākim 6756.]"
"3921","(3921) In the hadith of al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām, and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, that Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr, and he saluted after two rakʿahs. Then he mentioned it.

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; he said: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yūnus informed me from Ibn Shihāb, and he mentioned it. And Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that he witnessed this incident, and the arrival of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was while he was at Khaybar. [Authenticated; narrated more than once previously.]"
"3922","(3922) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us that al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated to us that al-Zuhrī narrated to us; ʿAnbasah ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated to me from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and his Companions at Khaybar after they had conquered it. Then he mentioned the hadith. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī. [Authenticated; narrated by al-Bukhārī 2827.]"
"3923","(3923) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Qaṭṭān informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Abī Sulaymān narrated to us; he said: I heard ʿIrāk ibn Mālik saying: I heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I came to Madīnah while the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was at Khaybar, and a man from Banī Ghifār was leading the people in prayer.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Then he followed the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and came to him while he was at Khaybar. Authenticated; narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān 7156."
"3924","(3924) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated to us that Ismāʿīl, meaning Ibn Abī Khālid, narrated to us; he said: I heard Qays, meaning Ibn Abī Ḥāzim, saying: I heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) for three years. [Authenticated; narrated by al-Bukhārī 3591.]"
"3925","(3925) Abū Saʿīd al-Isfarāʾinī informed us that Abū Baḥr al-Barbahārī narrated to us that Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, and al-Ḥumaydī said — while mentioning this issue and applying the hadith of Ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) to the case of deliberate action: ""If someone asks: what indicates that? And its apparent meaning encompasses deliberate action, forgetfulness, and ignorance?"" We say: You are correct, this is the apparent meaning. However, the arrival of Ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) from the land of Abyssinia was before Badr, and he witnessed Badr after this statement. When we found that the Islam of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) occurred while the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was at Khaybar, three years before the passing of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he was present at the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and at the statement of Dhū al-Yadayn; and we found that ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn was present at the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on another occasion and at the statement of al-Khirbāq, and the Islam of ʿImrān was after Badr; and we found that Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥudayj was present at the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and at the statement of Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, and the Islam of Muʿāwiyah was two months before the passing of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him); and we found that Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) affirmed the action of Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) in that, and mentioned that it was the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and Ibn ʿAbbās was ten years old when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) passed away; and we found that Ibn ʿUmar narrated that, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) gave Ibn ʿUmar permission on the day of al-Khandaq, after Badr — we came to know that the hadith of Ibn Masʿʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) was specifically about deliberate action, not forgetfulness. Had that hadith been about both forgetfulness and deliberate action at that time, the later prayers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would have abrogated it, because they came after it. [Weak.]"
"3926","(3926) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Dalaj ibn Aḥmad narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Abbār narrated to us that Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Baḥr al-Khashshāb narrated to us that al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us from al-Awzāʿī who said: The Islam of Muʿāwiyah ibn al-Ḥakam was toward the end of the matter, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) did not command him to repeat the prayer. So whoever speaks in his prayer out of forgetfulness or ignorance, his prayer stands; and whoever speaks deliberately, he must restart the prayer. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) has pointed to most of what we have narrated from others in the book Ikhtilāf al-Aḥādīth. And in what has been narrated to us from others there is confirmation of his statement.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Someone says: ""Is Dhū al-Yadayn, from whom you narrate, the one who was killed at Badr?"" I say: No. ʿImrān calls him al-Khirbāq and says he was short-handed or long-handed. The one killed at Badr was Dhū al-Shimālayn.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The one who was killed at Badr was Dhū al-Shimālayn ibn ʿAbd ʿAmr ibn Naḍlah, an ally of Banī Zuhrah from Khuzāʿah. Thus ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr mentioned it. [Weak.]"
"3927","(3927) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī narrated to us that Abū ʿUlāthah Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Abū al-Aswad from ʿUrwah, who said: Among those who witnessed Badr alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was Dhū al-Shimālayn ibn ʿAbd ʿAmr ibn Naḍlah ibn Ghabshān from Khuzāʿah. He said: Among the Muslims who were martyred on the day of Badr from Banū Zuhrah ibn Kilāb were two men: ʿUmayr ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Dhū al-Shimālayn ibn ʿAbd ʿAmr ibn Naḍlah, an ally of theirs from Khuzāʿah, from Banī Ghabshān.

And thus said Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah in his Maghāzī and Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār. Muḥammad said: He had no offspring.

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: As for Dhū al-Yadayn, who informed the Prophet ﷺ of his forgetfulness (sahw), he remained alive after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Thus mentioned our Shaykh Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, and he cited as evidence that which… [Weak]"
"3928","(3928) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār al-Zāhid narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Baḥr ibn Barrī narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Maʿdī ibn Sulaymān narrated to me that Shuʿayb ibn Muṭayr narrated from his father, and Muṭayr was present and confirmed him. Shuʿayb said: O father, you informed me that Dhū al-Yadayn met you at Dhī Khushub and told you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in one of the two afternoon prayers, which was ʿAṣr, and he prayed two rakʿahs and then gave salām. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, followed him, and the people went out quickly. Dhū al-Yadayn, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar caught up with him, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget? He said: ""The prayer has not been shortened, nor did I forget."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: ""What does Dhū al-Yadayn say?"" They said: ""He has spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah."" So he returned, and the people had dispersed, and he prayed two rakʿahs and then performed two prostrations of forgetfulness (sahw). [Weak]"
"3929","(3929) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us that Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us that my father, Naṣr ibn ʿAlī, and Bundār said: Maʿdī ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that Shuʿayb ibn Muṭayr narrated from his father, and his father Muṭayr was present when he narrated this hadith to me. He said: He said to him: O father, you narrated to me that Dhū al-Yadayn met you at Dhī Khushub and narrated to you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in one of the two afternoon prayers, which was ʿAṣr, for two rakʿahs, then gave salām. Then he mentioned the hadith and said in it: He led them in two rakʿahs, then gave salām, then prostrated.

Some of the narrators said in the hadith of Abū Hurayrah that Dhū al-Shimālayn said: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget? The two Shaykhs of the Ṣaḥīḥayn, al-Bukhārī and Muslim, did not authenticate any of those narrations due to the apparent error contained therein. Our Shaykh Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say: Whoever says that has erred, for Dhū al-Shimālayn died early and left no offspring, and he has no narrator. [Weak. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3930","(3930) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Khubayb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: I heard Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim narrate from Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Muʿallā that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the mosque and I was praying, and he called me. He said: I prayed and then came to him, and he said: ""What prevented you from answering me when I called you? Have you not heard Allah say: 'O you who believe, respond to Allah and to the Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life' [al-Anfāl: 24]? I shall teach you the greatest sūrah in the Qurʾān before I leave the mosque."" He said: I walked with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until we were about to reach the door of the mosque, and I said [to myself]: He has forgotten. So I reminded him and said: O Messenger of Allah, you said such and such. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Al-ḥamdu lillāhi rabbi al-ʿālamīn [al-Fātiḥah: 2] — the seven oft-repeated verses and the great Qurʾān that I have been given."" [Authenticated by al-Bukhārī, 4474]"
"3931","(3931) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Faqīh in Bukhārā informed us that Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Jushamī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated the hadith with a similar meaning, except that he said in his isnād ""from from"" and said: ""I called you and you did not answer me."" He said: ""I was praying."" Then he mentioned its meaning.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān.

In this there is evidence that the response of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ when he asked them about what Dhū al-Yadayn was saying did not invalidate their prayer, contrary to what has been narrated from Ḥammād ibn Zayd in that story, that they gestured. [Authenticated. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"3932","(3932) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Jawzajānī narrated to us that Abū ʿUbaydah ibn Abī al-Safar narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me that ʿAbdullāh ibn Zaydān and Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Khālid, Abū Jaʿfar al-Qammāṭ al-Kūfiyyān, both said: Abū ʿUbaydah ibn Abī al-Safar narrated to us. He said: I heard Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf ibn Abī Isḥāq narrate from his father, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ, who said: The Prophet ﷺ sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd to the people of Yemen to call them to Islam, but they did not respond to him. Then the Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and ordered him to send back Khālid and those who were with him, except that any man who was with Khālid and wished to join ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, could do so.

Al-Barāʾ said: I was among those who joined him. When we drew near the people, they came out to us, and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in prayer and arranged us in a single row. Then he went forward before them and read to them the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Ḥamdān all embraced Islam. ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about their acceptance of Islam. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ read the letter, he fell prostrating, then raised his head and said: ""Peace be upon Ḥamdān, peace be upon Ḥamdān.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated the beginning of this hadith from Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān from Sharīḥ ibn Maslamah from Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf, but he did not narrate it in its entirety. The prostration of gratitude (sujūd al-shukr) in the complete hadith is authentic according to his conditions. [Weak]"
"3933","(3933) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us that al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik that ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb, the guide of Kaʿb when he became blind, said: I heard Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrate his hadith about when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the expedition of Tabūk. He mentioned the hadith in its entirety, up to his saying: Until fifty nights had been completed since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade [people] from speaking to us. When I prayed the Fajr prayer on the morning of the fifty nights, I was on the roof of one of our houses. While I was sitting in the condition that Allah has described of us — my soul had become constrained for me, and the earth had become narrow despite its vastness — I heard the voice of a caller who had ascended the mountain of Salʿ: ""O Kaʿb ibn Mālik, receive glad tidings!"" He said: I fell prostrating and knew that relief had come, and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had announced Allah's acceptance of our repentance when he prayed the Fajr prayer. The people went to give us glad tidings, and messengers of glad tidings went toward my two companions. A man galloped toward me in joy, and a runner from Aslam ascended the mountain. The voice reached me faster than the horse. When the one whose voice I had heard came to me giving me glad tidings, I took off my two garments and clothed him with them as a reward for his glad tidings. By Allah, I possessed nothing else that day, so I borrowed two garments, wore them, and set out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he mentioned the hadith.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another route from al-Layth. [Authenticated. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 4418]"
"3934","(3934) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm al-Qanṭarī in Baghdād narrated to us that Abū Qilābah al-Raqāshī narrated to us that Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānʾ informed me that al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us that Abū Salamah Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed me that Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz narrated to us that Khālid ibn Khidāsh narrated to us. All of them said: Bakkār ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Bakrah narrated from his father, from Abū Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When something came to the Prophet ﷺ that pleased him or he was gladdened by it, he would fall prostrating in gratitude to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from Makhlad ibn Khālid from Abū ʿĀṣim. [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 2774]"
"3935","(3935) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us that Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to me that Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb narrated from Ibn ʿUthmān — Abū Dāwūd said: He is Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUthmān — from Ashʿath ibn Isḥāq ibn Saʿd from ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd from his father, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Makkah heading toward Madīnah. When we were near ʿAzwar, he dismounted, then raised his hands and supplicated to Allah for a while, then fell prostrating and remained [in prostration] for a long time. Then he stood, raised his hands for a while, then fell prostrating. Aḥmad mentioned this three times. He said: ""I asked my Lord and interceded for my Ummah, and He gave me one-third of my Ummah, so I fell prostrating to my Lord in gratitude. Then I raised my head and asked my Lord for my Ummah, and He gave me one-third of my Ummah, so I fell prostrating to my Lord in gratitude. Then I stood and asked my Lord for my Ummah, and He gave me the last third, so I fell prostrating to my Lord, the Mighty and Majestic.""

Abū Dāwūd, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Ashʿath ibn Isḥāq was abandoned by Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ when he narrated it to us, so Mūsā ibn Sahl al-Ramlī narrated it to me from him. [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd, 2775]"
"3936","(3936) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us that my father and Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth said: al-Layth informed us from Ibn al-Hād from ʿAmr, meaning Ibn Abī ʿAmr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, from Muḥammad ibn Jubayr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said: I entered the mosque and saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ coming out of the mosque. I followed him, walking behind him, and he was unaware of me until he entered a palm grove. He faced the qiblah and prostrated, prolonging the prostration, while I was behind him, until I thought that Allah, the Most High, had taken his soul. I approached walking until I came to him, and I lowered my head to look at his face. He raised his head and said: ""What is the matter with you, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān?"" I said: ""When you prolonged the prostration, O Messenger of Allah, I feared that Allah had taken your soul, so I came to look."" He said: ""When you saw me enter the palm grove, I met Jibrīl, peace be upon him, and he said: 'I give you glad tidings that Allah, the Most High, says: Whoever sends salām upon you, I send salām upon him, and whoever sends ṣalāh upon you, I send ṣalāh upon him.'"" [Weak]"
"3937","(3937) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Shaʿrānī informed me: my grandfather narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to me, from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I met Jibrīl (peace be upon him), and he gave me glad tidings and said: 'Your Lord says to you: Whoever sends blessings upon you, I send blessings upon him, and whoever sends salutations upon you, I send salutations upon him.' So I prostrated to Allah in gratitude.""

In this chapter there are narrations from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, Anas ibn Mālik, and Abū Juḥayfah from the Prophet (ﷺ), and what we have mentioned suffices over the narration of the weak narrators. [Weak. Al-Ḥākim narrated it, 1/735]"
"3938","(3938) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, who said: Jābir narrated to me, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw a man of short stature who was called Zunaym, and the Prophet (ﷺ) fell into prostration and then said: ""I ask Allah for well-being.""

This narration is munaqaṭiʿ (disrupted), and it is the narration of Jābir al-Juʿfī, but it has a corroborating witness from another source. [Very weak. Mābir al-ʿAjamī]"
"3939","(3939) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us, from Misʿar, from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from ʿArfajah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw a man afflicted with a chronic illness and he prostrated.

Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh said: And Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was given news of a victory and he prostrated; and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was given news of a victory or saw a man afflicted with a chronic illness and he prostrated. It is said that this is ʿArfajah al-Sulamī, and they do not consider him to have been a Companion, so it would be a mursal (disconnected) corroborating witness for what preceded.

It is also narrated from Misʿar, from Abū ʿAwn Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from Yaḥyā ibn al-Jazzār, from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration, and then from him from Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both). [Weak]"
"3940","(3940) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Misʿar narrated to us, from Abū ʿAwn, from a man, that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), when the conquest of al-Yamāmah was announced to him, he prostrated. [Weak]"
"3941","(3941) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharzah informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Qays, from a man called Abū Mūsā — meaning Mālik ibn al-Ḥārith — who said: I was with ʿAlī, and he said: ""Search for him"" — meaning al-Mukhdaj. They could not find him, so his brow began to perspire and he said: ""By Allah, I have not lied nor have been lied about."" So they extracted him from beneath his legs, and he prostrated. [Weak]"
"3942","(3942) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī informed me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Nājiyah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq and Abū Saʿīd al-Thaqafī both said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar: Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd narrated to me, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ﷺ) entered the mosque, and a man entered and prayed, then came and greeted the Prophet (ﷺ). The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""And upon you be peace. Go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" He did this three times, and the man said: ""By the One who sent you with the truth, I know no better than this, so show me and teach me."" He said: ""When you stand for prayer, say the takbīr, then recite whatever is easy for you from the Qurʾān, then bow until you are at rest in rukūʿ, then raise your head until you stand upright, then prostrate until you are in a state of rest in sujūd, then raise your head until you are at rest while seated, then do that in all your prayer.""

The wording is that of the Qāḍī's ḥadīth. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, 757]"
"3943","(3943) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ʿAbdān al-Jawālīqī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and the wording is his — who said: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Abū Usāmah Ḥammād ibn Usāmah narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that a man entered the mosque and prayed, while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in a corner of the mosque. He came and greeted him, and he said: ""And upon you — go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" He went back and prayed, then greeted him, and he said to him: ""And upon you — go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" On the third occasion, the man said: ""Teach me, O Messenger of Allah."" He said to him: ""When you stand for prayer, perform wuḍūʾ thoroughly, then face the qiblah and say the takbīr, then recite whatever is easy for you from the Qurʾān, then bow until you are at rest in rukūʿ, then raise your head until you are at rest standing upright, then prostrate until you are at rest in sujūd, then raise until you are level and at rest while seated, then prostrate until you are at rest in sujūd, then raise until you stand upright, then do that in all your prayer.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāh ibn Manṣūr, from Abū Usāmah, with this wording.

Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, except that he did not include what Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and others affirmed from Abū Usāmah in his second instance: ""Then prostrate until you are at rest in sujūd, then raise until you stand upright.""

Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī also did not preserve it from ʿAbdān.

That addition is preserved in this ḥadīth from various routes from Abū Usāmah.

Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, and added at its end: ""When you do this, your prayer is complete, and whatever you diminish of this, you diminish from your prayer."" And he said in it: ""When you stand for prayer, perform wuḍūʾ thoroughly."" He did not include what Abū Usāmah affirmed at the end of the ḥadīth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, 6251; Muslim narrated it, 397]"
"3944","(3944) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was preceded by way of evidence in the one before it]"
"3945","(3945) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Layth — meaning ibn Saʿd — narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā from the family of Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ, from his father, from his paternal uncle who was a Badrī Companion, who narrated to him that a man entered the mosque and prayed, while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was watching him, and we were unaware. When he finished, he came forward and greeted the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said to him: ""Go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" He went back and prayed, then came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said to him: ""Go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" This happened two or three times, and the man said: ""By the One who has honored you, O Messenger of Allah, I have strived, so teach me."" He said to him: ""When you stand intending prayer, perform wuḍūʾ and perform your wuḍūʾ well, then face the qiblah and say the takbīr, then recite, then bow and be at rest in rukūʿ, then raise until you stand upright, then prostrate and be at rest in sujūd, then raise until you are at rest while seated, then prostrate until you are at rest in sujūd, then raise, then do that until you complete your prayer."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Dārimī narrated it, 1329]"
"3946","(3946) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Miqdām ibn Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated to us, from Ibn ʿAjlān, from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā al-Zuraqī, from his father, from his paternal uncle who was a Badrī Companion, that he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when a man entered the mosque and stood in a corner of it to pray, and he mentioned the ḥadīth. In it there is the additional wording: ""Then stand and face the qiblah."" And regarding the second prostration he said: ""Then prostrate until you are at rest in sujūd. When you do that, you have completed your prayer, and whatever you diminish of this, you diminish from your prayer.""

Muḥammad ibn Isḥāh ibn Yasār narrated it from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād ibn Rāfiʿ, from his father, from his paternal uncle Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ.

Likewise, Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated it from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād.

Likewise, Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah narrated it from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā, from the narration of Hammām ibn Yaḥyā from him.

Ḥammād ibn Salamah fell short in it, saying: from Isḥāq, from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād, from his paternal uncle. And Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr said: from ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād, from Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ.

The correct narration is that of those who preceded.

Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar concurred with them, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād ibn Rāfiʿ al-Zuraqī, from his father, from his grandfather, from Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ.

Some of the narrators fell short in the lineage of Yaḥyā from Ismāʿīl, and some of them in his isnād.

The ruling is in favor of the one who preserved it. The narration that we have mentioned with its full context is in agreement with the established ḥadīth from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) on this matter, even though some of these narrators add or omit certain wordings.

There is no ḥadīth in this chapter more authentic than the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was preceded in the one before it]"
"3947","(3947) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'quob narrated to us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar informed you, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) saw a man praying one day while he was in the mosque. When the man finished, he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: ""And upon you be peace. Go back and pray, for you have not prayed."" So he went back and prayed, then came and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said the same to him. He said: So he went back and prayed two or three times, then he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I cannot do better than what you see, so teach me how to pray."" He said to him: ""When you stand for prayer, perfect your wudu', then say the takbir. When you are standing upright, recite Umm al-Qur'an, then recite whatever you wish of the Qur'an. Then bow until you are at rest in ruku', then raise your head until you stand upright and say: 'Sami' Allahu liman hamidah.' Then prostrate until you are at rest in sujud, then raise your head until you are at rest sitting. Then do that throughout all your prayers."" [Sahih. Previously cited under no. 3942, 3943.]"
"3948","(3948) And Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr informed us, saying: Abu al-Abbas narrated to us; Bahr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: Dawud ibn Qays al-Madani informed you, from 'Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad al-Zurqi, who said: My father narrated to me, from his uncle who was a Badri companion, that he was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) in the mosque. He said: Then he mentioned this, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: ""When you complete your prayer in this manner, your prayer is complete, and whatever you fall short of in this, you diminish from your prayer.""

Ibn Wahb referred this narration back to what had preceded. Others besides Ibn Wahb narrated it from Dawud ibn Qays but did not affirm the specification of the recitation.

Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated it from Ibn Wahb, from Dawud ibn Qays, and he related the hadith and mentioned in it the recitation of Umm al-Qur'an. [Sahih. Previously cited under no. 3946.]"
"3949","(3949) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dassah informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Khalid, from Muhammad — meaning ibn 'Amr — from 'Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', with this story, who said: ""When you stand and face the qiblah, say the takbir, then recite Umm al-Qur'an and whatever Allah wills you to recite. When you bow, place your palms upon your knees and extend your back."" And he said: ""When you prostrate, make your sujud firm. When you raise your head, sit upon your left thigh."" [Sahih. Previously cited in the one before it.]"
"3950","(3950) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no prayer for one who does not recite Umm al-Qur'an and more.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh and 'Abd ibn Humayd, from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari cited it as no. 756.]"
"3951","(3951) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Walid al-Faqih narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Jabalah narrated to us; al-Hulwani — meaning al-Hasan ibn 'Ali — narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; my father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shahab, that Mahmud ibn al-Rabi' — the one upon whose face the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had spat water from their well — informed him that 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no prayer for one who does not recite Umm al-Qur'an.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Hulwani, and al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyyaynah, from al-Zuhri. [Munkar. The detail of that has been previously cited under no. 2915.]"
"3952","(3952) And Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Qadi informed us from his original copy; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Mu'mal ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Isa al-Masarjisi informed us; al-Fadl ibn Muhammad al-Sha'rani informed us; Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us; my father narrated to us, from al-'Ala', who said: I heard from my father and from Abu al-Sa'ib, both of whom were companions of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and they said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite the Opening of the Book, it is deficient, it is deficient, it is incomplete."" He said: I said: ""O Abu Hurayrah, sometimes I am behind the imam."" So he squeezed my arm and said: ""O Persian, recite it to yourself, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say"" — meaning: ""Allah says: 'I have divided the prayer between Myself and My servant into two halves; half of it is for Me and half of it is for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks. My servant says: ""Al-hamdu lillahi Rabb al-'alamin"" — Allah says: ""My servant has praised Me."" He says: ""Al-Rahman al-Rahim"" — Allah says: ""My servant has extolled Me."" My servant says: ""Maliki yawm al-din"" — Allah says: ""My servant has glorified Me."" And this verse is between Me and My servant. My servant says: ""Iyyaka na'budu wa iyyaka nasta'in"" — this verse is between Me and him, and the end of the surah is for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks. My servant says: ""Ihdina al-sirat al-mustaqim"" to the end of the surah.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Abi Uways, from al-'Ala', from both of them. [Sahih. Muslim cited it as no. 395.]"
"3953","(3953) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Wuhaib ibn Khalid narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Abu 'Uthman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded me to announce in Madinah that: ""There is no prayer except with the recitation of the Opening of the Book."" [Da'if. Ahmad cited it as 2/428.]"
"3954","(3954) And Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Mahmish al-Faqih informed us; Abu 'Uthman 'Amr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Basri informed us; Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ja'far — meaning Ibn Abi Kathir al-Madani — narrated to me; al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) passed by Ubayy ibn Ka'b while he was standing in prayer. He called out to him: ""Come, O Ubayy!"" So Ubayy hastened in his prayer, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: ""What prevented you, O Ubayy, from answering me when I called you? Does not Allah, the Most High, say: 'O you who believe, respond to Allah and to the Messenger when he calls you' [al-Anfal: 24]?"" Ubayy said: ""I have committed an offense, O Messenger of Allah. You will not call me except that I will respond to you, even if I am praying."" He said: ""Would you like me to teach you a surah the like of which was not revealed in the Torah, nor in the Gospel, nor in the Psalms, nor in the Furqan?"" Ubayy said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Do not exit through the gate of the mosque until you have learned it."" And the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was walking, intending to leave the mosque. When he reached the gate to exit, Ubayy said: ""The surah, O Messenger of Allah?"" So he stopped and said: ""Yes. How do you recite in your prayer?"" So Ubayy recited Umm al-Qur'an. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, the like of it was not revealed in the Torah, nor in the Gospel, nor in the Psalms, nor in the Furqan. And it is al-Mathani al-Sab' (the Seven Oft-Repeated) which Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has given me.""

'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated it from al-'Ala', from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, with its meaning, in the story of al-Fatihah, without the story of the response.

Jahdam ibn 'Abd Allah narrated it from al-'Ala', from his father, from Abu Hurayrah.

Malik ibn Anas contradicted them and narrated it from al-'Ala', from Abu Sa'id, the freedman of 'Amir ibn Kurayz, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and he related it as a mursal narration. Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar narrated it from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah. [Hasan, except his statement ""Umm al-Qur'an is al-Mathani al-Sab' and al-Qur'an al-'Azim,"" for that is sahih. Al-Tirmidhi cited it as 2875.]"
"3955","(3955) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Hafiz in Hamadan informed us; Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us; Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us; Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""'Al-hamdu lillah' is Umm al-Qur'an, and al-Mathani al-Sab' (the Seven Oft-Repeated), and al-Qur'an al-'Azim (the Magnificent Qur'an).""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, and Nuh ibn Abi Bilal narrated it from al-Maqburi, more complete than that. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari cited it as 4427.]"
"3956","(3956) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Tamtam narrated to us; Ishaq ibn 'Abd al-Wahid al-Mawsili narrated to us; al-Mu'afi ibn 'Imran narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Nuh ibn Abi Bilal, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""'Al-hamdu lillahi Rabb al-'alamin' — seven verses, counting 'Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim' — and it is al-Mathani al-Sab' (the Seven Oft-Repeated), and it is Umm al-Qur'an, and the Opening of the Book.""

And similarly Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated it from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far. He said: Then I met Nuh and he narrated it to me from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, as a mawquf narration, not marfu'.

And we have narrated from 'Ali and Ibn 'Abbas and others that which indicates the same. [Sahih. Previously cited in the one before it.]"
"3957","(3957) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; Mūsā ibn Sahl narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Rumḥ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdillāh Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-'Anbarī, the grandson of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, both informed us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muhammad ibn Ismā'īl al-Ismā'īlī informed us; 'Īsā ibn Ḥammād said: Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me; Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Sa'īd ibn Jubayr and from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn 'Abbās, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach us the Tashahhud just as he used to teach us the Qur'an, and he used to say:

""All compliments, blessed prayers, and pure words are due to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of 'Īsā. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muhammad ibn Rumḥ and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 403.]"
"3958","(3958) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; 'Abdullāh ibn Muhammad al-Ka'bī narrated to us; Ismā'īl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us; 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd narrated to me; Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach us the Tashahhud just as he used to teach us a sūrah from the Qur'an.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
"3959","(3959) Abū Muhammad 'Abdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār al-Sukkari and others informed us in Baghdād; Ismā'īl ibn Muhammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us; Ma'mar narrated from Qatādah, from Yūnus ibn Jubayr, from Ḥiṭṭān ibn 'Abdillāh al-Raqāshī, that Abū Mūsā led the people in prayer. He mentioned the ḥadīth, and in it is from the Prophet (ﷺ):

""When one of you is seated [in prayer], let him say the first thing he speaks: 'All compliments, pure words, and prayers that purify are due to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others, from 'Abd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 404.]"
"3960","(3960) Abū Muhammad 'Abdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdād; Ismā'īl ibn Muhammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us; al-Thawrī informed us; Ḥammād, Manṣūr, al-A'mash, Abū Hāshim, and Ḥuṣayn ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān — all of them narrated from Abū Wā'il, from 'Abdullāh. And Abū Isḥāq narrated from al-Aswad and Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, from 'Abdullāh, who said: When we used to pray, we did not know what to say. We would say: ""Peace be upon Allah, peace be upon Jibrīl, peace be upon Mīkā'īl, peace be upon the Prophets."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) taught us and said:

""Indeed, Allah is al-Salām. When you pray, say: 'All compliments are due to Allah, and the prayers and pure words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.'""

Abū Wā'il said in his ḥadīth from 'Abdullāh, from the Prophet (ﷺ): ""When you say it, it reaches every angel brought near, every Messenger sent, and every righteous servant. I bear witness that there is no deity except Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 800/5876.]"
"3961","(3961) Abū Muhammad informed us; Ismā'īl narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Yazīd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ and Abū 'Ubaydah, from 'Abdullāh, from the Prophet (ﷺ), similarly.

Both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Manṣūr and al-A'mash, from Abū Wā'il, from 'Abdullāh, as has preceded. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
"3962","(3962) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī, narrated to us; Maḥmūd ibn 'Alī al-Bazzāz narrated to us; Sa'īd ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Makhzūmī narrated to us; Sufyān — he is ibn 'Uyaynah — narrated from al-A'mash and Manṣūr, from Abū Wā'il, from 'Abdullāh, who said: We used to say before the Tashahhud was made obligatory upon us: ""Peace be upon Allah before His creation, peace be upon Jibrīl and Mīkā'īl."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) taught us the Tashahhud.

(T) And similarly, Ibn Ṣā'id narrated it from al-Makhzūmī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
"3963","(3963) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Aḥmad al-Tamīmī narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Hārūn al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Abī Isrā'īl narrated to us; Abū Ma'shar al-Barā' — Yūsuf ibn Yazīd — narrated from Abū Ḥamzah, from Ibrāhīm, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abdullāh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach us the Tashahhud just as he used to teach us a sūrah from the Qur'an, and he would say: ""There is no prayer without the Tashahhud.""

Similarly, Ṣughdī ibn Sinān narrated it from Abū Ḥamzah. And with its authentic supporting chains, it possesses some degree of strength. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
"3964","(3964) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; 'Abdullāh ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Abū Wakī' narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, from 'Abdullāh, who said: The Tashahhud is the completion of the prayer.

And it has been narrated to us from 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""No prayer is valid without the Tashahhud."" [Ḍa'īf.]"
"3965","(3965) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; 'Alī ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; Abū al-Azhar Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar narrated to us; Ya'qūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Sa'd narrated to us; my father narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: And Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Tamīmī narrated to me regarding sending ṣalawāt upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when a Muslim invokes blessings upon him in his prayer, from Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn Zayd ibn 'Abd Rabbih al-Anṣārī, the brother of Banī al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, from Abū Mas'ūd al-Anṣārī — 'Uqbah ibn 'Amr — who said: A man came and sat before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while we were with him, and he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, as for the salām upon you, we know it, but how do we send ṣalawāt upon you when we invoke blessings upon you in our prayer?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) remained silent until we wished the man had not asked him. Then he said:

""When you send ṣalawāt upon me, say: 'O Allah, send Your prayers upon Muhammad, the unlettered Prophet, and upon the family of Muhammad, as You sent Your prayers upon Ibrāhīm and upon the family of Ibrāhīm. And bless Muhammad, the unlettered Prophet, and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed Ibrāhīm and the family of Ibrāhīm. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.'""

'Alī said: This is a ḥasan, connected chain of narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 405.]"
"3966","(3966) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Khālid ibn Yazīd, from Sa'īd ibn Abī Hilāl, from Yaḥyā ibn al-Sabbāq, from a man from Banī al-Ḥārith, from Ibn Mas'ūd, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said:

""When one of you recites the Tashahhud in prayer, let him say: 'O Allah, send Your prayers upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad, and bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, and have mercy upon Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You sent Your prayers, blessed, and had mercy upon Ibrāhīm and the family of Ibrāhīm. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.'""

Thus said 'Abdullāh ibn Mas'ūd (may Allah be pleased with him), and Allah knows best. [Ḍa'īf. Narrated by al-Ḥākim, 1/402.]"
"3967","(3967) 'Abd al-Muhaymin ibn 'Abbas ibn Sahl al-Sa'idi reported: I heard my father narrate from my grandfather that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: ""There is no prayer for one who does not perform wudu, and there is no wudu for one who does not mention the name of Allah upon it, and there is no prayer for one who does not send blessings upon the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ).""

Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad al-Saffar narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Bahr al-Bari narrated to us; my father said: 'Abd al-Muhaymin narrated it to me, and he mentioned it.

'Abd al-Muhaymin is weak (da'if) and his narrations cannot be used as evidence. It has also been narrated from 'Aisha as a marfu' hadith, and its chain of narration is weak.

[Weak. Al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Kabir, 5562.]"
"3968","(3968) Al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastawayh narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us; Isra'il narrated from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn 'Ali, from Abu Mas'ud, who said: ""If I were to perform a prayer in which I did not send blessings upon the family of Muhammad (ﷺ), I would consider my prayer to be incomplete.""

[Jabir al-Ju'fi is the sole narrator of it, and he is weak.]"
"3969","(3969) Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Khushaysh al-Tamimi informed us in Kufa; Abu Bakr 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn Mu'awiya al-Talhi narrated to us; Abu Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Habib narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Maymun narrated to us; Abu Malik al-Janbi narrated from Sharik, and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated from Isra'il — all of them — from Jabir, from Abu Ja'far, from Abu Mas'ud al-Badri, who said: ""If I were to perform a prayer in which I did not send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad, I would not consider it to be complete.""

Jabir al-Ju'fi is the sole narrator of it, and he is weak. What has preceded here and in the chapter on the description of the prayer is sufficient, and with Allah is the success. It has been narrated to us from al-Sha'bi that he said: ""Whoever does not send blessings upon the Prophet (ﷺ) in the tashahhud, let him repeat his prayer,"" or he said: ""His prayer will not suffice."" Its meaning has also been narrated to us from al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah from Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn.

[Weak. Referred to in the one before it.]"
"3970","(3970) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us; Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to us; Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The key of prayer is purification (tahara), its consecration (ihram) is the takbir, and its dissolution (ihlal) is the salam.""

[Hasan due to supporting narrations.]"
"3971","(3971) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; Abu Muslim narrated to us; Abu 'Umar informed us; Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated from Sa'id ibn Masruq al-Thawri, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The key of prayer is wudu, its consecration is the takbir, and its dissolution is the salam.""

Abu 'Umar al-Darir is the sole narrator of it — thus claimed Ibn Sa'id and many of the Huffaz. However, Hibban ibn Hilal narrated it from Hassan, so it is Hassan who is the sole narrator of it.

[Hasan. Ibn Abi Shaybah narrated it in al-Musannaf, 2307.]"
"3972","(3972) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu 'Amr narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas ibn Abi al-Dumayk narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah al-'Ayshi narrated to us; Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated from Abu Sufyan, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The key of prayer is wudu, the takbir is its consecration, and the salam is its dissolution.""

This is the preserved (mahfuz) version from Abu Sufyan Tarif al-Sa'di, and the hadith of Abu Sa'id revolves around him.

[Hasan due to supporting narrations. Al-Tirmidhi narrated it, 237.]"
"3973","(3973) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us; Abu 'Abd al-Rahman — meaning al-Muqqri' — narrated from Abu Hanifah, from Abu Sufyan, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Wudu is the key of prayer, the takbir is its consecration, and the salam is its dissolution. There is a salam (greeting) in every two rak'ahs, and no prayer is valid except with the Opening of the Book (Surah al-Fatiha) along with something else besides it.""

Abu 'Abd al-Rahman said: I said to Abu Hanifah, ""What does 'a salam in every two rak'ahs' mean?"" He said: ""He means the tashahhud."" Similarly, 'Ali ibn Mushir and others narrated it from Abu Sufyan.

[Sahih due to supporting narrations. It has a corroborating witness narrated by al-Tabarani in Musnad al-Shamiyyin, 2/289.]"
"3974","(3974) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from 'Umar ibn Dharr, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat at the end of his prayer before the tashahhud, he would turn his face toward the people, and that was before the salam was prescribed.

[Weak.]"
"3975","(3975) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Abbad ibn Musa al-Khutalli narrated to us; Isma'il — meaning Ibn Ja'far — narrated to me; Yahya ibn 'Ali ibn Khallad ibn Rafi' al-Zuraqi narrated from his father, from his grandfather, from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — he related the hadith of the man who prayed, and in it was what the Prophet (ﷺ) taught him: ""When you stand for prayer, perform wudu as Allah has commanded you, then recite the tashahhud and stand straight, then say the takbir. If you have any Quran with you, recite it; otherwise, praise Allah, magnify Him, and declare His oneness (say La ilaha illallah).""

And at the end of it he said: ""And if you fall short in any of it, you will have fallen short in your prayer.""

[Sahih due to supporting narrations. Abu Dawud narrated it, 861.]"
"3976","(3976) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Mahbubi informed us; Abu 'Isa Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Tirmidhi narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id al-Thaqafi and 'Ali ibn Hajr al-Sa'di both narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated from Yahya ibn 'Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad ibn Rafi' al-Zuraqi, and he mentioned it similarly.

[Sahih due to supporting narrations. Al-Tirmidhi narrated it, 302.]"
"3977","(3977) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Khalīl al-Barjalānī informed us; Abū al-Naḍr informed us; al-Masʿūdī narrated from Ibrāhīm al-Saksakī from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, who said:

A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""I am not able to recite the Qurʾān well, so teach me something that will suffice me in place of the Qurʾān."" He ﷺ said: **""Al-ḥamdu lillāhi wa subḥānallāhi, wa lā ilāha illallāhu wallāhu akbar, wa lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh.""** When he had committed these words to memory, he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, these are for my Lord — so what shall I say for myself?"" He ﷺ said: **""Say: Allāhumma ighfir lī warḥamnī wahdinī warzuqnī wa ʿāfinī""** (O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, provide for me, and grant me well-being). He (the narrator) said: He then grasped these words and departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""This one has filled his hands with goodness.""**

[Ḥasan for other than its own chain. Al-Ṭayālī narrated it (851).]

---"
"3978","(3978) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Shaybānī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥasan al-Hilālī informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; Miʿsar narrated from Ibrāhīm al-Saksakī from Ibn Abī Awfā, who said:

A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""I am unable to retain anything from the Qurʾān, so teach me that which will suffice me in place of the Qurʾān."" He ﷺ said: **""Subḥānallāhi wal-ḥamdu lillāhi, wa lā ilāha illallāhu wallāhu akbar, wa lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh.""** He (the narrator) said: He stood up, or went away, or did something of that nature, and then said: ""This is for Allah — so what is for me?"" He ﷺ said: **""Say: Allāhumma ighfir lī warḥamnī wa ʿāfinī warzuqnī""** (O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, grant me well-being, and provide for me).

Miʿsar said: I would sometimes ask Abū Khālid about it, and Sufyān al-Thawrī also narrated it from Abū Khālid Yazīd al-Dālānī from Ibrāhīm.

[Ḥasan for other than its own chain. See the preceding narration.]

---"
"3979","(3979) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed him; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Qūhyār informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Sufyān narrated from Yazīd al-Wāsiṭī from Ibrāhīm from Ibn Abī Awfā, who said:

A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am not able to recite anything from the Qurʾān, so teach me that which will suffice me in place of it."" He ﷺ said: **""Say: Subḥānallāhi wal-ḥamdu lillāhi, wa lā ilāha illallāhu wallāhu akbar, wa lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh.""** He went away, then returned and said: ""These are for my Lord — so what is for me?"" He ﷺ said: **""Say: Allāhumma ighfir lī warḥamnī, wahdinī warzuqnī, wa ʿāfinī wa ʿfu ʿannī""** (O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, provide for me, grant me well-being, and pardon me). When the man turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""As for this one, he has filled his hand with goodness.""**

[Ḥasan for other than its own chain. See the preceding narration.]

---"
"3980","(3980) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābillāh be pleased with him — used to lead the people in Maghrib prayer and did not recite therein. When he finished, it was said to him: ""You did not recite."" He said: ""How was the bowing and the prostration?"" They said: ""Good."" He said: ""Then there is no harm.""

Al-Shāfiʿī used to hold this opinion in the old view (al-qadīm), and he also narrated it from a man from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from ʿUmar, with the same meaning as the narration of Abū Salamah. He (al-Shāfiʿī) weakened what was narrated in this story from al-Shaʿbī and Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that ʿUmar repeated the prayer, saying that both of those narrations are mursal. He said: ""And Abū Salamah narrates it in Madīnah, in the very place of the incident, and none among the family of ʿUmar denies it.""

[Weak. Mālik narrated it, and al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from him in al-Umm (7/251).]

---"
"3981","(3981) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us regarding the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm and al-Shaʿbī; Abū Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Shaybān al-Baghdādī al-Harwī informed us therein; Muʿādh ibn Najdah informed us; Kāmil ibn Ṭalḥah informed us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Ḥammād ibn Abī Sulaymān from Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābillāh be pleased with him — led the people in Maghrib prayer and did not recite anything until he gave the salām. When he finished, it was said to him: ""You did not recite anything."" He said: ""I had equipped a caravan for Syria, and I kept stopping it at stage after stage until it reached Syria, where I sold it along with its saddles, its harnesses, and its loads."" He (the narrator) said: ""So ʿUmar repeated the prayer, and they repeated it with him.""

[Weak.]

---"
"3982","(3982) Abū Naṣr informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Baghdādī informed us; Muʿādh ibn Najdah informed us; Kāmil informed us; Ḥammād narrated from Abū Ḥamzah from Ibrāhīm that Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, did you recite to yourself?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then you did not recite."" So he repeated the prayer.

[Weak.]

---"
"3983","(3983) Abū Naṣr informed us; Abū Muḥammad informed us; Muʿādh informed us; Kāmil informed us; Ḥammād narrated from Ibn ʿAwn from al-Shaʿbī that Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābillāh be pleased with him: ""O Commander of the Faithful, did you recite to yourself?"" He said: ""No."" So he instructed the muezzins, and they gave the adhān and the iqāmah, and he repeated the prayer with them.

These narrations from Ibrāhīm and al-Shaʿbī are mursal, as al-Shāfiʿī stated. And the narration of Abū Salamah, even though it is mursal, is the most authentic of the mursal narrations, and his ḥadīth in Madīnah at the very site of the incident — as al-Shāfiʿī stated — is not denied by anyone. However, the ḥadīth of al-Shaʿbī has been narrated with a complete chain (musnad) from another source.

Repeating the prayer is more in accordance with the Sunnah regarding the obligation of recitation, and that it is not waived due to forgetfulness, just like the other pillars.

[Weak.]

---"
"3984","(3984) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us; Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī informed us; Qabīṣah informed us; Yūnus narrated from ʿĀmir, meaning al-Shaʿbī, from Ziyād, meaning Ibn ʿIyāḍ — the son-in-law of Abū Mūsā — who said:

ʿUmar prayed and did not recite, so he repeated.

A third narration has also been reported from ʿUmar billāh be pleased with him regarding this matter, uniquely narrated by ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Tārīkh al-Kabīr (3/365).]

---"
"3985","(3985) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī informed us; ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Sadūsī informed us; ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī informed us; ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ ibn al-Ḥammāmī informed us in Baghdād; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū ʿAttāb informed us; Shuʿbah informed us; ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated from Ḍamḍam ibn Jaws from ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥanẓalah ibn al-Rāhib, who said:

ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābillāh be pleased with him — led us in Maghrib prayer and did not recite anything in the first rakʿah. When he stood for the second rakʿah, he recited Sūrat al-Fātiḥah and a sūrah, then he repeated and recited Sūrat al-Fātiḥah and a sūrah. When he finished his prayer, he performed two prostrations after he had given the salām.

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. In the narration of ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī: ""Then he continued and completed his prayer, then he performed the two prostrations of sahw, then he gave the salām."" And he added, at his words ""anything"": ""he forgot it.""

This narration, in this form, is uniquely narrated by ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār from Ḍamḍam ibn Jaws, while the other narrations are more numerous and more well-known, even though some of them are mursal. And Allah knows best.

In the narration of al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī billāh be pleased with him: A man said: ""I prayed and did not recite."" He said: ""Did you complete the bowing and the prostration?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Your prayer is complete.""

If this is authentic, it is interpreted as referring to the omission of the loud recitation or the recitation of the additional sūrah, based on the preceding musnad narrations regarding the obligation of recitation. And al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar is not used as a proof.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated it in al-Muṣannaf (2/123).]

---"
"3986","(3986) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārīʾ that they both heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābillāh be pleased with him say:

I passed by Hishām ibn Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām while he was reciting Sūrat al-Furqān during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I listened to his recitation, and lo, he was reciting it in many modes (ḥurūf) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not taught me. I was about to confront him during the prayer, but I waited until he gave the salām. When he gave the salām, I seized him by his cloak and said: ""Who taught you this sūrah that I heard you reciting?"" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught it to me."" I said: ""You have lied, by Allah! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me this very sūrah that you are reciting."" I then took him, leading him, to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I heard this one reciting Sūrat al-Furqān in modes that you did not teach me, and you taught me Sūrat al-Furqān."" The Prophet ﷺ said: **""Release him, O ʿUmar. Recite, O Hishām.""** So he recited it in the manner I had heard. The Prophet ﷺ said: **""It was revealed in this way.""** Then the Prophet ﷺ said: **""Recite, O ʿUmar.""** So I recited it in the manner the Prophet ﷺ had taught me. The Prophet ﷺ said: **""It was revealed in this way.""** Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""Indeed, the Qurʾān was revealed upon seven modes (aḥruf), so recite from it whatever is easy for you.""**

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿUqayl and Yūnus from al-Zuhrī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it (4992).]"
"3987","(3987) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ and ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Dārajirdī both said: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĪsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from his grandfather ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said:

I was sitting in the mosque when a man entered and recited a recitation which I found objectionable. Then another man came and recited a recitation different from that of his companion. When we departed, we went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this man recited a recitation which I found objectionable, and then this other one recited something different from that of his companion.""

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to the man: ""Recite."" So he recited. Then he said to the other: ""Recite."" So he recited. Then he said: ""You have both done well"" (or: ""You have both been correct"").

When I saw that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) approved of both of them, something fell within my soul, and I wished I had been in the time of Jāhiliyyah. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw what had come over me, he struck his hand upon my chest, and I broke out in sweat, and it was as though I were looking at Allah out of awe. Then he said: ""O Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, my Lord sent to me that I should recite the Qurʾān upon one ḥarf (letter/mode).""

He said: ""I sent him back, saying: 'O my Lord, make things easy for my Ummah.' He responded to me a second time: 'Recite upon one ḥarf.'"" He said: ""I said: 'O my Lord, make things easy for my Ummah.' He responded to me a third time: 'Recite upon seven ḥurūf (letters/modes), and for every time you send me back, you shall have a request that you may ask of me.' I said: 'O Allah, forgive my Ummah. O Allah, forgive my Ummah.' And I postponed the third [request] for a day when all creation would desire my intercession, even Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr, from his father, from Ismāʿīl, except that he said: ""There fell within my soul a thought of denial, and not that I had been in the time of Jāhiliyyah."" And another narrator said: ""It fell within my soul and weighed heavily upon me, and not that I had been in the time of Jāhiliyyah, what weighed heavily upon me."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 920.]"
"3988","(3988) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us — reading to him from his original — Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: al-Ḥakam informed me, from Mujāhid, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, that:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was at the pond of Banī Ghifār when Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to him and said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commands you and your Ummah to recite the Qurʾān upon one ḥarf."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I ask Allah for His pardon and His forgiveness; my Ummah cannot bear this.""

Then he returned and said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commands you and your Ummah to recite the Qurʾān upon two ḥurūf."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""My Ummah cannot bear this.""

Then he returned and said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commands you and your Ummah to recite the Qurʾān upon three ḥurūf."" He said: ""I ask Allah for His pardon and His forgiveness; my Ummah cannot bear this.""

Then he came to him and said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, commands you and your Ummah to recite the Qurʾān upon seven ḥurūf; whichever ḥarf they recite upon, they will have hit upon what is correct.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar and Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim, 821.]"
"3989","(3989) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī said: ʿAffān narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us: Qatādah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmur narrated to me, from Sulaymān ibn Ṣurd, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said:

I recited an ʀyah, and Ibn Masʿūd recited it differently. We came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and I said: ""Did you not teach me such-and-such an ʀyah?"" He said: ""Yes."" Ibn Masʿūd said: ""Did you not teach me it as such-and-such?"" He said: ""Yes."" Then he said: ""Both of you have done well and spoken rightly.""

I said: ""How can both of us have done well and spoken rightly?"" He said: He struck my chest and said: ""O Ubayy, I was taught the Qurʾān, and it was said to me: 'Upon one ḥarf or upon two ḥurūf?' The angel who was with me said: 'Upon two ḥurūf.' I said: 'Upon two ḥurūf.' Then it was said to me: 'Upon two ḥurūf or upon three?' The angel who was with me said: 'Upon three.' I said: 'Upon three,' until it reached seven ḥurūf. He said: 'There is nothing in it that is not a healing and a sufficiency.' I said: Such as Ghafūr Raḥīm, ʿAlīm Ḥalīm, Samīʿ ʿAlīm, ʿAzīz Ḥakīm, and the like — so long as you do not conclude an ʀyah of punishment with mercy, or an ʀyah of mercy with punishment.""

Maʿmar narrated it from Qatādah, but sent it as a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has a supporting witness from the previous ḥadīth…]"
"3990","(3990) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:

""Jibrīl (peace be upon him) taught me [the Qurʾān] upon one ḥarf, and I kept asking him for more, and he kept increasing me, until it reached seven ḥurūf.""

Al-Zuhrī said: ""These seven ḥurūf pertain to a single matter; there is no difference between them regarding what is ḥalāl or ḥarām.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus and ʿUqayl, from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 4991.]"
"3991","(3991) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Shaqīq.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Yūsuf al-Qāḍī and Abū Muslim both said: ʿAmr — Ibn Marzūq — narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aʿmash, who said: I heard Abū Wāʾil narrating from ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, who said:

""I listened to the recitations and found them close to one another. Recite what you have learned, and beware of excessive pedantry and disagreement. It is only like one of you saying: 'Halumma' (come on!), 'Taʿāla' (come!), and 'Aqbil' (come forward!).""

This is the wording of Shuʿbah's ḥadīth. In the ḥadīth of Ibn Numayr, he said: ʿAbdullāh said: ""Indeed, I have heard…"" and he said: ""Recite as you have learned,"" and he did not mention his words ""and come forward."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has a supporting marfūʿ witness from the ḥadīth of Abū Bakrah, as mentioned in al-Taḥdīth of Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr, 1399.]"
"3992","(3992) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: Abū ʿUbayd said:

His statement ""seven ḥurūf"" means seven dialects (lughāt) of the Arabs. It does not mean that a single letter (ḥarf) has seven different forms — this has never been heard of. Rather, he says: these seven dialects are distributed throughout the Qurʾān. Some of it was revealed in the dialect of Quraysh, some in the dialect of Hawāzin, some in the dialect of Hudhayl, some in the dialect of the people of Yemen, and likewise the other dialects. Their meanings in all of this are one and the same. What clarifies this for you is the statement of Ibn Masʿūd, which he mentioned.

He said: And likewise Ibn Sīrīn said: ""It is only like your saying: 'Halumma,' 'Taʿāla,' and 'Aqbil.'"" Then Ibn Sīrīn explained it, saying: ""In the recitation of Ibn Masʿūd, it is 'In kānat illā zaqiyyatan wāḥidah,' while in our recitation it is 'Ṣayḥatun wāḥidah' [Yā Sīn: 29], and the meaning of both is the same. And so it is with the other dialects."" [Ḥasan.]"
"3993","(3993) Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Khunb narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Anas ibn Mālik, in the story of the compilation of the Qurʾān when ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) summoned Zayd ibn Thābit and commanded him, along with ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām, to copy the ṣuḥuf (manuscripts) into maṣāḥif (codices). He said: ""Whatever you and Zayd ibn Thābit differ upon, write it in the language of Quraysh, for it was revealed in their language."" So they copied the ṣuḥuf into maṣāḥif.

Then they and Zayd ibn Thābit differed regarding ""al-Tābūt."" The Qurayshī group said: ""al-Tābūt,"" and Zayd said: ""al-Tābūh."" They raised their disagreement to ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: ""Write it as 'al-Tābūt,' for it is in the language of Quraysh.""

Ismāʿīl said: Thus Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us the story of al-Tābūt as a connected narrative at the end of his ḥadīth. Abū al-Walīd separated it from the ḥadīth and attributed it to al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī, 3244, 460.]"
"3994","(3994) Abū Sahl narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Khunb narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd, who said: Ibn Shihāb said:

""They differed that day regarding 'al-Tābūt.' Zayd said: 'al-Tābūh,' and Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ and Ibn al-Zubayr said: 'al-Tābūt.' They raised their disagreement to ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: 'Write it as al-Tābūt, for it is in their language.'"" [Ḥasan in virtue of other supporting narrations. The author's chain. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā, 1/64.]"
"3995","(3995) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from his father, from Khārijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thābit, who said:

""Recitation is a Sunnah.""

What he meant — and Allah knows best — is that following those who came before us regarding the letters and the recitations is a Sunnah to be followed. It is not permissible to oppose the Muṣḥaf which is the imām, nor to oppose the recitations that are well-known and established, even if other readings are permissible in the Arabic language or more evident. And with Allah lies success.

As for the reports that have come regarding the permissibility of reciting ""Ghafūr Raḥīm"" in place of ""ʿAlīm Ḥakīm,"" that is because all of that was revealed by way of divine revelation. So if one recites that in a place other than its proper location, so long as one does not conclude an ʀyah of punishment with an ʀyah of mercy, or an ʀyah of mercy with an ʀyah of punishment, it is as though one recited one ʀyah from one sūrah and another ʀyah from another sūrah — one does not incur sin by reciting it thus. The foundation is what the recitation was established upon in the year in which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, after Jibrīl (peace be upon him) had reviewed the Qurʾān with him twice in that year. Then the Ṣaḥābah agreed upon its being fixed between the two covers. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr, 2/260.]"
"3996","(3996) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnbarī informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us: Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā al-Qanṭarī narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awzāʿī, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""The worst of people in theft is the one who steals his Ṣalāh.""

They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how does one steal his Ṣalāh?"" He said: ""By not perfecting its rukūʿ (bowing) and its sujūd (prostration)."" [Ḥasan in virtue of other supporting narrations. Narrated by al-Ḥākim, 1/353.]"
"3997","(3997) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us that Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Abī al-ʿIshrīn narrated to me that al-Awzāʿī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me that Abū Salamah narrated to us that Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Indeed, the worst of people in terms of theft is the one who steals his prayer."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how does one steal his prayer?"" He said: ""He does not perfect its rukūʿ (bowing) nor its sujūd (prostration)."" And this was also narrated in the ḥadīth of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. [Ḥasan for something other than itself. Al-Ḥākim narrated it, 1/353.]"
"3998","(3998) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us that Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Ḥudhayfah saw a man who did not perfect his rukūʿ (bowing) or his sujūd (prostration). He said: ""For how long have you been praying?"" He said: ""For forty years."" He said: ""You have not prayed at all, and if you were to die, you would die upon other than the fiṭrah (natural disposition)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, 758.]"
"3999","(3999) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Abū Saʿīd, and Ṣafwān said: Al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Namir narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, who said: Ḥarmalah, the freedman of Usāmah ibn Zayd, narrated to me: ""While he was sitting with ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, al-Ḥajjāj ibn Ayman ibn Umm Ayman entered — and he was a man from the Anṣār, and Ayman was the brother of Usāmah ibn Zayd and was older than Usāmah"" — Ḥarmalah said: ""Al-Ḥajjāj prayed a prayer in which he did not perfect his rukūʿ (bowing) or his sujūd (prostration). When he gave the salām, Ibn ʿUmar called him and said: 'O my nephew, do you think you have prayed? Indeed, you have not prayed, so repeat your prayer.' When al-Ḥajjāj turned to leave, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said to me: 'Who is this?' I said: 'Al-Ḥajjāj ibn Ayman ibn Umm Ayman.' Ibn ʿUmar said: 'If the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had seen this one, he would have loved him.' Then he mentioned the reason for his love, that Umm Ayman had given birth to him, and she had been the nurse of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it, 3529.]"
"4000","(4000) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Ismāʿīl — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-ʿAdl narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dawraqī narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us that Yūnus narrated from al-Ḥasan from Anas ibn Ḥakīm al-Ḍabbī, who said: He feared Ziyād — Abū Dāwūd said in his narration: ""from Ziyād or Ibn Ziyād"" — so he came to al-Madīnah and met Abū Hurayrah. He said: ""He asked me about my lineage, so I told him my lineage, and he said: 'O young man, shall I not narrate to you a ḥadīth?' I said: 'Yes, may Allah have mercy on you.' Yūnus said: 'And I think he attributed it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.' He said: 'Indeed, the first thing for which the people will be taken to account on the Day of Resurrection from among their deeds is the prayer.' He said: 'Our Lord, Mighty and Majestic, will say to His angels — and He is most knowing: ""Look at the prayer of My servant — did he complete it or did he fall short in it?"" If it was complete, it will be recorded as complete for him. And if he fell short in any part of it, He will say: ""Look — does My servant have any voluntary prayers?"" If he has voluntary prayers, He will say: ""Complete the obligatory prayer for My servant from his voluntary prayers."" Then the rest of the deeds will be dealt with accordingly.'"" [Ḥasan for something than itself. Ibn al-Jāshibah narrated it, 34242.]"
"4001","(4001) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us that Ḥammād narrated from Ḥumayd from al-Ḥasan from a man from Banī Salīṭ from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, with a similar meaning. This is a ḥadīth in which there has been disagreement regarding al-Ḥasan through many routes, and what we have mentioned is the most authentic of them, if Allah, the Most High, wills. It was also narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in a marfūʿ (elevated) narration. [Ḥasan for something other than itself. Aḥmad narrated it, 4/103.]"
"4002","(4002) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī and Muḥammad ibn Ghālib both said: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind from Zurārah ibn Awfā from Tamīm al-Dārī that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Indeed, the first thing for which the servant will be taken to account on the Day of Resurrection is his prayer. If he has completed it fully, it will be recorded as complete for him. And if he has not completed it, Allah, the Most High, will say to His angels: 'Do you find any voluntary prayers for My servant, so that you may complete with them what he has neglected of his obligatory prayer?' Then the zakāh will be dealt with in the same manner, and then all other deeds will be dealt with accordingly."" Ḥammād ibn Salamah raised it (as marfūʿ), and others stopped it (as mawqūf). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ḥākim narrated it, 2/479.]"
"4003","(4003) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād that Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us that Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind narrated from Zurārah ibn Awfā from Tamīm al-Dārī, who said: ""Indeed, the first thing for which the servant will be taken to account on the Day of Resurrection is the obligatory prayer. Whoever has completed it fully will be taken to account for the rest of his deeds. And if he has fallen short in it, it will be said: 'Look — does he have any voluntary prayers?' If he has voluntary prayers, the obligatory prayer will be completed from the voluntary prayers. And if he has no voluntary prayers, the obligatory prayer will not be completed, and he will be seized by his two sides and cast into the Fire."" Sufyān al-Thawrī and Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth likewise stopped it (as mawqūf) from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind. Yazīd al-Raqāshī narrated it from Anas ibn Mālik from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, with the meaning of the ḥadīth of Tamīm al-Dārī from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, regarding the prayer and the zakāh, and he completed it from him. Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah, who is weak, narrated in this meaning something that resembles the opposite of this. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was previously mentioned in the one before it.]"
"4004","(4004) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq, known as Ibn al-Bayāḍ, informed us in Baghdād that ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Qurashī narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us that Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to me that Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah narrated to me that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥunayn narrated from his father from ʿAlī, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said — (ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us that Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us that Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad al-Qurashī narrated to us that Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah al-Rabadhī narrated from Ibn Ḥunayn from his father from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""O ʿAlī, the likeness of the one who does not complete his prayer is like that of a pregnant woman who becomes pregnant, and when her time of delivery approaches, she miscarries — so she is neither one who has a child nor one who is pregnant. And the likeness of the one who prays is like that of a merchant whose profit is not realized until his capital is realized. Likewise, the one who prays — his voluntary prayer is not accepted until he fulfills the obligatory prayer."" Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah is not used as a proof, and there has been disagreement regarding his isnād. Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb and Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad narrated it in this manner. And Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated it from Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah from Ṣāliḥ ibn Suwayd from ʿAlī, likewise as marfūʿ, and it is, if authentic, as such. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ḥākim narrated it.]"
"4005","(4005) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me that Mūsā narrated from Ṣāliḥ ibn Suwayd from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The likeness of the one who does not complete his prayer is like that of a pregnant woman who becomes pregnant, and when her time of delivery approaches, she miscarries — so there is no pregnancy and she is not one who has a child. And the likeness of the one who prays is like that of a merchant whose profit is not realized until his capital is realized. Likewise, the one who prays — his voluntary prayer is not accepted until he fulfills the obligatory prayer."" And if this is authentic, it is understood to refer to voluntary prayers that are part of the obligatory prayer, so their validity depends on the validity of the obligatory prayer. The previously mentioned narrations are understood to refer to voluntary prayers that are outside the obligatory prayer, so their validity does not depend on the validity of the obligatory prayer. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ḥākim narrated it, and from him al-Muṣaffā.]"
"4006","(4006) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us that Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb narrated to us that Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī narrated to us that al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān narrated to me that Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me that Sulaymān ibn Yasār narrated to me, who said: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: ""I have not seen anyone whose prayer more closely resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, than so-and-so"" — referring to a man who was the governor of al-Madīnah. Sulaymān said: ""I prayed behind him, and he would lengthen the first two rakʿahs of Ẓuhr and shorten the last two, and he would shorten ʿAṣr, and he would recite in the first two rakʿahs of Maghrib from the shorter sūrahs of al-Mufaṣṣal, and he would recite in the first two rakʿahs of ʿIshāʾ from the middle sūrahs of al-Mufaṣṣal, and he would recite in Fajr from the longer sūrahs of al-Mufaṣṣal."" Al-Ḍaḥḥāk said: ""And someone who heard Anas ibn Mālik narrated to me, who said: 'I have not seen anyone whose prayer more closely resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, than this young man' — meaning ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz."" Al-Ḍaḥḥāk said: ""And I prayed behind him, and he would pray in the manner that Sulaymān ibn Yasār described."" [Ḥasan. Aḥmad narrated it, 7/58.]"
"4007","(4007) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Kabīr al-Ḥanafī, that is, Abū Bakr, narrated the hadith with a similar wording through both chains of narration. [Hasan. It was mentioned before it.]"
"4008","(4008) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrating from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that he said: ""There is no sūrah from the Mufaṣṣal—neither a small one nor a large one—except that I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reciting it to the people in the obligatory prayer."" [Weak. Abū Dāwūd narrated it (814).]"
"4009","(4009) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓafar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharazah narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā, and al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us—all from Misʿar ibn Kidām, from al-Walīd ibn Sarīʿ, from ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, who said: ""I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) reciting in the Fajr prayer: {"
"4010","(4010) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Sharīk and Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us—from Ziyād ibn ʿIllāqah, from Quṭbah ibn Mālik, that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited in the Fajr prayer: {"
"4011","(4011) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿImrān ibn Mūsā and Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr said: Abū Kāmil al-Jaḥdarī narrated to us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us—from Ziyād ibn ʿIllāqah, from Quṭbah ibn Mālik, who said: ""I prayed, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the Ṣubḥ prayer, and he recited {"
"4012","(4012) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Zāʾidah narrated to us—from Simāk, from Jābir ibn Samurah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited in the Fajr prayer: {"
"4013","(4013) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Maymūnī narrated to us; Rūḥ ibn ʿIbādah narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I heard Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar narrating to me; Abū Salamah ibn Sufyān, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Musayyib al-ʿĀbidī narrated to me—from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Sāʾib, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in the Ṣubḥ prayer in Makkah and began Sūrah al-Muʾminūn until the mention of Mūsā and Hārūn came, or the mention of ʿĪsā came—Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād was uncertain, or they differed about it—and a cough seized the Prophet (ﷺ), so he cut short the recitation and went into rukūʿ, and Ibn al-Sāʾib was present for that."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad and others from Ibn Jurayj as mentioned above. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Shāfiʿī narrated it in his Musnad (750).]"
"4014","(4014) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us; Yazīd, meaning Ibn Hārūn, informed us; Sulaymān narrated to us—from Sayyār, Abū al-Minhāl, from Abū Barzah al-Aslamī, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to recite in the morning prayer from sixty to one hundred verses. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through other routes from Abū al-Minhāl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī narrated it (541).]"
"4015","(4015) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us—from Ibn Shihāb, from Anas, that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in the Ṣubḥ prayer and recited Sūrah al-Baqarah. ʿUmar said to him: ""The sun is about to rise!"" He said: ""Even if it had risen, it would not have found us heedless."" (T) Qatādah narrated something similar from Anas and said: ""The sun was about to rise."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Shāfiʿī narrated it (7/228).]"
"4016","(4016) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik ibn Anas narrated to us—from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed the Ṣubḥ and recited Sūrah al-Baqarah in both rakʿahs. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was mentioned in what preceded it.]"
"4017","(4017) And with their chain of narration, from Maalik, from Hishaam ibn `Urwah, from his father, that he heard `Abdullaah ibn `Aamir saying: ""We performed the Fajr prayer behind `Umar ibn al-Khattaab (may Allah be pleased with him), and he recited therein Soorah Yoosuf and Soorah al-Hajj, reciting slowly."" Hishaam said: ""I said: 'By Allah, then he must have been standing when the dawn broke.' He said: 'Yes.'"" [Saheeh. Reported by al-Shaafi`ee in his Musnad 1/215]"
"4018","(4018) And with their chain of narration, from Maalik, from Yahyaa ibn Sa`eed and Rabi`ah ibn Abi `Abd al-Rahmaan, from al-Qaasim ibn Muhammad, that Farafisah ibn `Umayr al-Hanafee said: ""I did not learn Soorah Yoosuf except from `Uthmaan's recitation of it in the Fajr prayer, due to how frequently he would repeat it."" [Saheeh. Reported by Maalik in al-Muwatta' 1/82/184]"
"4019","(4019) And with their chain of narration, from Maalik, from Naafi`, from `Abdullaah ibn `Umar: ""That he used to recite in the Fajr prayer during travel the first ten soorahs of al-Mufassal, reciting one soorah in each rak`ah. Al-Shaafi`ee did not mention the soorahs; he said: 'The first ten.'"" [Saheeh. Reported by Maalik in al-Muwatta' 1/82/185]"
"4020","(4020) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattaan informed us in Baghdad; `Abdullaah ibn Ja`far informed us; Ya`qoob ibn Sufyaan narrated to us; Sa`eed ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; al-Daarwardee said: Khuthaym ibn `Iraak ibn Maalik narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out (for battle) and appointed Siba` ibn `Arfatah as his deputy over al-Madinah.""

Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I came to al-Madinah as a muhaajir and performed the Fajr prayer behind Siba`. He recited Soorah Maryam in the first rak`ah and {Woe to the defrauders} [al-Mutaffifeen: 1] in the second."" Abu Hurayrah said: ""I said: 'Woe to Abu Ful' — or he said, 'Woe to Abu Fulan' — referring to a man from the land of al-Azd who had two measures: one with which he measured for himself and another with which he cheated the people."" [Saheeh. Reported by Ya`qoob ibn Sufyaan in al-Ma`rifah wa al-Taareekh 1270]"
"4021","(4021) Abu `Ali al-Rawdbaaree informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Daasah informed us; Abu Daawood narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Saalih narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; `Amr informed me, from Ibn Abi Hilaal, from Mu`aadh ibn `Abdullaah al-Juhani, that a man from Juhaynah informed him: ""That he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) reciting {When the earth is shaken} [al-Zalzalah: 1] in both rak`ahs of the Fajr prayer. I do not know whether the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forgot or recited that deliberately.""

And it has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he performed the Fajr prayer for the people with al-Mu`awwidhatayn. That will come. [Hasan. Reported by Abu Daawood 816]"
"4022","(4022) Abu al-Qaasim Zayd ibn Abi Haashim al-`Alawi informed us in al-Koofah; Abu Ja`far ibn Duheem al-Shaybaanee informed us; Ibraaheem ibn `Abdullaah informed us; Wakee` narrated to us, from al-A`mash, from al-Ma`roor ibn Suwayd, who said: ""We went out with `Umar ibn al-Khattaab (may Allah be pleased with him) as pilgrims, and he led us in the Fajr prayer, reciting {Have you not seen} [al-Feel] and {For the security of Quraysh} [Quraysh]."" [Daeef. Reported by al-Musannaf in al-Shu`ab 6/30/2420; reported by `Abd al-Razzaaq 2/118/2734]"
"4023","(4023) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishraan informed us in Baghdad; Abu Ja`far al-Razzaaz narrated to us; Yahyaa ibn Ja`far narrated to us; Zayd ibn al-Hubaab narrated to us; Sufyaan narrated to us, from Abi Ishaaq, from `Amr ibn Maymoon al-Awdi: ""That when `Umar ibn al-Khattaab (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed, they put `Abd al-Rahmaan ibn `Awf forward, and he led them in the Fajr prayer, reciting {When the help of Allah comes} [al-Nasr] and {Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar} [al-Kawthar]."" [Daeef]"
"4024","(4024) Muhammad ibn `Abdullaah al-Haafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Ja`far al-Qattee`ee narrated to us; `Abdullaah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; `Abd al-Rahmaan ibn Mahdee narrated to us; Mu`aawiyah ibn Saalih narrated to us, from Rabi`ah ibn Yazeed; Qaza`ah narrated to me, who said: ""I came to Abu Sa`eed while he was surrounded by people. When the people dispersed from him, I said: 'I will not ask you what these people are asking you. I have come to ask you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).' He said: 'There is no good in that for you.' I repeated the question to him, and he said: ""The Zuhr prayer would be established, and one of us would go to al-Baqi`, relieve himself, then come to his family and perform wudhoo', then return to the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would still be in the first rak`ah.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Saheeh from Muhammad ibn Haatim from `Abd al-Rahmaan ibn Mahdee. [Saheeh. Reported by Ahmad in al-Musnad 22/426/10881]"
"4025","(4025) Muhammad ibn `Abdullaah al-Haafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaaq informed us; Ismaa`eel ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yahyaa ibn Yahyaa informed us; Hushaym informed us.

(Another chain) And Abu al-Waleed informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyaan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansoor, from al-Waleed ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Siddeeq, from Abu Sa`eed al-Khudri, who said: ""We used to estimate the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) in Zuhr and `Asr. We estimated his standing in the first two rak`ahs of Zuhr to be the length of the recitation of {Alif-Laam-Meem. The revelation} [al-Sajdah], and we estimated his standing in the last two rak`ahs to be half of that. We estimated his standing in the first two rak`ahs of `Asr to be equal to his standing in the last two rak`ahs of Zuhr, and in the last two rak`ahs of `Asr to be half of that.""

The wording is from the hadeeth of Yahyaa ibn Yahyaa.

Muslim narrated it in al-Saheeh from Yahyaa ibn Yahyaa and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Saheeh. Reported by Ibn Abi Shaybah 3462]"
"4026","(4026) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Foorak informed us; `Abdullaah ibn Ja`far informed us; Yoonus ibn Habeeb informed us; Abu Daawood narrated to us; Shu`bah narrated to us, from Simaak ibn Harb, who said: ""I heard Jaabir ibn Samurah saying: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to recite in Zuhr and `Asr {By the night when it covers} [al-Layl: 1] and the like of it, and he used to recite in the Fajr prayer longer than that.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Saheeh from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from Abu Daawood, except that he did not mention `Asr and said: ""He used to recite {Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High} [al-A`laa: 1]."" [Hasan. Reported by Ibn Abi Shaybah 3462]"
"4027","(4027) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr b. Abī Jaʿfar informed me — al-Ḥasan b. Sufyān narrated to us — Abū Bakr b. Abī Shaybah narrated it, and he mentioned it.

And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Mahdī reported from Shuʿbah, similar to the narration of Yūnus b. Ḥabīb from Abū Dāwūd. [Ḥasan; previously referenced in the entry preceding it.]"
"4028","(4028) And Abū Bakr b. Fūrak narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh b. Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus b. Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ḥammād narrated —

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan b. ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad b. ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Bishr b. Mūsā narrated to us — Abū Zakariyyāʾ, meaning al-Ṣāliḥīnī, narrated to us — Ḥammād b. Salamah narrated from Simāk from Jābir: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr prayers {"
"4029","(4029) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr, Muḥammad b. Isḥāq b. Khuzaymah, narrated to us — Bundār, meaning Muḥammad b. Bashshār, narrated to us — Abū Bakr, meaning al-Ḥanafī, narrated to us — al-Ḍaḥḥāk, and he was ʿUthmān, narrated to us — Bukayr b. ʿAbdullāh b. al-Ashajj narrated to us — Sulaymān b. Yasār narrated to us that he heard Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: ""I have not seen anyone whose prayer more closely resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than a certain man who was an amīr in Madīnah."" Sulaymān said: ""So I prayed behind him, and he would make the first two rakʿahs of Ẓuhr lengthy and make the last two brief, and he would make the ʿAṣr brief, and he would recite in the first two rakʿahs of Maghrib from the short al-Mufaṣṣal, and in the first two rakʿahs of ʿIshāʾ from the middle al-Mufaṣṣal, and in the Fajr from the long al-Mufaṣṣal."" [Ḥasan; narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān, 5/145/1837.]"
"4030","(4030) And it has been reported to us from Jābir b. Samurah, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the Maghrib prayer on the night of Jumuʿah {"
"4031","(4031) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Naṣr ʿUmar b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz b. Qatādah informed us, saying: Abū ʿAmr b. Najīd narrated to us — Muḥammad b. Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated from Abū ʿUbayd, the mawlā of Sulaymān b. ʿAbd al-Malik, that ʿUbādah b. Nuṣayy informed him that he heard Qays b. al-Ḥārith saying: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣunābihī informed me that he came to Madīnah during the caliphate of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, and he prayed Maghrib behind Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq. Abū Bakr recited in the first two rakʿahs of Maghrib with Umm al-Qurʾān and a sūrah from the short al-Mufaṣṣal, then he stood for the third rakʿah — he said — so I drew close to him until my garments were nearly touching his garments, and I heard him reciting Umm al-Qurʾān and this verse: {"
"4032","(4032) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr b. Dāsah narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — ʿUbaydullāh b. Muʿādh narrated to us — my father narrated to us — Qurrah narrated from al-Nazzāl b. ʿAmmār from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī: that he prayed Maghrib behind Ibn Masʿūd, and he recited {"
"4033","(4033) And Abū ʿAlī informed us — Abū Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Mūsā b. Ismāʿīl narrated to us — Ḥammād informed us — Hishām b. ʿUrwah informed us: that his father used to recite in the Maghrib prayer with sūrahs similar to what you recite with {"
"4034","(4034) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh b. Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad b. Muḥammad b. Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān b. Muḥammad b. Manṣūr al-Ḥārithī narrated to us — Yaḥyā b. Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated from Mālik b. Anas from al-Zuhrī —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh narrated to us — Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Jaʿfar b. Muḥammad narrated to us — Yaḥyā b. Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read to Mālik b. Anas from Ibn Shihāb, from Muḥammad b. Jubayr b. Muṭʿim, from his father, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting with al-Ṭūr in the Maghrib prayer.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā b. Yaḥyā. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh b. Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā b. Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ; narrated by al-Bukhārī, 3798, 4573.]"
"4035","(4035) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh b. Yūsuf and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated to us — al-Rabīʿ b. Sulaymān al-Murādī informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik b. Anas informed us —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad b. Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh b. Yūsuf narrated to us — Mālik narrated —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Yaḥyā b. Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Muḥammad b. ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us — Yaḥyā b. Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbaydullāh b. ʿAbdullāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith heard him reciting {"
"4036","(4036) Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad b. ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad b. Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad b. Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Abū ʿĀṣim al-Nabīl informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed us — Ibn Abī Mulaykah informed us — from ʿUrwah — from Marwān, who said: Zayd b. Thābit said to me: ""What is the matter with you that you recite in Maghrib with the short al-Mufaṣṣal? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in Maghrib with the two long ones."" He (Marwān) said: ""So I said to ʿUrwah: 'What are the two long ones?' He said: 'Al-Aʿrāf.'"" He said: ""So I said to Ibn Abī Mulaykah: 'What are the two long ones?' He said: 'Al-Anʿām and al-Aʿrāf.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū ʿĀṣim al-Nabīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ; narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq, 2691.]"
"4037","(4037) It is narrated from Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) recited Surah al-A'raf in the Maghrib prayer and divided it over the two rak'ahs.

It was reported to us by 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan, who reported to us Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd, who narrated to us Shadhan ibn Zakariyya, who narrated to us Abu 'Abd Allah, who narrated to us 'Umar ibn 'Uthman ibn Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn Dinar, who narrated to us Abu Haywah and Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid, who both said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

And similarly, Abu Taqiyy narrated it from Baqiyyah, and Muhadir ibn al-Mawra' narrated it from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), with the same meaning. And the correct narration is the first one. [Hasan. Narrated by al-Nasa'i in al-Sughra, 991.]"
"4038","(4038) Abu al-Salih al-'Anbar ibn al-Tayyib ibn Muhammad al-'Anbari reported to us; my grandfather, Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi, narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: Mu'adh ibn al-Jabal al-Ansari led his companions in the 'Isha' prayer and made it lengthy upon them. A man among us left and prayed separately. When Mu'adh was informed about him, he said: ""Indeed, he is a hypocrite."" When this reached the man, he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him of what Mu'adh had said to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: ""Do you want to be a source of tribulation, O Mu'adh? When you lead the people, then recite 'By the sun and its brightness' (al-Shams), 'Glorify the name of your Lord Most High' (al-A'la), 'By the night when it covers' (al-Layl), and 'Read in the name of your Lord' (al-'Alaq).""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 6106.]"
"4039","(4039) Abu Ahmad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-'Adl reported to us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah reported to us; Yazid ibn Harun reported to us; Yahya reported that 'Adiyy ibn Thabit informed him that al-Bara' ibn 'Azib informed him: He prayed the 'Isha' prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he recited ""By the fig and the olive"" (al-Tin).

Muslim narrated it from the hadith of al-Layth ibn Sa'd from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 733.]"
"4040","(4040) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari reported to us in Tus; Abu Muhammad ibn Shawdhab al-Muqri' in Wasit narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from 'Adiyy ibn Thabit, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led us in the 'Isha' prayer during a journey and recited ""By the fig and the olive"" (al-Tin) in one of the two rak'ahs.

Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Sahih. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"4041","(4041) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz reported to us; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq and 'Ali ibn Hamshadh narrated to us. 'Ali said: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya reported to us; Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would hear the crying of a child with its mother while he was in prayer, so he would recite a short surah or a brief surah.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim, 470.]"
"4042","(4042) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz reported to us; Abu al-Fadl al-Hasan ibn Ya'qub al-'Adl narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' reported to us; Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, that Anas ibn Malik narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Indeed, I enter into prayer intending to lengthen it, then I hear the crying of a child, so I shorten my prayer due to what I know of the intensity of the mother's distress because of his crying."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 709.]"
"4043","(4043) Abu 'Abd Allah reported to us; Makhlad ibn Ja'far al-Daqqaq narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Tahir ibn Abi al-Dumayk narrated to us; 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us.

(And) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' reported to us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Indeed, I enter the prayer intending to lengthen it, then I hear the crying of a child, so I make it brief due to what I know of the intensity of the mother's distress because of him.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it from 'Ali ibn al-Madini, and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn al-Minhal.

And al-Bukhari also narrated it from the hadith of Sharik ibn Abi Namr from Anas, and from the hadith of Abu Qatadah al-Ansari from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). [Sahih. It has been mentioned in the one before it.]"
"4044","(4044) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf reported to us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi reported to us.

(And) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran reported to us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar reported to us; both said: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b about the Mu'awwidhatayn (the two surahs of refuge), and he said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) about the Mu'awwidhatayn, and he said: 'It was said to me, and I said it.' So we say as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tayalisi, 543.]"
"4045","(4045) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz reported to us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq reported to us; Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us; al-Humaydi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us; 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah and 'Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated that they both heard Zirr ibn Hubaysh say: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b about the Mu'awwidhatayn, and I said: ""O Abu al-Mundhir, indeed your brother Ibn Mas'ud erases them from the mushaf."" He said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and it was said to me, and I said it. So we say as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah and 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah from Sufyan. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 4977.]"
"4046","(4046) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran reported to us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us; 'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us.

(And) Abu Dharr ibn Abi al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Qasim al-Mudhakkir narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Sa'di narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us; both said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Verses have been revealed to me the like of which have never been seen"" — meaning the Mu'awwidhatayn. The wording is that of the hadith of Ya'la. And in the narration of Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd: ""There were revealed to me this night verses the like of which I have never seen — the Mu'awwidhatayn."" Muslim narrated it from various routes from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid. [Sahih. Narrated by Ahmad, 4/194.]"
"4047","(4047) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: Abu al-Husayn Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us: from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih: he narrated to me: al-'Ala' ibn Kathir al-Hadrami narrated to me: from al-Qasim, freedman of Mu'awiyah: from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani, who said: I was driving the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) ahead of him, and he said to me: ""O 'Uqbah, shall I not teach you the two best surahs that have been recited?"" I said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" So he taught me {Qul a'udhu bi-Rabbil-Falaq} and {Qul a'udhu bi-Rabbinnas}. He did not see that I was greatly pleased with them. Then he led the people in the morning prayer and recited them. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) finished the prayer, he turned to me and said: ""O 'Uqbah, how did you find them?"" Thus said al-'Ala' ibn Kathir. And Ibn Wahb narrated from Mu'awiyah, from al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith, and it is more authentic. [Sahih. Ahmad referenced it (4/194).]"
"4048","(4048) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Amr ibn al-Sarh informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Mu'awiyah narrated to me: from al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith: from al-Qasim, freedman of Mu'awiyah: from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, who said: I was driving the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) during a journey, and he said to me: ""O 'Uqbah, shall I not teach you the two best surahs that have been recited?"" So he taught me {Qul a'udhu bi-Rabbil-Falaq} and {Qul a'udhu bi-Rabbinnas}. He did not see that I was greatly pleased with them. When he descended to lead the dawn prayer, he recited them in the dawn prayer for the people. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) finished the prayer, he turned to me and said: ""O 'Uqbah, how did you find them?"" And al-Thawri narrated it from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih similarly. [Sahih. It has been referenced previously.]"
"4049","(4049) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi narrated to us: Abu Usamah narrated to us: from Sufyan al-Thawri: from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih: from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr: from his father: from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir: that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the Mu'awwidhatayn, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) recited them during the Fajr prayer. [Sahih. It has been referenced previously.]"
"4050","(4050) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us: from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi: from his father: from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, who said: While I was traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) between al-Juhfah and al-Abwa', a wind and intense darkness overtook us. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) began seeking refuge with {A'udhu bi-Rabbil-Falaq} and {A'udhu bi-Rabbinnas}, and he said: ""O 'Uqbah, seek refuge with them, for no one has ever sought refuge with the like of them."" He (the narrator) said: And I heard him reciting them while leading us in prayer. [Sahih. It has been referenced previously and in (4046).]"
"4051","(4051) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah narrated to us: from Malik, who said: And Ibrahim ibn 'Ismah ibn Ibrahim informed us: my father narrated to me: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to me: I read to Malik: from Nafi': from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The example of the companion of the Qur'an is like that of a hobbled camel — if he tends to it, he restrains it, and if he releases it, it wanders away."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya, both from Malik. [Sahih. Malik referenced it (243).]"
"4052","(4052) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us: from al-A'mash: from Shaqih, who said: 'Abd Allah said: ""Be committed to these copies of the Qur'an, for they slip from the breasts of men more readily than camels slip from their hobbles."" And none of you should say, ""I have forgotten such-and-such a verse."" Rather, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Nay, he has been made to forget."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr, from his father. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari referenced it (5032).]"
"4053","(4053) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us: Jarir — (H) and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Imran ibn Musa narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Jarir narrated to us: from Mansur: from Abu Wa'il: from 'Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""How terrible it is for one of you to say, 'I have forgotten such-and-such a verse.' Nay, he has been made to forget. So be committed to the Qur'an, for it slips from the breasts of men more readily than camels slip from their hobbles."" Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in the Sahih from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. It has been referenced previously.]"
"4054","(4054) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us: Qatadah narrated to us: I heard Zurarah ibn Awfi narrating from Sa'd ibn Hisham: from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The example of the one who recites the Qur'an while he has memorized it is like the noble, righteous scribes. And the example of the one who recites it while he strives to commit it to memory, and it is difficult for him — he shall have two rewards."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Adam. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari referenced it (4937).]"
"4055","(4055) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Imran ibn Musa, and al-Hasan ibn Sufyan, and Ahmad ibn al-Nadr ibn al-Wahhab narrated to us: they said: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd ibn Hasssan narrated to us: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us: from Qatadah: from Zurarah ibn Awfi: from Sa'd ibn Hisham: from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The one proficient in the Qur'an shall be with the noble, righteous scribes. And the one who recites the Qur'an and stumbles over it, and it is difficult for him — he shall have two rewards."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd and others, and al-Bukhari referenced it from the hadith of Shu'bah from Qatadah. [Sahih. It has been referenced previously.]"
"4056","(4056) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Tusi informed us: Abu Hatim al-Razi informed us: Abu Tawbah narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn Salam ibn Abi Salam al-Habashi narrated to us: from his brother Zayd ibn Salam: he heard Abu Salam say: I heard Abu Umamah al-Bahili (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""Recite the Qur'an, for it shall come on the Day of Resurrection as an intercessor for its companions. Recite al-Baqarah and Al 'Imran, for they are the two radiant ones. They shall come on the Day of Resurrection as if they were two clouds, or two canopies, or two flocks of birds with outspread wings, pleading on behalf of their companion. Recite Surah al-Baqarah, for taking hold of it is a blessing, and abandoning it is a cause of regret, and the sorcerers have no power over it."" Mu'awiyah said: ""Al-batatuh (the sorcerers)."" Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from al-Hasan al-Hulwani, from Abu Tawbah. [Sahih. Muslim referenced it (804).]"
"4057","(4057) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi informed us; Sa'id ibn Mas'ud narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us; Shayban narrated from Yahya, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Abu Salamah — he said: ""And I think I heard it directly from Abu Salamah"" — from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: ""Recite the Qur'an in a month."" I said: ""I find the strength [to do it faster]."" He said: ""Then recite it in twenty nights."" I said: ""I find the strength."" He said: ""Then recite it in fifteen nights."" I said: ""I find the strength."" He said: ""Then recite it in ten nights."" I said: ""I find the strength."" He said: ""Then recite it in seven, and do not go below that."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari, 5054.]"
"4058","(4058) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; 'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Sa'd ibn Hafs al-Dakhm narrated to us; Shayban ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, the mawla of Banu Zahrah. Then he mentioned the hadith with the same wording exactly. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ishaq, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, and from Sa'd ibn Hafs. Muslim narrated it from al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya, from 'Ubayd Allah. [Sahih. Preceded in the one before it.]"
"4059","(4059) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu Mansur al-'Abbas ibn al-Fadl ibn Zakariyya al-Dabbi informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from Abi al-Ahwas, who said: 'Abd Allah — meaning Ibn Mas'ud — said: ""Recite the Qur'an in seven [days], and do not recite it in less than three. And let a man maintain, in his day and night, his portion [of recitation]."" It has been narrated to us from Ibn Mas'ud that he used to complete the Qur'an in Ramadan in three [days], and outside of Ramadan from one Jumu'ah to the next. And from Ubayy ibn Ka'b: that he used to complete the Qur'an in every eight [days]. And from Tamim al-Dari: that he used to complete it in every seven [days]. And from 'Uthman ibn 'Abd Allah — may Allah be pleased with him — that he used to spend the entire night [in worship], reciting the entire Qur'an in a single rak'ah. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tabari in al-Kabir, 9/143/8707.]"
"4060","(4060) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Za'farani narrated to us; Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah narrated from Ayyub, from Abi Hamzah, who said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: ""I am a quick reciter; I recite the Qur'an in three [days]."" He said: ""That I recite al-Baqarah in a single night, pondering over it and reciting it with measured deliberation, is more beloved to me than reciting it as you do."" [Sahih. Narrated by 'Abd al-Razzaq, 2/489/4187.]"
"4061","(4061) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us; al-Za'farani narrated to us; Shababah narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us; Abi Hamzah narrated: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: ""I am a man of quick recitation, and sometimes I recite the Qur'an in a night once or twice."" Ibn 'Abbas said: ""That I recite a single surah is more beloved to me than that I do the like of what you do. If you must do so, then recite it in a manner that your ears hear it and your heart retains it."" [Sahih. Also recorded by the author in al-Shu'ab, 5/175/2091, and preceded in the one before it.]"
"4062","(4062) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ja'far informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad narrated to us; my father narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us. (H) And Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; Abu Ya'la narrated to us; Abu Khaythamah narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Dinari al-Madani narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""They [the imams] pray on your behalf. If they get it right, it is for you and for them; and if they err, it is for you and against them."" The wording of both their narrations is the same, except that Abu Khaythamah did not mention Ibn Dinari and said: ""'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Allah al-Madani."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from al-Fadl ibn Sahl, from al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab. [Sahih. Bukhari, 694.]"
"4063","(4063) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Hammad narrated from Ziyad al-A'lam, from al-Hasan, from Abi Bakrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the Fajr prayer and gestured with his hand: ""Stay in your places."" Then he came back while his head was dripping [with water], and he led them in prayer. He [the narrator] said: And 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated with its chain and its meaning. He said at the beginning: ""He said the Takbir,"" and at the end: ""When he finished the prayer, he said: 'I am only a human being, and I was in a state of janabah.'"" [Sahih. Ahmad, 19907.]"
"4064","(4064) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi and others said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; al-Shafi'i narrated to us; Malik informed us from Isma'il ibn Abi Hakim, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said the Takbir in one of the prayers, then gestured with his hand: ""Stay [in your places]."" Then he returned, and upon his skin were traces of water. [Da'if. Muwatta' Imam Malik, 100.]"
"4065","(4065) And Abu Zakariyya informed us; Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; al-Thiqah narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd, from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Abu Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — from the Prophet (ﷺ), with the same meaning. [This is another chain of narration for the preceding hadith.]"
"4066","(4066) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Ali Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Kilabi in Halab narrated to us; Muhammad — meaning Ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Madh'ur — narrated to us; Waki' narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd, from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Ibn Thawban, from Abu Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to the prayer. When he said the Takbir, he turned back and gestured to them: ""As you are."" Then he went out while his head was dripping, and he led them in prayer. When he finished, he said: ""I was in a state of janabah and I forgot to perform ghusl."" [Sahih in meaning. Al-Amr, 7/18.]"
"4067","(4067) Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Asbahani al-Faqih informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan, known as Abu al-Shaykh, informed us; 'Abdan narrated to us; Abu al-Rabi' al-Harithi narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Harithi narrated to us from Ibn 'Awn, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the takbir with them in the Fajr prayer, then gestured to them, then departed and went out while his head was dripping. He then led them in prayer and said: ""I am only a human being; I was in a state of janabah and forgot.""

Al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Harithi is the sole narrator of this hadith.

(T) It was also narrated by Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah and others from Ibn 'Awn, from Muhammad, from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration. Likewise, Ayyub and Hisham narrated it from Muhammad from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration, and this is the preserved version. All of that serves as corroboration for the hadith of Abu Bakrah.

[Weak. al-Shafi fi al-Amr 278–1621]"
"4068","(4068) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn 'Umar informed us; Yunus narrated from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The prayer was established and the rows were straightened. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to us, and when he stood in his place of prayer, he remembered that he was in a state of janabah. He gestured to us and entered, performed ghusl, then came out while his head was dripping, and led us in prayer.

[Sahih. al-Bukhari 639, Muslim 605]"
"4069","(4069) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Ishaq informed us; al-Hasan, meaning Ibn Mukram, narrated it with the same wording.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Musnadi from 'Uthman ibn 'Umar. Ibn Wahb also narrated it from Yunus and said in the hadith: ""before he pronounced the takbir.""

[Sahih. By reference to the aforementioned]"
"4070","(4070) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed him; Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Tamim ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Yunus informed me from Ibn Shihab, who said: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf informed me; he heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The prayer was established, so we stood and straightened the rows before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came, and when he stood in his place of prayer before he pronounced the takbir, he remembered. He turned back and said to us: ""Stay in your places."" We remained standing, waiting for him, until he came out to us having performed ghusl, his head dripping with water. He pronounced the takbir and led us in prayer.

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Harmalah.

(T) It was narrated with the same meaning by Salih ibn Kaysan from al-Zuhri, and by al-Awza'i from al-Zuhri in a manner similar to the narration of 'Uthman ibn 'Umar.

The narration of Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) is more authentic than the narration of Ibn Thawban from him. However, alongside the narration of Ibn Thawban from him is the musnad narration of Abu Bakrah. The narration of 'Ata' ibn Yasar and Ibn Sirin is mursal. It was also narrated from Anas ibn Malik.

[Sahih. By reference to the aforementioned]"
"4071","(4071) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us; Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus al-Tar'ifi narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) entered the prayer and pronounced the takbir, and we pronounced the takbir with him. Then he gestured to the people to remain as they were. We remained standing until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us having performed ghusl, his head dripping.

(T) 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' contradicted him, narrating it from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Muzani from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration.

[Weak. al-Tabari in al-Awsat 4078]"
"4072","(4072) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb said: and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah ibn 'Ubayd Allah al-Taymi informed you from his uncle 'Isa ibn Talhah, from Mu'i' ibn al-Aswad, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in the Fajr prayer. Then he and I rode to our land. When he sat on a stream of it to perform wudu from it, he found semen on his thigh. He said: ""This semen is on my thigh and I did not feel it."" He scraped it off and then said: ""By Allah, I have reached a point, when I eat fatty food and have advanced in age, that something comes out of me without my awareness."" Muhammad said: ""…without my awareness."" He then performed ghusl and repeated the Fajr prayer, and he did not command anyone to repeat the prayer.

[Weak]"
"4073","(4073) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami and Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; they said: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Azraq narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abu Salamah narrated from Ibn al-Munkadir, from al-Sharid al-Thaqafi: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer while he was in a state of janabah. He repeated the prayer but did not command them to repeat it.

[Hasan. al-Daraqutni 1/364]"
"4074","(4074) Abu Bakr and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman informed us; they said: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Azraq narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us. And 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Mubashshir narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from Khalid ibn Salamah, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar: that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) led the people in prayer while he was in a state of janabah. When morning came, he looked at his garment and found semen on it. He said: ""I have grown old, by Allah! I consider myself to be in a state of janabah and then I do not realize it."" He then repeated the prayer but did not command them to repeat it.

'Abd al-Rahman said: I asked Sufyan about it and he said: ""I heard it from Khalid ibn Salamah, but I do not narrate it as I would like."" 'Abd al-Rahman said: ""And this is the matter upon which there is consensus: the one in a state of janabah repeats the prayer, and the followers do not repeat it. I know of no difference of opinion regarding it.""

'Ali said: Abu 'Ubayd said in his narration: ""I heard it from Khalid ibn Salamah, but I do not memorize it,"" and he added nothing beyond this.

[Weak. al-Daraqutni 1/364/12]"
"4075","(4075) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari in Baghdad informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar narrated from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar: that he led them in prayer while not in a state of wudu. He repeated the prayer but did not command them to repeat it. A musnad hadith has been narrated on this matter.

[Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq in al-Amali 1/181/143]"
"4076","(4076) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu 'Utbah Ahmad ibn al-Faraj al-Hijazi narrated to us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us; 'Isa ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated from Jubayr ibn Sa'id, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed while not in a state of wudu. The prayer was completed for the people, and the Prophet (ﷺ) repeated it. This is not strong, and what has preceded is sufficient.

[Weak. al-Daraqutni 1191]"
"4077","(4077) And that which is narrated in its chain from Abū Jābir al-Bayāḍī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) led the people in prayer while he was in a state of janābah (major ritual impurity), so he repeated the prayer and they repeated it as well.

This was narrated to us by Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us — Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAṭāʾ al-Jallāb narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us — Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Abū Jābir al-Bayāḍī, and this is a mursal (disconnected) hadith. And Abū Jābir al-Bayāḍī is matrūk al-ḥadīth (abandoned in hadith); Mālik ibn Anas did not approve of him, and Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn used to say: ""Abū Jābir al-Bayāḍī is a liar."" And this hadith is: [Weak]."
"4078","(4078) It was narrated to us by Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us — Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us — Abū Ḥafṣ al-Abbār narrated from ʿAmr ibn Khālid from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): that he led the people in prayer while he was in a state of janābah, so he repeated it, and then he commanded them and they repeated it.

This is only narrated by ʿAmr ibn Khālid, Abū Khālid al-Wāsiṭī, and he is matrūk (abandoned); the ḥuffāẓ (hadith critics) accused him of lying. [Fabricated (Mawḍūʿ)]."
"4079","(4079) And it was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmmār al-Mawṣilī narrated to us — al-Qāsim narrated from Isrāʾīl from ʿAmr ibn Khālid, the mawlā of ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib. Muḥammad ibn ʿAmmār said: ""I asked Wakīʿ about him and he said: 'He was a liar.' When we came to know him as a liar, he moved to another place."" He narrated from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): that he led them in prayer while he was not in a state of purity, so he repeated it, and he commanded them to repeat it. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic)]."
"4080","(4080) And it was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Sufyān al-Thawrī — I heard him say: ""Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit never narrated anything at all from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah."""
"4081","(4081) And it was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Jarrāḥī in Marw narrated to me — Yaḥyā ibn Sāsawayh narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Sukkarī narrated to us — Wahb ibn Zamʿah narrated to us — Sufyān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated, he said: ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn al-Mubārak, said: ""There is no strength in the hadith for the one who says that when the imām leads the prayer without wuḍūʾ (ablution), his companions should repeat it. And the other hadith is more established, that the people should not repeat it. This is for the one who wishes to be fair regarding the hadith."" [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic)]."
"4082","(4082) It was narrated to us by Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah — Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Quhistānī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us — ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Sufyān and Shuʿbah from Mughīrah from Ibrāhīm regarding a man who leads a people in prayer while not in a state of wuḍūʾ: he said: ""He should repeat it, and they should not repeat it."" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I said to Sufyān: ""Do you know of anyone who said that he should repeat it and they should repeat it other than Ḥammād?"" He said: ""No."" [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic)]."
"4083","(4083) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Wakīʿ narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah — he said: Fāṭimah narrated to me from Asmāʾ — she said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""One of us gets menstrual blood on her garment, so what should she do with it?"" He said: ""She should scrape it off, then rub it with water, then sprinkle water over it, and then pray in it.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Muwaṭṭaʾ of Imam Mālik 166]."
"4084","(4084) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh al-Shīrāzī — they both said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us — Muḥāḍir ibn al-Muwariʿ narrated to us — Hishām, meaning Ibn ʿUrwah, narrated from his father from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) — she said: Fāṭimah bint Abī Ḥubaysh came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""I am a woman experiencing istiḥāḍah (irregular bleeding) and I never become pure, so should I abandon the prayer?"" He said: ""No, that is only a vein and not menstruation. So when the menstrual period begins, abandon the prayer, and when it ends, wash the blood from yourself and pray.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah in their Ṣaḥīḥ collections. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). al-Bukhārī 226, Muslim 333]."
"4085","(4085) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī in Ṭūs and Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ in Naysābūr — they both said: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us — Abū Ghassān narrated to us — Abū Ḥāzim narrated from Sahl ibn Saʿd — he said: The helmet was shattered upon the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), his front tooth was broken, and his face was wounded.

Abū Ḥāzim said: Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), used to wash the blood from him, and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) would bring her water in a shield. When the water touched the wound, his blood increased and would not stop. So she took a piece of mat, burned it until it became ashes, then placed it upon the wound, and the blood stopped.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sahl ibn ʿAskar from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through various chains from Abū Ḥāzim. [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). al-Bukhārī 2754, Muslim 1790]."
"4086","(4086) It was narrated to us by Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us — Abū Naʿāmah al-Saʿdī narrated from Abū Naḍrah from Abū Saʿīd: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed in his sandals, so the people prayed in their sandals. Then he removed his sandals, so the people removed their sandals while they were in the prayer. When he completed his prayer, he said: ""What compelled you to throw off your sandals during the prayer?"" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we saw you do it, so we did it."" He said: ""Indeed, Jibrīl (peace be upon him) informed me that there is filth in them. So when one of you comes to the mosque, let him look; if he sees filth in his sandals, he should remove them, otherwise let him pray in them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ (Authentic). Aḥmad 11467, Ibn Ḥibbān 2185]."
"4087","(4087) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us that Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us, from Abū Naʿāmah, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed and removed his sandals, so the people removed their sandals. When he finished, he said: ""Why did you remove your sandals?"" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we saw you remove yours, so we removed ours."" He said: ""Indeed, Jibrīl came to me and informed me that there was some impurity on them. When one of you comes to the mosque, let him turn over his sandals and look at them for any impurity. If he finds any impurity, let him wipe them with the earth, then let him pray in them.""

This hadith is known through Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Abū Naʿāmah ʿAbd Rabbih al-Saʿdī, from Abū Naḍrah.

(T) It has also been narrated from al-Ḥajjāj ibn al-Ḥajjāj, from Abū ʿĀmir al-Khazzāz, from Abū Naʿāmah, but it is not strong. It has also been narrated through another route that is not preserved, from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, from Abū Naḍrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reference as above]"
"4088","(4088) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿUthmān ibn Shāfiʿ al-Shāfiʿī in Makkah narrated to us that my uncle narrated to us that Dāwūd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, from Abū ʿUrwah Maʿmir ibn Rāshid, from Ayyūb, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in his sandals, then removed them. He was asked about it, and he said: ""Indeed, Jibrīl came to me and informed me that there was some impurity on them. When you come to the mosque, look at your sandals. If anyone of you finds anything on them, let him scrape it off.""

(T) Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī narrated it from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, from Maʿmir, from Ayyūb, from a man who narrated to him, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, and he said: ""filth"" (qadaran) instead of ""impurity"" (khabath). [Munkar al-isnād. Reference as above]"
"4089","(4089) Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Khamb informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us that Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us that Muḥammad ibn Muṭarrif informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: I saw Ibn ʿUmar praying in his cloak while there was blood on it. Nāfiʿ came and removed his cloak from him, cast his own cloak over him, and he continued in his prayer. [Ḥasan]"
"4090","(4090) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmir informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, that Ibn ʿUmar, while he was praying, saw blood on his garment. He finished his prayer and gestured to them. They brought water and he washed it, then he completed what remained of his prayer based on what had already passed, and he did not repeat it.

(Q) The Sheikh said: This was the position of al-Shāfiʿī in the earlier view (al-qadīm), may Allah have mercy on him, and he cited the hadith of Abū Saʿīd and Ibn ʿUmar in the meaning of what we have narrated. Then he retracted from that in the later view (al-jadīd) and said: He should repeat the prayer, whether he was aware of what was on his garment or not, just as in the case of wuḍūʾ.

The Sheikh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: This was also the view of al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and Abū Qilābah. It is as though al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, was disinclined toward the hadith of Abū Saʿīd due to its renown through Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Abū Naʿāmah al-Saʿdī, from Abū Naḍrah, and each one of them is differed regarding in his reliability. For this reason, al-Bukhārī did not cite either of them in al-Ṣaḥīḥ, and Muslim did not include it in his book despite citing them in narrations other than this one. It is possible that he was disinclined toward it because he considered Jibrīl's informing him, peace be upon him, of that to be the initiation of a new ruling, or he understood the harm mentioned therein to refer to what is considered repulsive among impurities. And Allah knows best.

(T) This hadith has also been narrated from Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī from the Prophet ﷺ as a mursal narration, and from the hadith of Ibn Masʿūd, Ibn ʿAbbās, and Abū Hurayrah as mawṣūl narrations. However, the hadith of Ibn Masʿūd was narrated only by Abū Ḥamzah al-Rāʿī, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, from ʿAbd Allāh, and Abū Ḥamzah is not relied upon as evidence. It has also been narrated through another route that is even weaker than it.

The hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās was narrated only by Furāt ibn al-Sāʾib, from Maymūn ibn Mihrān, from Ibn ʿAbbās, and Furāt ibn al-Sāʾib was abandoned (by the hadith scholars). The hadith of Abū Hurayrah was narrated only by ʿAbbād ibn Kathīr, and ʿAbbād is not relied upon as evidence, except that it has also been narrated from Anas ibn Mālik through a chain that is not bad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. (Al-Bukhārī, muʿallaq) Chapter: The one who does not consider wuḍūʾ obligatory except from the two passages, front and back]"
"4091","(4091) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānʾ and Ibrāhīm ibn ʿIṣmah both said that al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us.

(H) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah both informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us that Muṭayyin narrated to us that Ibrāhīj ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us — both saying that ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, from Thumāmah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not remove his sandals during prayer except on one occasion. The people removed theirs, so he said: ""What is the matter with you?"" They said: ""You removed yours, so we removed ours."" He said: ""Indeed, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, informed me that there was filth on them."" The wording is that of Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj. ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Muthannā is alone in narrating it, and Allah knows best.

(Q) As for what Ibn ʿUmar used to do regarding building upon the prayer in this case and in the case of nosebleed (ruʿāf), we have narrated from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah that he used to say: He should start the prayer anew (yastaʾnif). And this is the analogy (qiyās) applied to wuḍūʾ in this matter. And through Allah is success. [Ḥasan. Al-Ḥākim 1/235/486]"
"4092","(4092) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Fāris narrated to us that Abū Maʿmar narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us that Umm Yūnus bint Shaddād narrated to us. She said: My maternal aunt Umm Jaḥdir al-ʿĀmiriyyah narrated to me that she asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased her, about menstrual blood that gets on a garment. She said: I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and we had our undergarments on, over which we had thrown a cloak. When morning came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the cloak and wore it, then went out and prayed the morning prayer (al-ghadāh). Then he sat, and a man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is a spot of blood."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ grasped the area near it and sent it to me folded up in the hand of a boy, saying: ""Wash this and dry it, then send it back to me."" I called for my bowl and washed it, then dried it and returned it to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came at midday while he was wearing it. (Gh) Her statement ""fa aḥartuhā ilayh"" means: I returned it to him. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 388]"
"4093","(4093) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Naẓīf al-Miṣrī in Makkah informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Isḥāq al-Rāzī informed us orally that Rūḥ ibn al-Faraj narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn ʿAdī narrated to us that al-Qāsim ibn Mālik informed us, from Rūḥ ibn Ghutayf, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, raising it (to the Prophet ﷺ), who said: ""The prayer is to be repeated due to an amount of blood the size of a dirham."" [Mawḍūʿ. Al-ʿUqaylī in al-Ḍuʿafāʾ 2/56/491]"
"4094","(4094) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Jarrāḥī in Marw narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Sāsawayh narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Sukkarī narrated to us that Wahb ibn Zamʿah informed us that Sufyān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us. He said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak said: I saw Rūḥ ibn Ghutayf, the narrator of ""the blood the size of a dirham"" from the Prophet ﷺ, so I sat with him on one occasion, and I felt shy before my companions that they should see me sitting with him, due to the abundance of munkar narrations in his hadith."
"4095","(4095) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us that Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Muḥammad ibn Munīr narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us. He said: I said to Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn: ""Do you preserve from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: 'The prayer is to be repeated due to an amount of blood the size of a dirham'?"" He said: ""No, by Allah."" Then he said: ""From whom?"" I said: ""Muḥriz ibn ʿAwn narrated to us."" He said: ""Thiqah (trustworthy). From whom?"" I said: ""From al-Qāsim ibn Mālik al-Muzanī."" He said: ""Thiqah (trustworthy). From whom?"" I said: ""From Rūḥ ibn Ghutayf."" He said: ""Ah."" I said: ""O Abū Zakariyyā, I do not think we have been afflicted except through Rūḥ ibn Ghutayf."" He said: ""Indeed.""

Abū Aḥmad said: This hadith is not narrated from al-Zuhrī, to my knowledge, by anyone other than Rūḥ ibn Ghutayf, and it is munkar with this chain. And from what has reached me from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhalī, he said: ""I fear that this may be fabricated (mawḍūʿ)."" And this Rūḥ is unknown (majhūl). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil 3/138/660]"
"4096","(4096) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥayyān, known as Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī, narrated to us that Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us that Abū ʿĀmir Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us that al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed us that Baqiyyah informed me, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave concession regarding the blood of swellings (dam al-ḥubūn), that is, boils (damāmīl). And ʿAṭāʾ used to pray while blood was on his garment.

A group narrated it from al-Walīd ibn Muslim in this manner. Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd is alone in narrating it from Ibn Jurayj.

(J) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us and said: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ said: This hadith is not known except through Baqiyyah, from Ibn Jurayj. He said: It is likely that there is some unknown person or some weak narrator between Baqiyyah and Ibn Jurayj, because Baqiyyah frequently did such things. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil 2/65]"
"4097","(4097) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Kathīr al-ʿAbdī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Ḥasan, meaning Ibn Muslim, narrate it from Mujāhid, who said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""Each of us had only one garment in which she would menstruate. If any blood from her menstruation got on it, she would moisten it with her saliva and then rub it with her fingernail."""
"4098","(4098) It was narrated to him by Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith, who was informed by Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, who narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad narrated to us that Abū Zurʿah narrated to us that Abū Nuʿaym narrated it, with the same isnād and text of al-Bukhārī, except that he said: ""Then she would rub it.""

The well-known narration from Ibrāhīm, from al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim ibn Yannāq, from Mujāhid, and from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, has also been narrated. And Khallād ibn Yaḥyā narrated it from Ibrāhīm just as Abū Nuʿaym narrated it, so it is authentic from both routes together."
"4099","(4099) It was narrated to him by Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah, who informed us that Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Baghdādī in Harāt narrated to us that Muʿādh ibn Najdah narrated to us that Khallād narrated to us that Ibrāhīm narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, who said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""Each of us had only one garment in which she would menstruate. If any of her blood got on it, she would moisten it with her saliva and then rub it with her fingernail.""

And in the ḥadīth of ʿAṭāʾ from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, it says: ""A drop of blood."" This has already been mentioned in the book of Purification."
"4100","(4100) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us that ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us that Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated from ʿAmmār ibn Abī ʿAmmār, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he said: ""If the blood is excessive, then one must repeat the prayer. But if it is little, then there is no need to repeat it.""

And we have narrated from Ibn Masʿūd and Ibn ʿUmar regarding leniency concerning a small amount of blood, and their narrations have already been mentioned in the book of Purification. It has also been narrated from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī."
"4101","(4101) And Abū Aḥmad al-ʿAdl informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, who said: ""My father saw me leaving the prayer, so he said: 'Why did you leave?' I said to him: 'I saw the blood of a fly on my garment.' He disapproved of that and said: 'Why did you not complete your prayer?'""

In the narration of al-Thawrī from Hishām, it says: ""Blood like that of a fly.""

And al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī used to say: ""Whether it is little or much, it is the same."" The view of the rest of the jurists differs from his regarding the distinction between excessive blood and a small amount. ʿAṭāʾ, al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī, al-Shaʿbī, and Ṭāwūs gave concession regarding the blood of fleas."
"4102","(4102) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us that Ibn Wahb narrated to us. And Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us that Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Mālik ibn Anas narrated to you from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyay ibn ʿUmarah, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from the mother of a child of Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, that she asked Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, saying: ""I am a woman who lengthens her garment and walks in filthy places."" Umm Salamah said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'What comes after it purifies it.'""

That has also been narrated from Abū Hurayrah in a marfūʿ narration, but it is not strong."
"4103","(4103) Abū Saʿid al-Mālinī informed us that Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that al-Haytham ibn Khālid al-Dūrī narrated to us that Abū Kurayb narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl al-Yashkurī narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ḥabībah, from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from Abī Sufyān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we wish to go to the mosque, but we tread upon impure paths.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'The paths purify one another.'"" This isnād is not strong."
"4104","(4104) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Murādī narrated to us that Ayyūb ibn Suwayd narrated to us that Yūnus narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAwf, that he sought a fatwā from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding a garment in which a man has intercourse. Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""If something gets on it and you see it, but then it becomes unclear to you, wash the entire garment. But if you are uncertain about something you are not certain of, then sprinkle the garment and pray in it.""

And we have narrated from Nāfiʿ that he said: ""Ibn ʿUmar used to say: 'If you know its location, wash it. Otherwise, wash the entire garment.'"""
"4105","(4105) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, who said: Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir narrated to me from Asmāʾ, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""One of us menstruates in her garment—what should she do?"" He said: ""Scrape it, then rub it with water, then sprinkle it, then pray in it.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd."
"4106","(4106) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us that Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us that al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated to us that Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us that he heard Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir narrate from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, who said: ""A woman asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about menstrual blood that gets on a garment."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Scrape it, then rub it with water, then sprinkle it, then pray in it."""
"4107","(4107) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Ziyad narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Musa narrated to us; 'Abdah narrated from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir from Asma' bint Abi Bakr, who said: I heard a woman asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""What should she do with her garment when she becomes pure from her menses?"" He said: ""If she sees blood in it, she should scrape it off, then rub it with water, then sprinkle water over the rest of her garment, and then pray in it.""

[Sahih. Reference as above]"
"4108","(4108) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Isma'il ibn Ahmad al-Jurjani narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-'Asqalani narrated to us; Harmalah informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us; 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to him from his father from 'A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""One of us would menstruate, then at the time of her purity she would rub the blood from her garment, wash it, sprinkle water over the rest of it, and then pray in it.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Asbagh from Ibn Wahb. [Sahih. Bukhari 308]"
"4109","(4109) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Harun ibn Sulayman narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated from Bakkar ibn Yahya from his grandmother, who said: ""I visited Umm Salamah, and a woman from Quraysh asked her."" Umm Salamah said: ""We used to experience menstruation during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One of us would remain for the days of her menses, then become pure and look at the garment in which she had been sleeping. If blood had gotten on it, we would wash it and pray in it. If nothing had gotten on it, we would leave it, and that would not prevent us from praying in it. As for the woman who used to comb her hair (with henna), one of us would be so adorned, and when she performed the major ritual bath (ghusl), she would not undo it. Rather, she would scoop three handfuls of water over her head. When she felt moisture at the roots of her hair, she would rub it in, then pour water over the rest of her body."" [Abu Dawud 359]"
"4110","(4110) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yahya narrated from Sufyan; Thabit al-Haddad narrated to me; 'Adi ibn Dinar narrated to me. He said: I heard Umm Qays bint Mihsan say: ""I asked the Prophet ﷺ about menstrual blood that gets on a garment."" He said: ""Scrape it with a rib-bone, and wash it with water and lotus leaves.""

[Sahih. Ahmad 6/355–356]"
"4111","(4111) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Sulayman ibn Suhaym narrated to me from Umayyah bint Abi al-Salt. The Sheikh said: ""Thus it is in my book,"" but others said: ""Aminah bint Abi al-Salt,"" and that is correct. She narrated on the authority of a woman from Banu Ghifar, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among a group of women from Banu Ghifar and we said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we wish to go out with you on this expedition of yours to Khaybar, to treat the wounded and assist the Muslims as best we can.'"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Go, by the blessing of Allah."" So we went out with him. I was a young girl, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made me ride behind him on the saddlebag of his mount. When dawn came and he dismounted, I dismounted, and behold, there was blood on the saddlebag from me. That was the first menses I had ever experienced. I drew close to the she-camel and was overcome with shyness. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw my condition and saw the blood on me, he said: 'Perhaps you have entered your postpartum period?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then set yourself right, take a vessel of water and throw salt into it. Wash what has gotten on the saddlebag, perform the major ritual bath, and then return to your mount.'"" She said: ""Whenever she became pure from her menses, she would include salt in her purification water, and she bequeathed that salt be placed in her washing (ghusl) when she died."" [Da'if. Ahmad 6/380]"
"4112","(4112) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razi narrated to us; Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated to me; Muhammad, meaning Ibn Ishaq, narrated to me from Sulayman ibn Suhaym from Umayyah bint Abi al-Salt from a woman of Banu Ghifar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made me ride behind him."" He mentioned the meaning of the previous hadith, except that he did not mention his words: ""and perform the major ritual bath (wa-ghtasili)."""
"4113","(4113) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir and Bishr ibn 'Umar both said: Shu'bah narrated from Yazid al-Rishk from Mu'adhah, who said: ""I asked 'A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) about the menstruating woman whose garment is stained with blood."" She said: ""She should wash it. If the trace of it does not disappear, she should change it with something yellow (i.e., saffron)."" She also said: ""I menstruated in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ three times altogether, and I did not wash a single garment for myself."" [Da'if. Ahmad 6/250]"
"4114","(4114) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir and Bishr ibn 'Umar both said: Shu'bah narrated from Yazid al-Rishk from Mu'adhah, who said: ""I asked 'A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) about blood that gets on a garment."" And Bishr said in his hadith: ""I said: 'What do you think of a garment that is stained with blood? I wash it, yet the trace of it does not disappear.'"" She said: ""Water is purifying (al-ma' tahur)."" [Sahih. al-Darimi 1012]"
"4115","(4115) Abu al-Hasan al-Muqri' informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us; Sa'id, meaning Ibn Abi 'Arubah, narrated from Qatadah from Mu'adhah: ""A woman asked 'A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) about menstrual blood that gets on a garment and is washed, yet its trace remains."" She said: ""It is of no consequence."" It has also been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ through two weak chains of narration. [Da'if]"
"4116","(4116) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us.

(Continuing:) And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: ""Ibn Lahi'ah informed you from Ibn Abi Habib from 'Isa ibn Talhah from Abu Hurayrah: Khawlah bint Yasar said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'What do you think if the blood does not come out of the garment?' He said: 'Water is sufficient for you, and its trace will not harm you.'""

This was narrated uniquely by Ibn Lahi'ah. [Da'if]"
"4117","(4117) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baghdādī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Ibn Lahīʿah narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to me that ʿĪsā ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to them from Abū Hurayrah that Khawlah bint Yasār came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""I have only one garment, and I menstruate in it — what should I do?"" He said: ""When you become pure, wash your garment, then pray in it."" She said: ""What if the blood does not come out of the garment?"" He said: ""Water is sufficient for you, and its trace will not harm you.""

Ibn Lahīʿah is the sole narrator of it. [Weak]"
"4118","(4118) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī narrated to us; Mahdī ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Thābit narrated from al-Wāziʿ ibn Nāfiʿ from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Khawlah bint Nimār, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I menstruate and I have only one garment, and the blood gets on it.""

He said: ""Wash it and pray in it."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, its trace remains."" He said: ""It will not harm.""

Abū Bakr said: Ibrāhīm al-Ḥarbī said: ""Al-Wāziʿ ibn Nāfiʿ — other narrators are more reliable than him. And Khawlah bint Nimār or Yasār has not been known except through these two hadiths."" [Weak. Al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr 615]"
"4119","(4119) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim narrated from Wakīʿ. Isḥāq said: Wakīʿ informed us; Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray at night while I was beside him and I was menstruating, and I had a wrap (mirṭ) over me, part of which was upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Wakīʿ. [Authentic. Muslim 514]"
"4120","(4120) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Shaybānī, who heard it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaddād, who narrated it from Maymūnah: ""The Prophet ﷺ prayed while wearing a wrap (mirṭ), part of which was upon one of his wives while she was menstruating, and he was praying while wearing it.""

[Authentic. Abū Dāwūd 369, Ibn Mājah 619]"
"4121","(4121) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Yaʿqūb al-Iyādī in Baghdad both said: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Ibn Abī Ghanīyah ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Ḥumayd ibn Abī Ghanīyah narrated from Thābit ibn ʿUbayd al-Anṣārī from al-Qāsim from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed me to hand him the prayer mat (khumrah). I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I am menstruating.' He said: 'Your menstruation is not in your hand.'""

Muslim extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ibn Abī Ghanīyah. [Authentic. Muslim 298]"
"4122","(4122) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim from his father from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: ""She did not see any harm in the sweat of a menstruating woman on a garment."" [Weak]"
"4123","(4123) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬāʾ informed us; Hishām — Ibn Ḥassān — narrated from ʿIkrimah: ""Ibn ʿAbbās was asked about a woman who menstruates in her shift (dirʿ) and sweats in it during the days of her menstruation — may she pray in it?"" He said: ""Yes, so long as there is no blood in it. Likewise, the person in a state of janābah who sweats in his garment may pray in it."" [Good]"
"4124","(4124) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥumayd, the neighbour of Abū Salamah, narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar Ghundar narrated from al-Ashʿath.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Manṣūrī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Bakhtarī narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; my father narrated to us; al-Ashʿath narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaqīq from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not pray in our shuʿur or our liḥuf."" ʿUbayd Allāh said: ""My father was uncertain."" And in the hadith of Ghundar: ""In our liḥuf"" — without doubt. [Denounced (Munkar)]"
"4125","(4125) Salamah ibn ʿAlqamah narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, without mentioning Ibn Shaqīq. She said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not pray in our shuʿur.""

Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; Abū Bakr ibn Bālawayh narrated to us; Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us; Muʿallā ibn Asad narrated to us; Wuhayb narrated from Salamah ibn ʿAlqamah with that.

(T) And likewise Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated it from Ibn Sīrīn, without mentioning Ibn Shaqīq in his chain, except that he said: ""In our milāḥif."" [Weak]"
"4126","(4126) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated from Hishām from Ibn Sīrīn from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: ""The Prophet ﷺ would not pray in our milāḥif."" Ḥammād said: ""And I heard Saʿīd ibn Abī Ṣadaqah say: 'I asked Muḥammad about it, and he did not narrate it to me. He said: ""I heard it a long time ago, and I do not know from whom I heard it, and I do not know whether I heard it from Thābat or not, so ask about it.""'"" [Weak — as previously noted]"
"4127","(4127) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī both reported to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us. And Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Al-Layth ibn Saʿd, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahiʿah, and ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed you from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Suwayd ibn Qays, from Muʿāwiya ibn Ḥudayj, who said: I heard Muʿāwiya ibn Abī Sufyān (may Allah be pleased with him) — and Baḥr said: from Muʿāwiya ibn Abī Sufyān — saying: I asked Umm Ḥabībah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, ""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray in the garment in which he had intercourse with her?"" She said: ""Yes, so long as he did not see any impurity in it."" Reports concerning the purity of his sweat have already been mentioned in the Book of Purification. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 26858–26220, Ibn Ḥibbān 2331]"
"4128","(4128) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Ismāʿīl informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd ibn al-Sabbāq narrated to us from his father, from Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, who said: ""I used to suffer severely from madhī (pre-seminal fluid), and I would frequently perform ghusl because of it. So I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that, and he said: 'That (wudu) is sufficient for you regarding that.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what about what gets on my garment from it?' He said: 'It is sufficient for you to take a handful of water and sprinkle it on your garment where you believe it has reached.'"" The Sheikh said: What is meant by ""sprinkling"" (naḍḥ) mentioned in this report is washing it, and Allah knows best. It is authentically established from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he commanded its washing from the body. [Ḥasan. Tirmidhī 115, Ibn Ḥibbān 1103]"
"4129","(4129) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: Abū al-Walīd narrated us: Zāʾidah narrated to us from Abū Ḥuṣayn, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I was a man who suffered much from madhī, and the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with me (in marriage), so I felt too shy to ask him about it. So I instructed a man to ask him, and he said: 'When you find that, wash your private part and perform wudu.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 132–178–269]"
"4130","(4130) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) instructed al-Miqdād to ask the Prophet ﷺ about madhī, saying: ""I feel too shy to ask him."" So he asked him, and he said: ""Let him wash his private part and his testicles, and let him perform wudu as he would for prayer."" Al-Thawrī, Ibn ʿUyaynah, and a group narrated it from Hishāh, from his father, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ. And we have narrations concerning that from ʿUmar, Ibn ʿUmar, and Ibn ʿAbbās from their own statements. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than the above chain]"
"4131","(4131) Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥarafī narrated to us in the Jāmiʿ of al-Ḥarbiyyah in Madīnat al-Salām: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me: Muʿāwiya ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith, from Ḥarām ibn Ḥakīm, from his paternal uncle ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about what necessitates ghusl, about the fluid that comes after the (seminal) fluid, about praying in my house, about praying in the mosque, and about eating with a menstruating woman. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, Allah is not shy of the truth."" And ʿĀʾishah was beside him. He continued: ""As for me, when intercourse occurs from me, I come and perform wushu, then I perform ghusl. As for the fluid that comes after the fluid, that is madhī, and every man emits madhī. So wash your private part and your testicles from that, and perform wudu as you would for prayer. As for prayer in the mosque and prayer in my house, you see how close my house is to the mosque — I prefer to pray in my house rather than in the mosque, unless it is an obligatory prayer. As for eating with a menstruating woman, then eat with her."" [Ḥasan. Fawāʾid Ibn … 1/120/4]"
"4132","(4132) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Ḥurayḥ, the judge of ʿUkbarā, narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us. And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Hannād narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us from Hishām, from his father, from Abū Ayyūb, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who has intercourse with his woman and then becomes lethargic (i.e., does not ejaculate). He said: ""Let him wash what has reached him from the woman, then let him perform wudu and pray."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. Shuʿbah narrated it from Hishām, and he said: ""Let him wash his private part and perform wudu."" The Sheikh said: What has been abrogated from it is the omission of ghusl; as for washing what has reached him from the woman, we know of nothing that abrogated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 293, Muslim 346]"
"4133","(4133) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Sharīk narrated to us from Qays ibn Wahb, from a man from Banī Sawāʾah ibn ʿĀmir, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), regarding what flows from a man and a woman of the fluid. She said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would take a handful of water and pour it over it."" [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 24675]"
"4134","(4134) Abū ʿAlī — al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Shādhān informed us in Baghdad: Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dawrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muṣʿab narrated to us: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""It is befitting for a woman, when she is of sound mind, to keep a cloth ready. When her husband has intercourse with her, she should hand it to him so that he may wipe himself with it, and then she should wipe herself with it. Then they may both pray in their garments, so long as janābah (major ritual impurity) has not occurred."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 280]"
"4135","(4135) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from al-Qāsim, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she was asked about a garment in which a man has intercourse with his family — may he pray in it? She said: ""The woman should prepare a cloth for her husband, so that he may wipe the impurity with it so that it does not reach the garment. If he does that, then let him pray in it."" The Sheikh said: Whoever holds the other opinion cites as evidence the ḥadīth of Abū Dharr regarding the tayammum of the junub, and its mention has already passed in the Book of Purification. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4136","(4136) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. I said to Mālik ibn Anas: ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated to you from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqī, from Abū Qatādah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray while carrying Umāmah, the daughter of Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the daughter of Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbd Shams. When he stood, he would carry her, and when he prostrated, he would set her down. Yaḥyā said: ""Yes."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 516]"
"4137","(4137) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah and Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us, they both said — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from al-A'mash, from Muslim, from Masruq, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, who said: I was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey when he said, ""O Mughirah, take the vessel."" I took it and then went out with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ proceeded until he was hidden from me, fulfilled his need. Upon him was a Syrian jubbah with narrow sleeves. He attempted to extract his hand from its sleeve, but it was too tight, so he brought his hand out from below it. I poured water over it, and he performed wudu' for the prayer, then wiped over his khuffs and prayed.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya, from Abu Mu'awiyah; and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah."
"4138","(4138) Abu al-Fath Hilal ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Haffar in Baghdad informed us — al-Husayn ibn Yahya ibn 'Ayyash al-Qattan narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Mujashshir narrated to us — Waki' narrated to us — al-Rabi' narrated from al-Hasan, who said: There is no harm in praying in the garments of the Jews and the Christians."
"4139","(4139) Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Tahir al-Daqqaq in Baghdad narrated to us — Abu al-Husayn Ahmad ibn 'Uthman al-Adami dictated to us — Abu Qilabah narrated to us — Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Prophet ﷺ passed by two graves and said, ""Verily, they are being punished — for namimah (backbiting) and for urine."" He took a fresh palm frond and split it into two, placing one upon each grave, and said, ""Perhaps their punishment will be lightened as long as these two remain fresh.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ahmad ibn Yusuf, from Mu'alla ibn Asad; and al-Bukhari also cited it as previously mentioned in the Book of Purification."
"4140","(4140) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us — they both said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-'Ataridi narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by two graves and said, ""Verily, they are being punished, and they are not being punished for a major sin. As for one of them, he used to walk about with namimah (backbiting), and as for the other, he used not to protect himself from urine."" Then he took a fresh palm frond and split it in two, placing one upon each grave. He said — they said, ""O Messenger of Allah, why did you do this?"" He said, ""Perhaps their punishment will be lightened so long as they have not dried.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abu Mu'awiyah."
"4141","(4141) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi narrated to us — Abu Qilabah narrated to us — Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us — Abu 'Awanah narrated from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayradh Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""The most frequent cause of the punishment of the grave is urine.""

And Abu Yahya narrated it from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet ﷺ, and added therein: ""So guard yourselves against urine."""
"4142","(4142) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Ka'bi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us — Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us — 'Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us — 'Ikrimah narrated to us — Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to me — Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, who said: While we were in the mosque with the Prophet ﷺ, a Bedouin came and began to urinate in the mosque. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Stop! Stop!"" He said, ""Leave him."" So they left him until he had urinated. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called him and said to him, ""Verily, these mosques are not suited for any of this — neither urine nor filth. They are only for the remembrance of Allah, the Most High, and for prayer, and for the recitation of the Qur'an"" — or as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. Then he ordered a man of the people, who came with a bucket of water, and he poured it over the place.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb, from 'Umar ibn Yunus."
"4143","(4143) Abu al-Qasim Zayd ibn Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Husayni in Kufah, from the original of his hearing, informed us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duhaym narrated to us — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Hunayn narrated to us — Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us — 'Ikrimah, that is, ibn 'Ammar, narrated — Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah narrated from Anas ibn Malik, who said: A Bedouin came to the mosque and urinated in it. The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ said, ""Stop! Stop!"" The Prophet ﷺ said, ""Do not cut him off."" When he had finished, the Prophet ﷺ called him and said, ""Verily, these mosques were not built for this — for defecation, urine, and filth. They were only built for the recitation of the Qur'an and for the remembrance of Allah, the Most High."" Then he ordered one of his Companions with a bucket or pail of water, and he poured it over the place."
"4144","(4144) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us — the Imam Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Isma'ili informed me — Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Sharik al-Asadi informed me — Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us — Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us — Abu Ishaq said: It was not Abu 'Ubaydah who mentioned it, but rather 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad from his father, that he heard 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud saying: The Prophet ﷺ came to a place of relieve and commanded me to bring him three stones. I found two stones and searched for the third but could not find it, so I took a piece of dung and brought them to the Prophet ﷺ. He took the two stones and cast away the dung, saying, ""This is riks (impure).""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Nu'aym, from Zuhayr."
"4145","(4145) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Zurarah informed us — Isma'il informed us — 'Umarah ibn Abi Hafsah narrated from Abu Mijlaz, who said: I said to Ibn 'Umar, ""A man of us sends his she-camel and is splashed by its urine."" He said, ""Wash whatever has afflicted you of it."""
"4146","(4146) Abu 'Abd Allah informed us — Abu Bakr narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sahl narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us — Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated — Shu'bah narrated — Yunus narrated — al-Hasan said: Every animal — its urine is to be washed.

As for the hadith of Anas regarding the story of the 'Urani people, the Prophet ﷺ commanded them to stay among the camels and drink their milk and urine. Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: This was on the basis of necessity, just as the eating of carrion is permitted on the basis of necessity, and the ruling on matters of necessity differs from other rulings. We shall mention it, if Allah the Most High wills, in its appropriate place in the book."
"4147","(4147) As for the hadith which Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, he informed us that Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us, that Ibn Manīʿ narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, that Sawwār ibn Muṣʿab narrated from Muṭarrif ibn Ṭarīf, from Abū al-Jahm, from al-Barāʾ, who said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""That whose flesh is eaten, there is no harm in its urine.""

Thus did Sawwār narrate it from this chain from him. However, Yaḥyā ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Rāzī contradicted him and narrated it as follows. [Weak]"
"4148","(4148) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, that Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Ahwāzī informed us, that ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥuṣayn informed us, that Yaḥyā ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated from Muṭarrif ibn Ṭarīf, from Muḥārib ibn Dithār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: the Prophet ﷺ said: ""That whose flesh is eaten, there is no harm in its urine.""

And ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥuṣayn al-ʿUqaylī and Yaḥyā ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Rāzī are both weak, and Sawwār ibn Muṣʿab is weak. And it was narrated from him: ""That whose flesh is eaten, there is no harm in its leftover water (i.e., its drink)."" And this has already been mentioned in the book of Purification, and nothing is authentically established regarding this from the Prophet ﷺ. [Weak]"
"4149","(4149) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us, that ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmar, from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) used to command the killing of dogs and the slaughtering of pigeons. [Authentic. Ibn Abī Shaybah 19923]"
"4150","(4150) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, that Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah informed us, that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Zaʿfarānī informed us, that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Umm Qays bint Miḥṣan, who said: ""I brought to the Prophet ﷺ a son of mine who had not yet eaten food, and he urinated upon him. So he called for water and sprinkled it upon him.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and a group from Sufyān. [Authentic. Bukhārī 5693, Muslim 287]"
"4151","(4151) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Fawāriṣ al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Murādī narrated to us, that Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Mālik ibn Anas, al-Layth ibn Saʿd, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, and Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated to me that Ibn Shihāb narrated to them from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, from Umm Qays bint Miḥṣan, that she came to the Prophet ﷺ with a young child who had not yet eaten food. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated him in his lap, and he urinated upon him. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for water and sprinkled it upon him and did not wash it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb from Yūnus, and from Muḥammad ibn al-Rumḥ from al-Layth. [Authentic. Bukhārī 223]"
"4152","(4152) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh in Baghdād informed us, that Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, that al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""A child was brought to the Prophet ﷺ and he urinated upon him. So he called for water and poured it over the urine.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Authentic. Bukhārī 222]"
"4153","(4153) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, that Abū al-Walīd informed me, that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, that Hishām narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to be brought young children, upon whom he would invoke blessings and upon whom he would perform taḥnīk (chewing dates and placing the juice in the infant's mouth). A child was brought to him and he urinated upon him. So he called for water and poured it over it and did not wash it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.

And Jarīr said from Hishām: ""So he poured it upon him."" [Authentic. As previously mentioned]"
"4154","(4154) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, that Asad ibn Mūsā narrated to us, that Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Qābūs ibn Abī al-Mukhāriq, from Lubābah bint al-Ḥārith, who said: ""Al-Ḥusayn (peace be upon him) urinated in the lap of the Prophet ﷺ. I said: 'Give me your garment so that I may wash it.' He said: 'The urine of a female child is washed, and the urine of a male child is sprinkled.'""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the book of Sunan from Musaddad and others from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ. (T) Likewise, Isrāʾīl and Sharīk narrated it from Simāk, and Lubābah is Umm al-Faḍl. [Good. Aḥmad 26336]"
"4155","(4155) It was narrated from ʿAlī ibn Ṣāliḥ from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Qābūs, from his father, who said: ""Umm al-Faḍl came to the Prophet ﷺ,"" and he mentioned a story in which the Prophet ﷺ said: ""The urine of a young girl is washed, and the urine of a young boy is sprinkled.""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, that Ḥasnūn al-Bannāʾ al-Kūfī narrated to us, that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Murī narrated to us from ʿAlī ibn Ṣāliḥ, and he mentioned it, except that he said: ""Qābūs ibn al-Mukhāriq."" [Good. As previously mentioned]"
"4156","(4156) Ḥumayd said that ʿAṭāʾ — meaning al-Khurāsānī — used to narrate it from Abū ʿIyāḍ from Lubābah, Umm al-Faḍl. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, that ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us, that Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: Ḥumayd said, and he mentioned it."
"4157","(4157) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mujāhid ibn Mūsā and ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm al-Maʿnī both said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to me; Yaḥyā ibn al-Walīd narrated to me — and al-ʿAbbās said: Yaḥyā ibn al-Walīd narrated to me — Muḥill ibn Khalīfah narrated to me; he said: Abū al-Samḥ narrated to me; he said: I used to serve the Prophet ﷺ. When he wished to perform ghusl, he would say: ""Turn your back toward me."" So I would turn my back to him and screen him. Then Ḥasan or Ḥusayn was brought, and he urinated upon his chest. I came to wash it, and he said: ""The urine of the female infant is to be washed, and the urine of the male infant is to be sprinkled.""

Abū Dāwūd said: He is Abū al-Zaʿrāʾ, meaning Yaḥyā ibn al-Walīd.

Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal also narrated it from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, and he said in the hadith: ""Sprinkle it with a sprinkling. For indeed the urine of the female infant is washed, and the urine of the male infant is sprinkled."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 376, Ibn Mājah 526, Ibn Khuzaymah 283]"
"4158","(4158) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us in Baghdād; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to me; my father narrated to me from Qatādah, from Abū Ḥarb ibn Abī al-Aswad, from his father, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah honour his face — that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said regarding the urine of the nursing infant: ""The urine of the male infant is to be sprinkled, and the urine of the female infant is to be washed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 1/76/563, Ibn Khuzaymah 284]"
"4159","(4159) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī narrated to us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Isḥāq al-Khurāsānī al-ʿAdl informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Haytham al-Bazzāz narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us, and he mentioned it similarly. And he added: Qatādah said: ""This is so long as they have not yet eaten solid food. When they begin to eat, both are to be washed.""

Ibn Abī ʿArūbah also narrated it from Qatādah as a mawqūf narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See above]"
"4160","(4160) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed him; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Abū Ḥarb ibn Abī al-Aswad, from his father, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""The urine of the female infant is to be washed, and the urine of the male infant is to be sprinkled, so long as he has not eaten solid food.""

And from what has reached me from Abū ʿĪsā, that he said: ""I asked al-Bukhārī about this hadith, and he said: Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah does not raise it (to the Prophet), whereas Hishām al-Dastawāʾī raises it, and he is a ḥāfiẓ."" I (al-Bukhārī) said: ""However, other than Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated it from Hishām as a mursal narration."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See above]"
"4161","(4161) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sahl ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hishām narrated from Qatādah, from Ibn Abī al-Aswad, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The urine of the male infant is to be sprinkled, and the urine of the female infant is to be washed."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4162","(4162) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Muḥammad ibn Naṣr ibn Ṣuhayb al-Adamī al-ʿAdl narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣādirā narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīd al-Madāʾinī, known as Ibn Ṣādirā, narrated to us; al-Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān al-Numayrī narrated to us; Kathīr ibn Qāwand narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥazm informed us from Muʿādhah ibn Ḥubaysh, from Umm Salamah — the wife of the Prophet ﷺ — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting, and in his lap were Ḥasan and Ḥusayn, or one of them. The boy urinated. She said: I stood up and said: ""Shall I wash the garment?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The urine of the male infant is to be sprinkled, and the urine of the female infant is to be washed.""

This hadith is ṣaḥīḥ from Umm Salamah from her action. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4163","(4163) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Abī al-Ḥajjāj, Abū Maʿmar, narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, from his mother, that she saw Umm Salamah — may Allah be pleased with her — sprinkling water upon the urine of the male infant so long as he had not eaten solid food. When he began to eat, she would wash it. And she would wash the urine of the female infant.

The musnad ahādīth concerning the distinction between the urine of the male infant and the female infant in this chapter, when taken together, are strengthened. It is as though they were not established in the view of al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy on him — when he said: ""No distinction between the urine of the male infant and the female infant is clear to me from the established Sunnah.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim held a similar view, in that they did not include any of these in their two books. However, al-Bukhārī considered the hadith of Abū al-Samḥ to be good (istaḥsanahu), and he considered Hishām correct in raising the hadith of ʿAlī. Alongside that, the action of Umm Salamah — may Allah be pleased with her — is ṣaḥīḥ from her, together with the previously mentioned established ahādīth regarding sprinkling upon the urine of the male child. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 379]"
"4164","(4164) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān, Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Burhān, Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān, and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī in Baghdād informed us; they said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah narrated to us; Hushaym ibn Bashīr narrated from Mughīrah, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: ""I would find manī (semen) on the garment of the Prophet ﷺ, and I would scrape it off.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Hushaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 290, Ibn Khuzaymah 288]"
"4165","(4165) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Khālid, from Abū Maʿshar, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah and al-Aswad, that a man stayed as a guest with ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — and in the morning he began washing his garment. ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — said: ""It would have sufficed you, had you seen it, to wash only the place where it was. And if you did not see it, you could have sprinkled water around it. And indeed I would scrape manī from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ by scraping, and he would pray in it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 288]"
"4166","(4166) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, that ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — said: ""I used to scrape manī from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would pray in it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 372]"
"4167","(4167) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ narrated to us, Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated from Wāṣil al-Aḥdab, from Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī, from al-Aswad, who said:

ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saw me washing the trace of janābah that had soiled my garment. She said, ""What is this?"" I said, ""The trace of janābah that has soiled my garment."" She said, ""I have indeed seen myself when it would afflict the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we would do nothing more than this to it"" — meaning, rubbing it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, from Mahdī ibn Maymūn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"
"4168","(4168) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, Manṣūr narrated from Ibrāhīm, from Hammām, who said:

A guest visited ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she sent for him to call him. They said to her, ""He has been afflicted with janābah and has gone to wash his garment."" ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, ""Why did he wash it? If I were to rub the manī from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ…""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 288.]"
"4169","(4169) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, Ibn al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from Hammām, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

""I used to rub the manī from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and then he would pray in it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"
"4170","(4170) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, al-Masʿūdī narrated from al-Ḥakam and Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, from Hammām ibn al-Ḥārith, that he was a guest of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her.

He mentioned the hadith, and she said: ""I remember myself wiping it from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when it dried, I would scrape it off."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"4171","(4171) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Jawwās Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Shabīb ibn Gharqadah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shahāb al-Khawlānī, who said:

I was staying as a guest with ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. I experienced a wet dream in my garment, so I immersed it in water. A maidservant of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saw me and informed her. ʿľishah, may Allah be pleased with her, sent for me and said, ""What compelled you to do what you did with your garment?"" I said, ""I saw what a sleeping person sees in his dream."" She said, ""Did you see anything [on the garment]?"" I said, ""No."" She said, ""Had you seen something, you would have washed it. I remember myself scraping it dry from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with my fingernails.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī ʿĀṣim Aḥmad ibn Jawwās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 290.]"
"4172","(4172) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah narrated from al-Awzāʿī, from Yaḥyī ibn Saʿīd, from al-Qāsim, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

""I used to rub the manī from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""

(T) It was also narrated from Bishr ibn Bakr, from al-Awzāʿī, from Yaḥyī, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains.]"
"4173","(4173) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr narrated from al-Qāsim, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

""I remember myself rubbing the janābah from the bosom of the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would not wash its place."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains.]"
"4174","(4174) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us, Yazīd ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd ibn Maymūn ibn Mihrān narrated in Makkah, dictating from his book, ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbayd narrated, saying: ʿľishah said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to remove the manī from his garment with a piece of idhkhir (lemon grass), and then he would pray in it.""

He said, and al-Qāsim said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would notice the manī on his garment, then scrape it off and pray in it.""

Ismāʿīl ibn Sinān followed him in narrating from ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 294–295.]"
"4175","(4175) Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzzakī narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār and Ibn Jurayj, both of them reporting from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he said regarding manī that afflicts a garment:

""Remove it from yourself."" One of the two narrators added: ""With a stick of idhkhir, for it is of the same status as saliva or nasal mucus.""

This is ṣaḥīḥ as a statement of Ibn ʿAbbās.

It has also been narrated in marfūʿ form, but its elevation [to the Prophet] is not ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"4176","(4176) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Qaḥṭabah narrated to us, Sarīʿ al-Khādim narrated to us, Isḥāq al-Azraq narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said:

The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about manī that afflicts a garment, and he said: ""It is merely of the same status as saliva or nasal mucus. It is sufficient for you to wipe it with a rag or idhkhir.""

Wakīʿ narrated it from Ibn Abī Laylā, as a mawqūf report from Ibn ʿAbbās, and that is the correct version. [Munkar.]"
"4177","(4177) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh informed us, my father informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr, who said: I heard Mujāhid narrating from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd, from his father, that he used to rub off janābah (seminal discharge) from his garment. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 919]"
"4178","(4178) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, al-Thiqah narrated from Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, that al-Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ informed me, from his father, that when maniyy (seminal fluid) would get on his garment, if it was wet he would wipe it, and if it was dry he would scrape it off, and then he would pray in it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4179","(4179) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl both informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, ʿAmr ibn Maymūn ibn Mihrān informed me, Sulaymān ibn Yasār informed me, ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed me, that when maniyy (seminal discharge) would get on the garment of the Prophet ﷺ, he would wash what had gotten on his garment, then he would go out to pray, and I would see the traces of the washed spots on his garment in the place of washing. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, with the wording of the ḥadīth of Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 229]"
"4180","(4180) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of that, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Muthannā informed us, Musaddad informed us, ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād informed us, ʿAmr ibn Maymūn narrated from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, who said: I asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about maniyy that gets on a garment. She said: I used to wash it from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would go out to pray, and the trace of the washing on his garment was spots of water. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Kāmil al-Jaḥdarī, from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak and Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, similar to the narration of ʿAbd al-Wāḥid, attributing the washing to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated it from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, and Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, attributing the washing to the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See above]"
"4181","(4181) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed me, ʿAmr ibn Maymūn said: I asked Sulaymān ibn Yasār about maniyy that gets on a garment — should one wash it or wash the garment? He said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed me that the Prophet ﷺ would wash the maniyy, then go out to pray in that garment, and I would see the trace of the washing on it. Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. Likewise, Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah and Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, attributing the washing to him. The ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Bishr indicates that the context of the ḥadīth pertains to the purity of the sweat of the junub (one in a state of major ritual impurity), and that it is not obligatory to wash the garment in which he became junub. The maniyy may be washed for the purpose of cleanliness, just as mucus and other things are washed from a garment for cleanliness, not because of impurity. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5799, Muslim 274]"
"4182","(4182) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us, Abū Nuʿaym informed us, Zakariyyā narrated from ʿĀmir, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Mughīrah, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, in the story of wiping (al-masḥ), that he was wearing a jubbah (long coat) of wool, and he could not extract his arms from it until he brought them out from the bottom of the jubbah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Zakariyyā. Masrūq narrated it from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, saying: He was wearing a Syrian jubbah with narrow sleeves, then he mentioned his wiping and his prayer. Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated it from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Mughīrah, from his father, saying: He was wearing a jubbah of wool from the jubbahs of the Romans, with narrow sleeves. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5799]"
"4183","(4183) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah said: My father informed me from Muṣʿab ibn Shaybah, from Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet ﷺ went out one morning wearing a decorated cloak (mirṭ) of black hair. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tirmidhī 2813, Muslim 2081]"
"4184","(4184) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Hammām narrated from Qatādah, from Muṭarrif, from ʿľʾishah, that she said: I made for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a black woolen cloak (burdah), and he wore it and it pleased him. But when he perspired in it and found the smell of the nimarah (a type of animal skin or wool with a strong odor), he cast it aside. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 6/132, Abū Dāwūd 4074]"
"4185","(4185) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah, from Abū Burdah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays al-Ashʿarī, from his father ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays, who said: O my son, had you been present with us when we were with our Prophet ﷺ and the sky rained upon us, you would have thought our smell was that of sheep, on account of our woolen garments. [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 4/407, Abū Dāwūd 4033]"
"4186","(4186) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us, Abū Usāmah narrated from al-Aḥwaṣ ibn Ḥakīm, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day wrapped in a small shamlah (cloak) of his, having tied its two ends between his shoulders. He led us in prayer wearing nothing other than it. [Ḍaʿīf. al-Shāshī 1292]"
"4187","(4187) And through his chain of narration from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day wearing a Roman woolen tunic with narrow sleeves, and he led us in prayer wearing nothing other than it. [Weak — previously referenced]"
"4188","(4188) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Faqīh in al-Rayy narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us — Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us — Shaybān, Abū Muʿāwiyah, narrated to us from Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to ride a donkey, wear wool, milk goats, and accept the invitation of the weak. Thus he informed us with this chain of narration, though it is not preserved. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4189","(4189) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us — Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah informed us that he heard Mujāhid saying: Seventy prophets prayed in this mosque — the Mosque of al-Khayf, meaning the Mosque of Minā — their garments were of wool and their sandals were of leather. [Weak]"
"4190","(4190) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz narrated to us — Abū Ghassān al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ṣāmit, from Abū Dharr, who said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying in two sandals made of cowhide. Abū Ghassān Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr al-ʿAnbarī is alone in narrating it, as I know. [Weak — referenced by al-Dāraquṭnī in al-ʿIlal 1106]"
"4191","(4191) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb narrated to us — Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Ṭāriq informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb that Abū al-Khayr narrated to him, saying: I saw a fur garment on Ibn Waʿlah al-Sabāʾī, so I touched it. He said: Why do you touch it? I asked Ibn ʿAbbās about it, saying: We are in the Maghreb and among us are Berbers and Magians. A sheep is brought to them and they slaughtered it, and we do not eat their slaughtered animals, and a water-skin containing rendered fat is brought to us. Ibn ʿAbbāh said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Its tanning is its purification (dibāghuhu ṭahūruhu).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and others, from ʿAmr ibn al-Rabīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 366]"
"4192","(4192) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Mukram ibn Aḥmad al-Qāḍī in Baghdad narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān narrated to us — Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us — Yūnus ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from Abū ʿAwn Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh al-Thaqafī, from his father, from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, that the Prophet ﷺ used to pray on a mat and on a tanned fur garment. Abū Dāwūd extracted it in Kitāb al-Sunan from the ḥadīth of Abū Aḥmad. [Weak — Aḥmad 4/254/18414, Abū Dāwūd 659]"
"4193","(4193) Al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān informed us — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us — Khālid ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us — Yūnus ibn al-Ḥārith narrated it, except that he did not say ""from his father,"" and he added: ""He used to like it (kāna yastaḥibbu)."""
"4194","(4194) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us — ʿUbaydullāh, meaning Ibn Mūsā, informed us — Ibn Abī Laylā informed us from Thābit al-Bunānī, who said: I was sitting with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā in the mosque when a man with two braids came to him and said: O Abā ʿĪsā, narrate to me what you heard from your father regarding wild donkey hide (al-farāʾ). He said: My father narrated to me, saying: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when a man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I pray in wild donkey hide? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Then where is the tanning (al-dibāgh)?"" When the man turned away, I said: Who is that? They said: Suwayd ibn Ghafalah. [Weak — Ibn Abī Shaybah 8/189, 24756; Aḥmad 4/348/19270]"
"4195","(4195) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd and ʿAmr ibn Marzūq both narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from al-Shaybānī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaddād ibn al-Hād, from his maternal aunt Maymūnah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray on a small mat (al-khumrah).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim extracted it through another chain from Abū Isḥāq al-Shaybānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 381]"
"4196","(4196) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us — Wuhibb ibn Khālid narrated from Ayyūb, from Abī Qilābah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Prophet ﷺ used to visit Umm Sulaym and take a nap at her house. He would pray on a leather mat (niṭāʿ), and he would sweat profusely. Umm Sulaym would collect the sweat from the mat and place it in small bottles along with perfume. And he would pray on a small mat (al-khumrah). [Ṣaḥīḥ — Ibn Ḥibbān 6305]"
"4197","(4197) Abū al-Ḥasan informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Tamtām narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Wuhaib narrated to us, Ayyūb narrated from Abū Qilābah from Anas from Umm Sulaym: that the Prophet ﷺ used to pray upon a khumrah (small mat). ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated it from Ayyūb from Anas ibn Sīrīn from Anas ibn Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 4023]"
"4198","(4198) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated from Ayyūb from Anas ibn Sīrīn from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to take a siesta at the house of Umm Sulaym, so she would spread out a leather cloth for him. She would then collect his perspiration and place it in her perfume, and she would spread out the khumrah for him, and he would pray upon it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 11589, Ibn Ḥibbān 4528]"
"4199","(4199) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī informed us, Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray upon a khumrah (small mat). [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Aḥmad 2808, Ibn Ḥibbān 2310]"
"4200","(4200) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us, Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Yaʿlā — (Ḥ) and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us, ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us, al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Sufyān from Jābir, who said: Abū Saʿīd narrated to me, saying: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying upon a ḥaṣīr (woven mat). Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ through various routes from al-Aʿmash. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim agreed upon the ḥadīth of Anas ibn Mālik in this chapter, and that will be mentioned in its appropriate place, in shāʾ Allāh taʿālā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 661]"
"4201","(4201) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated — from what he read to Mālik — (Ḥ) and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: I read to Mālik from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a striped garment (ḥullah siyarāʾ) at the gate of the mosque and said: O Messenger of Allah, if you were to purchase this and wear it on Fridays and when delegations come to you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Only the one who has no share in the Hereafter wears this."" Then some garments came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he gave ʿUmar one of them. ʿUmar said: O Messenger of Allah, you have clothed me with it, yet you said what you said about the garment of ʿUṭārid. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I did not give it to you to wear it."" So ʿUmar gave it to a polytheist brother of his in Makkah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī and Muslim, other than this place]"
"4202","(4202) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that ʿUmar saw a striped garment being sold at the gate of the mosque. Then he mentioned the ḥadīth similarly, up to his saying: ""You said about it what you said?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I did not give it to you to wear it; rather, I gave it to you so that you might sell it or give it to someone else to wear."" So ʿUmar gave it to a polytheist brother of his through his mother in Makkah. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 886, Muslim 2068]"
"4203","(4203) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī and Ṣāliḥ Jazarah both said: ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Dhubyān — Khalīfah ibn Kaʿb — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, who said: I heard ʿUmar. And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī in Baṣrah informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī informed us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Abū Dhubyān Khalīfah ibn Kaʿb narrated, saying: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr say: Do not wear silk, for I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever wears silk in this world shall not wear it in the Hereafter."" ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr added on his own: And whoever does not wear it in the Hereafter shall not enter Paradise, because Allah taʿālā said: ""And their garments therein shall be of silk"" (al-Ḥajj: 23). In the narration of ʿAlī, Ibn al-Zubayr said: And that is because of the words of Allah taʿālā: ""And their garments therein shall be of silk"" (al-Ḥajj: 23). Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5834, Muslim 2069]"
"4204","(4204) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmawayh informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad informed us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhaib, who said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say — Shuʿbah said: I asked: From the Prophet ﷺ? He said emphatically: From the Prophet ﷺ — that he said: ""Whoever wears silk in this world shall not wear it in the Hereafter."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUlayyah from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5832, Muslim 2073]"
"4205","(4205) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Muslim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿīd ibn Hārūn al-Aṣbahānī informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Rabīʿ al-Jurjānī informed me, Wahb ibn Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed me, my father narrated to me, saying: I heard Ibn Abī Najīḥ narrate from Mujāhid from Ibn Abī Laylā: that Ḥudhayfah asked for water, and a landowner (dihqān) brought him a vessel of silver. He took it and threw it, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us from drinking from vessels of gold and silver, and from eating from them, and from wearing silk and brocade, and from sitting upon them. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī from Wahb ibn Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5837]"
"4206","(4206) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāl al-Muzzakī, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed me, a man and al-Layth ibn Saʿd — (Ḥ) and Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah and Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq both said: Qutaybah narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Khayr from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, who said: A silk garment (farj ḥarīr) was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he wore it and then prayed in it. Then he turned away and removed it with a forceful removal, as though he disliked it, and then said: ""This is not befitting for the God-fearing (muttaqīn)."" The wording is that of Qutaybah's ḥadīth. And in the ḥadīth of Ibn Wahb: He heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day wearing a silk garment. He prayed in it, then turned away and removed it, saying: ""The wearing of this is not befitting for the God-fearing."" Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it together in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 375]"
"4207","(4207) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us; Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī narrated from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in a garment that had decorative borders. He looked at its borders, and when he completed the prayer, he said: ""Take this garment of mine to Abū Jahm, for it distracted me during my prayer, and bring me his Anbijāniyyah (a plain garment)."" Abū Dāwūd said: Abū Jahm ibn Ḥudhayfah was from Banū ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl and others, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī and Muslim in various places]"
"4208","(4208) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us; Qatādah narrated; he said: I heard Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī say: The letter of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while we were with ʿUtbah ibn Farqad in Azerbaijan, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade silk except this much, and he indicated with the two fingers next to the thumb. He said: We understood that he meant the decorative borders. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 5828]"
"4209","(4209) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, i.e., ibn ʿAṭāʾ, informed us; Saʿīd narrated from Qatādah from al-Shaʿbī from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people at al-Jābiyah and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade wearing silk except the extent of one finger or two fingers or three or four, and he indicated with his palm and counted fifty. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Rūzī from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — see previous]"
"4210","(4210) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Khālid ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān from ʿAbdillāh, the freed slave of Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, who was the maternal uncle of the children of ʿAṭāʾ. He said: Asmāʾ sent me to ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUmar and said: ""It has reached me that you declare three things forbidden: the decorative border (ʿalam) in garments, the purple silk cushion (mītharah) made of urjūwān, and fasting the entire month of Rajab."" ʿAbdillāh said to me: ""As for what you mentioned about Rajab, what about the one who fasts perpetually? As for what you mentioned about the decorative border in garments, I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Only the one who has no share in the Hereafter wears silk.' So I feared that the border might be from it. As for the purple silk cushion, this is ʿAbdillāh's cushion."" When I saw it, it was indeed urjūwān (purple). I returned to Asmāʾ and informed her. She said: ""This is the jubbah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" She then brought out to me a jubbah of ṭaylasān whose collar was of brocade and whose openings were lined with brocade. She said: ""This was with ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, until she passed away. When she passed away, I took it. The Prophet ﷺ used to wear it, and we would wash it for the sick to seek healing through it."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — see previous]"
"4211","(4211) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibn Nufayl narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Khuṣayf narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only forbade garments made entirely of silk. As for the silk border (ʿalam) and the warp (sadā) of the garment, there is no harm in it. All other narrations that came in this chapter, or regarding its undesirability, are mentioned at the end of the Book of Ṣalāt al-Khawf, where al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, cited them. [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning — Aḥmad 1/313/2859]"
"4212","(4212) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Ḥunayn from his father from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade me from wearing a gold ring, from wearing silk garments (qassī), from reciting Qurʾān during rukūʿ and sujūd, and from wearing dyed (muʿaṣfar) garments. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. Al-Walīd ibn Kathīr also narrated from Ibrāhīm in a manner similar to the narration of al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 2078]"
"4213","(4213) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated from al-Walīd; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Ḥunayn narrated to me from his father that he heard ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade me from wearing a gold ring, from wearing silk garments and dyed (muʿaṣfar) garments, and from reciting the Qurʾān while in sujūd. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me a ḥullah of Sīrāʾ (striped silk). I went out wearing it, and he said: ""O ʿAlī, I did not give it to you to wear."" He said: So I returned and tore it apart, then gave it to Fāṭimah and said: ""Wear it and clothe your women with it."" Zayd ibn Aslam also narrated similarly from Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Nasāʾī narrated it in al-Kubrā 9495]"
"4214","(4214) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr said: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated to me from Kurayb, the freed slave of Ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a gold ring on a man's hand. He removed it and threw it away, saying: ""One of you takes a burning coal of fire and places it on his hand."" It was said to the man after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had gone: ""Take your ring and benefit from it."" He said: ""No, by Allah, I shall never take it, since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw it away."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn ʿAskar from Ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 2090]"
"4215","(4215) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a gold ring made, and he used to place its stone (faṣṣ) on the side of his palm. The people then began making rings. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ subsequently discarded it and had a silver ring made, which remained in his hand, then in the hand of Abū Bakr, then in the hand of ʿUmar, then in the hand of ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with them all, until it was lost in the well of Arīs. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Shaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through various chains from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī and Muslim in various places]"
"4216","(4216) Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and ʿAmr ibn Marzūq both narrated from Shuʿbah from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Mayssarah from Zayd ibn Wahb from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A ḥullah of Sīrāʾ (striped silk) was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He sent it to me, and I wore it. I saw anger on his face, so I tore it into pieces to be used as head-coverings among my wives. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī 5840]"
"4217","(4217) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Ashbahani informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani informed us — 'Affan and Shababa both informed us — Shu'ba informed us.

(And) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — and the wording is his — Abu Bakr ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari informed us — Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi informed us — Abu Iyas informed us — Shu'ba informed us — Abu 'Awn Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah informed us — from Abu Salih al-Hanafi — from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""A silk-striped garment was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he sent it to me. I wore it and went out in it. He looked at me, and it was as though he disliked it. He said to me: 'I did not give it to you to wear.' So he commanded me, and I cut it up and distributed it among my wives.""

In the hadith of 'Affan, from Shu'ba, from Abu 'Awn al-Thaqafi, he said: ""I heard Abu Salih al-Hanafi,"" and he added: ""I recognized anger in his face,"" and he said: ""I cut it up and distributed it among his wives.""

The remainder carries the same meaning. Muslim recorded it in *al-Sahih* through various chains from Shu'ba. [Sahih. Previously referenced.]"
"4218","(4218) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadd al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us — Abu al-Yamani informed me — Shu'yaib said — and Hajjaj informed me — my grandfather — entirely — from al-Zuhri, who said:

""And 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us — Yahya ibn Hamzah informed us — Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi informed us — from al-Zuhri — from Anas ibn Malik, who said:

He saw Umm Kulthum, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, wearing a silk-striped garment.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in *al-Sahih* from Abu al-Yamani, and Ma'mar narrated it from al-Zuhri, but he said: ""Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari, 5842.]"
"4219","(4219) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani informed us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us.

(And) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn Ahmad ibn Shawdhab in Wasit informed us — Shu'yaib ibn Ayyub informed us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — from Yazid ibn Abi Habib — from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi al-Sa'bah — from Abu Aflah al-Hamdani — from 'Abd Allah ibn Zurayr al-Ghafiqi, who said:

I heard 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took gold in his right hand and silk in his left hand, then raised both his hands and said: 'These two are forbidden for the males of my Ummah.'""

In the hadith of al-Za'farani, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us with gold in one hand and silk in the other, and he said: 'These two are forbidden for the males of my Ummah.'"" [Da'if. Ahmad, 1/115/935.]"
"4220","(4220) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Hajib ibn Ahmad informed us — 'Abd al-Rahman al-Marwazi informed us — Ibn al-Mubarak informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar informed us — from Nafi' — from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind — from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:

""Gold and silk have been made lawful for the females of my Ummah, and forbidden for its males.""

That was also narrated from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr from the Prophet ﷺ. [Da'if. Ibn Hibban, 5525.]"
"4221","(4221) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi informed us — Abu al-Ashhab informed us — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Tarafah — from his grandfather 'Arfajah ibn As'ad, who said:

His nose was cut off on the Day of al-Kulab in the pre-Islamic period, so he had a nose made of silver. It became foul-smelling, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to have a nose made of gold. [Da'if.]"
"4222","(4222) Yazid ibn Harun also narrated it from Abu al-Ashhab. Then Yazid said: ""I asked Abu al-Ashhab: 'Did 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Tarafah meet his grandfather 'Arfajah?' He said: 'Yes.'""

Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed him — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud al-Sijistani informed us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan informed us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us, and he mentioned it. [Likewise.]"
"4223","(4223) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu 'Uthman 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar al-Basri informed us — Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us — al-Husayn ibn al-Walid informed us — Ja'far ibn Hayyan al-'Ataridi, that is, Abu al-Ashhab — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Tarafah ibn 'Arfajah ibn As'ad al-'Ataridi — from his father — from his grandfather, that:

His nose was cut off on the Day of al-Kulab in the pre-Islamic period, so he had a nose made of silver. It became foul-smelling. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he commanded him to have a nose made of gold. [Munkar.]"
"4224","(4224) Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah narrated it from Abu al-Ashhab — from 'Abd al-Rahman — from his father — that 'Arfajah, with the same meaning.

Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed him — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Mu'ammal ibn Hisham informed us — Isma'il informed us, and he mentioned it. [Likewise.]"
"4225","(4225) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi informed us — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ashbahani informed us — Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Faris informed us — Muhammad ibn Isma'il informed me — Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir informed us — Ma'n ibn 'Isa informed us — Muhammad ibn Sa'dan, a freedman of Quraysh, from his father, who said:

I saw Anas ibn Malik being carried by his sons on his shoulders, his teeth having been bound with gold.

We have also received narrations on this from al-Hasan al-Basri, al-Nakha'i, and other Successors. [Da'if. Al-Bukhari narrated it in *al-Tarikh al-Kabir*, 293.]"
"4226","(4226) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Tahir al-Faqih, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr all said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Anas ibn Iyad informed us — from Hisham ibn 'Urwah — that Fatimah bint al-Mundhir informed him — from Asma' bint Abi Bakr, who said:

A woman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""I have a daughter who is a bride, and she was afflicted with al-hasbah (a scalp disease), causing her hair to fall out. May I attach hair to her head?"" Asma' said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Allah has cursed the woman who attaches hair (al-wasilah) and the woman who has it attached (al-mustawsilah).""

Both (al-Bukhari and Muslim) recorded it in their *Sahih* collections through various chains from Hisham ibn 'Urwah. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari, Muslim.]"
"4227","(4227) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim narrating from Ṣafiyyah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that: A woman from the Anṣār had her hair fall out, and they wished to attach (false) hair to it. This was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and he cursed the one who attaches hair and the one who has it attached. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Mūsā and Bundār, from Abū Dāwūd, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ādam, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī, Muslim]"
"4228","(4228) Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmish al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: Yūnus — (ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, and the wording is his: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad al-Muʾaddib narrated to us: Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Allah has cursed the one who attaches hair and the one who has it attached, the one who tattoos and the one who is tattooed."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ""And Ibn Abī Shaybah said: Yūnus narrated to us…"" and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf — Bukhārī, taʿlīqan]"
"4229","(4229) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī — informed us by dictation, and Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us by recitation upon him, both saying: Abū Ṭāhir — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadhābādhī — narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: The Prophet ﷺ forbade a woman from attaching anything to her hair. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥulwānī and Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — ʿAbd al-Razzāq 5070–5096]"
"4230","(4230) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, that he heard Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān (may Allah be pleased with him) in the year of his Ḥajj, while he was upon the minbar, take a lock of hair that was in the hand of one of his guards, and say: ""O people of Madīnah, where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid the like of this, and he said: 'Indeed, the Children of Israel were destroyed when their women adopted this.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 3468]"
"4231","(4231) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Yaḥyā al-Ḥimmānī narrated from Abū Ḥanīfah, from al-Haytham, from Umm Thawr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no harm in attaching (something) to the hair if it is made of wool."" And Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Jābir, from Umm Thawr. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4232","(4232) Abū ʿAlī — al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī — informed us: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us: Ḥafṣ narrated from Hishām, from Anas ibn Sīrīn, from Anas ibn Mālik, that: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stoned the Jamrah al-ʿAqabah on the Day of Sacrifice, then returned to his dwelling in Minā. He called for a sacrificial animal and sacrificed it, then called for the barber. The barber took the right side of his head and shaved it, and the Prophet ﷺ began distributing the hair — one strand, two strands — among those near him. Then the barber took the left side of his head and shaved it, and the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Is Abū Ṭalḥah here?"" And he gave it to Abū Ṭalḥah. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ, Abū Kurayb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Ibn Sīrīn. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 5920]"
"4233","(4233) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan — Ḥumayd ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Ramlī — narrated to us: Muʾammal narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shaved his head on the Day of Sacrifice, he took hold of his hair with his right hand. When the barber shaved the right side of his head, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Anas, take this to Abū Ṭalḥah and Umm Sulaym."" He said: When the people saw what he had specially bestowed upon them, they competed for the remainder of his hair — this one taking a tuft, and this one taking strands, and this one taking a portion. Muḥammad said: I narrated this narration to ʿUbaydah al-Salmānī, and he said: ""That I should have a single hair of his is more beloved to me than all the gold and silver upon the face of the earth and within it."" [Ḍaʿīf — Aḥmad 13710]"
"4234","(4234) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, both saying: Abū ʿAbdullāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he had finished his need, he went to a corner of the mosque and urinated. The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ shouted at him, but he restrained them. Then he ordered a bucket of water, which was poured over his urine. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ through various chains from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī and Muslim in other places]"
"4235","(4235) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, who said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: A Bedouin urinated in the mosque. The people hastened toward him, but the Prophet ﷺ forbade them and said: ""Pour a bucket of water over it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — as in the preceding narration]"
"4236","(4236) And Abū Saʿīd al-Khaṭīb informed us: Abū Baḥr al-Barbahārī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to me: Sufyān narrated it with a similar chain."
"4237","(4237) ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Ibn Abī Uways informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn narrated from Ḥammād ibn Zayd.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar al-Bakrāwī and Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah al-Ḍabbī both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, Thābit narrated from Anas: that a Bedouin urinated in the mosque, whereupon some of the people rushed toward him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do not interrupt him."" He then called for a bucket of water and poured it over the spot.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥajabī, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah, both from Ḥammād ibn Zayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding hadith.]"
"4238","(4238) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Hishām ibn ʿAlī informed us, Ibn Rajāʾ informed us, Hammām narrated from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah from Anas ibn Mālik: that he saw a Bedouin urinating in the mosque, and the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Leave him."" When he had finished, he called for water and poured it over the spot.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā from Hammām, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār from Isḥāq, and he said: ""He commanded a man from the people, so he brought a bucket of water and poured it over the spot."" Its meaning has already been mentioned. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding hadith.]"
"4239","(4239) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Qarqūb al-Tammār in Hamadhān informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, Abū al-Yamān al-Ḥakam ibn Nāfiʿ informed us, Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd informed me that Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A Bedouin stood up and urinated in the mosque. The people moved toward him, but the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Leave him, and pour over his urine a bucket of water (sajlan min māʾin) or a large bucket of water (dhanūban min māʾin), for you have been sent to make things easy and not to make things difficult.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān.

Thus Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated it from al-Zuhrī from ʿUbayd Allāh from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in the story of the urination; and from al-Zuhrī from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Hurayrah in the story of the supplication. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī 220, 6128.]"
"4240","(4240) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Sufyān said: I preserve this from the words of al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: A Bedouin entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting. He prayed two rakʿahs and then said: ""O Allah, have mercy on me and on Muhammad, and do not have mercy on anyone alongside us."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""You have restricted what is vast."" It was not long before he urinated in the mosque. The people hastened toward him, but he forbade them and said: ""Pour over it a bucket of water or a large bucket of water, for you have been sent to make things easy and not to make things difficult.""

He said: Sufyān narrated it to me on another occasion and said: al-Zuhrī said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab informed me from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding hadith.]"
"4241","(4241) Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū Baḥr al-Barbarī informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us, al-Ḥumaydī informed us, Sufyān narrated, al-Zuhrī narrated — as I am telling you, there is no need for anyone else in this — he said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab informed me from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it with a similar meaning."
"4242","(4242) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us, Jarīr, meaning Ibn Ḥāzim, said: I heard ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr narrating from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿqil ibn Muqarrin, who said: A Bedouin prayed with the Prophet ﷺ — with this story. In it he said, meaning the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Take the earth upon which he has urinated, throw it away, and pour water over its place.""

Abū Dāwūd said: It is mursal. Ibn Maʿqil did not meet the Prophet ﷺ.

The Shaykh said: This has also been narrated in the ḥadīth of Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, but it is not ṣaḥīḥ. We have discussed it in the Khilāfiyyāt. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain.]"
"4243","(4243) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Shabīb informed us, my father narrated from Yūnus from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd Allāh from his father, who said: Dogs used to urinate and come and go in the mosque during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, and they would not change anything of that. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī 174.]"
"4244","(4244) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Abū Yaʿlā informed us, Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yūnus informed me from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to me from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say in the mosque in his loudest voice: ""Avoid idle talk in the mosque."" Ibn ʿUmar said: I used to spend the night in the mosque during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I was a young unmarried man. Dogs used to urinate and come and go in the mosque, and they would not sprinkle anything of that.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ, and he said: Aḥmad ibn Shabīb said: My father narrated to me, and he mentioned the musnad ḥadīth in abridged form, and he said in the wording of the ḥadīth: ""They would not sprinkle anything of that."" In some copies, from Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Bukhārī, the word ""urine"" is absent.

Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, he said: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī said regarding the meaning of the report: The mosque was not locked against them, and they used to move about in it. It is possible that they urinated, but the knowledge of where they urinated was not with the Prophet ﷺ, his Companions, or the narrator — which specific spot it was. And given that he commanded regarding the Bedouin's urine what he commanded, that indicates that the urine of other than him falls under the same ruling of impurity, even if the severity of their impurity differs.

The Shaykh said: We have narrated in the ḥadīth of Maymūnah, in the story of the puppy of a dog, that the Prophet ﷺ commanded regarding it, so it was removed, and then he took water with his hand and sprinkled its place.

And we have narrated from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding the washing of a vessel from a dog's lapping, with a specified number of washings, and the pouring out of the water in which the dog lapped — and in all of that there is evidence of its impurity. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"4245","(4245) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Khālid ibn Khalī informed us, Bishr ibn Shuʿayb narrated from his father, from al-Zuhrī. Ibn al-Sabbāq informed me that Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated to me that the Prophet ﷺ woke up one day in a state of concern. Maymūnah said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have noticed something unusual about your condition since today."" He said: ""Jibrīl had promised to meet me last night but did not meet me. By Allah, he has never broken his promise to me."" She said: He spent his day like that, and then it occurred to him that there was a puppy of a dog beneath one of our couches. He commanded regarding it, so it was removed. Then he took water with his hand and sprinkled its place. When evening came, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, met him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""You had promised to meet me last night."" He said: ""Indeed, but we do not enter a house in which there is a dog or an image."" He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ woke up that morning and commanded the killing of dogs that day, to the extent that he commanded the killing of the dog of a small garden and left the dog of a large garden.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd from Ibn Shihāb.

In it is the affirmation of sprinkling the place of the dog with water. And in it, and in what has preceded in the book of purification regarding the washing of a vessel from a dog's lapping and the pouring out of the water in which the dog lapped, there is evidence of its impurity, and evidence of the abrogation of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar regarding the dog, if it contradicts it — although it is possible what al-Ismāʿīlī and others mentioned, in which case it would not contradict it. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 2105.]"
"4246","(4246) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḣāfiẓ, Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed us, my father informed me, I heard al-Awzāʿī say: I was informed that Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you steps with his two shoes upon filth, then the earth is their purifier (ṭahūr).""

And in the ḥadīth of al-Sūsī: ""with his shoe."" [Munkar; al-Dāraquṭnī in al-ʿIlal 1479.]"
"4247","(4247) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn 'Uthmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Bazzāz and Abū 'Abdillāh Muhammad ibn 'Alī ibn Makhlad al-Jawharī both said: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Haytham al-Baladī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: al-Awzā'ī narrated from Ibn 'Ajlān from Sa'īd al-Maqburī from his father from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When one of you steps with his shoes in filth, then the earth is a purification for it.""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the book of Sunan from Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, except that he said: ""with his khuffs (leather socks).""

[Munkar. See the one before it.]"
"4248","(4248) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Khālid narrated to us: Muḥammad, meaning Ibn 'Ā'idh, narrated to us: Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Ḥamzah, narrated from al-Awzā'ī from Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd — and Sa'īd ibn Abī Sa'īd also informed me — from al-Qa'qā' ibn Ḥakīm from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the same meaning."
"4249","(4249) Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us: Abū Na'āmah al-Sa'dī narrated from Abū Naḍrah from Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was once praying with his Companions when he removed his shoes and placed them to his left. When his Companions saw that, they removed their shoes. When he finished, he said: ""Why did you remove your shoes?"" They said: ""We saw you remove yours, so we removed ours."" He said: ""Jibrīl (peace be upon him) informed me that there was filth on them."" Then he said: ""When one of you comes to prayer, let him look at his shoes. If there is any harm on them — or he said: filth — let him wipe them and pray in them.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Ḥātim in al-'Ilal 330]"
"4250","(4250) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Mūsā ibn Ismā'īl narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Abū Na'āmah — and he mentioned it with its chain and its meaning, and said in it: ""When one of you comes to the masjid, let him look. If he sees any filth or harm on his shoes, let him wipe it and pray in them.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it.]"
"4251","(4251) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismā'īl narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah — and he mentioned it with its chain and its meaning, and said in both places: ""filth"" or ""harm,"" and he said: ""let him wipe it and pray in them."""
"4252","(4252) He said: And Mūsā ibn Ismā'īl narrated to us: Abān narrated to us: Qatādah narrated to me: Bakr ibn 'Abdillāh al-Muzanī informed me from the Prophet ﷺ with this, and he said in both places: ""khabath (impurity)."""
"4253","(4253) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shu'bah informed me: Abū Maslamah Sa'īd ibn Yazīd said: I asked Anas ibn Mālik: ""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray in his shoes?"" He said: ""Yes.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 388]"
"4254","(4254) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Abū al-Rabī' narrated to us: 'Abbād ibn al-'Awwām narrated to us: Abū Maslamah Sa'īd ibn Yazīd mentioned it with the same wording.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabī'."
"4255","(4255) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-'Adl informed us in Baghdād: Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Sa'dān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Badr narrated from Ziyād ibn Khaythamah from 'Abdillāh ibn 'Īsā from 'Abdillāh ibn 'Aṭā' from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying barefoot and in shoes, drinking while sitting and while standing, and departing to his right and to his left — he did not mind whichever of that it was.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4256","(4256) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: 'Alī ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us: Ḥusayn al-Mu'allim narrated from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb from his father from his grandfather, who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying barefoot and in shoes.""

And we have narrated it previously in the hadith of Abū al-Awbar from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet ﷺ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Abū Dāwūd 653]"
"4257","(4257) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Shādhān narrated to us: Qutaybah narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī narrated to us from Hilāl ibn Maymūn al-Ramlī from Yaʿlā ibn Shaddād ibn Aws from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Differ from the Jews, for they do not pray in their leather socks (khifāf) nor in their shoes (niʿāl)."" Abū Dāwūd narrated it from Qutaybah. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 652]"
"4258","(4258) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh in Baghdād narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram al-Bazzāz narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar from Abū Salamah ibn Sufyān from ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Sāʾib, who said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Year of the Conquest (Fatḥ), and he prayed the morning prayer (al-ṣubḥ), then removed his two sandals and placed them to his left. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 3/410/15467]"
"4259","(4259) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Ṣāliḥ ibn Rustum, Abū ʿĀmir, narrated to us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Qays from Yūsuf ibn Māhik from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you prays, let him not place his sandals to his right or to his left, so that they would be to the right of someone else, unless there is no one to his left. Rather, let him place them between his two feet."" The wording of the ḥadīth is that of Abū Dāwūd, both in the isnād and the matn. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 654]"
"4260","(4260) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Shuʿayb al-Kaysānī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī from his father from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""When one of you prays and removes his sandals, let him not cause harm to anyone with them, and let him place them between his two feet, or let him pray in them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 655]"
"4261","(4261) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: ʿAbdillāh — meaning al-Qaʿnabī — narrated to us from Mālik from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you puts on his sandals, let him begin with the right foot, and when he removes them, let him begin with the left foot. Let the right be the first of the two to be put on and the last of the two to be removed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5856]"
"4262","(4262) And with this isnād, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Let none of you walk in a single sandal; rather, let him wear them both or remove them both."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated the second ḥadīth from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik, and the first ḥadīth from the narration of Muḥammad ibn Ziyād from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 5855]"
"4263","(4263) al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī and ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān informed us, both saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated to us from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from his father from Abū Dharr, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which mosque was first placed on the earth?"" He said: ""Al-Masjid al-Ḥarām."" I said: ""Then which?"" He said: ""Then al-Masjid al-Aqṣā."" I said: ""How much time was between them?"" He said: ""Forty years. So wherever the prayer catches you, then pray, for it is a mosque."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb and others from Abū Muʿāwiyah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 3366–3425]"
"4264","(4264) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us: Sayyār narrated to us: Yazīd al-Faqīr narrated to us: Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I have been given five things that no one before me was given: I was aided by terror (ruʿb) for a distance of one month's journey; the spoils of war (ghanāʾim) were made lawful for me, and they were not made lawful for anyone before me; the earth was made for me a place of prayer (masjid) and a means of purification (ṭahūr), so whenever the time of prayer comes upon anyone of my Ummah, let him pray; and I was given intercession (shafāʿah); and every prophet was sent only to his own people specifically, while I was sent to all of mankind generally."" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hushaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 355–438]"
"4265","(4265) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Bukhārī narrated to us: Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, Surayj ibn Yūnus, and Dāwūd ibn Rashīd narrated to us, all saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us from his father from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""I have been favoured over the prophets with six things: I was given comprehensive speech (jawāmiʿ al-kalim); I was aided by terror; the spoils of war were made lawful for me; the earth was made for me a means of purification and a place of prayer; I was sent to all of creation; and the prophets were sealed with me."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 2977]"
"4266","(4266) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Sālim, Abū Ḥammād, narrated to us from al-Suddī from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I was given five things that no prophet before me was given: the earth was made for me a means of purification and a place of prayer, and no prophet among the prophets could pray until he reached his place of prayer (miḥrāb); I was given terror for a distance of one month's journey, which is between me and the polytheists — a distance of one month — and Allah casts terror into their hearts; a prophet was sent only to his specific people, while I was sent to the jinn and mankind; the prophets used to set aside the one-fifth (khums), and fire would come and consume it, but I was commanded to distribute it among the poor of my Ummah; and there remained no prophet except that his supplication was granted, but I have deferred my intercession for my Ummah."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4267","(4267) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us. Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdūyah ibn Sahl al-Marwazī informed us. Abū Dāwūd Sulaymān ibn Maʿbad al-Sanji narrated to us. Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us. Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated to us from Sayyār, from Abū Umāmah that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""I have been given preference by four things: the earth has been made for me a place of prostration (masjid) and a means of purification (ṭahūr). So whenever a man from my Ummah reaches the time of Ṣalāh and finds nothing upon which to pray, he finds the earth to be a masjid and a means of purification. I have been sent to all of mankind. I have been aided by awe (ruʿb) cast into the hearts of those within a two-month journey ahead of me. And the spoils of war (ghanāʾim) have been made lawful for my Ummah.""

And we narrated it in the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh and Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Aḥmad 21706–21632]"
"4268","(4268) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us. ʿAbbās al-Asfaṭī narrated to us. Abū al-Walīd narrated to us. And Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us. Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us. Abū Khalīfah narrated to us. Abū al-Walīd narrated to us. Zuhayr narrated to us from ʿAbdillāh ibn ʪsā, from Mūsā ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Yazīd, from a woman of Banī ʿAbd al-Ashhal who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, between me and the masjid there is a foul path. What should we do when it rains?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Is not the path after it better than it?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'This (good deed) is in recompense for that (hardship).'"" The wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū Khalīfah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited under no. 4102]"
"4269","(4269) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us. Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us. Qays ibn Ḥafṣ ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ narrated to us. ʿAmr ibn al-Nuʿmān narrated to us from Muʿādh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ — Hishām said: and he is the brother of Abū ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAlāʾ — from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""I came with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, to Jumuʿah, and he was walking. A pool of water and mud came between him and the masjid, so he removed his sandals and his trousers. I said: 'Give them to me, O Commander of the Faithful, so that I may carry them for you.' He said: 'No.' So he waded through it, and when he had crossed, he put on his trousers and his sandals, then he led the people in Ṣalāh and did not wash his feet.""

Muʿādh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ is Ibn ʿAmmār, Abū Ghassān. And it has been narrated through another chain from ʿAlī.

And we have narrated from al-Aswad, ʿAlqamah, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, Mujāhid, and a group of the Tābiʿūn regarding its meaning.

[Ḥasan in other than this chain. Ibn al-Mundhir narrated it in al-Awsaṭ 2/171]"
"4270","(4270) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us. ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us. My father narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq, from Yaḥyā ibn Waththāb, who said: ""I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: 'May one perform wuḍūʾ and then walk to the masjid barefoot?' He said: 'There is nothing wrong with that.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn al-Mundhir narrated it in al-Awsaṭ 2/171]"
"4271","(4271) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us. Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us. Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us from Manṣūr, from Tamīm ibn Salamah, that Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, when it was the day of Jumuʿah and there was mud along the way, would carry with him a jug of water. When he reached the masjid, he would wash his feet and then enter the masjid.

And we have narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, Makḥūl, and a group regarding its meaning. And with Allah lies all success.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4272","(4272) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us by dictation. Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us. Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us. Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā, from his father, and Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā, from his father, from Abū Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The entire earth is a masjid except the graveyard (maqbirah) and the bathhouse (ḥammām).""

The ḥadīth of al-Thawrī is mursal. It has been narrated as mawṣūl but it is not established. The ḥadīth of Ḥammād ibn Salamah is mawṣūl, and ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād and al-Dāruwardī followed him in narrating it as mawṣūl. As for the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wāḥid:

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Rajab in al-Fatḥ 2/399]"
"4273","(4273) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us. Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us. Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Musaddad and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb both said: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us. ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā al-Anṣārī narrated to us from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The entire earth is a masjid except the bathhouse (ḥammām) and the graveyard (maqbirah).""

And as for the ḥadīth of al-Dāruwardī:

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited in the one before it]"
"4274","(4274) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us. Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā narrated to us. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to us. ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAmmārah narrated to us from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The entire earth is a masjid except the bathhouse (ḥammām) and the graveyard (maqbirah).""

And it has been narrated from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAmmārah through another chain as mawṣūl."
"4275","(4275) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us. Abū al-Muthannā informed us. Musaddad narrated to us. Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to us. ʿImārah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAmmārah al-Anṣārī, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The entire earth is a masjid except the bathhouse (ḥammām) and the graveyard (maqbirah).""

Some of the scholars used as evidence for the dislike (karāhiyyah) of praying in graveyards the established ḥadīth from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Perform some of your Ṣalāh in your homes and do not make them into graves.""

And the established ḥadīth from ʿĀʾishah and Ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet ﷺ: ""The curse of Allah is upon the Jews and the Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as places of prostration (masājid)."" — warning against doing what they did. Both ḥadīths have been recorded in their proper places.

And we have narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ that he used to dislike a man praying in the bathhouse.

[Previously cited before it]"
"4276","(4276) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us. Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us. ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir, who said: Busr ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated to me. Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī narrated to me. He said: I heard Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ say: Abū Marthad al-Ghanawī narrated to me. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Do not sit upon the graves, and do not pray towards them.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ from Ibn al-Mubārak.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/135]"
"4277","(4277) Muhammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, Abū al-‘Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya‘qūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Hishām narrated to us, Marwān ibn Mu‘āwiya narrated to us, Ḥumayd narrated from Anas, who said: ""One day I was performing Ṣalāh and there was a grave in front of me of which I was unaware. Then ‘Umar called out to me: 'The grave! The grave!' I thought he was referring to al-Qamar (the moon). Then someone beside me said: 'He means the grave.' So I moved away from it.""

And it has been narrated to us from Abū Ẓabyān from Ibn ‘Abbās that he disliked performing Ṣalāh facing a privy, a bathhouse, or a grave.

All of that is on the basis of dislike (karāhiyyah), provided one does not know that the place upon which one's body or clothes fall contains any impurity, because of what has been narrated to us in the authentic ḥadīth from the Prophet ﷺ: ""The earth has been made for me pure (ṭayyibah), a means of purification (ṭahūr), and a place of prostration (masjid). When the time of Ṣalāh comes upon anyone of you, let him pray wherever he is."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī, Mu‘allaq]"
"4278","(4278) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ‘Abdullāh ibn Ja‘far narrated to us, Ya‘qūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me, ‘Abd al-‘Azīz ibn ‘Imrān narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us. He said: ""I asked Nāfi': 'Did Ibn ‘Umar dislike performing Ṣalāh among the graves?' He said: 'We performed the funeral prayer (Ṣalāh) for ‘Ā’ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the middle of al-Baqī‘. The Imām on the day we prayed for ‘Ā’ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Umar was present for that.'"" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Ya‘qūb ibn Sufyān in al-Ma‘rifah wa al-Tārīkh 1/78]"
"4279","(4279) Abū ‘Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-‘Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya‘qūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, ‘Abd al-Wārith narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the best of people in character. Sometimes the time for Ṣalāh would come upon him while he was in our house. He would order the mat beneath him to be swept, then sprinkled with water, then he would stand and we would stand behind him, and he would lead us in prayer."" He said: ""Their mat was made from palm leaves."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīh from Shaybān and others from ‘Abd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2310]"
"4280","(4280) Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-‘Aṭṭār narrated to us, Abū al-‘Abbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ‘Alī al-Warrāq narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ibn ‘Awn from Anas ibn Sīrīn from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered a house in which there was a male (resident). He swept a corner of it, sprinkled water, and then prayed upon it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 4825, Aḥmad 11693–11894]"
"4281","(4281) Abū ‘Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-‘Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya‘qūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ‘Ubaydullāh ibn ‘Ā’ishah narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited Umm Ḥarām. He was brought ghee and dates. He said: ""Return this to its container and this to its water-skin, for I am fasting."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and performed two rak‘ahs of voluntary prayer. Umm Sulaym and Umm Ḥarām stood behind us. Thābit said — and I do not know him to have said anything other than — ""He placed me at his right side, and he led us in voluntary prayer upon his mat as an expression of gratitude."" He then mentioned the remainder of the ḥadīth. Reports have already passed regarding his praying upon a small mat (khumrah), upon a reed mat (ḥaṣīr), and upon a tanned fur (farwah madbūghah). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad Sirāj 1207]"
"4282","(4282) ‘Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri’ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Ya‘qūb narrated to us, ‘Amr ibn Marzūq narrated to us, Mālik ibn Miqwal informed us. He said: ""I heard Muqātil ibn Bashīr narrate from his father from Sharīḥ ibn Hāni’, who said: ""I asked ‘Ā’ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the Ṣalāh of the Prophet ﷺ."" He mentioned the ḥadīth until he said: ""And she said: 'I remember that I saw him pray on a rainy day. We spread beneath him a small mat (batt) with rags in it, and water was seeping through it.'"" (T) Ibn al-Mubārak narrated it from Mālik ibn Miqwal, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""We spread beneath him a small mat (batt),"" meaning a leather carpet (niṭā‘). And he did not mention it from his father. [Ḍa‘īf. Ibn al-Mubārak in al-Zuhd 1272, Aḥmad 23785]"
"4283","(4283) Abū ‘Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī narrated to us, ‘Alī ibn ‘Abd al-‘Azīz narrated to us. He said: Abū ‘Ubayd said regarding the ḥadīth of ‘Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to prostrate upon an ‘abqarī carpet.

Abū ‘Ubayd said: Yaḥyā ibn Sa‘īd narrated it to me from Sufyān from Tawbah al-‘Anbarī from ‘Ikrimah ibn Khālid from ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Ammār, that he saw ‘Umar do that. Yaḥyā said: ""He is ‘Abdullāh ibn Abī ‘Ammār, but Sufyān said: from ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Ammār.""

Abū ‘Ubayd said: ""His saying ‘abqarī refers to these carpets that have dyes and designs upon them; the singular is ‘abqariyyah. It is called ‘abqarī, as it is said, by way of attribution to a land called ‘Abqar, where ornamental work (washy) is produced."" [Ḥasan]"
"4284","(4284) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Ja‘far ibn Muḥammad ibn ‘Alī al-‘Alawī informed us in Kufah, Abū Ja‘far ibn ‘Alī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ‘Abdullāh al-‘Absī informed us, Wakī‘ narrated from al-A‘mash, Sa‘īd ibn Jubayr narrated to us, who said: ""Ibn ‘Abbās led us in Ṣalāh upon a ṭinfisah that had covered the house."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 4054]"
"4285","(4285) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad, Abū ‘Alī Ismā‘īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Ja‘far ibn Muḥammad al-Warrāq narrated to us, ‘Āmir ibn Abī al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated from Ya‘lā ibn Ḥakīm from ‘Ikrimah, who said: ""Ibn ‘Abbās led us in Ṣalāh upon a darnūk that had covered the house, performing rukū‘ and sujūd upon it. I said: 'Do you pray upon this?' He said: 'Yes, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray upon it and prostrate.'"" [Ḍa‘īf]"
"4286","(4286) Abū ‘Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ‘Amr ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr al-‘Adl narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn al-Ḥārith al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, Abū ‘Āṣim al-Nabīl narrated to us, Zama‘ah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Salamah ibn Wahram from ‘Ikrimah from Ibn ‘Abbās, that he prayed upon a carpet and then said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed upon a carpet."" Zama‘ah has another chain of narration for it. [Ḍa‘īf]"
"4287","(4287) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Qāḍī al-Zuhrī in Makkah narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us that Zamʿah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Kurayb from Ibn ʿAbbās that he prayed in Baṣrah upon a mat, and he held the view that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed upon a mat."
"4288","(4288) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl — he being al-Bukhārī — narrated to us that Abū ʀāṣim narrated from al-Awzāʿī from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Sawdah from Khulayd from Umm al-Dardāʾ from Abū al-Dardāʾ who said: ""I would not mind if I were to pray upon five carpets."""
"4289","(4289) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā al-Miṣrī narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to me that ʿAmr informed me that Bukayr narrated to him that ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to him that he heard ʿUbaydullāh al-Khawlānī mentioning that he heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, when the people spoke at the time of the construction of the Mosque of the Messenger ﷺ, saying: ""You have indeed gone to excess. And I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever builds a mosque'"" — Bukayr said: ""I think he said: 'seeking by it the Countenance of Allah, Allah will build for him a house like it in Paradise.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān from Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā."
"4290","(4290) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū ʀāṣim al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad narrated from ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar from his father from Maḥmūd ibn Labīd from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah will build for him a house like it in Paradise.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and others from Abū ʀāṣim."
"4291","(4291) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us that Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from his father from Abū Dharr who said: ""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, even if it be the size of a sandgrouse's nesting hollow, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise."""
"4292","(4292) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from his father from Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, even if it be the size of a sandgrouse's nesting hollow, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise.'""

Al-ʿAbbās said: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus said: ""It was said to Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh: 'The people differ with you regarding this ḥadīth; they do not raise it (to the Prophet).' So Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh said: 'We heard this from al-Aʿmash when he was a young man.'"""
"4293","(4293) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl al-Khuzāʿī informed us that Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us that Quṭbah narrated from al-Aʿmash, and he mentioned it in a raised (marfūʿ) chain: ""Whoever builds a mosque, even if it be the size of a sandgrouse's nesting hollow, a house will be built for him in Paradise.""

And similarly it was narrated from Sharīk and Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd from al-Aʿmash in a raised chain, and it was narrated from al-Ḥakam from Yazīd ibn Sharīk from Abū Dharr in a raised chain."
"4294","(4294) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥamdān al-Qaṭīʿī informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad narrated to me that my father narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us that my father narrated from Ṣāliḥ who narrated to us that Nāfiʿ informed him that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him: ""The mosque in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was built of bricks, its roof was of palm fronds, and its pillars were of palm trunks. Abū Bakr did not add anything to it. ʿUmar added to it and built it upon its original construction in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, using bricks and palm fronds, and he restored its pillars with wood. Then ʿUthmān changed it and added considerable additions to it. He built its walls of carved stone and plaster, and he made its pillars of carved stone, and he roofed it with teak wood (sāj).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī from Yaʿqūb."
"4295","(4295) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Madīnah and settled in the upper part of Madīnah, among a clan called Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, and he stayed among them for fourteen nights. Then he sent for Banū al-Najjār, and they came with their swords girded. Anas said: 'It is as though I am looking at the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon his mount, with Abū Bakr behind him, and the people of Banū al-Najjār surrounding him, until he dismounted in the courtyard of Abū Ayyūb.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray wherever the prayer time overtook him, and he would pray in the sheep pens. He commanded the building of the mosque, so he sent to Banū al-Najjār: 'Give me the price of this walled garden of yours.' They said: 'By Allah, we will not seek its price except from Allah.' Anas said: 'In it were what I shall tell you: it contained the graves of the polytheists, and it contained ruins, and it contained palm trees. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that the graves of the polytheists be dug up, so they were exhumed; and that the ruins be levelled, so they were levelled; and that the palm trees be cut down, so they were cut. They lined up the palm trees as the qiblah of the mosque, and they made its doorposts of stones. They carried the rocks while chanting poetry, and the Prophet ﷺ was with them, and they were saying:

'O Allah, there is no good except the good of the Hereafter,
so grant victory to the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.'"""
"4296","(4296) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām and Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, both saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, and he mentioned it in a similar manner.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā."
"4297","(4297) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Shayban narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Mansur ibn Safiyyat from his maternal uncle Musafi' ibn Shaybah from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, Umm Mansur, who said: A woman from Banu Sulaym, who had given birth to most of the people of our household, informed me. She said: The Prophet ﷺ sent for 'Uthman ibn Talhah and said: ""I saw the horns of the ram when I entered the House, and I forgot to instruct you to slaughter it. Indeed, it is not befitting that there should be anything in the House that distracts one who prays."""
"4298","(4298) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Saffar narrated to us: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Mihran ibn Khalid al-Isbahani narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Qadam narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated.

(Additionally,) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyyaynah narrated from Sufyan al-Thawri from Abu Fazarah from Yazid al-Asam from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I was not commanded to make the mosques lofty."" Ibn 'Abbas said: ""You will certainly adorn them as the Jews and the Christians adorned theirs.""

The wording is that of al-Rudhbari's narration. In the narration of Abu Sa'id, it is ""the mosques"" (al-masajid), and he did not mention ""the Christians."""
"4299","(4299) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us: Abu Tahir al-Muhammadabazi narrated to us: Abu Qilabah narrated to us: 'Affan narrated to us: Hammad narrated to us: Ayyub narrated from Abu Qilabah from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The Hour will not be established until people boast among themselves over the mosques."""
"4300","(4300) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran narrated to us: Abu 'Amr ibn al-Sammak narrated to us: Hasan ibn Salam al-Sawwaq narrated to us: Abu Ghassan narrated to us: Huraym narrated from Layth from Ayyub from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Build the mosques and keep them open (jumman)."""
"4301","(4301) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas al-Qasim ibn al-Qasim al-Sayyari in Marw narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Musa al-Bashani narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us: Abu Hamzah al-Sukkari narrated to us: from Layth from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani from Anas from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""I was commanded to make the mosques open (jumman).""

And from Layth from Salim ibn 'Atiyyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Adorn the people as Musa adorned"" — meaning that he disliked arches (al-taq) around the mosque."
"4302","(4302) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih narrated to us: Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us: al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya ibn Dinar narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us: Huraym ibn Sufyan narrated from Layth from Mujahid from Ibn 'Umar, who said: We were forbidden — or it was prohibited for us — to pray in a mosque that is elevated (mushrif)."
"4303","(4303) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated to us: Abu al-Hasan al-Karizi narrated to us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated. Abu 'Ubayd said regarding the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas: ""We were commanded to build the mosques open (jumman) and the cities with elevated structures (shurfan)."" His statement ""jumman"" refers to that which has no elevated structures (shuraf). Similarly, a building that has no elevated structures is called ""ajam"" (plural: jum)."
"4304","(4304) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah narrated to us: Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Sarraj narrated to us: Mutayyin narrated to us: Sahl ibn Zanjalah al-Razi narrated to us: Abu Zuhayr 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Maghra' narrated from Ibn Abjar from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Beware of these slaughtering places"" — meaning the prayer niches (al-maharib)."
"4305","(4305) Abu 'Ali ibn Shadh al-Baghdadi narrated to us therein: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Darastuyah narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu Muhammad al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Dirham narrated from Ka'b ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari from his father from Abu Qatadah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a people who had laid the foundation of a mosque to build it. He said: ""Make it spacious; fill it (with worshippers)."" He said: So they made it more spacious."
"4306","(4306) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Dirham narrated from Ka'b ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from his father from Abu Qatadah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon a group of the Ansar who were building a mosque for themselves. He said: ""Make it spacious; fill it (with worshippers).""

This is a hadith regarding whose chain of narration there is disagreement."
"4307","(4307) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʢUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Muḥabbab, Abū Hammām al-Dallāl, narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn al-Sāʾib narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿIyāḍ from ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ that the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to place the mosque of Ṭāʾif at the site where their idol (ṭāghiyah) had been. [Weak]"
"4308","(4308) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, ʿĀmir ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Zubayrī narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that mosques be built in the neighbourhoods (al-dūr), and he commanded that they be perfumed and kept clean.

Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah also narrated it from Hishām. (Q) And what is meant by al-dūr are their tribes and clans, and Allah knows best. [Denounced]"
"4309","(4309) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Yaḥyā, i.e., ibn Ḥassān, narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Saʿd ibn Samurah narrated to us, Khubayb ibn Sulaymān narrated to me from his father Sulaymān ibn Samurah from his father Samurah that he wrote to his sons: ""To proceed: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to build mosques in our settlements, to maintain their construction properly, and to keep them clean."" [Weak]"
"4310","(4310) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī informed us, Abū Maʿmar narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn Sulaym narrated to me, Abū al-Walīd said: I asked Ibn ʿUmar: ""What was the origin of this saffron (fragrance) in the mosque?"" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and saw a piece of sputum in the direction of the qiblah of the mosque, and he said: 'Something other than this would be better than this.' A man heard that and brought saffron, scraped it, and then applied saffron in its place. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw that, he said: 'This is better than the first.'"" He said: ""And the people adopted the practice.""

The ḥadīth of Jābir on this matter has already passed in the chapter on spitting. [Weak]"
"4311","(4311) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to me, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr informed me, al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from his father from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet ﷺ missed a black woman who used to pick up rags and sticks from the mosque, and he said: ""Where is so-and-so?"" They said: ""She has died."" And he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth. [Weak]"
"4312","(4312) Al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Rāwād narrated from Ibn Jurayj from al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥanṭab from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The rewards of my Ummah were presented to me, even the reward for a man removing a small pebble from the mosque. And the sins of my Ummah were presented to me, and I did not see a sin greater than a sūrah or a verse of the Qurʾān that a man was given and then forgot it.""

Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah also narrated it from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn al-Ḥakam al-Warrāq. [Weak]"
"4313","(4313) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Abū Maʿmar narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn Sulaym narrated, and Abū al-Walīd said: I asked Ibn ʿUmar about the origin of these pebbles in the mosque. He said: ""Yes, it rained during the night, and we went out for the morning prayer (ṣalāt al-ghadāh). A man would pass over the open ground (al-baṭḥāʾ) and collect pebbles in his garment, then pray on them."" He said: ""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw that, he said: 'How excellent this carpet (bisāṭ) is!'"" That was the first origin of the practice. [Weak]"
"4314","(4314) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Nāfiʿ ibn Isḥāh al-Khuzāʿī in Makkah informed us, al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Muḥammad al-Janadī narrated to me, Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, who said: ""The first person to extend (baṭṭaḥa) the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: 'Extend it to the blessed valley' — meaning al-ʿAqīq."" Thus said ʿUrwah.

The ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar is connected (muttaṣil), and its chain of narration is not objectionable. [Good]"
"4315","(4315) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us, ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us, Isrāʾīl narrated from Abī Ḥuṣayn from Abī Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, or from Kaʿb, who said: ""Indeed, the pebbles of the mosque call out to the one who takes them out of the mosque."" [Authentic]"
"4316","(4316) Al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Nufaylī narrated to us, Miskīn narrated from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz from Ibn Abī Sawdah from Maymūnah, the freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling concerning Bayt al-Maqdis."" He said: ""Go to it and pray therein."" And at that time the lands were in a state of war. He said: ""If you cannot go to it and pray therein, then send oil to be lit in its lamps."" [Authentic]"
"4317","(4317) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Isfarayini narrated to me — Abu Muslim Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated from 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, who said — 'Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id ibn Suwayd al-Ansari narrated to me from Abu Humayd or Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When any one of you enters the mosque, let him offer salutations (salām) and say: 'O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy.' And when he exits, let him say: 'O Allah, I ask You from Your bounty.'"" [Sahih. Ahmad 3/497/16154]"
"4318","(4318) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated to us — Abu Mansur Muhammad ibn al-Qasim, i.e., al-'Ataki, narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Samad al-Quhunduzi narrated to us — Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us — Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman narrated it with a similar wording, except that he did not say: ""let him offer salutations (salām).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and from Hamid ibn 'Umar from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, using the wording of the hadith of Yahya ibn Yahya. The wording of the salām (greeting) in it is preserved (mahfuz)."
"4319","(4319) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan narrated to us in Baghdad — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn 'Uthman ibn Thabit al-Saydalani narrated to us — 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us — Abu al-Jamahir narrated to us — 'Abd al-'Aziz, i.e., al-Darawardi, narrated from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id ibn Suwayd, who said: I heard Abu Humayd or Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When any one of you enters the mosque, let him offer salutations upon the Prophet ﷺ, then let him say: 'O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy.' And when he exits, let him say: 'O Allah, I ask You from Your bounty.'"" [Previously mentioned]"
"4320","(4320) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari narrated to us — Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Uthman al-Dimashqi narrated to us — 'Abd al-'Aziz, i.e., al-Darawardi, narrated from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, and he mentioned it with a similar wording, adding: ""let him offer salutations (salām) or let him send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ."" [Previously mentioned]"
"4321","(4321) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Qazzaz narrated to us — Abu Bakr 'Abd al-Kabir ibn 'Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi narrated to us — al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman narrated to us — Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When any one of you enters the mosque, let him offer salutations upon the Prophet ﷺ and say: 'O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy.' And when he exits, let him offer salutations upon the Prophet ﷺ and say: 'O Allah, protect me from the accursed Satan (al-Shaytan al-Rajim).'"" [Hasan. Ibn Majah 773, Ibn Khuzaymah 452-2706]"
"4322","(4322) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu Hafs 'Umar ibn Ja'far al-Basri al-Mufid narrated to us in Baghdad — Abu Khalifah al-Qadi narrated to us — Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us — Shaddad, Abu Talhah, narrated to us. He said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah narrating from Anas ibn Malik, who used to say: ""It is from the Sunnah that when you enter the mosque, you begin with your right foot, and when you exit, you begin with your left foot.""

Shaddad ibn Sa'id, Abu Talhah al-Rasibi, is the sole narrator of this hadith, and he is not strong (laysa bi-l-qawiyy). [Hasan]"
"4323","(4323) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Faqih narrated to us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us — Aflah ibn Khalifah narrated to us — Jassrah ibn Dajajah narrated to me. She said: I heard 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came when the doors of the houses of his Companions were opening into the mosque. He said: ""Turn these houses away from the mosque."" Then the Prophet ﷺ entered, and the people did nothing, hoping that a concession (rukhsah) might be revealed for them. So he came out to them afterwards and said: ""Turn these houses away from the mosque, for I do not permit the mosque for a menstruating woman (ha'id) or a person in a state of major ritual impurity (junub).""

Abu Dawud said: He is Fulayt al-'Amiri.

The Shaykh said: Musa ibn Isma'il added in it from 'Abd al-Wahid: ""Except for Muhammad ﷺ and the family of Muhammad."" [Da'if]"
"4324","(4324) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Isbahani narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Faris narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari narrated to us — Musa narrated it with his addition.

Al-Bukhari said: ""Jassrah has peculiar narrations ('ajā'ib).""

Al-Bukhari said, and 'Urwah and 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet ﷺ: ""Close these doors except the door of Abu Bakr."" And this is more correct (asahh).

The Shaykh said: And if this is authentic, then regarding the person in a state of major ritual impurity (junub), it is interpreted as referring to remaining (mukth) in the mosque, not to passing through it, based on the evidence of the Book (i.e., the Qur'an). [Da'if. Previously mentioned]"
"4325","(4325) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us — Abu Ja'far, i.e., al-Razi, narrated to us — Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from 'Ata' ibn Yasar from Ibn 'Abbas regarding Allah's statement: ""Nor in a state of major ritual impurity (junub), except as a traveler on a path (ʿābirī sabīl), until you have bathed"" [al-Nisa': 43]. He said: ""Do not enter the mosque while you are in a state of major ritual impurity unless your path lies through it, and do not sit (therein).""

Abu Nu'aym narrated it from Abu Ja'far and said: ""Unless you are a passerby passing through it."" [Hasan]"
"4326","(4326) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Thaqafi narrated in his Tafsir — Ziyad ibn Ayyub narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — Abu al-Zubayr narrated from Jabir, who said: ""Any one of us would pass through the mosque while in a state of major ritual impurity (junub), merely passing through ( mujtāzan)."" [Ibn Khuzaymah 1331]"
"4327","(4327) Abū Ḥāzim informed us, Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Thaqafī informed us, Muḥammad bin Rāfiʿ narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar reported from ʿAbd al-Karīm, from Abū ʿUbaydah bin ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn Masʿūd: that he used to permit the person in a state of janābah to pass through the mosque as a wayfarer. He said: ""And I do not know him except that he said: '…nor in a state of janābah, except as passers-through [a way]' [al-Nisāʾ: 43]."" [ʿAbd al-Razzāq 1613]"
"4328","(4328) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us, Ibrāhīm bin Marzūq informed us, Muslim bin Ibrāhīm reported from al-Ḥasan bin Abī Jaʿfar al-Azdī, from Sarm al-ʿAlawī, from Anas bin Mālik regarding His statement — Exalted is He — ""…nor in a state of janābah, except as passers-through [a way]"" [al-Nisāʾ: 43]: He said: ""He may pass through but may not sit."" [Weak]"
"4329","(4329) Abū Bakr bin al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad bin Ḥayyān informed us, Ibrāhīm bin Muḥammad bin al-Ḥasan informed us, Aḥmad bin ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, al-Walīd bin Muslim informed us, Abū ʿAmr said: I heard ʿAṭāʾ say: ""The menstruating woman and the person in a state of janābah shall not undo braids or plaits, and no menstruating woman shall pass through the mosque except under duress."" [Weak]"
"4330","(4330) Muḥammad bin ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad bin Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Aḥmad bin Salamah informed us, Qutaybah bin Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth reported from Saʿīd bin Abī Saʿīd that he heard Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent cavalry toward Najd, and they brought a man from Banī Ḥanīfah called Thumāmah bin Uthāl. They tied him to one of the pillars of the mosque, and he mentioned the hadith regarding his acceptance of Islam."" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1764]"
"4331","(4331) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad bin Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿĪsā bin Ḥammād informed us, al-Layth reported from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Sharīk bin ʿAbd Allāh bin Abī Namir that he heard Anas bin Mālik say: ""A man entered upon a camel, made it kneel in the mosque, then hobbled it, and said: 'Which one of you is Muḥammad?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was reclining among them. We said to him: 'This white man, reclining.' The man then said: 'O son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.' The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'I have answered you.' The man said: 'O Muḥammad, I am going to ask you.' And he narrated the hadith."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh bin Yūsuf. It is also narrated from Kurayb from Ibn ʿAbbās, who mentioned it and named the man Ḍimām bin Thaʿlabah, and said on the authority of al-Layth: ""He made his camel kneel at the door of the mosque, then hobbled it, then entered the mosque."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 63, Ibn Khuzaymah 2358]"
"4332","(4332) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr bin Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad bin Yaḥyā bin Fāris narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar reported from al-Zuhrī, a man from Muzaynah narrated to us while we were with Saʿīd bin al-Musayyib, from Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""The Jews came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was sitting in the mosque among his Companions, and they said: 'O Abā al-Qāsim, [what is the ruling] concerning a man and a woman among them who committed adultery?'"" [Weak. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 13330]"
"4333","(4333) Abū Bakr Muḥammad bin al-Ḥasan bin Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh bin Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus bin Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Shuʿbah reported from Saʿīd bin Ibrāhīm, who said: ""One of my brothers narrated to me from my father, from Jubayr bin Muṭʿim, who said: 'I came to Madīnah for the ransom of [the prisoners of] Badr.' He said: 'And at that time I was a polytheist.' He said: 'I entered the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying the Maghrib prayer, reciting al-Ṭūr in it. It was as if my heart was split open by the recitation of the Qurʾān.'"" [Weak]"
"4334","(4334) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī bin Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan bin Muḥammad bin Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf bin Yaʿqūb informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Ḥammād bin Salamah reported from Ḥumayd, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿUthmān bin Abī al-ʿĀṣ: ""That the delegation of Thaqīf came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he lodged them in the mosque so that it might soften their hearts. They stipulated upon the Prophet ﷺ that they would not be gathered [for prayer], nor would they be levied the ʿushr [tithe], nor would they be subjugated, and no one from among others would be appointed as governor over them. He said: 'You shall not be gathered, nor levied the ʿushr, nor subjugated, and no one from among others shall be appointed as governor over you. And there is no good in a religion in which there is no bowing [rukūʿ].'"" [Weak]"
"4335","(4335) Abū Bakr bin Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh bin Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus bin Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ḥammād bin Salamah reported from Ḥumayd, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿUthmān bin Abī al-ʿĀṣ: ""That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lodged them in a tent in the mosque, so that it might soften their hearts. Then he mentioned the hadith regarding their stipulations when they accepted Islam."" It is also narrated by al-Ashʿath from al-Ḥasan, who sent it as a mursal narration with part of its meaning, adding: ""It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, did you lodge them in the mosque while they are polytheists?' He said: 'The earth does not become impure; rather it is the son of Ādam that becomes impure.'"" [Previously mentioned]"
"4336","(4336) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Bakr Aḥmad bin Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Muthannā informed us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā reported from ʿUbayd Allāh, who said: ""Nāfiʿ narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh: that he used to sleep as a young unmarried man in the mosque of the Prophet ﷺ."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 440, Muslim 2479]"
"4337","(4337) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, from Talhah al-Nasri, who said: I came to Madinah as a migrant. When a man would come to Madinah as a migrant, if he had an acquaintance ('arif), he would stay with him; if he had no acquaintance, he would stay in the Suffah. I arrived without having any acquaintance there, so I stayed in the Suffah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pair two men together and divide a mudd of dates between them. One day, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in his prayer, a man called out to him, saying: ""O Messenger of Allah, the dates have burned our bellies and our chests have split."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised Allah and glorified Him, then mentioned what he had suffered from his people, and then said: ""I and my companion remained for more than ten nights with no food except barir."" — And barir is the fruit of the arak tree: ""Until we came to our brothers among the Ansar, and they provided for us from their food, the greater part of which was dates. By Allah, besides Whom there is no deity, if I were able to provide you with bread and meat, I would certainly feed you. A time will come — or one among you will reach it — when you will wear garments like the curtains of the Ka'bah, and you will be served morning and evening with platters of food."" They asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, are we better then or will we be better on that day?"" He ﷺ said: ""Nay, you are better today. Today you are brothers, whereas on that day you will strike one another's necks."" [Sahih. Ahmad 15558, Ibn Hibban 6684]"
"4338","(4338) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Ma'dani narrated to us in Marw: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Buznani narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sayyar narrated to us: Abu Hamid al-Rawwadi informed me: 'Uthman ibn al-Yaman said: When the Muhajirun became numerous in Madinah and had no homes or shelter, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed them in the mosque and called them the Companions of the Suffah. He would sit with them and keep them company.

And it has been related to us from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib: He was asked about sleeping in the mosque, and he said: ""Where would the People of the Suffah sleep?"" — meaning they would sleep in it. [Da'if]"
"4339","(4339) Abu 'Abd Allah Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Susi informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Baghdadi informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us: 'Umar ibn Dharr narrated to us: Mujahid narrated that Abu Hurayradh, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: By Allah, besides Whom there is no deity, I used to press my liver against the ground out of hunger, and I used to tie a stone over my stomach out of hunger. One day I sat on the path by which they would go out, and Abu Bakr passed by me. I asked him about a verse from the Book of Allah, the Exalted — I asked him only so that he would take me with him — but he passed by and did not do so. Then 'Umar passed by me, and I asked him about a verse from the Book of Allah — I asked him only so that he would take me with him — but he passed by and did not do so. Then Abu al-Qasim ﷺ passed by me, and when he saw me he smiled, and he recognized what was in my soul and what was on my face. Then he said: ""Ya Aba Hirr!"" I said: ""Here I am, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Al-Haqq"" (Come along). He went, and I followed him. He entered, and I sought permission, and he granted me permission, so I entered. He found milk in a vessel, and he said: ""Where has this milk come from?"" They said: ""So-and-so or so-and-so's wife gifted it to you."" He said: ""Aba Hirr!"" I said: ""Here I am, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Go to the People of the Suffah and invite them to me."" The People of the Suffah were the guests of Islam — they had no family nor wealth. When charity came to him, he would send it to them and would not take anything from it. When a gift came to him, he would send it to them and would partake of it and include them in it. That troubled me. I said: ""What is this milk to the People of the Suffah? I was hoping to get a drink from it to strengthen myself, and the messenger would be with me. If he comes, he will command me to give it to them, so what hope do I have of any of this milk?"" Yet there was no alternative but to obey Allah and His Messenger. So I went to them and invited them, and they came and sought permission, and he granted them permission, and they took their places in the house. He said: ""Ya Aba Hirr!"" I said: ""Here I am, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Take it and give it to them."" So I took the vessel and began giving it to a man, and he would drink until he was satisfied, then return the vessel to me, and I would give it to another, and he would drink until he was satisfied, then return the vessel to me, until I reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and all the people had been satisfied. He took the vessel, placed it on his hand, looked at me, and smiled. He said: ""Ya Aba Hirr!"" I said: ""Here I am, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""I and you remain."" I said: ""You have spoken truly, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Sit and drink."" So I sat and drank. He said: ""Drink."" So I drank. He said: ""Drink."" So I drank. He kept saying: ""Drink, drink,"" until I said: ""No, by the One Who sent you with the truth, I find no more room for it."" He said: ""Give it to me."" So I gave him the vessel, and he praised Allah, mentioned His name, and drank the remainder. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Nu'aym. [Sahih. Bukhari 6246–6452]"
"4340","(4340) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Faddl ibn Ibrahim informed us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated from Abi Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: A man from the family of Marwan was appointed governor over Madinah. He called for Sahl ibn Sa'd and ordered him to revile 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Sahl refused, so he said to him: ""Since you refuse, then say: 'May Allah curse Abu Turab.'"" Sahl said: ""Ali had no name more beloved to him than Abu Turab, and he would indeed be joyful when called by that."" He said to him: ""Tell us the story of how he came to be called Abu Turab."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the house of Fatimah but did not find 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in the house. He said to her: 'Where is your cousin's son?' She said: 'Something occurred between me and him; he became angry and left, and did not stay with me.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a man: 'Look where he is.' The man came and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, he is sleeping in the mosque.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him as he was lying down, his cloak having slipped from his side, and dust had covered him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began wiping it off him, saying: 'Rise, Abu Turab, rise, Abu Turab.'"" Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in their Sahihs from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. Bukhari 3703, Muslim 2409]"
"4341","(4341) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Isa narrated to us: Yunus narrated that al-Hasan was asked about taking a midday nap (qaylulah) in the mosque. He said: ""I saw 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and he was then the caliph, taking a midday nap in the mosque. When he would rise, the marks of pebbles would be visible on his side, and he would say: 'This is the Commander of the Faithful, this is the Commander of the Faithful.'""

Yunus said with his finger, and Abu Bakr moved his index finger, and at that time we were boys. I said to Yunus: ""How old was al-Hasan on the day 'Uthman was killed?"" He said: ""Fourteen years old. Al-Hasan was born when two years remained of the caliphate of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him.""

And it has been related to us from Ibn Mas'ud, Ibn 'Abbas, then from Mujahid and Sa'id ibn Jubayr, indicating their dislike of sleeping in the mosque, as though they preferred that one who had a dwelling should not resort to the mosque for sleeping. [Da'if]"
"4342","(4342) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'Atikah al-Azdi narrated to us: 'Umayr ibn Han'i al-'Ansi narrated from Abu Hurayradh, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever comes to the mosque for a purpose, that is his share (hazz)."" [Abu Dawud 472]"
"4343","(4343) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from Abu 'Abd Allah, the mawla of Shaddad ibn al-Had, who informed him that he heard Abu Hurayradh, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said —

And Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd Allah al-Turqufi narrated to us: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us: Haywah said: I heard Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal say: Abu 'Abd Allah informed me that he heard Abu Hurayradh, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Whoever hears a man announcing a lost item in the mosque, let him say: 'May Allah not return it to you, for mosques were not built for this.'"" This is the wording of the hadith of Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri'.

And in the hadith of Ibn Wahb: ""May Allah not restore it to you, for mosques were not built for this.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Tahir from Ibn Wahb and from Zuhayr ibn Harb from al-Muqri'. [Hasan. Ahmad 9161–8382]"
"4344","(4344) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man saying in the mosque: ""Who will call to the red camel?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""You will not find it. Mosques were only built for what they were built for."" Muslim narrated it from the hadith of 'Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri. [Muslim 569, Ibn Khuzaymah 1301]"
"4345","(4345) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us: Yazid ibn Khusayfah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban narrated from Abu Hurayradh, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When you see anyone buying or selling in the mosque, say: 'May Allah not make your trade profitable.' And when you see anyone announcing a lost item in it, say: 'May Allah not return it to you.'"" [Hasan. Tirmidhi 1321, Ibn Hibban 1650]"
"4346","(4346) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Hayyan, known as Abu al-Shaykh al-Isfahani, informed us: Abu Khalifah narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us: al-Ju'ayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me: Yazid ibn Khusayfah narrated from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, who said: I was sleeping in the mosque when a man threw pebbles at me. I looked, and it was 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: ""Go and bring these two men to me."" I brought them to him, and he said: ""Where are you two from?"" They said: ""From the people of Ta'if."" He said: ""If you were from the people of this city, I would punish you. You are raising your voices in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madini. [Sahih 470]"
"4347","(4347) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to me, al-Layth narrated from Ibn ʿAjlān, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""He forbade the recitation of poetry in the mosque."" [Ḥasan. Tirmidhī 322, Nasāʾī 714]"
"4348","(4348) And Abū al-Ḥasan informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥinnāʾī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Bishr narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated — and he mentioned it similarly, adding: his prohibition against announcing lost property in the mosque, and against selling and buying in the mosque. [Ḥasan. Previously mentioned]"
"4349","(4349) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: Ḥassān ibn Thābit recited poetry in the mosque, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by him and glared at him, saying: ""In the mosque?"" He said: ""By Allah, I used to recite in the presence of one who was better than you."" He said: ""He feared that he would implicate the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he let him pass and left him alone.""

And from Ibn al-Musayyab that Ḥassān ibn Thābit said — meaning to a group among whom was Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: ""I adjure you by Allah, did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Answer on my behalf; may Allah aid you with the Rūḥ al-Qudus (the Holy Spirit)'?"" He said: ""O Allah, yes.""

Muslim narrated the musnad hadith from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and others from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and he narrated the story of ʿUmar from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah from al-Zuhrī. And we see no harm in the recitation of the kind of poetry that Ḥassān used to compose in defence of Islam and its people, neither in the mosque nor elsewhere. The first hadith pertains to the recitation of the poetry of Jāhiliyyah and other poetry that is inappropriate for the mosque. And with Allah lies success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3040, Muslim 2484]"
"4350","(4350) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh and Abū Bakr ibn Bālawayh both said: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr informed us, Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, Zāʾidah narrated from Simāk, from Jaʿfar ibn Abī Thawr, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ while I was with him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, should I perform ablution after eating the meat of sheep?"" He said: ""If you wish, do so; and if you wish, leave it."" He said: ""May I pray in the sheepfolds?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Should I perform ablution after eating the meat of camels?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""May I pray in the resting places of camels?"" He said: ""No.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr. [Ḥasan… Ṭayālisī 803, Muslim 360]"
"4351","(4351) Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us, Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Kūfī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah informed us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated from Shaybān, from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mawhab and Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from Jaʿfar ibn Abī Thawr, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to pray in the sheepfolds and not to pray in the resting places of camels.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā ibn Dīnār from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā. [Ḥasan. Ibn Abī Shaybah 36056, Ibn Ḥibbān 1125]"
"4352","(4352) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated from Saʿīd — that is, Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""When you come upon the resting places of camels, do not pray therein; and when you come upon the resting places of sheep, pray therein if you wish."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 4/85/16911]"
"4353","(4353) And Abū Zakariyyā informed us, Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī in al-Kūfah informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq informed us, Jaʿfar — meaning Ibn ʿAwn — informed us, Saʿīd narrated it with his chain of narration, except that he said: ""When you are in the resting places of camels, do not pray; and when you are in the sheepfolds, pray therein if you wish."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"4354","(4354) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ḥarmalah — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Sabrah — narrated to me, my uncle — meaning ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Sabrah — narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Pray in the enclosures of sheep, and do not pray in the enclosures of camels."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4355","(4355) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl and Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr both said: Yazīd ibn Zuraʿy narrated to us, Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the prayer time arrives and you find nothing but sheepfolds and the resting places of camels, then pray in the sheepfolds and do not pray in the resting places of camels."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 795, Ibn Ḥibbān 2317]"
"4356","(4356) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Abū Mūsā informed us; both said: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, al-Aʿmash narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about praying in the resting places of camels, and he said: ""Do not pray therein, for they are from the devils."" And he was asked about praying in the sheepfolds, and he said: ""Pray therein, for they are a blessing.""

Their two hadiths are the same. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 493]"
"4357","(4357) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us, Hushaym informed us, Yūnus narrated from al-Ḥasan from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Pray in the resting places of sheep, and do not pray in the enclosures of camels, for they were created from the devils.""

A group narrated it from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd. Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ said from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd: ""We were commanded,"" without mentioning the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced under number 4352]"
"4358","(4358) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, Abū al-Qāsim al-Sarrāj, and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ṭalḥah ibn Karīz from al-Ḥasan from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When the prayer overtakes you while you are in the resting place of sheep, then pray therein, for it is a place of tranquility and blessing. And when the prayer overtakes you while you are in the enclosures of camels, then come out of it and pray elsewhere, for they are jinn created from jinn. Do you not see that when they become agitated, they raise their noses?""

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said in the narration of Abū Saʿīd, regarding the statement of the Prophet ﷺ: ""Do not pray in the enclosures of camels, for they are jinn created from jinn"" — this is evidence that he prohibited it just as he said when he overslept and missed the prayer: ""Take us out of this valley, for it is a valley in which there is a devil."" He disliked praying near a devil, and likewise he disliked praying near camels because they were created from jinn — not due to the impurity of their location. And regarding sheep, he said: ""They are from the animals of Paradise.""

The Sheikh said: As for the hadith regarding oversleeping and missing the prayer, it has already passed. And as for his hadith regarding sheep — [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced]"
"4359","(4359) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿUmar ibn Sinān informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Kāsib informed us, Ibn Abī Ḥāzim informed us from Kathīr ibn Zayd from al-Walīd ibn Rabāḥ from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Pray in the resting places of sheep and wipe their nasal discharge, for they are from the animals of Paradise.""

Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī from al-Zuhrī from Ibn al-Musayyab from him from the Prophet ﷺ. Ḥumayd ibn Mālik narrated it from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, as a mawqūf narration from him, and it was also said to be marfūʿ, though the mawqūf version is more authentic. We also received it through another chain as marfūʿ. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4360","(4360) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥājib informed us, Sakhtawayh ibn Māziyār informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUyaynah informed us. He said: I heard Abā Ḥayyān mention from Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Sheep are from the animals of Paradise, so wipe their nasal discharge and pray in their resting places.""

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: He commanded that one pray in their resting places — meaning, and Allah knows best, in the place to which the name of their resting place applies, where there is no dung or urine.

He said: And I consider it disliked (makrūh) to pray in the enclosures of camels, even if there is no filth therein, due to the prohibition of the Prophet ﷺ. If one were to pray there, his prayer would be valid, because the Prophet ﷺ was praying when a devil passed by him, and he choked it until he felt the coldness of its tongue upon his hand, and that did not invalidate his prayer. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4361","(4361) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz informed us, Aḥmad ibn Badīl informed us, Muḍḍal ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us, Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: We prayed an obligatory prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he tucked his hand during the prayer. When he finished, we said: ""O Messenger of Allah, has something new occurred regarding the prayer?"" He said: ""No, except that the devil attempted to pass in front of me, so I choked him until I felt the coldness of his tongue upon my hand. By Allah, had my brother Sulaymān not preceded me in his supplication, I would have tied him to one of the pillars of the mosque until the children of the people of Madīnah played around him.""

The meaning of this has already appeared in the hadith of Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and in the hadith of Ibn Masʿūd, and in the hadith of Abū al-Dardāʾ. [Ḥasan. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 2338, Aḥmad 20495]"
"4362","(4362) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us, Tamīm ibn Muḥammad informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us, Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated from ʿUbaydullāh from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Prophet ﷺ prayed toward a she-camel.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl from Abū Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 430, Muslim 502]"
"4363","(4363) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Ibn Numayr informed us, and al-Manīʿī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us. They both said: Abū Khālid informed us, and he mentioned it similarly, except that al-Manīʿī said: ""toward his she-camel.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr.

Although this is not a prayer in the enclosure of camels, it is a prayer in proximity to camels. The prayer was permissible due to the purity of the location, just as prayer near a devil was disliked in another hadith, yet when the devil passed by him during his prayer and he choked him, it did not invalidate his prayer. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced in the one before it]"
"4364","(4364) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Ibn Lahīʿah and Yaḥyā ibn Azhar narrated from ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd al-Murādī from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ghifārī that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by Bābyl while he was traveling. The muezzin came to him to inform him of the ʿAṣr prayer. When he emerged from it, he commanded the muezzin, who then called the iqāmah for the prayer. When he finished, he said: ""My beloved ﷺ forbade me from praying in a graveyard, and he forbade me from praying in the land of Bābyl, for it is a cursed land."" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 490–491]"
"4365","(4365) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Yaḥyā ibn Azhar and Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Shaddād from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ghifārī from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, with the meaning of the hadith of Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd. He said: ""When he came out from it"" — in place of ""when he emerged.""

(T) It has been narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Muḥill al-ʿĀmirī, who said: We were with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, and we passed by the sunken land of Bābyl, and he did not pray until he had passed beyond it.

And from Ḥujr al-Ḥaḍramī from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""I would not pray in a land that Allah has caused to sink three times.""

(Q) This prohibition regarding prayer therein, if it is established as marfūʿ, is not for a meaning that pertains to the prayer itself. If one were to pray therein, he would not need to repeat it. Rather it is — and Allah knows best — as [Ḍaʿīf. Previously referenced]"
"4366","(4366) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī informed us in Makkah, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī informed us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿUmar that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Do not enter upon these people"" — meaning the people of Thamūd — ""unless you are weeping. And if you are not weeping, then I fear that what befell them will befall you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī, Muslim 2980]"
"4367","(4367) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Asfāṭī — meaning ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl — informed us, Ismāʿīl informed us, Mālik narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿUmar that the Prophet ﷺ said to his Companions: ""Do not pass by these people"" — meaning the punished ones — ""unless you are weeping. And if you are not weeping, then do not enter upon them, lest that which befell them befall you.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it from another route from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See earlier.]"
"4368","(4368) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd ibn Aḥmad al-Zawzanī informed us, Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī narrated from ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī from Sālim from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by al-Ḥijr, he said: ""Do not enter the dwellings of those who wronged themselves, unless you are weeping, lest there befall you the like of what befell them."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ covered his head and hastened his pace until he crossed the valley.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Musnadī from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus from al-Zuhrī.

He (the Prophet ﷺ) desired to leave those dwellings and disliked staying in them except while weeping. Thus, staying there for Ṣalāh or other purposes is included in this. And with Allah is the success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See earlier.]"
"4369","(4369) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us — dictating — Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, Abū ʿUmar al-Ḥawḍī informed us, Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbdullāh narrated from Qatādah from Abū al-ʿĀliyah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Reliable men testified before me — among them ʿUmar, and the most pleasing of them to me was ʿUmar — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade Ṣalāh, or said: 'There is no Ṣalāh after al-Ṣubḥ until the sun rises, and after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets.'""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī ʿUmar, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 586, Muslim 827.]"
"4370","(4370) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, Musaddad informed us, Yaḥyā narrated from Shuʿbah from Qatādah, who said: I heard Abā al-ʿĀliyah narrating from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""People narrated to me — the most pleasing of them to me was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade Ṣalāh after al-Fajr until the sun rises, and Ṣalāh after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See earlier.]"
"4371","(4371) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, he said: I read to Mālik from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade Ṣalāh after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets, and Ṣalāh after al-Ṣubḥ until the sun rises.

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See earlier. Bukhārī 588, Muslim 825.]"
"4372","(4372) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from Khubayb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade two prayers: Ṣalāh after al-Fajr until the sun rises, and Ṣalāh after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets.

Both (al-Bukhārī and Muslim) narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See earlier.]"
"4373","(4373) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us, Rūḥ ibn ʿĀbidah informed us, Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah and Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbdullāh narrated from Qatādah from Qazāʿah from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Journeys are not to be undertaken except to three mosques: the Mosque of Ibrāhīm (i.e., al-Masjid al-Ḥarām), the Mosque of Muḥammad (i.e., al-Masjid al-Nabawī), and al-Masjid al-Aqṣā."" And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade Ṣalāh at two times: after al-ʿAṣr until the sun sets, and after al-Ghadāh (morning) until the sun rises. And he forbade fasting on two days: the day of al-Fiṭr and the day of al-Aḍḥā. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a woman from travelling for more than three days except with a maḥram.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿArūbah and Hishām, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah from Qatādah, and both narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yazīd al-Laythī and Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah al-Māzinī from Abū Saʿīd regarding the prohibition of these two prayers. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1197.]"
"4374","(4374) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū Ṣāliḥ and Ibn Bukayr both said: al-Layth informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī informed us, al-Layth — meaning Ibn Saʿd — narrated from Khayr ibn Nuʿaym al-Ḥaḍramī from Ibn Hubayrah — meaning ʿAbdullāh — from Abū Tamīm al-Jayshānī from Abū Baṣrah al-Ghifārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in al-ʿAṣr prayer at al-Mukhmiṣ and said: ""This prayer was presented to those before you, and they neglected it. Whoever guards it shall have his reward twice, and there is no Ṣalāh after it until the witness (al-shāhid) rises.""

Al-shāhid means the star. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Qutaybah. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 830, Ibn Ḥibbān 1744.]"
"4375","(4375) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥamdān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad informed us, he said: My father informed us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ, who said: I heard Ḥumrān ibn Abān narrating from Muʿāwiyah, who said: ""You indeed perform a prayer that we accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and we never saw him perform it, and he forbade it"" — meaning the two rakʿahs after al-ʿAṣr.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abān from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar Ghundar. (T) And likewise it was narrated by Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh and Muḥammad ibn Bakr from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 587.]"
"4376","(4376) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, Abū al-Tayyāḥ informed me from Maʿbad al-Juhanī, who said: Muʿāwiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) delivered a sermon and said: ""What is the matter with people who perform a prayer? I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and I never saw him perform it, and we heard him forbid it"" — meaning the two rakʿahs after al-ʿAṣr.

And likewise it was narrated by ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar from Shuʿbah, and it is as though Abū al-Tayyāḥ heard it from both of them. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See earlier.]"
"4377","(4377) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād that Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us that Saʿdān informed us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Hishām ibn Ḥujayr, who said: Ṭāwūs used to perform two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr. Ibn ʿAbbās said to him: ""Leave them."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only prohibited them from being taken as a regular practice (sullam)."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Indeed, the Prophet ﷺ forbade prayer after ʿAṣr, so I do not know whether you will be punished for them or rewarded, for Allah, the Most High, said: *'It is not for a believing man or a believing woman, when Allah and His Messenger have decided a matter, that they should have any choice in their affair'* [al-Aḥzāb: 36]."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Dārimī 434]"
"4378","(4378) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us — among the last of what he narrated — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Mālik informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ṣaffār narrated that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated — he said: I read to Mālik — from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""Let none of you seek to pray at the rising of the sun or at its setting.""** Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 585, Muslim 828]"
"4379","(4379) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us that Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that the Prophet ﷺ said: **""Do not deliberately time your prayers with the rising of the sun or its setting, for it rises between the two horns of Satan.""** Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See above. Bukhārī, Muslim]"
"4380","(4380) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated that Musaddad narrated that Yaḥyā narrated from Hishām from his father from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""When the edge of the sun appears, delay the prayer until it rises fully, and when the edge of the sun begins to set, delay the prayer until it has completely set.""** Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Numayr and Muḥammad ibn Bishr from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See above]"
"4381","(4381) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī narrated that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated that Wuhayb narrated — he said: Ibn Ṭāwūs narrated to me from his father from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: ""ʿUmar was mistaken. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only prohibited deliberately timing prayer with the rising and setting of the sun."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Wuhayb. She said that — and Allah knows best — because she had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform the two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr, and those two rakʿahs, as established from her and from Umm Salamah, were performed as makeup (qaḍāʾ). And the Prophet ﷺ, when he performed an action, he would make it consistent. As for the prohibition, it is firmly established from the Prophet ﷺ through ʿUmar and others, as has been mentioned above. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 833]"
"4382","(4382) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī narrated that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUlayy ibn Rabāḥ al-Lakhmī — I heard my father say: I heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir say: **""There are three hours in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us from praying therein or burying our dead: when the sun is rising until it has fully risen, when the sun is at its zenith until it begins to decline, and when the sun is tilting toward setting until it has completely set.""** [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 831, Ibn Ḥibbān 1546]"
"4383","(4383) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn and Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUlayy ibn Rabāḥ from his father, and he mentioned it with the same meaning, except that he said: ""He forbade us"" (yanhānā), and he said: ""setting"" (al-ghurūb), and he did not say: ""the one standing"" (qāʾim), and he said: ""until it declines"" (tamīl). Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā."
"4384","(4384) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakkī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Mālik informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated that Ibn Qaʿnab and Ibn Bukayr narrated from Mālik from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣunābihī that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""The sun rises with the horn of Satan accompanying it; when it rises fully, it departs from it. Then, when it reaches its zenith, it accompanies it again; when it declines, it departs from it. Then, when it approaches setting, it accompanies it again; when it sets, it departs from it.""** And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade prayer during those hours. This is how Mālik ibn Anas narrated it. And Maʿmar ibn Rāshid narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abī ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣunābihī. Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said: The correct narration is that of Maʿmar, and he is Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣunābihī, whose name is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUṣaylah. [Weak. Mālik 182]"
"4385","(4385) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated that my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated that Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated that Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Azdī narrated that al-Naḍr ibn Muḥammad narrated that ʿIkrimah narrated that Shaddād ibn ʿAbd Allāh Abū ʿAmmār and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from Abī Umāmah — ʿIkrimah said: Shaddād met Abī Umāmah and Wāthilah, and he accompanied Anas to al-Shām, and he praised him with virtue and goodness — from Abī Umāmah, who said: ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah al-Sulamī said: ""I was in the Jāhiliyyah, and I used to think that the people were upon misguidance and that they were upon nothing, while they were worshipping idols. I heard of a man in Makkah who was reporting news, so I mounted my camel and went to him. I found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in concealment, his people being audacious toward him. I made my way gently until I entered upon him in Makkah and said: 'Who are you?' He said: **'I am a Prophet.'** I said: 'What is a Prophet?' He said: **'Allah has sent me.'** I said: 'With what has He sent you?' He said: **'He has sent me to join the ties of kinship, to break the idols, and to declare Allah's Oneness, that nothing be associated with Him.'** I said: 'Who is with you in this?' He said: **'A free man and a slave.'** He said: At that time, Abū Bakr and Bilāl were among those who had believed in him. I said: 'I will follow you.' He said: **'You cannot bear that today. Do you not see my condition and the condition of the people? But return to your family, and when you hear that I have prevailed, come to me.'** I went to my family, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Madīnah. I was with my family, and I kept inquiring about the news and asking every person who came from the people, until a group from the people of Yathrib, from the people of Madīnah, came to me. I said: 'What has this man done who came to Madīnah?' They said: 'The people are rushing toward him, and his people wanted to kill him, but they could not.' I came to Madīnah and entered upon him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, do you recognize me?' He said: **'Yes. Are you not the one who met me in Makkah?'** I said: 'O Prophet of Allah, tell me about what Allah has taught me and I do not know. Tell me about the prayer.' He said: **'Perform the Ṣubḥ prayer, then refrain from prayer until the sun rises and ascends, for it rises between the two horns of Satan, and at that time the disbelievers prostrate to it. Then pray, for the prayer is witnessed and attended, until the shadow is equal to a spear. Then refrain from prayer, for at that time Hell is kindled. Then when the shadow lengthens, pray, for the prayer is witnessed and attended, until you pray ʿAṣr. Then refrain from prayer until the sun sets, for it sets between the two horns of Satan, and at that time the disbelievers prostrate to it.'** I said: 'O Prophet of Allah, tell me about wuḍūʾ.' He said: **'There is no man among you who brings his wuḍūʾ water, rinses his mouth, and snuffs water into his nose and then blows it out, except that the sins of his face fall from the edges of his beard and his nostrils along with the water. Then he washes his hands up to the elbows, except that the sins of his hands fall from his fingertips along with the water. Then he wipes his head, except that the sins of his head fall from the edges of his hair along with the water. Then he washes his feet up to the ankles, except that the sins of his feet fall from his fingertips along with the water. Then if he stands and prays, praising Allah, glorifying Him, and extolling Him as He deserves, and devotes his heart to Allah, he departs from his sin in the same state as the day his mother bore him.'** ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah narrated this ḥadīth to Abī Umāmah, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Abī Umāmah said to him: 'O ʿAmr, look at what you are saying — that a man is given all of this in one sitting!' ʿAmr said: 'O Abī Umāmah, my age has advanced, my bones have weakened, and my appointed time has drawn near. I have no need to lie upon Allah or upon His Messenger. Had I not heard it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except once, twice, or three times — up to seven times — I would never have narrated it. But I heard it more than that.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Maʿqirī from al-Naḍr ibn Muḥammad, except that he added in the mention of wuḍūʾ, at his words ""then he blows it out, except that the sins of his face, in it, and his nostrils fall with the water,"" and then: ""when he washes his face as Allah has commanded him, except that the sins of his face fall from the edges of his beard with the water."" It is as though this portion fell from our copy. It has a corroborating witness from the ḥadīth of Abī Sallām from Abī Umāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 16571, Muslim 832]"
"4386","(4386) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated that Abū Dāwūd narrated that al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ narrated that Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated from al-ʿAbbās ibn Sālim from Abī Sallām from Abī Umāmah from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah al-Sulamī, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which part of the night is most heard?"" He said: **""The last part of the night. So pray as much as you wish, for the prayer is witnessed and recorded until you pray Ṣubḥ. Then refrain until the sun rises and ascends to the measure of a spear or two spears, for it rises between the two horns of Satan, and the disbelievers pray to it. Then pray as much as you wish, for the prayer is witnessed and recorded until the spear is equal to its shadow. Then refrain, for Hell is kindled and its gates are opened. Then when the sun declines, pray as much as you wish, for the prayer is witnessed until you pray ʿAṣr. Then refrain until the sun sets, for it sets between the two horns of Satan, and the disbelievers pray to it.""** He said: Then he narrated a long ḥadīth. [Ibn Khuzaymah 260]"
"4387","(4387) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us, saying: al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān narrated to me, from al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am asking you about a matter of which you are knowledgeable and of which I am ignorant. Are there, among the hours of the night and the day, hours in which prayer is disliked?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes. When you have prayed the morning prayer (al-Ṣubḥ), then leave the prayer until the sun rises, for it rises between the two horns of Satan. Then prayer is witnessed and accepted until the sun is directly overhead like a spear. When it is directly overhead like a spear, then leave the prayer, for at that hour Jahannam is kindled and its gates are opened, until the sun rises above your right side. When the sun has declined, then prayer is witnessed and accepted until you pray the ʿAṣr prayer. Then leave the prayer until the sun sets.""

And ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Qurashī narrated it from Saʿīd al-Maqburī with a similar chain, except that he did not name the questioner, and he added at the end: ""Then prayer is witnessed, attended, and accepted until you pray the morning prayer (al-Ṣubḥ)."" [Munkar. Al-Dāraquṭnī said in al-ʿIlal 1466]"
"4388","(4388) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, saying: Abū Salamah narrated to us, saying: Hammām narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Anas, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it; there is no expiation for it other than that."" Then he ﷺ recited thereafter: {Establish prayer for My remembrance} [Ṭā Hā: 14].

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Salamah Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, and Muslim narrated it from Hudbah ibn Khālid, from Hammām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 597]"
"4389","(4389) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us, saying: ʿAmr ibn Ḥakkām narrated to us, saying: al-Muthannā al-Qaṣīr narrated to us, saying: Qatādah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd — Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When one of you sleeps through a prayer or is heedless of it, let him pray it when he remembers it, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: {Establish prayer for My remembrance} [Ṭā Hā: 14]."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Muthannā. And in the ḥadīth of Saʿīd, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, for Allah, the Most High, says: {Establish prayer for My remembrance}.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿArūbah and al-Muthannā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"4390","(4390) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to me, saying: Yūnus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abū Hurayrah, that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the expedition of Khaybar, he mentioned the ḥadīth, and at the end of it he said: ""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, for Allah, the Most High, says: {Establish prayer for My remembrance}."" Yūnus said: Ibn Shihāb used to recite it thus.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 680]"
"4391","(4391) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us, from Ibn Qays.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Isfarāyīnī narrated to us — and the wording is his — saying: Abū Baḥr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Kawthar al-Barbahārī narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us, saying: Saʿd ibn Saʿīd ibn Qays al-Anṣārī narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from Qays, the grandfather of Saʿd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me while I was praying the two rakʿahs of Fajr after the morning prayer (al-Ṣubḥ). He said: ""What are these two rakʿahs, O Qays?"" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had not prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr, so these are those two rakʿahs."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then fell silent.

Al-Ḥumaydī added in his ḥadīth that Sufyān said: ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ used to narrate this ḥadīth from Saʿd.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 1/16]"
"4392","(4392) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Tamtām narrated to me, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Bishshār al-Ramādī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, saying: Saʿd ibn Saʿīd — the brother of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from Qays ibn Qahd, who said: The Prophet ﷺ saw me while I was praying the two rakʿahs after al-Ṣubḥ. He mentioned its meaning and mentioned the statement of Sufyān. Qays ibn Qahd said it thus, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr also said it thus from Saʿd in one of the two narrations from him, and in the other narration from him he said: Qays ibn ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"4393","(4393) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm al-Nasawī narrated to us, saying: Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me, saying: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me, from Bukayr, from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Azhar, and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah sent him to ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and said: ""Convey our greetings of peace to her, and ask her about the two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr, for we have been informed that you pray them, and it has reached us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade them."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""And I, along with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, used to strike the people on account of them.""

Kurayb said: ""So I entered upon her and conveyed to her what they had sent me with. She said: 'Ask Umm Salamah.' I went out to them and informed them of her words. They sent me back to Umm Salamah with the same message with which they had sent me to ʿĀʾishah. Umm Salamah said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid them, and then I saw him pray them. As for the time he prayed them, he had prayed ʿAṣr, then he entered while I had with me some women of Banī Ḥarām from the Anṣār, and he prayed them. So I sent the slave-girl to him and said: ""Stand beside him and say to him: Umm Salamah says: O Messenger of Allah, I hear you forbid these two rakʿahs, yet I see you praying them. If he gestures with his hand, then move away from him.""' She said: 'The slave-girl did so, and he gestured with his hand, so she moved away from him. When he finished, he said: ""O daughter of Abī Umayyah, you asked about the two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr. People from ʿAbd al-Qays came to me with the matter of Islam from their people, and they occupied me from the two rakʿahs after Ẓuhr, so these are those two.""'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān, from Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Ḥarmalah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"4394","(4394) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, saying: Maʿmar narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salmah, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray after ʿAṣr at all except once. A people came to him and occupied him, so he did not pray anything after Ẓuhr. When he prayed ʿAṣr, he entered my house and prayed two rakʿahs."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
"4395","(4395) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Darābardī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Ibrāhīm al-Juddī narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Dhakwān, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet ﷺ used to pray on a khumrah (a small mat).

ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""And Umm Salamah narrated to me that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon her and prayed two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr. I said: 'These are two prayers that you did not use to pray.' He said: 'What occupied me from two rakʿahs after Ẓuhr — so these are those two.'""

(Q) These reports agree that the first time the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed them, he prayed them as a make-up for a prayer he used to perform but had neglected. Even though they were not obligatory, the Prophet ﷺ then established them for himself after ʿAṣr, and when he prayed a prayer, he would make it a regular practice. [Ḥasan]"
"4396","(4396) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us regarding that, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥarmalah narrated to me, saying: Abū Salamah narrated to me that he asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the two rakʿahs that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray after ʿAṣr. She said: ""He used to pray them before ʿAṣr, but then he was occupied from them or forgot them, so he prayed them after ʿAṣr. Then he made them a regular practice, and when he prayed a prayer, he would make it a regular practice.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and others, from Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 835]"
"4397","(4397) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ṭūsī al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Shādyākhī, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ al-Laythī al-Madanī narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) never abandoned the two rakʿahs in my presence after ʿAṣr, ever."" It was narrated in the two Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 591, Muslim 835]"
"4398","(4398) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation in the year four hundred. Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah. Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us. ʿAbīdah ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ narrated to us, saying: ""I saw ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr praying two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr, and he reported that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) had informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) never entered her house except that he prayed them."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1631, Muslim 835]"
"4399","(4399) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūh al-Shaybānī narrated to us. ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsā narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn ʿArʿarah narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, who said: ""I saw al-Aswad and Masrūq testify upon ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: 'The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would not come to me on any day after ʿAṣr except that he prayed two rakʿahs.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿArʿarah, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 593]"
"4400","(4400) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us. Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us. Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq narrated to us. Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, from Miʿsar, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Abī al-Ḍuḥā, from Masrūq, who said: ""The truthful daughter of the truthful one, Ḥabībah, the beloved of Allah, the absolved one (may Allah, the Most High, be pleased with her), narrated to me that he (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray these two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 25513]"
"4401","(4401) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us. Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us. Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us. ʴAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ayman narrated to us, saying: ""My father narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that a person came to her asking about the two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr. She said: 'By the One Who took his soul'—meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)—'he never abandoned them until he met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. And he did not meet Allah until prayer became difficult for him, and he would pray much of his prayer sitting down.' He said to her: 'ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to forbid them and punish for them.' She said: 'You have spoken the truth, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray them, and he would not pray them in the mosque for fear of burdening his Ummah, and he loved that which was made easy for them.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 590]"
"4402","(4402) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. ʿUbaydullāh ibn Saʿd narrated to us. My paternal uncle narrated to us. My father narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from Dhakwān, the freed slave of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray after ʿAṣr and forbid it, and he used to practice wiṣāl (continuous fasting) and forbid wiṣāl. In this and in some of what has preceded, there is an indication that the continuation of these two rakʿahs was specific to him (peace and blessings be upon him) after the ruling was established by what he did in the house of Umm Salamah. And there has preceded in the narration of Ṭāwūs, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) only forbade seeking out the rising and setting of the sun."" And it is as though when she saw him persist in them, she understood the prohibition to refer to those two times, while the prohibition is established in those two times and before them, as has preceded. Therefore, the understanding that this was specific to him is more appropriate, and Allah knows best. And there has been narrated from ʿAlī, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that which indicates the permissibility of them when ʿAṣr is prayed at the beginning of its time. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4403","(4403) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Hilāl—meaning Ibn Yisāf—from Wahb ibn al-Ajdaʿ, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not pray after ʿAṣr unless you pray while the sun is still bright."" And Shuʿbah said, from Manṣūr, in this ḥadīth: ""while the sun is still high."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭayālisī 110]"
"4404","(4404) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Ibn Dāsah informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us. ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us. Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us. Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Manṣūr, who said: ""I heard Hilāl ibn Yisāf narrating from Wahb ibn al-Ajdaʿ, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Do not pray after ʿAṣr unless you pray while the sun is still high.'"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Ṭayālisī. And although Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī narrated it in the book of Sunan, it is not narrated in the books of al-Bukhārī and Muslim. And Wahb ibn al-Ajdaʿ is not from their conditions. And this is a single ḥadīth, and what has preceded regarding the prohibition of them extending until the setting of the sun is a ḥadīth with multiple chains, and it is more deserving of being the preserved narration. And there has been narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) that which contradicts this, and there has been narrated that which agrees with it. As for that which contradicts it outwardly, it is in what... [Ṣaḥīḥ, as has preceded. This is one ḥadīth, but there are several ḥadīths regarding the prohibition extending until the setting of the sun, and that is more appropriate.]"
"4405","(4405) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbāh—al-Aṣamm—narrated to us. Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us. Al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, who said: Abū Isḥāq informed me, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray two rakʿahs after every obligatory prayer, except Fajr and ʿAṣr."" And as for that which agrees with it, it is in what... [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4406","(4406) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us. Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us. Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, who said: ""We were with ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) on a journey, and he led us in ʿAṣr with two rakʿahs, then he entered his tent while I was watching, and he prayed two rakʿahs."" And al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) related these three ḥadīths from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), then he said: ""These are ḥadīths that contradict one another."" The Sheikh said: ""What is incumbent upon us is to follow that in which there is no disagreement, and then it would be specific to prayers that have no specific cause, and prayers that have a cause would be exempted from the prohibition by the ḥadīth of Umm Salamah and others, and Allah knows best."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4407","(4407) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Nāʿif: that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to perform the funeral prayer after ʿAṣr and after Ṣubḥ when they were performed at their prescribed times. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4408","(4408) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz and Ḥarmalah both said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Makhramah informed me from his father, from Nāʿif: that he performed the funeral prayer with Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, over ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, when they had prayed Ṣubḥ.

And it is narrated from Abū Lubābah — Marwān — from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: that he performed a funeral prayer while the sun was upon the edges of the walls.

(Q) A group among them disliked the funeral prayer at the rising of the sun and at its setting. [Ḍaʿīf. Previously cited under no. 4278]"
"4409","(4409) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us, al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, from Abū Bakr ibn Ḥafṣ, who said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar, at the funeral of Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, saying: ""If you do not pray over him until the sun has fully risen, then do not pray over him until it sets."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Cited by al-Faswī in al-Maʿrifah wa al-Tārīkh 1/85]"
"4410","(4410) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Ibn Qaʿnab and Ibn Bukayr narrated from Mālik, from Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥarmalah, the freedman of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Sufyān ibn Ḥuwayṭib: that Zaynab bint Umm Salamah passed away and Ṭāriq was the governor of Madīnah. Her bier was brought after the Ṣubḥ prayer and was placed in al-Baqīʿ. He said: Ṭāriq used to perform Ṣubḥ in the darkness of dawn.

Ibn Abī Ḥarmalah said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar say to her family: ""Either you pray over your deceased now, or you leave it until the sun rises.""

And something to this effect has been narrated from Abū Barzah al-Aslamī and Anas ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī.

Some of those who held this opinion cited as evidence the ḥadīth of ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir from the Prophet ﷺ regarding the prohibition of prayer and of the grave during the three times. That is a sound ḥadīth. And with Allah lies success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muwaṭṭāʾ Imām Mālik 536]"
"4411","(4411) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us.

(H) And Abū ʿAmr al-Faqīh and Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbdullāh informed us — and the wording is from both of them — they said: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyá narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb informed us, Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me. Ibn Shihāb said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik informed me: that ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb — who was the guide of Kaʿb from his sons after he lost his eyesight — said: I heard Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrating his account of when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the expedition of Tabūk. He mentioned the full ḥadīth regarding his repentance. He said: ""Then I prayed the Fajr prayer on the morning of the fifty night upon the roof of one of our houses. While I was sitting in the state that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, described regarding us — my soul had become constrained for me, and the earth had become narrow for me despite its vastness — I heard the voice of a caller who had ascended the mountain of Salʿ, saying in his loudest voice: 'O Kaʿb ibn Mālik, receive glad tidings!' I fell down in prostration and knew that relief had come. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had announced Allah's acceptance of our repentance when he prayed the Fajr prayer. The people went out giving us glad tidings,"" and he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abā Ṭāhir from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ from Ibn Wahb.

The apparent meaning of this is that he performed the prostration of gratitude after the Fajr prayer and before the rising of the sun. The prostration of recitation is analogous to it. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar disliked it, as has been narrated from him. This is more appropriate due to its established nature and its being in the meaning of what is established from the Prophet ﷺ regarding the two rakʿahs that he made up after ʿAṣr, having been occupied from them by the delegation. Every prayer and prostration that has a cause is analogous to them both. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited. Muslim 2769]"
"4412","(4412) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān informed us.

(H) And Abū al-Ḥusayn — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān — in Baghdād informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī and Ibn Qaʿnab both said: Sufyān narrated to us, Abū al-Zubayr narrated to us that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Bābāḥ narrating from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib — or O Banī ʿAbd Manāf — if any of you is given authority over any affair of this matter, let him not prevent anyone from circumambulating this House and praying at any hour he wishes, by night or by day.""

The wording is from the ḥadīth of al-Ḥumaydī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥumaydī 561, Tirmidhī 868]"
"4413","(4413) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Bābāḥ, from Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Banī ʿAbd Manāf, whoever among you is given authority over any affair of the people, let him not prevent anyone from circumambulating this House and praying at any hour he wishes, by night or by day.""

Ibn ʿUyaynah established his isnād, and those who differed with him in his isnād do not match him. The narration of Ibn ʿUyaynah is more deserving of being preserved, and Allah knows best.

It has also been narrated through other routes from Nāʿif ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim from his father from the Prophet ﷺ, and from ʿAṭāʾ from the Prophet ﷺ as a mursal narration.

If the prayer mentioned alongside the Ṣawāf refers to the two rakʿahs of Ṭawāf, then the meaning of its permissibility is that it is a prayer with a cause, thus it falls under the first category of specification. If what is meant by it is all other supererogatory prayers, then the specification reverts to the place. The former is more consistent with the narrations.

A disconnected report has been narrated in support of the second view, but there is question regarding its authenticity. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited]"
"4414","(4414) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Khwārizmī narrated to us in Bayt al-Maqdis, Ibn Miqlāṣ — meaning ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿImrān ibn Miqlāṣ — narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal narrated from Ḥumayd, the freedman of ʿAfrāʾ, from Qays ibn Saʿd, from Mujāhid, from Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him: that he stood and grasped the ring of the door of the Kaʿbah and said: ""Whoever knows me, he knows me, and whoever does not know me, then I am Junḍab, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'There is no prayer after ʿAṣr until the sun sets, and there is no prayer after Ṣubḥ until the sun rises — except in Makkah, except in Makkah, except in Makkah.'""

And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Abū ʿAlī Sakhawayh narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal narrated it with his isnād, saying: ""Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us and grasped the ring of the door of the Kaʿbah, then called out in his loudest voice: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ,'"" and he mentioned it with its meaning.

Saʿīd ibn Sālim al-Qaṭṭāḥ narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal from Ḥumayd, the freedman of ʿAfrāʾ, from Mujāhid, without mentioning Qays ibn Saʿd.

Likewise, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal from Ḥumayd al-Aʿraj from Mujāhid.

This ḥadīth is counted among the unique narrations of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal, and ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal is weak, except that Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān corroborated him in that from Ḥumayd and established his isnād. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4415","(4415) Abū Naṣr — ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah — informed him, Abū Muḥammad — Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Shaybān al-Baghdādī al-Harawī — therein informed us, Muʿādh ibn Najdah narrated to us, Khallād ibn Yaḥyá narrated to us, Ibrāhīm — meaning Ibn Ṭahmān — narrated to us, Ḥumayd, the freedman of ʿAfrāʾ, narrated from Qays ibn Saʿd, from Mujāhid, who said: ""Abū Dharr came to us and grasped the ring of the door, then said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say with these two ears of mine: ""There is no prayer after ʿAṣr until the sun sets, and no prayer after Fajr until the sun rises — except in Makkah, except in Makkah, except in Makkah.""'""

Ḥumayd al-Aʿraj is not strong, and Mujāhid's hearing from Abū Dharr is not established.

His statement ""came to us"" means he came to our town. And Allah knows best. It has also been narrated through another route from Mujāhid. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4416","(4416) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yūnus al-ʿUṣfurī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Ḥarashī narrated to me, al-Yasaʿ ibn Ṭalḥah al-Qurashī, from the people of Makkah, narrated: I heard Mujāhid say: ""It has reached us that Abū Dharr said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grasping the two rings of the Kaʿbah, saying three times: ""There is no prayer after ʿAṣr except in Makkah.""'""

Al-Yasaʿ ibn Ṭalḥah has been declared weak, and the ḥadīth is disconnected, for Mujāhid did not meet Abū Dharr. And Allah knows best. A weak report has also been narrated in support of the first view. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4417","(4417) Abū Saʿd — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Mālinī — informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdiyy al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʀāṣim — narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʀammār — narrated to us; Marwān ibn Muʿāwiya — narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Rāshid — narrated from ʀāṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ — from Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""There is no prayer after Fajr until the sun rises, and there is no prayer after ʀAsr until the sun sets. Whoever performs ṭawāf, let him pray"" — meaning, at whatever time he performs ṭawāf.

Abū Aḥmad said: Saʿīd narrates this from ʀāṭāʾ and added in its text: ""Whoever performs ṭawāf, let him pray at whatever time he performs ṭawāf."" He said: He narrates from ʀāṭāʾ and others that which is not corroborated.

The Sheikh said: Al-Bukhārī mentioned him in al-Tārīkh and said: ""He is not corroborated in what he narrates."" [Weak]"
"4418","(4418) Abū ʀAmr al-Adīb — informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʀīlī — informed us; ʀAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ, the companion of al-Bukhārī, and ʀAbdullāh al-Baghawī — both said: Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ — narrated to us. One of them said: al-Zaʀfarānī — narrated to us; ʀAbīda ibn Ḥumayd — narrated to us; ʀAbd al-ʀAzīz ibn Rufayʀ — said: I saw ʀAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr performing ṭawāf after Fajr and then praying two rakʀāt.

ʀAbīda said, and ʀAbd al-ʀAzīz said: ""I saw."" And Ibn Ṣāliḥ said: I saw ʀAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr praying two rakʀāt after ʀAsr, and he informed me that ʀĀʀisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, did not enter her house except that he prayed. Ibn Ṣāliḥ said: ""Except that he prayed both of them.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad. [Sound. Previously cited under no. 4398]"
"4419","(4419) Abū ʀAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Manṣūr — informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr — informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʀrūf — informed us; Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾīnī — narrated to us; Abū Jaʀfar Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ — narrated to us; ʀAlī ibn al-Madīnī — narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʀ — narrated from Ḥabīb al-Muʀallim — from ʀāṭāʾ — from ʀUrwa — from ʀĀʀisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that some people performed ṭawāf of the House after the morning prayer and then sat down for remembrance (dhikr). ʀĀʀisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""They sat until, when it was a time in which prayer is disliked, they stood up to pray.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn ʀUmar ibn Shaqīq from Yazīd ibn Zuraiʀ, and added in its text: ""Then they sat for remembrance until, when the sun rose, they stood up to pray.""

It is as though ʀĀʀisha, may Allah be pleased with her, permitted the two rakʀāt of ṭawāf after the Fajr prayer and disliked them at the time of sunrise. And Allah knows best. [Sound. Bukhārī 1628]"
"4420","(4420) Abū ʀAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and others — informed us; they said: Abū al-ʀAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʀqūb — informed us; al-Rabīʀ ibn Sulaymān — informed us; al-Shāfiʀī — informed us; Sufyān ibn ʀUyayna — narrated from ʀAmr ibn Dīnār — who said: I saw myself and ʀāṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ — [that] Ibn ʀUmar performed ṭawāf after the morning prayer and prayed before the sun rose. [Sound]"
"4421","(4421) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʀīd ibn Abī ʀAmr — both said: Abū al-ʀAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʀqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us; Manṣūr ibn Abī Mazāḥim — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamza — narrated from Mūsā ibn Yasār — that he heard ʀāṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ say: I saw ʀAbdullāh ibn ʀUmar perform ṭawāf after the morning prayer before the sun rose, and then he prayed.

I mentioned that to Nāfiʀ, and Nāfiʀ said: ""The people of Makka have erred regarding Ibn ʀUmar.""

The Sheikh said: This rejection by Nāfiʀ is not acceptable, as it is as though he did not know the reliability of those from the people of Makka who narrated from Ibn ʀUmar. Had he known their reliability, it would have been more fitting for him to accept their narration rather than reject it. Ibn ʀUmar used to permit the funeral prayer after ʀAsr and after the morning prayer, and likewise the two rakʀāt of ṭawāf. The prohibition according to him was specifically regarding praying at the time of sunrise and sunset. What the people of Makka narrated from him regarding the two rakʀāt of ṭawāf is consistent with his position. And Allah, the Most High, knows best. [Sound. Previously cited]"
"4422","(4422) Abū ʀAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū al-ʀAbbās — informed us; al-Rabīʀ — informed us; al-Shāfiʀī — informed us; Sufyān — narrated from ʀAmmār al-Duhnī — from Abī Shuʀba that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn performed ṭawāf after ʀAsr and prayed. [Weak]"
"4423","(4423) And with his chain of narration: al-Shāfiʀī — informed us; Muslim and ʀAbd al-Majīd — narrated from Ibn Jurayj — from Ibn Abī Mulayka — who said: I saw Ibn ʀAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, perform ṭawāf after ʀAsr and pray. [Weak]"
"4424","(4424) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʀīd ibn Abī ʀAmr — both said: Abū al-ʀAbbās, al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Sābiq — informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān — narrated from Abī al-Zubayr — from ʀAbdullāh ibn Bābāh — from Abī al-Dardāʾ that he performed ṭawāf after ʀAsr at the time of the sun's setting, and he prayed two rakʀāt before the sun set. It was said to him: ""O Abā al-Dardāʾ, you are Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and you say: 'There is no prayer after ʀAsr until the sun sets'?"" He said: ""Indeed, this town is not like other towns.""

This statement from Abī al-Dardāʾ implies a specification of place in this regard. And Allah knows best.

It has also been narrated regarding their performance of the two rakʀāt after ṭawāf at this time from Ṭāwūs and al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad.

Saʀīd ibn Jubayr said: ""When you perform ṭawāf, then pray.""

It has been narrated from a group of the Companions and the Successors that they used to delay the two rakʀāt until the sun rose and became elevated. [Fair]"
"4425","(4425) Abū al-Qāsim ʀAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʀUbaydullāh ibn ʀAbdullāh al-Ḥurfī in Baghdād — narrated to us; Ḥamza ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʀAbbās — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʀĪsā al-Madānī — informed us; Sufyān ibn ʀUyayna — narrated from al-Zuhrī — from ʀUrwa — from ʀAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʀAbd al-Qārī — who said: ʀUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed the morning prayer in Makka, then performed ṭawāf of the House seven times, then departed intending to go to Madina. When he reached Dhī Ṭuwan and the sun had risen, he prayed two rakʀāt.

Al-Ḥumaydī likewise narrated it from Sufyān. The correct narration from al-Zuhrī is from Ḥumayd ibn ʀAbd al-Raḥmān. [Weak]"
"4426","(4426) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatāda — informed us; Abū ʀAmr ibn Najayd — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʀAbdī — narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us; Mālik — narrated from Ibn Shihāb — from Ḥumayd ibn ʀAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʀAwf — that ʀAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʀAbd al-Qārī informed him that he performed ṭawāf with ʀUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, after the morning prayer at the Kaʀba. When ʀUmar completed his ṭawāf, he looked and did not see the sun [i.e., it had not yet risen], so he rode until he halted at Dhī Ṭuwan and then prayed two rakʀāt of supererogatory prayer.

Maʀmar and others likewise narrated it from al-Zuhrī. [Sound. Mālik narrated it]"
"4427","(4427) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rafi'i informed me — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la recited — Sufyan narrated from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari, who mentioned the hadith in a manner similar to the narration of al-Mada'ini.

Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la said: Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said to me regarding this hadith: ""Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah followed [the wording] in his statement 'al-Zuhri from 'Urwah from 'Abd al-Rahman' — meaning the middle link (al-mujarrah), i.e., the adherence to the path [of the hadith].""

'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad said: That is because Malik, Yunus, and others narrated the hadith from al-Zuhri from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari from 'Umar. Al-Shafi'i intended thereby that Sufyan erred, and that the correct narration is what Malik reported."
"4428","(4428) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id al-A'rabi narrated to us — Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, who said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri came to us and performed tawahu after the morning prayer (Subh). We said: ""Look now — what will he do? Will he pray or not?"" He said: ""So he sat until the sun rose, then he prayed."" [Sahih]"
"4429","(4429) And Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Muqri' in Baghdad — informed us — Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih narrated to us — 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad narrated to us — al-Hawdi and Abu al-Walid both said — Shu'bah narrated from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Nasr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his grandfather Mu'adh ibn 'Afra', that he used to perform tawaf of the House after the afternoon prayer ('Asr) and would not pray. A man from Quraysh named Mu'adh said to him: ""What is the matter with you — why do you not pray?"" He said: ""Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade prayer after the two prayers: after the 'Asr until the sun sets, and after the morning prayer (Subh) until it rises."" And Abu Dawud narrated it from Shu'bah, saying: ""from his grandfather: that he performed tawaf with Mu'adh ibn 'Afra'.""

This is to be interpreted as meaning that the specific [evidence] did not reach him; had it reached him, he would have followed it. And success is by Allah."
"4430","(4430) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn Mukram narrated to us — 'Uthman ibn 'Umar and Abu al-Nadr both said — Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from his father, from Ibn Wadi'ah al-Ansari, from Salman, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever takes a bath on Friday and purifies himself as much as he is able, and applies some oil or perfume from his household, then goes to the Friday prayer and prays what is prescribed for him — when the imam emerges, he listens and remains silent — his sins between that Friday and the next will be forgiven.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam from Ibn Abi Dhi'b. [Sahih. Bukhari 910, Ibn Hibban 2776]"
"4431","(4431) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ibrahim al-Hashimi in Baghdad — narrated to us — 'Uthman ibn Ahmad al-Daqqaq narrated to us — 'Abd al-Karim ibn al-Haytham narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated to us — Hassan al-Kirmani narrated to us — Layth narrated from Mujahid, from Abi al-Khalil, from Abi Qatadah, from the Prophet ﷺ: ""He disliked praying at midday except on Friday, because Jahannam is heated every day except Friday."" [Da'if]"
"4432","(4432) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Isa narrated to us — Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, mentioning it with its isnad and its meaning. Abu Dawud said: ""This is mursal. Abi al-Khalil did not meet Abi Qatadah."" The Sheikh said: ""And it has supporting narrations, even though its chains are weak, including what…"""
"4433","(4433) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — Ibrahim ibn Muhammad narrated from Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade praying at midday until the sun declines, except on Friday. [Da'if]"
"4434","(4434) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan, Abu al-Shaykh al-Isfahani, narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us — Abu Kurayb narrated to us — Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated from a scholar of the people of Madinah named 'Abd Allah, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Prayer is forbidden — meaning — when the day reaches its midpoint, every day except Friday.""

And it has been narrated in that regard from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, 'Amr ibn 'Absah, and Ibn 'Umar, as a marfu' narration.

The reliance is upon the fact that the Prophet ﷺ loved the early attendance (al-tabkir) for Friday, then encouraged prayer until the emergence of the imam without specification or exception. We shall mention that, if Allah the Most High wills, in the Book of Friday. [Da'if]"
"4435","(4435) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-Khadir ibn Aban narrated to us — Sayyar narrated to us — Bishr ibn Ghalib said: I heard al-Hasan saying: ""Friday — its prayer is entirely [permissible at any time], for Jahannam is not heated on Friday.""

And the concession regarding that has been narrated from Tawus and Mak-hul. [Da'if]"
"4436","(4436) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us — Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us — Ghundar narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from Zayd ibn Muhammad, who said: I heard Nafi' narrating from Ibn 'Umar, from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: ""When the dawn rose, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not pray except two light rak'ahs.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam from Ghundar. [Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq 4771]"
"4437","(4437) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed me, from Qudāmah ibn Mūsā, from Ayyūb ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from Abū ʿAlqamah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: Yāsār, the freedman of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, narrated to me, saying: I stood to pray after Fajr, and I prayed a great deal of prayer. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar pelted me with pebbles and said: ""O Yāsār, how much have you prayed?"" I said: ""I do not know."" ʿAbd Allāh said: ""You have not understood! Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us while we were praying this prayer, and he became very angry with us, then he said: 'Let your present ones inform your absent ones: there is no prayer after the rising of Fajr except the two rakʿahs of Fajr.'""

ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb established its isnād from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl. Abū Bakr ibn Abī Uways also narrated it from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl but mixed up his isnād. The correct narration is that of Ibn Wahb. Wahb ibn Khālid narrated it from Qudāmah, from Ayyūb ibn Ḥuṣayn al-Tamīmī, from Abū ʿAlqamah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Yāsār, the freedman of Ibn ʿUmar, similarly. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4438","(4438) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī informed us: Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat narrated to us: al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Wahb mentioned its meaning.

Similarly, Ḥumayd ibn al-Aswad narrated it from Qudāmah."
"4439","(4439) ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī narrated it from Qudāmah ibn Mūsā, from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from Abū ʿAlqamah, from Yāsār, the freedman of Ibn ʿUmar, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no prayer after Fajr except two rakʿahs.""

Abū Muḥammad Junāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Junāḥ informed us in al-Kūfah: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Dārawardī mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4440","(4440) ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Qudāmah ibn Mūsā informed me: a man from Banī Ḥanẓalah informed me, from Abū ʿAlqamah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, and he mentioned it with the meaning of the ḥadīth of Ibn Wahb.

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated similarly.

It has a witness from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, although in its isnād there is one who cannot be used as a proof."
"4441","(4441) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād ibn Anʿum informed you, from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: ""There is no prayer after the rising of Fajr except the two rakʿahs of Fajr."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4442","(4442) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no prayer after the rising of Fajr except the two rakʿahs of Fajr.""

ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd is Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4443","(4443) Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated it from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""There is no prayer after the Fajr prayer has been performed except two rakʿahs.""

Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād mentioned it, as a mawqūf narration.

This differs from the narration of al-Thawrī and Ibn Wahb in both the text and the stopping. Al-Thawrī is the most memorizing of them, except that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ifrīqī cannot be used as a proof. It has a witness from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Musayyab, as a mursal narration. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4444","(4444) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥarmalah narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no prayer after the call to prayer except two prostrations"" — meaning Fajr.

It has been narrated as a connected narration mentioning Abū Hurayrah in it, but its being connected is not correct. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4445","(4445) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Dārakī narrated to us: Abū Zurʿah narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, from Abū Rabbāḥ, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab: that he saw a man praying after the rising of Fajr more than two rakʿahs, performing much bowing and prodding therein, so he forbade him. The man said: ""O Abū Muḥammad, will Allah punish me for prayer?"" He said: ""No, but He will punish you for contradicting the Sunnah."" [Ṣāliḥ]"
"4446","(4446) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — it was read to him in al-Muḥarram of the year thirty-three and three hundred — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated, from Abū Suhayl, from his father, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh: that a Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with dishevelled hair and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, tell me what Allah has obligated upon me of prayer."" He said: ""The five prayers, unless you voluntarily perform anything extra."" He said: ""Tell me what Allah has obligated upon me of fasting."" He said: ""The fasting of Ramaḍān, unless you voluntarily perform anything extra."" He said: ""Tell me what Allah has obligated upon me of zakāh."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed him of the ordinances of Islam."" He said: ""By the One Who has honoured you, I shall not voluntarily perform anything extra, nor shall I fall short of what Allah has obligated upon me in anything."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""He has succeeded, by his father, if he is truthful. He will enter Paradise, by Allah, if he is truthful.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it from Qutaybah, from Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar, except that he said: ""Or he will enter Paradise, by his father, if he is truthful."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 46]"
"4447","(4447) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: al-Hasan ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-'Adl narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Nasr ibn 'Ali al-Jahdami narrated to us: 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us: Hisham narrated from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The five prayers and one Jumu'ah to the next Jumu'ah are expiations for what occurs between them."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Nasr ibn 'Ali. [Sahih. Muslim 233]"
"4448","(4448) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Sariy ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated from Malik — (H) And Abu 'Abdillah informed me: Abu Bakr ibn 'Abdillah informed me: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated from Malik ibn Anas, from Abu Suhayl, from his father, that he heard Talhah ibn 'Ubaydillah say: A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from the people of Najd, his hair dishevelled, and we could hear the buzzing of his voice but could not understand what he was saying, until he drew near to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and lo, he was asking about Islam. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Five prayers in the day and the night."" He asked: ""Am I obligated with anything other than these?"" He said: ""No, unless you perform voluntary prayers."" And he said: ""And fasting the month of Ramadan."" He asked: ""Am I obligated with anything other than that?"" He said: ""No, unless you perform voluntary fasting."" And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned Zakah to him. He asked: ""Am I obligated with anything other than it?"" He said: ""No, unless you give voluntary charity."" The man turned away, saying: ""By Allah, I shall neither add to this nor diminish from it."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""He has succeeded, if he is truthful."" The wording is that of the hadith of Qutaybah. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Isma'il ibn Abi Uways, from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. [Sahih. Previously referenced under no. 4446]"
"4449","(4449) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ja'far informed us: al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The five prayers and one Jumu'ah to the next Jumu'ah are expiations for what occurs between them, so long as major sins are not committed."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah and others, from Isma'il. [Sahih. Previously referenced under no. 4447]"
"4450","(4450) And Abu Ahmad 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrani al-'Adl informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn Qays al-Ansari, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz: that a man from the Banu Kinanah known as al-Mukhajji heard a man in al-Sham known as Abu Muhammad saying: ""Witr is obligatory."" Al-Mukhajji said: ""So I went to 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit and met him as he was heading to the mosque, and I informed him of what Abu Muhammad had said. 'Ubadah said: 'Abu Muhammad has lied. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Allah has prescribed five prayers upon the servants. Whoever comes with them, having not neglected anything of them out of disdain for their right, shall have a covenant with Allah that He will admit him into Paradise. And whoever does not come with them shall have no covenant with Allah; if He wills, He shall punish him, and if He wills, He shall admit him into Paradise."" [Da'if. Previously referenced under no. 3166–2226]"
"4451","(4451) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn Milhan narrated to us: Yahya narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz: that a man from the Banu Kinanah, then from the Banu Mudlij, met a man from the Ansar called Abu Muhammad, and asked him about Witr. He said: ""It is obligatory."" The man from Kinanah said: ""I then met 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned that to him. He said: 'Abu Muhammad has lied. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Allah has obligated five prayers upon the servants. Whoever comes with them, having not neglected anything of them, shall have a covenant with Allah that He will admit him into Paradise. And whoever does not come with them shall have no covenant with Allah; if He wills, He shall punish him, and if He wills, He shall have mercy upon him."" [Sahih. Previously referenced]"
"4452","(4452) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — dictating —: Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Qazzaz narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Hamran narrated to us: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far ibn 'Abdillah ibn al-Hakam said: My father, Ja'far ibn 'Abdillah, narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah al-Najjari: that he asked 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, about Witr, and he said: ""A good and beautiful practice. The Prophet ﷺ and the Muslims after him acted upon it, but it is not obligatory."" [Sahih. Ibn Khuzaymah 1067]"
"4453","(4453) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us: Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih said: It was read to Yahya ibn Ja'far while I was listening: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us: Sufyan narrated — (H) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Marzuq informed us: Zuhayr — both of them together — narrated from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""This Witr is not a binding obligation, but it is a good practice (sunnah) from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Indeed, Allah is One (Witr) and loves the odd-numbered."" The wording is that of the hadith of Zuhayr. And in the narration of al-Thawri: ""Witr is not a binding obligation, but it is a sunnah established by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" [Hasan]"
"4454","(4454) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Isa narrated to us: 'Amr ibn 'Awn narrated from Abu 'Awanah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""Witr is not a binding obligation like the prescribed prayers, but it is a sunnah established by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" He ﷺ said: ""Perform Witr, O people of the Qur'an, for indeed, Allah, the Most High, is One (Witr) and loves the odd-numbered."" [Hasan. Previously referenced in the hadith before it]"
"4455","(4455) Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nudhayr ibn Janah in Kufah informed us: Abu Ja'far ibn Duhaym narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hazim informed us: 'Uthman narrated to us: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — (H) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Abu Hafs al-Abbar, who is 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated from al-A'mash, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from 'Abdullah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Indeed, Allah is One (Witr) and loves the odd-numbered, so perform Witr, O people of the Qur'an."" Abu Dawud added in his narration: A Bedouin said: ""What do you say?"" He said: ""It is not for you, nor for your companions."" [Da'if]"
"4456","(4456) And 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us: Abu Muhammad Dalaj ibn Ahmad ibn Dalaj narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us: Mihran, meaning al-Razi, narrated from Abu Sinan, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Perform Witr, O people of the Qur'an."" A Bedouin said: ""What does the Prophet ﷺ say?"" He said: ""You are not among its people."" And Sufyan al-Thawri narrated it from 'Amr ibn Murrah and sent it as a mursal hadith. [Da'if. Previously referenced]"
"4457","(4457) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Asid ibn 'Asim narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Sufyan, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu 'Ubaydah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Perform the Witr, O people of the Qur'an, for indeed Allah, the Most High, is One (Witr) and loves the Witr."" Then a Bedouin said: ""What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say?"" He replied: ""He did not say it for you or for your companions.""

A group narrated it from al-Thawri, and it is said that al-Thawri did not hear it directly from 'Amr; rather, he heard it from a man who heard it from 'Amr.

It was also narrated from 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad from al-Thawri, and he mentioned 'Abd Allah in it, but this is not preserved. Moreover, the hadith with the mention of 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud in it is munqati' (disconnected), because Abu 'Ubaydah did not meet his father. [Da'if (Weak)]"
"4458","(4458) Abu al-Fath al-'Umari, the Shaykh, narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman al-Shurayhi narrated to us: Abu al-Qasim al-Baghawi narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, who said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the Witr, and it is not obligatory upon you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered the sacrifice, and it is not obligatory upon you. He prayed Duha, and it is not obligatory upon you. He prayed before Dhuhr, and it is not obligatory upon you."" Qatadah said: I said: ""This is what is known apart from the Witr."" He said: ""He only said: 'O people of the Qur'an, perform the Witr, for indeed Allah, the Most High, is One (Witr) and loves the Witr.'"" [Hasan (Fair)]"
"4459","(4459) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Bishran, Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn 'Abdillah ibn Ibrahim al-Hashimi, and Abu 'Abdillah al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Ghadairi narrated to us, saying: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Abu Badr narrated to us: Abu Janab al-Kalbi narrated to us, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Three things are obligatory upon me and are voluntary (tatawwu') for you: the sacrifice (al-nahr), the Witr, and the two rak'ahs of Duha.""

Abu Janab al-Kalbi's name is Yahya ibn Abi Hayyah, and he is da'if (weak). Yazid ibn Harun used to confirm him and also accused him of tadlis (concealing defects in the chain). [Da'if (Weak)]"
"4460","(4460) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Muhammad ibn Musa ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: 'Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah and al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abdillah ibn Rashid, from 'Abdillah ibn Abi Murrah, from Kharijah ibn Hudhafah al-'Adawi, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has aided you with a prayer that is better for you than red camels, and it is for you between the prayer of 'Isha and the rising of Fajr — the Witr, the Witr."" He said it twice. [Da'if (Weak)]"
"4461","(4461) Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar narrated it from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, and he said: 'Abdillah ibn Murrah narrated to us. Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Sa'id ibn 'Uthman al-Tanukhi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Khalid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated it, mentioning its meaning.

This is another chain of narration for the preceding hadith."
"4462","(4462) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us: Abu Bakr al-Qattan narrated to us: Sahl ibn 'Ammar narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abdillah ibn Rashid al-Zawfi, from 'Abdillah ibn Murrah, from Kharijah ibn Hudhafah al-'Adawi, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day at the time of the morning prayer and said: ""Indeed Allah has aided you with a prayer that is better for you than red camels."" We said: ""What is it, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""The Witr, between 'Isha and the rising of Fajr.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Yazid ibn Harun, except that he did not say ""al-Zawfi."" Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar said: ""'Abdillah ibn Murrah.""

Abu Bakr al-Farisi narrated to us: Ibrahim al-Isbahani narrated to us: Abu Ahmad ibn Faris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari, who said: It is not known that any of them heard from one another in its chain of narration.

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: A similar narration has been reported regarding the two rak'ahs of Fajr with a chain of narration more authentic than this. [Da'if (Weak). Previously mentioned.]"
"4463","(4463) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to me: Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Junah al-Kushani narrated to us in Bukhara from the original of his book: 'Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Bujayr narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal narrated to us in Damascus: Marwan ibn Muhammad al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn Salam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Nadrah al-'Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has added to your prayers a prayer that is better for you than red camels. Behold, it is the two rak'ahs before the prayer of Fajr.""

Al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid said: Yahya ibn Ma'in said to me: ""This is a gharib (uncommon) hadith from the hadith of Mu'awiyah ibn Salam."" Mu'awiyah ibn Salam was a muhaddith of the people of Sham, and he was saduq al-hadith (truthful in hadith). Whoever does not record his hadith — both musnad (connected) and munqati' (disconnected) — is not a companion of hadith.

It has reached me from Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah that he said: ""If I could travel to Ibn Bujayr, I would travel to him for this hadith.""

Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us, saying: I heard Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abi 'Uthman say: I heard my father say: I heard Ibn Khuzaymah say, and he mentioned it in his narration of this hadith from Ibn Bujayr. [Hasan (Fair)]"
"4464","(4464) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Siddiq Muhammad ibn Abi al-Fawarisl-Saydalani narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us: Abu al-Munib 'Ubaydillah ibn 'Abdillah al-'Ataki narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The Witr is a right (haqq). Whoever does not perform the Witr is not from us.""

Abu Sa'id al-Malini narrated to us: Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi narrated to us, saying: I heard Ibn Hammad say: al-Bukhari said: ""'Ubaydillah ibn 'Abdillah, Abu al-Munib, from Ibn Buraydah — Zayd ibn al-Hubab heard from him, and he has manakir (objectionable narrations)."" Abu Ahmad said: ""He is, in my opinion, la ba'sa bihi (not a problem)."" Yahya ibn Ma'in also used to declare him thiqah (trustworthy). And Allah knows best. [Da'if (Weak)]"
"4465","(4465) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdillah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Sha'bi, who said: ""The Witr is voluntary (tatawwu'), and it is among the most noble of voluntary acts."" [Sahih (Authentic)]"
"4466","(4466) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Siddiq Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Saydalani narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: Abu 'Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': ""Are the two rak'ahs of Fajr obligatory, or are they something from the voluntary acts?"" He said: ""Do you not know?"" Then he narrated to me from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not more consistent in any of the voluntary prayers than he was in the two rak'ahs of Subh (morning) or Fajr. [Sahih (Authentic)]"
"4467","(4467) Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn Jurayj, ʿAṭāʾ narrated to me from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was not more vigilant in observing any of the supererogatory prayers than he was in observing the two rakʿahs before Fajr."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Bayān ibn ʿAmr, and Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, both of them from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1163]"
"4468","(4468) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAliyy ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʔUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl and ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar said: Musaddad narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated — (Ḥ) and Abū Zakariyyā al-Ḥanāʾī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Qatādah from Zurārah, meaning Ibn Awfā, from Saʿd ibn Hishām from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""The two rakʿahs of Fajr are better than the world and all that is in it."" In the narration of Musaddad, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said the same. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd ibn Ḥisāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 725]"
"4469","(4469) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Dallawayh al-Daqqāq narrated to us, Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad narrated from Sulaymān al-Taymī from Qatādah from Zurārah ibn Awfā from Saʿd from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The two rakʿahs of Fajr are better than the world and all that is in it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"4470","(4470) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Qatādah, and he said: Indeed the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said regarding the two rakʿahs of Fajr: ""They are more beloved to me than the red camels."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī 1498]"
"4471","(4471) Al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated it from his father, and he said regarding the two rakʿahs of Fajr: ""They are more beloved to me than the world and all that is in it."" Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Khalaf al-Bāhilī narrated to us, al-Muʿtamir said: My father said: Qatādah narrated to us, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb from al-Muʿtamir."
"4472","(4472) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Muqriʾ narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb, who said: Jaʿfar ibn Rabīʿah narrated to me from ʿIrak ibn Mālik from Abū Salamah from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed ʿIshāʾ, then he prayed eight rakʿahs standing, and two rakʿahs sitting, and two rakʿahs between the two calls (adhān and iqāmah), and he never abandoned them."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Muqriʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1159]"
"4473","(4473) Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to me, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to me, Abū Ziyādah ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyādah al-Kindī narrated from Bilāl that he narrated to him: He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to inform him of the morning prayer, but ʿĀʾishah occupied Bilāl with a matter she asked him about until the morning became very bright. He said: So Bilāl stood and gave him the call to prayer and followed up his adhān, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not come out. When he came out, he led the people in prayer. Bilāl informed him that ʿĀʾishah had occupied him with a matter she asked him about until the morning became very bright, and that he had delayed in coming out. He said: ""I had already prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you have become very bright (i.e., the morning has advanced considerably)."" He said: ""Even if I had become brighter than I did, I would have performed them well and beautified them."" It is also narrated from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not abandon them even if the horses drive you away."" This is in some of the copies of the book of Abū Dāwūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 23393]"
"4474","(4474) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAliyy ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — (Ḥ) and Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from Ayyūb from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""I preserved from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ten rakʿahs: two rakʿahs before Dhuhr and two rakʿahs after it, two rakʿahs after Maghrib in his house, two rakʿahs after ʿIshāʾ in his house, and two rakʿahs before the morning prayer. And it was a time at which no one would enter upon the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."" Ḥafṣah narrated to me: ""When the muʾadhdhin called the adhān and Fajr rose, he would pray two rakʿahs."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 723]"
"4475","(4475) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Abū al-Muthannā and Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb said: Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated — (Ḥ) and Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to me, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to me, Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us, Abū Khaythamah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated — (Ḥ) and Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, and the wording is his: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh narrated to me, Nāfiʿ narrated to me from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) two prostrations (i.e., two rakʿahs) before Dhuhr and two prostrations after it, and after Maghrib two prostrations, and after ʿIshāʾ two prostrations, and after Jumuʿah two prostrations. As for Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ, and Jumuʿah, they were in his house."" Ḥafṣah narrated to me: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray two brief rakʿahs when Fajr rose, before he prayed Fajr, and it was a time at which I would not enter upon the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Khaythamah Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referenced in the preceding hadith.]"
"4476","(4476) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Hushaym narrated — (Ḥ) and Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Khālid narrated, and Musaddad narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us, Khālid narrated with the same meaning, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq, who said: ""I asked ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about the voluntary prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and she said: 'He would pray four rakʿahs before Dhuhr in my house, then he would go out and lead the people in prayer, then he would return to my house and pray two rakʿahs. He would lead the people in Maghrib, then return to my house and pray two rakʿahs. He would lead them in ʿIshāʾ, then enter my house and pray two rakʿahs. He would pray nine rakʿahs at night, among them the Witr. He would pray at night for a long time standing, and for a long time sitting. When he recited while standing, he would bow and prostrate while standing, and when he recited while sitting, he would bow and prostrate while sitting. When Fajr rose, he would pray two rakʿahs, then go out and lead the people in the Fajr prayer.'"" The wording is that of the hadith of Abū Dāwūd. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1182]"
"4477","(4477) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar — informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb — informed us, Abū Dāwūd — informed us, Shuʿbah — informed me, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not omit four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, and two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through another route from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding hadith.]"
"4478","(4478) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb — informed us, Musaddad — informed us, Yaḥyā, from Shuʿbah, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: ""Indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ…"" and he said: ""before the morning prayer (ṣalāt al-ghadāh).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding hadith.]"
"4479","(4479) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar — informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb — informed us, Abū Dāwūd — informed us, Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Sālim.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ in Naysābūr and Abū al-Qāsim — Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣaqr in Baghdad — both informed us, saying: Abū al-Ḥusayn — Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Adamī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Māhān al-Simsār — informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī — informed us, Shuʿbah, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Sālim, who said: I heard ʿAmr ibn Aws narrating from ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān, from Umm Ḥabībah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever prays twelve rakʿahs every day as voluntary worship apart from the obligatory prayers, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. And in the ḥadīth of Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī: ʿAmr ibn Aws heard ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān narrating from Umm Ḥabībah that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever prays twelve rakʿahs in a day and night apart from the prescribed prayers, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise."" Umm Ḥabībah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""I never abandoned them after that."" ʿAnbasah said: ""I never abandoned them after that."" ʿAmr said: ""I never abandoned them after that."" Al-Nuʿmān said: ""And I would hardly ever abandon them after that.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar and others, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 728]"
"4480","(4480) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdawayh ibn Sahl — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥammād al-Āmulī — informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāliḥ — informed us, Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh — informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Khashshāb — informed us, Abū al-Azhar — informed us, Yūnus ibn Muḥammad — informed us, Fulayḥ, from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ, from ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān, from Umm Ḥabībah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever prays twelve rakʿahs, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise: four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, two after it, two before ʿAṣr, two after Maghrib, and two before Ṣubḥ.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ; see the preceding hadith.]"
"4481","(4481) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Tinnīsī — informed us, al-Haytham ibn Ḥumayd — informed me, al-Nuʿmān, from Makḥūl, from ʿAnbasah, from Umm Ḥabībah, that she informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever is vigilant in maintaining four rakʿahs before the Ẓuhr prayer and four after it, he is forbidden to the Hellfire.""

Sulaymān ibn Mūsā narrated it from Makḥūl similarly. [Ḥasan; Aḥmad 32516–426]"
"4482","(4482) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in al-Fawāʾid: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah — informed us, al-Awzāʿī, from Ḥassān ibn ʬīyah, who said: When ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān was in the throes of death, his distress became severe. It was said to him: ""What is this distress?"" He said: ""Indeed, I heard Umm Ḥabībah — meaning his sister — say: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: 'Whoever prays four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr and four after it, Allah forbids his flesh to the Fire.'"" He said: ""I have not abandoned them since I heard them from her.""

[Ḥasan li-ghayrihi; al-Nasāʾī 3/265]"
"4483","(4483) And Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us through it in al-Ziyādāt, and Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Sūsī in the ḥadīth of al-Awzāʿī, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Munādī — informed us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah, and he mentioned it similarly.

[This is another chain of narration for the preceding hadith.]"
"4484","(4484) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us, Shuʿyb ibn al-Layth ibn Saʿd — informed us, al-Layth.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh — informed us, ʿUbayd ibn ʴd al-Wāḥid — informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr — informed us, al-Layth, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, from Abū Isḥāq al-Hamadānī, from ʿAmr ibn Aws al-Thaqafī, from ʿAnbasah ibn Abī Sufyān, from his sister Umm Ḥabībah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever prays twelve rakʿahs in a day, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise: four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, two rakʿahs after Ẓuhr, two rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, two rakʿahs after Maghrib, and two rakʿahs before Ṣubḥ.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ; see no. 4480 above.]"
"4485","(4485) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar — informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb — informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī — informed us, Abū Ibrāhīm — Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""May Allah have mercy upon the one who prays four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr."" Thus I found it in my book. [Munkar al-isnād]"
"4486","(4486) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī — informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah — informed us, Abū Dāwūd, that is al-Sijistānī — informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm — informed us, Abū Dāwūd — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Mihrān al-Qurashī — informed me, my grandfather, Abū al-Muthannā, from Ibn ʿUmar, and he mentioned it similarly.

This is the correct version. He is Abū Ibrāhīm Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muslim ibn Mihrān al-Qurashī, who heard from his grandfather Muslim ibn Mihrān. He is also called Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, that is, the son of Abū al-Muthannā, because the kunyah of Muslim was Abū al-Muthannā. Al-Bukhārī mentioned him in al-Tārīkh. That was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī — informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Aṣbahānī — informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn Fāris, from Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl.

The Shaykh said: And the statement of the narrator in the first isnād ""from his father"" — I consider it to be an error, and Allah knows best. A group narrated it from Abū Dāwūd without mentioning ""from his father,"" among them Salamah ibn Shabīb and others. [Ḥasan; al-Ṭayālisī 130]"
"4487","(4487) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Abū Isḥāq narrated to us, he said: I heard ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah say: We asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he mentioned that in his prayer there were four rakʿahs before Dhuhr, two rakʿahs after Dhuhr, and four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr."
"4488","(4488) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Pray two rakʿahs before Maghrib."" Then he said: ""Pray two rakʿahs before Maghrib, for whoever wishes"" — out of fear that the people might take it as a Sunnah.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Maʿmar, from ʿAbd al-Wārith, except that he said: ""In the third time he said: 'For whoever wishes,'"" out of dislike that the people might take it as a Sunnah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1183, 7368]"
"4489","(4489) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsā narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, Khamas ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Between every two adhāns there is a prayer"" — three times. Then he said on the third occasion: ""For whoever wishes.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abī Usāmah and Wakīʿ, from Khamas. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 624–627]"
"4490","(4490) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl both said: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Saʿdī informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, al-Jurayrī narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Between every two adhāns there is a prayer, between every two adhāns there is a prayer, between every two adhāns there is a prayer — for whoever wishes.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Saʿīd al-Jurayrī.

Ḥayyān ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, but he erred in his isnād and added an addition to which no one corroborated him. And in the narration of Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim there is what invalidates it and testifies to his error in it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it]"
"4491","(4491) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, Ḥayyān ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, between every two adhāns there are two rakʿahs, except for (the prayer of) Maghrib."" [Munkar]"
"4492","(4492) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — meaning Ibn Khuzaymah — said, following this ḥadīth: This Ḥayyān ibn ʿUbaydillāh has erred in the isnād, because Khamas ibn al-Ḥasan, Saʿīd ibn Iyās al-Jurayrī, and ʿAbd al-Muʾmin al-ʿAtakī all narrated the report from Ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal — not from his father. And this is my knowledge of the type of error that al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) used to describe as ""taking the path of the galaxy"" (i.e., a far-fetched error). For when this shaykh saw the narrations of Ibn Buraydah from his father, he supposed that this report was also from his father. And perhaps when he saw that the general people do not pray before Maghrib, he supposed that there is no prayer before Maghrib, so he added this word to the report. And it is established as an error that Ibn al-Mubārak said in his ḥadīth from Khamas: ""Ibn Buraydah used to pray two rakʿahs before Maghrib."" Had Ibn Buraydah heard from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, this exception that Ḥayyān ibn ʿUbaydillāh added in the report — ""except the prayer of Maghrib"" — he would not have contradicted the report of the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4493","(4493) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us with the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Mubārak, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Aʿlāʾ Abū Kurayb al-Hamdānī narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Khamas ibn al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mughaffal, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Between every two adhāns there is a prayer, between every two adhāns there is a prayer."" Then he said on the third occasion: ""For whoever wishes."" He (the narrator) said: And Ibn Buraydah used to pray two rakʿahs before Maghrib.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See no. 4489]"
"4494","(4494) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us, Abū Kurayb narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak and Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Khamas, and he mentioned the ḥadīth and said: ""Between every two adhāns there is a prayer."" He said on the third occasion: ""For whoever wishes."" He said: And Ibn Buraydah used to pray two rakʿahs before Maghrib. Thus it is in our narration.

[See the one before it]"
"4495","(4495) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Aḥmad Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to us, he said: I heard Abū al-Khayr say: I saw Abū Tamīm al-Jayshānī — ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik — praying two rakʿahs when he heard the adhān of Maghrib. So I came to ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī and said: Shall I not tell you something remarkable about Abū Tamīm — he prays two rakʿahs before Maghrib? ʿUqbah said: Indeed, we used to do that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: What prevents you now? He said: Preoccupation.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Muqriʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1184]"
"4496","(4496) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us, from Mukhtāri ibn Fulful, who said: I asked Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) about the prayer after ʿAṣr. He said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to strike (people) for praying after ʿAṣr. He said: And in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ we used to pray two rakʿahs after the setting of the sun, before the prayer of Maghrib. I said: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray them? He said: He used to see us praying them, and he neither commanded us nor forbade us.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 836]"
"4497","(4497) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us; Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith narrated from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were in Madinah. When the mu'adhdhin gave the adhan for the Maghrib prayer, the people would hasten to the pillars and perform two rak'ahs, to the extent that if a stranger entered the mosque, he would think that the prayer had already been performed, due to the large number of people performing them.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Shayban ibn Farrukh. [Sahih. Bukhari 503]"
"4498","(4498) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar narrated from al-Zuhri from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Muhajirun did not perform two rak'ahs before Maghrib, while the Ansar used to perform them. He said: And Anas used to perform them. Thus said Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib.

And we have narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: We used to perform them. And he was among the Muhajirun, and it was as though he intended others besides himself, or the majority of them. [Sahih. 'Abd al-Razzaq 3984]"
"4499","(4499) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan and Abu Muhammad al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad, both saying: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; 'Abbas ibn 'Abd Allah al-Turqufi informed us; Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' informed us; Sa'id, meaning Ibn Abi Ayyub, narrated to us; Abu Marhum narrated from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, who said: We used to perform them when we stood between the adhan and the iqamah of Maghrib.

In the narration of al-Sukkari: When we stood, meaning between the adhan and the iqamah of Maghrib. [Da'if]"
"4500","(4500) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Asid ibn 'Asim informed us; al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated from Sufyan from 'Asim from Zarr, who said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf and Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib.

Sufyan said: We follow the statement of Ibrahim. Sufyan said: And 'Amr ibn 'Amir narrated to me, saying: I heard Anas say: The senior Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would hasten to the pillars, praying two rak'ahs before Maghrib.

Sufyan intended by ""the statement of Ibrahim"" what he narrated from Mansur from Ibrahim, who said: Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, did not pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib.

Al-Bukhari narrated from Qabisah from Sufyan the hadith of 'Amr ibn 'Amir. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tahawi in Mushkil al-Athar 14/122]"
"4501","(4501) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahbub al-Tajir informed us in Marw; Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Sahl al-Tirmidhi informed us; Hisham ibn 'Ammar informed us; Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated from al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith from Makhul from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We did not leave the two rak'ahs before Maghrib during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. [Da'if]"
"4502","(4502) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Mahbubi informed us; Sa'id ibn Mas'ud informed us; al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us; Shu'bah narrated from Yazid ibn Khumayr, who said: I heard Khalid ibn Ma'dan from Raghban, the freedman of Habib ibn Muslimah, who said: I saw the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, hasten towards them as they hasten towards the obligatory prayer — meaning the two rak'ahs before Maghrib. [Da'if]"
"4503","(4503) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us; 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' informed us; Shu'bah narrated from Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman from Rashid ibn Yasar, who said: I bear witness regarding five men among those who gave bay'ah under the tree, among them Mirdas or Ibn Mirdas, that they used to pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib. [Da'if. Abu Nu'aym in Ma'rifat al-Sahabah 6198]"
"4504","(4504) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us; Ibn Abi Maryam informed us; Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me; Ibn Tawus narrated from his father that Abu al-Ayyub al-Ansari, in whose house the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, stayed, prayed with Abu Bakr after the setting of the sun before the prayer. Then he did not pray with 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Then he prayed with 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. This was mentioned to him, and he said: I prayed with the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, then I prayed with Abu Bakr, and I feared 'Umar, so I did not pray with him, and I prayed with 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, for he was gentle. And 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did not consider them valid, so Abu al-Ayyub did not pray them with him, and he prayed them with 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him.

And this is the meaning of what has been narrated from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah that he said: We revived them during the caliphate of 'Uthman — meaning after they had abandoned them during the era of 'Umar. And Allah knows best. [Hasan]"
"4505","(4505) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dassah informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ibn Bashshar narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Abu Shu'ayb from Tawus, who said: Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about the two rak'ahs before Maghrib. He said: I did not see anyone during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, praying them. And he gave permission regarding the two rak'ahs after 'Asr.

Abu Dawud said: I heard Yahya ibn Ma'in say: He is Shu'ayb. And Shu'bah erred in his name.

The Shaykh said: The ruling in such a case is the statement of one who witnessed over the one who did not witness. And with Allah lies success."
"4506","(4506) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, and Abu Sadiq ibn Abi al-Fawaris all informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed me; 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, Malik ibn Anas, al-Layth ibn Sa'd, and Usamah ibn Zayd narrated from Nafi' from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to pray two rak'ahs before Dhuhr and two rak'ahs after it, and two rak'ahs after the Maghrib prayer in his house, and two rak'ahs after the 'Isha' prayer. And he did not pray anything after Jumu'ah in the mosque until he departed, whereupon he would perform two prostrations.

Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their two Sahihs from the hadith of Malik. [Sahih. Bukhari and Muslim, in various places]"
"4507","(4507) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us; al-Ḥakam narrated to us, he said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jubayr narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""I spent the night in the house of my aunt Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed ʿIshāʾ, then came to his house and prayed four rakʿahs, then slept. Then he woke up and said: 'Has the young boy gone to sleep?' — or a word similar to that. Then he stood up, and I stood to his left, but he moved me to his right. He then prayed five rakʿahs, then prayed two rakʿahs, then slept until I heard his snoring (ghaṭīṭ) or his breathing (khaṭīṭ). Then he went out for the prayer.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 117]"
"4508","(4508) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us; Abū al-Ḥusayn Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb al-ʿUkhlī narrated to us; Mālik ibn Miḥwal informed me; Muqātil ibn Bashīr al-ʿIjlī informed me; from Sharīḥ ibn Hāniʾ; from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""I asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never prayed ʿIshāʾ and then came to me without praying four rakʿahs, or six rakʿahs. And we were once rained upon at night, so we spread out a leather mat for him, and it is as though I can see the hole in it from which water was seeping out. And I never saw him shield the ground from anything of his garments at all.'""

[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1303]"
"4509","(4509) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baghdādī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to me; Ibn Farrūkh narrated to me; Abū Farwah narrated to me; from Sālim al-Afṭas; from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr; from Ibn ʿAbbās, raising it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever prays four rakʿahs after the last ʿIshāʾ, reciting in the first two rakʿahs {Qul yā ayyuhā al-kāfirūn} and {Qul huwa Allāhu aḥad}, and reciting in the last two rakʿahs {Tabāraka alladhī bi yadihi al-mulk wa huwa ʿalā kulli shayʾ qadīr} and {Alif Lām Mīm tanzīl} [al-Sajdah], it will be written for him as though he had prayed four rakʿahs on Laylat al-Qadr.""

Ibn Farrūkh al-Miṣrī is alone in narrating it. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Kabīr 12240]"
"4510","(4510) The well-known narration is: Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated to us; from ʿAbd al-Malik; from ʬAṭāʾ; from Ayman, the freed slave of Ibn al-Zubayr; from Tubayʿ; from Kaʿb, who said: ""Whoever performs wuduʾ and perfects the wuduʾ, then prays the last ʿIshāʾ, then prays after it four rakʿahs, completing their rukūʿ and sujūd, knowing what he recites therein, it will be for him — or he said: they will be for him — equivalent in rank to Laylat al-Qadr.""

[Ḥasan. Nasāʾī 4954]"
"4511","(4511) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Mazzī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; it was read to Ibn Wahb: Ibn Lahīʿah and al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed you; from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb; from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaddād; from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Murrah; from Khārijah ibn Ḥudhāfah al-ʿAdawī, that he said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Indeed, Allah has aided you with a prayer that is better for you than red camels. It is for you what is between the ʿIshāʾ prayer and the rising of Fajr — al-Witr, al-Witr.'""

Al-Bukhārī said: ""It is not known whether any of its narrators heard directly from one another.""

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Ḥammād mention it on the authority of al-Bukhārī. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4512","(4512) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Sābiq, narrated to us; Shaybān narrated to us; from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr; from Abū Naḍrah: that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him: ""They asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about al-Witr, and he said: 'Al-Witr is before Ṣubḥ (Fajr).'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shaybān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 754]"
"4513","(4513) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us; from Maʿmar; from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr; from Abū Naḍrah; from Abū Saʿīd; from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: ""Perform witr before you reach morning.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. And similarly in meaning, a group narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr. And Qatādah narrated it from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever reaches the time of Ṣubḥ without having performed witr, then there is no witr for him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded in the one before it]"
"4514","(4514) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us; ʿAbdān ibn Yazīd al-Daqqāq in Hamadhān informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Kassāʾī narrated to us; Abū Salamah Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; from Qatādah, and he mentioned it. And the narration of Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr is as though it is more appropriate. And we have narrated from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, from the Prophet ﷺ, regarding the making up of al-Witr, and that will come, Allah the Most High willing. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded in the one before it]"
"4515","(4515) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-ʿAdl informed me; al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Qabbānī narrated to us; Surayj ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us; ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal narrated to us; from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaqīq; from Ibn ʿUmar: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Hasten to perform witr before Ṣubḥ.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Surayj ibn Yūnus and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 750]"
"4516","(4516) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām narrated to us; Ḥajjāj narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, they said: Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us; Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: Sulaymān ibn Mūsā informed me; Nāfiʿ informed us: that Ibn ʿUmar used to say: ""Whoever prays at night, let him make the last of his prayer witr, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that. And when Fajr comes, the time for night prayer and witr has elapsed, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Al-Witr is before Fajr.'"" And in the narration of al-Faḥḥām: ""Because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Perform witr at the time of Fajr.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 751]"
"4517","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd al-Baqi ibn Qani' al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ziyad ibn al-Khalil al-Tustari narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated from his father, from Hilal ibn 'Ali, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When any one of you wakes up in the morning and has not performed the Witr prayer, then let him perform the Witr."""
"4518","Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Sufyan al-Farisi informed us in Bukhari; Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to me; I heard Abu 'asim al-Nabil say, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ziyad, that Abu Nahik informed him, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him), that he delivered a sermon and said: ""Whoever is overtaken by the morning, there is no Witr for him."" This was mentioned to 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: ""Abu al-Darda' has erred. The Prophet ﷺ would wake up in the morning and then perform the Witr."" It was said to Abu 'asim: ""Who is between Ziyad and Ibn Jurayj?"" He said: ""Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, and Ziyad — meaning Ibn Sa'd — informed me."""
"4519","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us; Muhammad ibn Ghalib ibn Harb narrated to us; Hatim ibn Salim al-Basri narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated from Khalid al-Hadha', from Abu Qilabah, from Umm al-Darda', from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""I would sometimes see the Prophet ﷺ performing the Witr while the people had already stood up for the Fajr prayer."" This narration is unique to Hatim ibn Salim al-Basri, who is also called al-A'raj. The hadith of Ibn Jurayj is more authentic than that. And Allah knows best."
"4520","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; 'Amr ibn 'Asim narrated to us; Hammam narrated to us; Qatadah narrated from Abu Mijlaz, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet ﷺ woke up in the morning and then performed the Witr. Thus I found it in al-Fawa'id al-Kabir."
"4521","Abu 'Abd Allah and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr also informed us in this section, and both said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Sa'id — meaning ibn 'Amir — narrated from Shu'bah, from 'Asim, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: ""Ibn 'Umar woke up and had not performed the Witr — or he was about to wake up, or he woke up, if Allah the Most High wills — and then he performed the Witr."" And this is more likely, and Allah knows best."
"4522","Abu 'Abd Allah — Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi — narrated to us; Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Mu'mal ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Isa — narrated to us; al-Fadl ibn Muhammad al-Bayhaqi narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us. (H) And Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us; Abu Muhammad Dalaj ibn Ahmad al-Sajzi informed us in Baghdad; Abu Shu'ayb 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani informed me; Ahmad ibn Waqid al-Harrani narrated to me; Zuhayr narrated to us; Khalid ibn Abi Karimah narrated to me; Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah narrated from al-Agharr al-Muzani that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, I woke up in the morning and did not perform the Witr."" He said: ""The Witr is only at night."" — three times — or four times: ""Stand and perform the Witr."""
"4523","Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu 'Uthman 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah al-Basri narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Ya'la ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated from Abu Zabyan who said: ""'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to the market, and I was following him. He stood on the steps, faced the dawn, and recited: {By the night as it envelops, and by the day when it appears} (81:15–16). 'Where is the one who asks about the Witr? This is an excellent hour for the Witr.'"""
"4524","Through this same chain of narration, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami who said: ""'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out through this door and said: 'An excellent hour for the Witr.' Then the Iqamah was called at that moment."""
"4525","Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas — al-Asamm — narrated to us; Asid ibn 'Asim narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated from Sufyan, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: ""'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out when Ibn al-Nabah called the Adhan and recited: {By the night as it envelops, and by the day when it appears} (81:15–16). 'Where are those who ask about the Witr? This is an excellent hour for the Witr.'"""
"4526","Abu al-Hasan al-Muqri' informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Marzuq informed us; Zuhayr narrated from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, that a group of people came to 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about the Witr. He said: ""Have you asked anyone about it?"" They said: ""We asked Abu Musa, and he said: 'There is no Witr after the Adhan.'"" He said: ""He has gone to extremes and exceeded the bounds in issuing the legal ruling. Everything between you and the morning prayer is Witr. Whenever you perform the Witr, it is good."""
"4527","(4527) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us; Ismāʿīl — he is Ibn Abī Khālid — from Abū Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, said: ""The witr is between two prayers — the last prayer of ʿIshāʾ and the prayer of Fajr."" [Weak]"
"4528","(4528) And Abū Ḥāziṃ al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī in Baghdād informed us; ʿAlī, meaning Ibn al-Jaʿd, informed us; Zuhayr informed us, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Aswad, who said: ""I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd calling out with a call: 'The witr is between the two prayers — the prayer of ʿIshāʾ and the prayer of Fajr. Whenever you perform the witr, it is good.'"" [Good]"
"4529","(4529) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ informed us, from Sufyān; Abū Isḥāq narrated to us, from al-Aswad, who said: ""I asked ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: 'When do you perform the witr?' She said: 'Between the adhān and the iqāmah. And they would not give the adhān until they reached the morning.'"" He said: ""His statement: 'And they would not give the adhān until they reached the morning' — I think it is the statement of al-Aswad or Abū Isḥāq, and there is room for consideration in it. For we have narrated that the first adhān in the Ḥijāz was before Ṣubḥ, and it is as though ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to pray before the rising of Fajr, or she meant by it the second adhān. And the narration of Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid from Abū Isḥāq points to that, who said: 'ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to perform the witr between the tathwīb and the iqāmah.' Thus, her position on that goes back to what has been narrated from ʿAlī and ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with them both. And Allah knows best."" [Weak]"
"4530","(4530) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik informed us, from ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn Abī al-Mukhāriq, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, fell asleep, then woke up and said to his servant: ""See what the people have done."" At that time his eyesight had gone. The servant went, then returned and said: ""The people have departed from the Ṣubḥ prayer."" So ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās stood and performed the witr, then prayed the Ṣubḥ. [Weak]"
"4531","(4531) And with his chain of narration, he said: Mālik informed us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said: ""I would not care if the prayer were established while I am performing the witr."" [Weak]"
"4532","(4532) And with his chain of narration, he said: Mālik informed us, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, that he said: ""ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit used to lead our people in prayer. One day he went out for the Ṣubḥ, and the muʾadhdhin called the iqāmah for the prayer. ʿUbādah silenced him until he performed the witr, then he led them in the Ṣubḥ prayer.""

Mālik said: ""The one who performs the witr after Fajr is only one who slept through the witr. It is not befitting for anyone to deliberately do that until he places his witr after Fajr."" [Weak. Mālik 280]"
"4533","(4533) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn Dīnār informed us; Abū Ghassān Muḥammad ibn Muṭarrif informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from Abū Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever sleeps through his witr or forgets it, let him pray it when he wakes in the morning or remembers it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 3/31/11284]"
"4534","(4534) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Miʿsar informed us, from Wabarah, who said: ""I asked Ibn ʿUmar about one who leaves the witr until the sun rises — should he pray it?"" He said: ""Tell me, if you were to leave the prayer of Ṣubḥ until the sun rises, would you pray it?"" He said: ""I said: 'Then what?'"" He said: ""Then what?"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4535","(4535) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAyyāsh informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm informed us; Ibn Abī ʿAdī informed us; Shuʿbah informed us, from Ibrāhīm — meaning Ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir — from his father, that he was in the mosque of ʿAmr ibn Sharḥbīl when the prayer was established. They began waiting for him. He came and said: ""I was performing the witr."" He said: ""And ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — was asked: 'Is there a witr after the adhān?' He said: 'Yes, and after the iqāmah as well.'"" He said: ""And he narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he slept through the prayer until the sun rose, then he got up and prayed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4536","(4536) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us. He said: ""I read to Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that Ḥafṣah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when the muʾadhdhin fell silent from the adhān for the Ṣubḥ prayer and the morning had dawned, he would perform two light rakʿahs before the prayer was established.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 618]"
"4537","(4537) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdūyah ibn Sahl al-Marwazī narrated to us: Maḥmūd ibn Ādam al-Marwazī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father: that the Prophet ﷺ, when dawn broke, would pray two rakʿahs. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4538","(4538) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustūyah narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Ibn Qaʿnab and Abū Ṣāliḥ both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us from his father, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah, from his father: that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a man who was praying, and the prayer of Fajr had been established (iqāmah called). He ﷺ said something to him — we do not know what it was. He (the narrator) said: When we finished, we surrounded him (asking): ""What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to you?"" He replied: He ﷺ said: ""One of you is about to pray Fajr as four (rakʿahs)."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī without mentioning ""from his father."" Then al-Qaʿnabī said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah from his father — and his saying ""from his father"" in this hadith is an error. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 663]"
"4539","(4539) Al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Uwaysī from Ibrāhīm, and he said: from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man…"" Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Jaʿfar al-Fāryābī narrated to us: Manṣūr ibn Abī Muzāḥim and Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khālid both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, and he mentioned it in a manner similar to the narration of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Uwaysī, without them saying ""from his father"" in it."
"4540","(4540) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim, from Ibn Buḥaynah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man praying two rakʿahs, and the prayer had been established (iqāmah called). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Fajr is four! Fajr is four!"" Yaʿqūb said: The correct version is this one, and Ibrāhīm erred in his saying ""from his father."" [Munkar al-Isnād]"
"4541","(4541) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Muqriʾ informed us in Baghdad: Aḥmad ibn Salmān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Bishr ibn ʿUmar informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from Ḥafṣ ibn ʿĀṣim, from Mālik ibn Buḥaynah: that the Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque, and the prayer had been established (iqāmah called), while a man was praying two rakʿahs. He ﷺ said: ""Will you pray Fajr as four?"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the hadith of Shuʿbah. Likewise, Abū ʿAwānah and Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it from Saʿd. Al-Bukhārī said: And Ibn Isḥāq said from Saʿd, from Ḥafṣ, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buḥaynah. Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān said: And al-Awzāʿī and Shaybān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah: that the Prophet ﷺ passed by him. The Shaykh said: The correct view is the one who said ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah. He is ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn al-Qishb from Azd Shanūʾah, and his mother is Buḥaynah bint al-Ḥārith ibn al-Muṭṭalib — thus stated ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī. [Munkar al-Isnād]"
"4542","(4542) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father: that the Prophet ﷺ entered when the prayer of Fajr had been established (iqāmah called), and he passed by Ibn al-Qishb while he was praying. He ﷺ said: ""Ibn al-Qishb, will you pray Fajr as four?"" Thus said Sufyān. [Previously mentioned under no. 4538]"
"4543","(4543) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out for the Fajr prayer and Bilāl was with him. He (Bilāl) established the prayer (called the iqāmah), and the Prophet ﷺ passed by me. He struck my shoulder and said: ""Will you pray Fajr as four?"" [Previously mentioned under no. 4538]"
"4544","(4544) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar al-Bakrāwī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us: ʿĀṣim narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Sarjis, who said: A man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the Fajr prayer. He prayed two rakʿahs before reaching the row. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he said to him: ""O so-and-so, which of your two prayers will you count — the one you prayed alone, or the one you prayed with us?"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥāmid ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 712]"
"4545","(4545) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Abū ʿĀmir al-Khazzāz narrated from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: I was praying and the muʾadhdhin began the iqāmah. The Prophet ﷺ pulled me and said: ""Will you pray Fajr as four?"" [Ḥasan. Ṭayālisī 2859]"
"4546","(4546) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār said: I heard ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār narrate from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When the prayer has been established (iqāmah called), there is no prayer except the obligatory one."" And he ﷺ said on another occasion: ""When the prayer has been stood up (iqāmah called)."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb, from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned. Muslim 710]"
"4547","(4547) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) He said: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Warqāʾ.

(Ḥ) He said: Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Ibn Jurayj.

(Ḥ) He said: Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated from Ḥammād ibn Zayd from Ayyūb.

(Ḥ) He said: Muḥammad ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us; Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — all of them from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the Ṣalāh has been commenced, there is no Ṣalāh except the obligatory one (al-maktūbah).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, and from al-Ḥasan al-Ḥulwānī from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, and from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.

Yazīd ibn Hārūn added in his ḥadīth: Yazīd ibn Hārūn said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd said: ""Then I met ʿAmr and he narrated it to me, but he did not raise it (to the Prophet ﷺ)."""
"4548","(4548) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us with it; Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us — and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its addition."
"4549","(4549) Abū ʿAbd Allāh — al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Makhzūmī al-Ghaḍāʾirī — informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn ʿUmar narrated to us. And al-Sayyid Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ḥasanī informed us; Abū al-Aḥraz Muḥammad ibn ʿUmad ibn Jamīl al-Azdī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm of Dānūqā narrated to us; Zakariyyā ibn ʿAdī narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""When the Ṣalāh has been commenced, there is no Ṣalāh except the obligatory one.""

Zakariyyā said: Ḥammād said: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam narrated this once and raised it (to the Prophet ﷺ). A man said to him: ""You used not to raise it."" He said: ""Yes (I did)."" He said: ""No, by Allah!"" He then fell silent.

The Shaykh said: Those who raised it from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, other than those we have mentioned, are Ziyād ibn Saʿd, Muḥammad ibn Juḥādah, Abān ibn Yazīd al-Aṭṭār, Muḥammad ibn Muslim al-Ṭāʾifī, and a group (of narrators). [Ṣaḥīh. Previously mentioned.]"
"4550","(4550) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ismāʿīl al-Marwazī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Sayyār narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Naṣr ibn Ḥājib al-Marwazī narrated to us; Muslim ibn Khālid al-Zanjī narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the Ṣalāh has been commenced, there is no Ṣalāh except the obligatory one."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, not even the two rakʿahs of Fajr?"" He said: ""Not even the two rakʿahs of Fajr.""

Abū Aḥmad said: ""I do not know of anyone other than Yaḥyā ibn Naṣr who mentioned this addition in its matn, from Muslim ibn Khālid from ʿAmr.""

The Shaykh said: ""It has been said that Aḥmad ibn Sayyār narrated it from Naṣr ibn Ḥājib, and that is an error. Naṣr ibn Ḥājib al-Marwazī is not strong (laysa bi-l-qawī), and his son Yaḥyā likewise. And in the authentic aḥādīth we have cited as evidence there is sufficient proof without this addition, and with Allah is the success.

It has also been narrated from Ḥajjāj ibn Nuṣayr from ʿAbbād ibn Kathīr from Layth from ʿAṭāʾ from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When the Ṣalāh has been commenced, there is no Ṣalāh except the obligatory one, except the two rakʿahs of al-Ṣubḥ (Fajr).'""

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Dāwūd narrated to us; Abū ʿAmr al-Ḥalabī al-Sūsī narrated to us; Ḥajjāj ibn Nuṣayr narrated it.

""This addition has no basis (lā aṣla lahā). Ḥajjāj ibn Nuṣayr and ʿAbbād ibn Kathīr are both weak (ḍaʿīfān).""

It has also been narrated from Ḥajjāj with his isnād from Mujāhid instead of ʿAṭāʾ, and it is nothing.

And we have narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) that when he saw a man praying while the Iqāmah was being called, he would strike him. [Weak]"
"4551","(4551) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Hudbah narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Ayyūb from Nāʿif from Ibn ʿUmar: ""He saw a man praying the two rakʿahs while the muʾadhdhin was calling the Iqāmah, so he pelted him with pebbles and said: 'Are you praying al-Ṣubḥ as four (rakʿahs)?'"" Mawqūf. [Ṣaḥīh. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 4006]"
"4552","(4552) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated from Saʿd ibn Saʿīd; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to me from Qays ibn ʿAmr who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying two rakʿahs after the Ṣalāh of al-Ṣubḥ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The Ṣalāh of al-Ṣubḥ is two rakʿahs.' The man said: 'I had not prayed the two rakʿahs that come before it, so I am praying them now.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell silent.""

Abū Dāwūd said: Ḥāmid ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: Sufyān said: ""ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ used to narrate this ḥadīth from Saʿd ibn Saʿīd.""

Abū Dāwūd said: ʿAbd Rabbih and Yaḥyā, the two sons of Saʿīd, narrated this ḥadīth in a mursal form: that their grandfather prayed with the Prophet ﷺ. The Shaykh said: ""It has also been narrated from another route."" [Munkar. Ibn Rajab in al-Fatḥ 3/318]"
"4553","(4553) From Yaḥyā from his father from his grandfather: ""He came while the Prophet ﷺ was praying the Ṣalāh of Fajr, so he prayed with him. When he (the Prophet ﷺ) gave the Salām, he stood and prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'What are these two rakʿahs?' He said: 'I had not prayed them before Fajr.' He fell silent and said nothing.""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Asad ibn Mūsā narrated to us; al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — and he mentioned it. [Munkar. Previously mentioned.]"
"4554","(4554) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Yazīd ibn Kaysān; he said: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""We encamped with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and did not wake up until the sun had risen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Let each of you take hold of the head of his mount, for this is a place where Shayṭān has been present with us.' Then he called for water and performed Wuḍūʾ, then he prostrated two prostrations. Then the Ṣalāh was commenced and he prayed al-Ghadāh (Fajr).""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim and others from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān.

(T) And in this story it has been narrated from Abū Qatādah and ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn from the Prophet ﷺ that he made up those two rakʿahs. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 680]"
"4555","(4555) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; Hammām narrated from Qatādah from al-Naḍr ibn Anas from Bashīr ibn Nahīk from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever does not pray the two rakʿahs of al-Ghadāh, let him pray them when the sun has risen."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 1117]"
"4556","(4556) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Abū Badr ʿAbbād ibn al-Walīd al-Ghubarī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us — and he mentioned it with a similar isnād, except that he said: from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever does not pray the two rakʿahs of Fajr until the sun rises, let him pray them.""

ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim is the only one who narrated it exclusively (tafarrada bihi), and Allah, the Most High, knows best. (J) And ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim is trustworthy (thiqah). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
"4557","(4557) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you, from Ibn Shihāb, that al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtba informed him, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, who said: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever sleeps through his nightly portion (ḥizb) or part of it, and then recites it between the Fajr prayer and the Ẓuhr prayer, it shall be recorded for him as though he had recited it at night.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmala, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 747]"
"4558","(4558) Mālik narrated it in al-Muwaṭṭaʾ from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz al-Aʿraj, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Whoever misses his nightly portion (ḥizb) and recites it when the sun declines until the Ẓuhr prayer, it is as though he did not miss it, or as though he caught it on time.""

Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ibn Qaʿnab and Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Mālik, and he mentioned it as a mawqūf narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 470]"
"4559","(4559) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Aḥmad al-Fārisī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Tawba al-Ṣūfī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Ḥātim al-Āmulī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muʿāwiya al-Jumaḥī narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, that Ibn ʿUmar used not to pray at the beginning of the day until the sun had declined. He said: One day he prayed and was asked about that, and that was after the sun had risen. He said: ""I had not yet prayed the two rakʿas of the morning (al-ghadāh)."" [Ḥasan]"
"4560","(4560) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us: that it reached him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar missed the two rakʿas of Fajr and prayed them after the sun had risen. Mālik said: And it reached me from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad the same. And Sufyān narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"4561","(4561) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salama narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Dhakwān, from ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her), from Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me after ʿAṣr and prayed two rakʿas. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what are these two rakʿas? You did not use to pray them."" He said: ""I used to pray them after Ẓuhr, but wealth came to me and distracted me from them, so I prayed them now."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1233–4370]"
"4562","(4562) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Baḥr al-Barrī informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Abū ʿAwāna narrated to us, from Qatāda, from Zurāra ibn Awfā, from Saʿd ibn Hishām, from ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he missed a prayer during the night due to pain or some other reason, would pray twelve rakʿas during the day.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 746]"
"4563","(4563) Shuʿba narrated it from Qatāda and added: ""When he performed a deed, he would make it consistent."" Then he said: ""And when he slept at night or fell ill, he would pray twelve rakʿas during the day.""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Dārimī narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Khashram narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us, from Shuʿba, and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Khashram. [Ṣaḥīḥ. As in the preceding hadith]"
"4564","(4564) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you, from Ibn Shihāb, that al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtba informed him, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, who said: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever sleeps through his nightly portion (ḥizb) or part of it, and then recites it between the Fajr prayer and the Ẓuhr prayer, it shall be recorded for him as though he had recited it at night.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmala, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. As in hadith no. 4557]"
"4565","(4565) Mālik narrated it in al-Muwaṭṭaʾ from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz al-Aʿraj, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Whoever misses his nightly portion (ḥizb) and recites it when the sun declines until the Ẓuhr prayer, it is as though he did not miss it, or as though he caught it on time.""

Abū Aḥmad al-ʿAdl informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Mālik narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ibn Qaʿnab and Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Mālik, and he mentioned it as a mawqūf narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. As in hadith no. 4558]"
"4566","(4566) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us; Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us; al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated to us; Shuʿba narrated to us, from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, who said: I heard Abū Salama, from ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: ""Which deeds are most beloved to Allah?"" He said: ""Those that are most consistent, even if they are few.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Shuʿba."
"4567","(4567) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd narrated to us, my father informed me, al-Awzāʿī narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Hishām narrated to us from Maʿdān ibn Ṭalḥah, who said: I said to Thawbān, the freed servant of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""Direct me to a deed by which Allah will benefit me."" He remained silent. I said: ""Direct me to a deed by which Allah will benefit me."" He remained silent. I said: ""Direct me to a deed by which Allah will benefit me."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'There is no servant who prostrates to Allah a single prostration, except that Allah raises him thereby one degree and erases from him thereby one sin.'"" Maʿdān said: ""Then I met Abū al-Dardāʾ and he narrated to me the same."" In the narration of al-Sūsī alone, it is Maʿdān ibn Abī Ṭalḥah. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Walīd ibn Muslim from al-Awzāʿī, and he added therein: ""Upon you is prostration to Allah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 488]"
"4568","(4568) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd informed us, my father informed me, al-Awzāʿī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us, Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, Rabiʿah ibn Kaʿb al-Aslamī narrated to us, who said: ""I used to spend the night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, bringing him his wuḍūʾ and what he needed. He would stand at night and say: 'Subḥāna Rabbiya wa bi-ḥamdihi, Subḥāna Rabbiya wa bi-ḥamdihi' — for a long time. Then: 'Subḥāna Rabb al-ʿĀlamīn, Subḥāna Rabb al-ʿĀlamīn' — for a long time."" Rabiʿah said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: 'Do you have a need?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, your companionship in Paradise.' He said: 'Something other than that?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, your companionship in Paradise.' He said: 'Then help yourself in this by increasing your prostrations.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hiql ibn Ziyād from al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tirmidhī 3416]"
"4569","(4569) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us from Sufyān, from Zubayd, from Murrah, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: ""So long as you are in prayer, you are knocking at the door of the King. And whoever knocks frequently at the door of the King, it will be opened for him."" [Ḥasan]"
"4570","(4570) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūṣuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation. Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Shaybānī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us from Mālik ibn Anas.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik, from Nāfiʿ and ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the night prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The night prayer is two by two (rakʿahs). When any of you fears the dawn, let him pray one rakʿah which will make odd what he has already prayed.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 749]"
"4571","(4571) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Sūsī and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq al-Baṣrī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us from Shuʿbah, from ʿUqbah ibn Ḥurayth, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The night prayer is two by two (rakʿahs). When you see that the dawn will overtake you, then make witr with one rakʿah."" A man said to Ibn ʿUmar: ""What does mathnā mathnā mean?"" He said: ""You give salām after every two rakʿahs.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muthannā from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous]"
"4572","(4572) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Abī Dhiʾb and Yūnus ibn Yazīd and ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that Ibn Shihāb informed them from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to pray between the time he finished the ʿIshāʾ prayer and the Fajr, eleven rakʿahs, giving salām after every two rakʿahs, and making witr with one rakʿah. He would prostrate in a prostration the length of which any of you would recite fifty verses before raising his head. When the muʾadhdhin fell silent from the Fajr prayer and the dawn became clear to him, he would stand and pray two light rakʿahs, then lie down on his right side until the muʾadhdhin came to him for the iqāmah, whereupon he would go out with him."" He said: ""Some of them add over others.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith and Yūnus ibn Yazīd regarding the salām after every two rakʿahs, similarly.

Abū Sulaymān al-Khaṭṭābī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Mubārak from al-Awzāʿī from al-Zuhrī, and he said: ""When the muʾadhdhin called the first adhān of Fajr."" Suwayd said: ""Sakaba means he gave adhān, and it is from al-ṣabb (pouring)."" [Bukhārī and Muslim in numerous places]"
"4573","(4573) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us. And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us, Shuʿbah narrated to us from Yaʿlā ibn ʿAṭāʾ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Bāriqī from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The night prayer and the day prayer are two by two (rakʿahs).""

And similarly Ghandar narrated it from Shuʿbah. [See previous]"
"4574","(4574) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAlī al-Khaṭabī in Baghdād informed us. I read to him and he confirmed it: Ḥusayn ibn Fahm narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn narrated to us, Ghandar narrated to us from Shuʿbah, from Yaʿlā ibn ʿAṭāʾ al-Azdī, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The night prayer and the day prayer are two by two (rakʿahs).""

And similarly Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated it from Shuʿbah, and similarly ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Ḥusayn narrated it from Yaʿlā ibn ʿAṭāʾ. [See previous]"
"4575","(4575) Abū Bakr al-Fārisī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Aṣfahānī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris narrated to us, who said: Abū ʿAbdillāh — meaning al-Bukhārī — was asked about the ḥadīth of Yaʿlā: ""Is it ṣaḥīḥ?"" He said: ""Yes."" (T) Abū ʿAbdillāh said: And Saʿīd ibn Jubayr said: ""Ibn ʿUmar would not pray four rakʿahs without separating them, except the obligatory prayer."""
"4576","(4576) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us. It was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed you from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdillāh from Ibn Abī Salamah from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, who narrated to him that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar say: ""The night prayer and the day prayer are two by two (rakʿahs)"" — meaning thereby the voluntary (prayer).

And similarly al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated it from ʿAmr. And Ibn Abī Salamah is ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Salamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"4577","(4577) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from ʿAbd Rabbihī ibn Saʿīd, from ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ ibn al-ʿAmyāʾ, from Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith, from al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The prayer is two and two (i.e., in units of two rakʿahs). One recites the tashahhud after every two rakʿahs, then one displays humility, submissiveness, and lowliness, and raises one's hands, turning one's face toward them, saying: 'O Lord, O Lord!' Whoever does not do so, his prayer is deficient (khidāj).""

Shuʿbah contradicted him in its chain of narration. [Weak]

---"
"4578","(4578) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar, Abū al-Naḍr, Rūḥ, Fahd ibn Ḥayyān, and Wahb ibn Jarīr said: Shuʿbah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd Rabbihī ibn Saʿīd, from Anas ibn Abī Anas, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ ibn al-ʿAmyāʾ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith, from al-Muṭṭalib, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The prayer is two and two, and one recites the tashahhud after every two rakʿahs. One displays humility and lowliness, and raises one's hands, saying: 'O Allah, O Allah.' Whoever does not do so, it is deficient, it is deficient.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū Dāwūd. In their ḥadīth it states: ""And you raise your hands and say: 'O Allah.' If you do not do so, it is deficient.""

And in what I read in the Kitāb al-ʿIlal of Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī, he said: I heard Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī say regarding this ḥadīth: ""The narration of al-Layth ibn Saʿd from ʿAbd Rabbihī ibn Saʿīd is more authentic than the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, and Shuʿbah erred in this ḥadīth in several places."" He said: ""He said: 'From Anas ibn Abī Anas,' when it is actually ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas."" And he said: ""He said: 'From ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith,' when it is actually from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ, from Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith."" And Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith is the son of al-Muṭṭalib, so he said it is from al-Muṭṭalib. And he did not mention in it: ""From al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās."" [Weak/Rejected chain]

---"
"4579","(4579) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿUbaydah narrated from Ibrāhīm, from Ibn Minjāb, from al-Qarthaʿ, from Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray four rakʿahs when the sun passed its meridian. Abū Ayyūb said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is this prayer?"" He said: ""The gates of the heavens are opened when the sun passes its meridian, and they are not closed until the Ẓuhr prayer is performed. I would like that some good (deeds) of mine ascend before the gates of the heavens are closed."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you recite in them, or is recitation made in them?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Is there a separating salām in them?"" He said: ""No, except in the last of them.""

Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated something similar from ʿUbaydah ibn Muʿattab. [Weak]

---"
"4580","(4580) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; ʿUbaydah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Rabīʿ also narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us; ʿUbaydah narrated from Ibrāhīm, from Sahm ibn Minjāb, from Qazaʿah, from al-Qarthaʿ, from Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ regularly performed four rakʿahs which he would pray when the sun passed its meridian in the house of Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī. He said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is this prayer that you pray?'"" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth with its meaning.

He said: ""And this is the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyā, and it is the more complete.""

And likewise Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālīsī and others narrated it from Shuʿbah, from ʿUbaydah. And it was said: from Shuʿbah, from ʿUbaydah, from Ibrāhīm, from Sahm ibn Minjāb, from Qazaʿah, from Qarthaʿ, from Abū Ayyūb. And it was said: from Qarthaʿ, from Qazaʿah, and that is an error. (C) And ʿUbaydah ibn Muʿattab is weak; his report is not used as evidence.

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; he said: Abū Dāwūd said: ""It has reached me from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān: 'Were I to narrate anything from ʿUbaydah, I would narrate this ḥadīth from him.'"" Abū Dāwūd said: ""ʿUbaydah is weak."" [Weak]

---"
"4581","(4581) The Shaykh said: It has been narrated from another source, which is not strong, from Abū Ayyūb. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū Qilābah narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd al-Ḥanafī narrated to us; Sharīk narrated from al-Aʿmash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ, from ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣalt, from Abū Ayyūb, from the Prophet ﷺ.

And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Muʾammal narrated from Sufyān, from al-Aʿmash, from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ, from a man, from Abū Ayyūb, that the Prophet ﷺ used to pray four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr. It was said to him: ""You persist in this prayer."" He said: ""When the sun passes its meridian, the gates of the heavens are opened, and I would like to send forth (good deeds) before they are closed."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Sufyān.

And the established ḥadīth has come regarding the permissibility of five (rakʿahs) in which one does not recite the tashahhud or give salām except in the last of them, in the case of Witr. And (likewise) the permissibility of nine (rakʿahs) in which one does not sit except in the eighth, and does not give salām except in the ninth. And that is also mentioned regarding Witr. [Weak]

---"
"4582","(4582) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf narrated to us; Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us; al-Awzāʿī narrated to us; he said: Hārūn ibn Riʾāb narrated to me; he said: al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays entered the mosque of Damascus and saw a man performing excessive rukūʿ and sujūd. He said: ""By Allah, I shall not depart until I see whether he concludes on an even (shafʿ) or an odd (witr) number."" He said: ""When the man completed his prayer, he said to him: 'O servant of Allah, do you know whether you concluded on an even or an odd number?'"" He said: ""Would that I knew! For Allah knows. Indeed, I heard my intimate friend Abū al-Qāsim—may the blessings and peace of Allah be upon him—say,"" and then he wept, then he said: ""Indeed, I heard my intimate friend Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ say: 'There is no servant who prostrates to Allah a single prostration except that Allah raises him thereby a degree, and erases thereby a sin from him.'"" He said: al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays then said: ""Who are you, may Allah have mercy on you?"" He said: ""Abū Dharr."" He said: ""Then I felt small within myself regarding what I had thought about him."" [Sound. ʿAbd al-Razzāq 3561]

---"
"4583","(4583) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawīh informed us; Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us; Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated to us. And he said: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, from Ibn Sīrīn, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaqīq, who said: ""I asked ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" She said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would perform much prayer standing and sitting. When he commenced the prayer standing, he would bow (rukūʿ) standing, and when he commenced the prayer sitting, he would bow sitting.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Sound. Muslim 732]

---"
"4584","(4584) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; al-Jurarī narrated.

(Ḥ) And he said: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me; Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraʿī narrated from Saʿīd al-Jurarī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaqīq, who said: ""I said to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her): 'Did the Prophet ﷺ pray while sitting?'"" She said: ""Yes, after the people had wearied him.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Sound. Muslim 732]

---"
"4585","(4585) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; he said: Ibn Jurayj said: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Sulaymān informed me that Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed him that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) informed him: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not pass away until most of his prayer was performed while he was sitting.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim and others, from Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad. [Sound. Muslim 732]

---"
"4586","(4586) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū ʿUtbah narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to me; al-Ḍaḥḥāk, meaning Ibn ʿUthmān, narrated to me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""Most of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he became heavy and his body became burdensome, was performed while he was sitting.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān. [Sound. As previously mentioned]"
"4587","(4587) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to me, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn, Mūsā ibn Muḥammad, and Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī al-Dhuhlī — all three — said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us. He said: I read before Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadāʿah al-Sahmī, from Ḥafṣah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray his supererogatory prayers sitting down until one year before his death. Then he would pray his supererogatory prayers sitting, and he would recite the sūrah and recite it with measured recitation (tartīl) so that it became longer than [any other sūrah] longer than it.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 733]"
"4588","(4588) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓufr ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharazah informed us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Simāk, from Jābir ibn Samurah, that the Prophet ﷺ did not pass away until he was praying while sitting.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 734]"
"4589","(4589) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us, both saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she informed him: ""She never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray the night prayer (ṣalāt al-layl) sitting down at all, until he grew old. Then he would recite while sitting, and when he wished to bow, he would stand and recite approximately thirty or forty verses, then bow.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it through other routes from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1118]"
"4590","(4590) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us, al-Qaʿnabī — in what he read before Mālik — and Abū Naṣr ibn ʿUmar informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us. He said: I read before Mālik, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd and Abū al-Naḍr, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray sitting and recite while sitting. When approximately thirty or forty verses of his recitation remained, he would stand and recite while standing, then bow, then prostrate. Then he would do the same in the second rakʿah.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See above]"
"4591","(4591) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated from al-Walīd ibn Abī Hishām, from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet ﷺ would recite while sitting. When he wished to bow, he would stand for the duration in which a person would recite forty verses.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah."
"4592","(4592) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us, Isḥāq ibn al-Azraq informed us, Ḥusayn al-Muktab informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān informed us, Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us, Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Abū al-Muthannā informed us, Musaddad informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith informed us, Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: ""I asked the Prophet ﷺ about a man's prayer while sitting, and he said: 'Whoever prays standing, that is most excellent. Whoever prays sitting, he receives half the reward of the one who stands. And whoever prays lying down, he receives half the reward of the one who sits.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wārith. In the ḥadīth of Isḥāq, it is: ""He asked the Prophet ﷺ about the prayer of the one who sits,"" and the remainder is the same. In the ḥadīth of Yazīd: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Maʿmar, from ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 371]"
